    "num_ad_vjp" => "Vector-Jacobian product `weights^T J` in one shared-tape reverse sweep. # Errors As [`forward_gradient`], plus mismatched or non-finite output weights.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_airy_ai" => "Airy Ai function. The Airy function `Ai(x)`. `Ai(x) -> 0` as `x -> +-inf`; for `x <= -10` the oscillatory asymptotic expansion is used (the Taylor continuation from the origin is only used on `(-10, 0]`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_airy_ai_prime" => "`Ai'(x)` (no limit at `-inf`: NaN there).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_airy_bi" => "The Airy function `Bi(x)`: `+inf` from `x > 105` (overflow) and at `+inf`, `0` at `-inf`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_airy_bi_prime" => "`Bi'(x)` (`+inf` from `x > 105`; no limit at `-inf`: NaN there).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_auto_solve_conjugate_gradient_tape" => "Actual native basin auto_solve_conjugate_gradient instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Minimises `problem` with nonlinear conjugate gradients. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_auto_solve_tape" => "Actual native basin auto_solve instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Runs any [`SolverKind`] on `problem`. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_band_lu_factor" => "Actual native band LU; exact triplet bounds and band widths are checked before allocation; singularity remains None. Factors the matrix given by triplets `(row, col, value)` (entries outside the band are an error). Returns `None` when singular or when an entry lies outside the band.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_band_lu_solve" => "Actual native band-factor solve in normalized coordinates. Solves `A x = b`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_mobius_apply" => "Native complex mobius_apply; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The image of `z` (infinite at the pole `-d / c`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_mobius_compose" => "Native complex mobius_compose; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `self ∘ other`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_mobius_identity" => "Native complex mobius_identity; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The identity map.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_mobius_inverse" => "Native complex mobius_inverse; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The inverse map (up to the projective scale).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_mobius_new" => "Native complex mobius_new; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `(a z + b) / (c z + d)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_arg" => "Native complex_scalar arg; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The argument, `arg z`, in `(-π, π]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_complex" => "Native complex_scalar complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `re + im i`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_cos" => "Native complex_scalar cos; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `cos z`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_magnitude" => "Native complex_scalar magnitude; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The modulus, `|z|`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_abs" => "Native complex_scalar method_abs; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The modulus.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_arg" => "Native complex_scalar method_arg; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The argument, in `(-π, π]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_conj" => "Native complex_scalar method_conj; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The conjugate.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_div" => "Native complex_scalar method_div; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `self / other`. Finite inputs with a nonzero divisor use binary scaling when their components are large or small enough to lose intermediate products. The zero divisor and nonfinite arguments follow the direct IEEE componentwise formula.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_exp" => "Native complex_scalar method_exp; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `exp(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_ln" => "Native complex_scalar method_ln; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `ln(self)`, the principal branch.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_mul" => "Native complex_scalar method_mul; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `self * other`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_new" => "Native complex_scalar method_new; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. A complex number.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_recip" => "Computes the complex reciprocal 1/z with a scaled evaluation when intermediate products leave the ordinary floating-point range. Returns the inverse physical dimension of the input. Zero or a nonfinite result produces a domain error. The reciprocal `1 / self`, with the scaling and special points of [`Self::div`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_method_sqrt" => "Returns the principal complex square root, with the imaginary sign selecting the side of the negative real axis. Rescaling protects finite large inputs and the quotient preserves representable small components. The result carries the square root of the input dimension. The principal square root, with a nonnegative real part for finite inputs. The sign of the imaginary part selects the side of the negative real axis, including when it is signed zero. Binary scaling avoids an intermediate overflow when a finite input's modulus exceeds `f64::MAX`. Nonfinite inputs follow the componentwise IEEE midpoint formula.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_norm_squared" => "Native complex_scalar norm_squared; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `z * conj(z)` is real; this returns that real part, `|z|^2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_pow" => "Native complex_scalar pow; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `z^n` for an integer exponent, by repeated squaring.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_powc" => "Native complex_scalar powc; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `z^w = exp(w ln z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_complex_scalar_sin" => "Native complex_scalar sin; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `sin z`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_convergence_levin_condition_number" => "Native convergence levin_condition_number; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Condition number `kappa = sum_j |w_j|` at the highest order the term count supports. [`LevinVariant::V`] consumes one extra look-ahead term. The transform is a weighted mean of the partial sums, `L = sum_j w_j s_j` with `sum_j w_j = 1`. Those weights are the solution of the Vandermonde-type system in the nodes `beta + n + j` that the Fessler-Ford-Smith recurrence inverts implicitly, so `kappa` — the 1-norm of that solution — is the factor by which rounding in `s_j` is amplified into the estimate, and a lower bound for the 1-norm condition number of the system. The accuracy floor of the transform is `kappa * eps`. `kappa` depends on the series, which is why `terms` is an argument: alternating terms give a convex combination with `kappa = 1`, while positive logarithmically convergent terms give weights of alternating sign whose absolute sum grows by a factor `~3.5` per order (for `1/(n+1)^2`: `4.6e5` at order 11, `2.3e8` at order 16). Returns infinity for a nonpositive or non-finite `beta`, an invalid remainder in the required prefix, or a singular or unrepresentable condition number. Fewer than two terms return `1.0` by convention.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_convergence_levin_condition_numbers" => "Returns one dimensionless Levin condition number per available order, from order zero. Infinite values indicate an undefined or unbounded condition number. Measured terms may share any dimension, beta is dimensionless, and the input budget is 4096 terms. Estimates the Levin weight condition number for each order starting at zero. Each entry is the absolute sum of the normalized weights for its order. This measures sensitivity to the partial sums with fixed remainder estimates. A singular or unrepresentable order contributes infinity at its original position. The profile ends at the first invalid remainder estimate. [`LevinVariant::V`] uses one additional term beyond each order's prefix. A nonpositive or non-finite `beta` gives an empty profile. The recurrence uses quadratic time in the valid prefix length and linear workspace in the number of terms. ``` use rssn::kernels::convergence::LevinVariant; use rssn::kernels::convergence::levin_condition_numbers; let profile = levin_condition_numbers(&[1.0, -0.5, 0.25], LevinVariant::T, 1.0); assert_eq!(profile.len(), 3); assert!(profile.iter().all(|kappa| (kappa - 1.0).abs() < 1e-14)); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_convergence_levin_sum_lazy" => "Native convergence levin_sum_lazy; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Sums a series whose terms arrive lazily, one at a time. Pulls at most `max_terms` items from `terms` — an unbounded generator is fine — and transforms the terms directly with [`levin_transform_terms`], so the acceleration never depends on differencing rounded partial sums. `beta` is the shift of the remainder estimate (`1.0` is usual). Returns `(estimate, error_estimate)`, chosen by the same smallest-successive-difference rule as [`levin_sum`]. # Errors Returns an error if `max_terms` is below four, if the iterator yields fewer than four terms, if a term is not finite, or if no finite transform can be formed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_convergence_levin_transform" => "Native convergence levin_transform; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Levin-type sequence transformation of the partial sums `s_0, s_1, ...`. With the term `a_n = s_n - s_{n-1}` (`a_0 = s_0`) and the remainder estimate `omega_n` of the chosen [`LevinVariant`], the transform `L_k^{(0)}` is computed with the stable Fessler-Ford-Smith recurrence for numerator and denominator, `L_k^{(n)} = N_k^{(n)} / D_k^{(n)}` with `N_{k+1}^{(n)} = N_k^{(n+1)} - (beta+n)(beta+n+k)^{k-1} / (beta+n+k+1)^k N_k^{(n)}`. Order zero uses the first partial sum and later orders use more terms. `beta` is the shift (`1.0` is the usual choice). The result lists finite estimates in order and omits any non-finite estimates. The terms are recovered by differencing, which cancels the leading digits that consecutive partial sums share; when the terms are available directly, [`levin_transform_terms`] avoids that loss.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_convergence_levin_transform_terms" => "Native convergence levin_transform_terms; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Levin-type transformation of the terms `a_0, a_1, ...` themselves. Same transform as [`levin_transform`], but the terms are used as given instead of being recovered from rounded partial sums by `a_n = s_n - s_{n-1}`. That difference throws away the leading digits `s_n` and `s_{n-1}` share — most of them late in a slowly converging series — so this entry point keeps the full precision of the terms. The partial sums are accumulated in the order the terms are given. This is the entry point for a series whose terms are known in closed form or produced one at a time (see [`levin_sum_lazy`]).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_cartesian_to_spherical" => "Converts the fixed shape [x, y, z] to [radius, polar angle from +z, azimuth from +x]. Express all lengths in one reference and use radians for both angles. The polar angle is zero at the origin. Converts Cartesian `[x, y, z]` to spherical `[r, theta, phi]`. `theta` is measured from the positive z axis in `[0, π]` and `phi` is the azimuth from the positive x axis in `[-π, π]` (both in radians). With horizontal length `h = hypot(x, y)`, the polar angle is `atan2(h, z)`. It is zero at the origin. Finite coordinates can produce an infinite radius if the norm overflows, with both angles still defined. An infinite z component produces a NaN polar angle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_from_cartesian" => "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Converts a Cartesian point to the system `to`. # Errors Returns an error if `point` has the wrong number of components (2 for polar, 3 for cylindrical and spherical).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_from_cartesian_pure" => "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Converts a Cartesian point to the system `to`, without checking its length: missing components are taken as zero. The polar angle of the origin in spherical coordinates is 0.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_jacobian_determinant" => "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Determinant of the Jacobian of the map `from -> to` at `at_point`. This is the local volume-element factor `dV_to = |det J| dV_from` of the two coordinate charts: for example `r` for polar-to-Cartesian and `r^2 sin(theta)` for spherical-to-Cartesian, while the inverse maps carry the reciprocal determinant. It is built from the central-difference Jacobian of [`numerical_jacobian`] and inherits its accuracy and precision caveat (no analytic Jacobian). # Errors As [`numerical_jacobian`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_numerical_jacobian" => "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Jacobian `J[i][j] = d(to_i) / d(from_j)` of the map `from -> to` at `at_point`, by central differences. # Errors Returns an error if the point is invalid for the transformation, see [`transform_point`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_spherical_to_cartesian" => "Converts [radius, polar angle from +z, azimuth from +x] to [x, y, z]. The incoming angles are radians and the result uses the same length reference as the radius. A negative radius reflects the point through the origin. Converts spherical `[r, theta, phi]` in radians to Cartesian `[x, y, z]`. Uses the angles in [`cartesian_to_spherical`]. A negative radius reflects the Cartesian point through the origin. See [DLMF 1.5.16] for the map. [DLMF 1.5.16]: https://dlmf.nist.gov/1.5.E16\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_to_cartesian" => "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Inverse map to Cartesian: converts a point of `from` to Cartesian. # Errors Returns an error if `point` has the wrong number of components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_to_cartesian_pure" => "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Converts a point of `from` to Cartesian coordinates, without checking its length: missing components are taken as zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_transform_point" => "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Transforms `point` from the system `from` to the system `to`, going through Cartesian coordinates, with validation of the dimensions. # Errors Returns an error if `point` has the wrong number of components for `from`, or if the dimension of `from` and `to` differ (2D polar against 3D systems).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_coordinates_transform_point_pure" => "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Like [`transform_point`] but without validation: a direct, allocation light evaluation for hot loops and for differentiating the map. Missing components are taken as zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_cubature_genz_malik_compiled" => "Native Genz-Malik cubature: physical same-unit coordinates, independently measured output, explicit evaluation limit and volume-dimensioned absolute tolerance. Adaptive Genz-Malik cubature over the box `[lo, hi]`. Each region is integrated with the degree-7 Genz-Malik rule (`1 + 4n + 2n(n-1) + 2^n` points) whose embedded degree-5 rule gives the error estimate; the region with the largest error is bisected along the axis with the largest fourth divided difference. The box is first split uniformly into a few sub-boxes so that narrow peaks are not missed. Stops when the total error is below `max(abs_tol, rel_tol * |value|)` or after `max_evals` integrand evaluations (`converged == false`). Intended for 2 to about 8 dimensions. A bisection only replaces a region by its two halves, so the regions always tile the box exactly: their volumes sum to the box volume and no two overlap. `value` and `error` are the sums of the region values and of the region estimates; the estimate is the embedded degree-5/7 difference, a heuristic rather than a rigorous bound (see the module limitations).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_cubature_nested_compiled" => "Native serial nested cubature; absolute tolerance has output times coordinate-volume dimension. Nested adaptive Gauss-Kronrod cubature over the box `[lo, hi]` (practical up to about four dimensions).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_cubature_nested_parallel" => "True native parallel nested cubature; absolute tolerance has output times coordinate-volume dimension. Parallel [`cubature_nested`]: the 15 nodes of every outer panel are inner integrals evaluated concurrently ([`integrate_gk_par`]); each node owns its inner state. Value and evaluation count are identical to the serial function.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dae_consistent_index1" => "Actual native index1 DAE consistency projection with independently measured variables, explicit constraint metric and original oracle errors. Computes consistent initial algebraic states `z0` and differential derivatives `dy0` for a semi-explicit Index-1 DAE system `y' = f(t, y, z)`, `0 = g(t, y, z)` at `t0`. Given fixed initial differential variables `y0` and an initial guess `z0_guess`, solves `g(t0, y0, z0) = 0` via damped Newton-Raphson iteration (`dg/dz` must be nonsingular for an index-1 DAE). Returns `(dy0, z0)`. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for non-finite values, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] for mismatched lengths, [`OdeGeomError::Singular`] if `dg/dz` is singular (e.g. higher-index DAE), and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if Newton iteration does not converge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dae_consistent_index2" => "Actual native index2 DAE consistency projection with independently measured variables, explicit constraint metric and original oracle errors. Computes consistent initial conditions `(y0, z0)` for a semi-explicit Hessenberg Index-2 DAE system `y'(t) = f(t, y, z)`, `0 = g(t, y)` at `t0`. An index-2 DAE has two constraint manifolds: 1. The explicit position/state constraint `g(t, y) = 0`. 2. The hidden velocity/index-1 constraint obtained by differentiating `g(t, y) = 0` with respect to time: `G(t, y) f(t, y, z) + \\\\partial_t g(t, y) = 0`, where `G = \\\\partial g / \\\\partial y` and `(G \\\\cdot \\\\partial f / \\\\partial z)` is nonsingular. This function first projects `y0_guess` onto `g(t0, y) = 0` via minimum-norm Gauss–Newton projection, then solves the hidden constraint for `z0`. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Empty`] if `y0_guess` or `z0_guess` is empty, [`OdeGeomError::Singular`] if `G (\\\\partial f / \\\\partial z)` is singular, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if projection fails to converge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dae_eval" => "Actual native DAE dense evaluation restoring differential/algebraic component units; outside the integrated interval remains None. Evaluates `(y(t), z(t))` at any time `t` inside the integrated span using cubic Hermite interpolation for `y(t)` and linear/Hermite interpolation for `z(t)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dae_fixed_index1" => "Actual native fixed backward-Euler index1DAE, with independently normalized differential/algebraic variables and physical time step. Returned states concatenate the original differential/algebraic order. Backward Euler for the semi-explicit index-1 DAE `y' = f(t, y, z)`, `0 = g(t, y, z)` with fixed step `h`. Returns the trajectory of `(y, z)` concatenated. # Errors [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if the nonlinear solve fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dae_max_constraint_violation" => "Actual native maximum constraint violation in the explicit independently scaled normalized constraint metric. No common physical residual unit is invented for heterogeneous equations. Maximum infinity-norm constraint violation `max_k ||g(t_k, y_k, z_k)||_inf`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dae_model_new" => "Build a genuine semi-explicit native DAE model. f tape receives(time,y,z) and outputs physical dy/dt. Constraint tape receives(time,y,z) for index1or(time,y) for index2. Positive independent constraint references define its normalized Newton/violation metric; physical differential/algebraic units may differ by component.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dae_solve_index1" => "Actual adaptive native index1 DAE solve with independent differential/algebraic physical domains, normalized error/constraint metrics, physical step bounds and explicit Newton/attempt/evaluation budgets. Adaptive semi-explicit DAE solver with bounded callback work. # Errors Invalid controls, malformed/nonfinite callbacks, failed consistency or stage solves, exhausted budgets, or an unsatisfied tolerance at minimum step.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dae_solve_index2" => "Actual adaptive native index2 DAE solve with independent differential/algebraic physical domains, normalized error/constraint metrics, physical step bounds and explicit Newton/attempt/evaluation budgets. Adaptive semi-explicit DAE solver with bounded callback work. # Errors Invalid controls, malformed/nonfinite callbacks, failed consistency or stage solves, exhausted budgets, or an unsatisfied tolerance at minimum step.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dde_eval" => "Actual native DDE Hermite history evaluation, restoring independent physical component units; outside the integrated interval returns None. Evaluates `y(t)` at any time `t` inside `[t_0, t_end]` using cubic Hermite interpolation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dde_fixed_rk4" => "Actual native fixed-delay RK4 method of steps. History tape(time)->state sets the initial condition; RHS tape receives(time,state,delayed state). tau/h are physical times, tau>=h, and the final native step clips the endpoint. Returned samples carry true physical coordinates, not an invented dense interpolant. Constant-delay DDE integrator (method of steps with RK4). Constant-delay DDE `y'(t) = f(t, y(t), y(t - tau))` with history `hist(t)` for `t <= t0`, integrated by classical RK4 (method of steps) with cubic Hermite interpolation of the stored solution. Requires `tau >= h`. Returns the grid and states. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for invalid span, delay or step size, or [`OdeError::StepLimit`] when the requested grid cannot advance in floating-point time.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_dde_state_dependent" => "Actual native state-dependent DDE. Each query tape(time,state)->physical past time invokes the native scoped history lookup at that stage; RHS tape receives(time,state,queried states in declared order). No lookup closure escapes or is retained. Independent state/history/rate references and explicit query/RHS evaluation budgets are preserved. Solves a general (possibly state-dependent or time-dependent) Delay Differential Equation (DDE) system `y'(t) = rhs(t, y(t), &|t_past| ...)` over `span = (t0, t1)`. The third argument passed to `rhs` is a continuous state lookup closure `&dyn Fn(f64) -> Vec<f64>` that evaluates `history(t_past)` for `t_past <= t0` and the continuous cubic Hermite extension of the solution for `t0 < t_past <= t`. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Empty`] if `y0` is empty, [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for invalid span or tolerances, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] for mismatched state dimensions, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if step size collapses.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fd_jacobian_colored" => "Actual native fd_jacobian_colored on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; RHS retains physical references and returned matrix reports them. Parallel mode calls the genuine Sync native implementation on an owned immutable tape. Finite-difference Jacobian restricted to `pattern`, computed with one evaluation of `f` per colour of `colors` (from [`color_columns`]). `f0 = f(t, y)`; `nfev` counts the evaluations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fd_jacobian_colored_par" => "Actual native fd_jacobian_colored_par on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; RHS retains physical references and returned matrix reports them. Parallel mode calls the genuine Sync native implementation on an owned immutable tape. Evaluates the columns of each colour together and writes the result on `pattern`. Each evaluation gets a zero-initialized output. Worker jobs reuse their perturbed state. Large colour sets on sparse patterns retain only the differences referenced by the pattern. Valid colours come from [`color_columns`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fd_jacobian_dense" => "Actual native fd_jacobian_dense on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; RHS retains physical references and returned matrix reports them. Parallel mode calls the genuine Sync native implementation on an owned immutable tape. Dense finite-difference Jacobian (one evaluation per column).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fd_jacobian_dense_par" => "Actual native fd_jacobian_dense_par on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; RHS retains physical references and returned matrix reports them. Parallel mode calls the genuine Sync native implementation on an owned immutable tape. Dense finite-difference Jacobian from independent right-hand side columns. Independent column groups share one work vector per job. Below 16 columns or with one active worker, evaluation runs in index order. Each evaluation receives a zero-initialized output buffer. # Panics Panics if `f0` has fewer components than `y`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fd_mode_parallel" => "Instantiate the real native parallel finite-difference mode for immutable owned RHS functions; no host callback is shared unsafely. Columns / colours evaluated concurrently ([`fd_jacobian_dense_par`], [`fd_jacobian_colored_par`]).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fd_mode_serial" => "Instantiate the real native serial finite-difference mode for owned compiled RHS functions. Sequential evaluation (any right-hand side).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fft_fft_axes" => "Native fft fft_axes; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The transform over `axes` of a row-major tensor of the given `shape`, computed one axis at a time (row-column decomposition) with the 1-D transform above. An empty `axes` is the identity. # Errors An axis out of range or repeated, or a `shape` that disagrees with the number of elements.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fft_fft_norm" => "Native fft fft_norm; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The forward transform under a chosen normalisation. `Backward` is the plain forward transform, `Forward` scales it by `1/N` (so the inverse needs none) and `Unitary` by `1/sqrt(N)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fft_ifft_norm" => "Native fft ifft_norm; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The inverse transform under a chosen normalisation: `Backward` scales by `1/N`, `Forward` not at all, `Unitary` by `1/sqrt(N)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_fftconv_fft" => "Native fftconv fft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Discrete Fourier transform of any length (`inverse` includes the `1/n` scaling).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_filon2d_compiled" => "Native2D Filon cosine/sine quadrature: phase coefficients have one dimension P,omega has1/(P*coordinate),amplitude references preserve arbitrary ordinate units. Two-dimensional Filon quadrature of an oscillatory integrand over a box. The integrand is `f(x, y) * cos(omega * phi(x, y))` (`cosine = true`) or `f(x, y) * sin(omega * phi(x, y))` (`cosine = false`), for the linear phase `phi(x, y) = a x + b y` with `phase = [a, b]`. The amplitude is interpolated by a tensor product of quadratic Lagrange polynomials, one per double panel and axis, and each oscillatory moment is integrated exactly (it is the 1-D [`filon`] functional), so the accuracy does not degrade as `|omega|` grows. The panel count is doubled from one until the difference between successive sweeps is at most `tol * max(|value|, 1)`, so `omega = 0` reproduces the classical Simpson rule (exact for a quadratic amplitude). Invalid input (a box dimension list other than two, `hi <= lo`, a non-finite frequency/slope/tolerance, `tol <= 0`) returns `NaN` values with `converged == false`; it never panics.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_filon2d_complex_compiled" => "Native complex2D Filon quadrature with physical coordinate,amplitude,linear-phase and frequency contracts. Two-dimensional complex-exponential Filon quadrature, `integral f(x, y) exp(i omega (a x + b y)) dx dy` over the box `[lo, hi]`, with `phase = [a, b]`. Same method and validity rules as [`filon2d`]; the value is returned as a complex number so both the cosine and sine components are available, and the error is the norm of the complex error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_gaussian_new" => "Gaussian model with same-unit physical mean and positive standard deviation. Creates a Gaussian with the given mean and standard deviation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_gaussian_pdf" => "Physical Gaussian density has the reciprocal coordinate dimension. Density at `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_gaussian_sample" => "Sample a measured Gaussian using the retained shared native RNG handle. Draws one sample with `rng`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_gaussian_weight" => "Dimensionless importance weight between measured Gaussian models in the same coordinate domain. Importance weight `target(x) / proposal(x)` of a point `x` drawn from `proposal` when `self` is the target density. The weight has mean one under the proposal whenever the proposal has the same support, which is what makes the estimator of [`importance_sampling_gaussian`] unbiased.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_gk_panel_parallel" => "Actual parallel native Gauss-Kronrod panel; returns physical integral/error and evaluation count without inventing a convergence verdict. [`gk_panel`] with the `2n - 1` node evaluations done in parallel. The panel's arithmetic is identical to the serial rule. Parallel evaluation is most useful when one evaluation of `f` is expensive and the callback is `Sync`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_annihilator" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. A differential operator annihilating `expr`. It is the operator of least order found in the vector space that the closure construction provides, which is usually but not always the minimal operator of the function. Returns `None` when an atom is invalid or a limit is exceeded.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_atom_annihilator" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. A differential operator annihilating the atom.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_atom_is_valid" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. Whether the atom meets the conditions stated on its variant.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_atom_new" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_atom_series" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The first `n` Taylor coefficients, or `None` for an invalid atom.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_deciding_terms" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. How many leading Taylor coefficients suffice to decide whether a solution of `op` is zero. Returns `None` for the zero operator or when the coefficient or search bound exceeds `limits.max_terms`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_expression_atom" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_expression_binary" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_expression_series" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The first `n` Taylor coefficients at the origin. Higher integer powers use a truncated recurrence over the nonzero base terms.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_expression_unary" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_is_zero" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. Whether `expr` is identically zero. `None` when it cannot be decided within the limits (or an atom is invalid); `Some` is a proof either way.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_operator_data" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_add" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The sum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_constant" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The constant `c`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_deriv" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The derivative.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_div" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The quotient, or `None` when dividing by zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_is_zero" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. Whether this is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_mul" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The product.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_neg" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The negation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_new" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. `num / den` in lowest terms, or `None` for a zero denominator.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_poly" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. A polynomial as a rational function.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_series" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The first `n` Taylor coefficients at the origin, or `None` when the function has a pole there.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_sub" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The difference.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_rational_zero" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. Zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_holonomic_recurrence" => "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The recurrence of the Taylor coefficients `a_n` of a solution of `op`: pairs `(s, c_s)` with `sum_s c_s(m) a_{m+s} = 0` for every integer `m` (coefficients with a negative index are zero), shifts ascending.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_homotopy_system_scaled" => "Actual native square polynomial homotopy for normalized coefficients/exponent rows/checked polynomial offsets. Positive coordinate/output references restore physical complex roots/residuals. Maximum6variables,4096terms,degree16,and256total-degree paths. Failed paths retain false status and optional residual; their undefined roots occupy empty offset ranges. Solves a square polynomial system by total-degree homotopy continuation with the default (fixed, generic) `gamma`; see [`homotopy_system_with_gamma`]. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for a non-square or malformed system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_homotopy_system_with_gamma_scaled" => "Actual gamma-selected native homotopy using normalized polynomial descriptors and independent physical root/residual references; all path statuses and defined coordinates are retained. Total-degree homotopy continuation for a square polynomial system `f_i(x_1..x_n) = 0` (`system[i]` lists the terms of `f_i`). The start system is `g_i = x_i^{d_i} - 1` with `d_i` the total degree of `f_i`; its `prod d_i` solutions are tracked along `H(x, t) = (1-t) gamma g(x) + t f(x)` from `t = 0` to `t = 1` with an RK4 predictor, a Newton corrector and adaptive step control. The complex constant `gamma` (the \"gamma trick\") makes the paths non-crossing with probability one. One [`PathResult`] is returned per start solution; paths diverging to infinity are marked `finite == false`. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for a non-square or malformed system, a zero or nonfinite gamma, or more than 200 000 total-degree paths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_hypergeometric_gamma_c" => "Native hypergeometric gamma_c; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The complex gamma function; poles (non-positive integers) give NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_hypergeometric_hyp1f1_cplx" => "Native hypergeometric hyp1f1_cplx; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Kummer's confluent hypergeometric function `M(a, b, z) = 1F1(a; b; z)`. All arguments are complex. For `Re z < 0` Kummer's transformation `M(a, b, z) = e^z M(b - a, b, -z)` is applied so that the dominant solution is the one being continued.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_hypergeometric_hyp2f1_cplx" => "Native hypergeometric hyp2f1_cplx; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Gauss hypergeometric function `2F1(a, b; c; z)` for complex parameters and argument (principal branch, cut along `[1, inf)`; the value on the cut is the limit from `Im z -> 0+`). For positive real parameters at `z = 1`, the gamma ratio in [Gauss's theorem](https://dlmf.nist.gov/15.4.E20) is evaluated in logarithmic form without forming large gamma values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_hypergeometric_hyp_pfq" => "Native hypergeometric hyp_pfq; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The generalised hypergeometric function `pFq(a; b; z)`. See the module documentation for the algorithm. `z = 0` gives `1`. Returns NaN for divergent series (`p > q + 1` without termination, `z != 0`), a denominator parameter at a non-positive integer (without earlier termination), and `+inf` at `z = 1` when `Re(sum b - sum a) <= 0`; [`hyp_pfq_checked`] tells which.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_hypergeometric_hyp_pfq_checked" => "Native hypergeometric hyp_pfq_checked; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. [`hyp_pfq`] with the reason when there is no value. # Errors [`HypError`]: NaN input, a denominator parameter at a non-positive integer (without earlier termination), or a divergent series (`p > q + 1`, `z != 0`). `z = 0` gives `1` in every case.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_hypergeometric_hyp_u_cplx" => "Native hypergeometric hyp_u_cplx; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Tricomi's confluent hypergeometric function `U(a, b, z)` for complex arguments on the principal branch (cut along the negative real axis; the upper side is returned for `z < 0`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_hypergeometric_ln_gamma_c" => "Native hypergeometric ln_gamma_c; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Logarithm of the complex gamma function. Complex log-gamma function, delegating to [`super::special_complex::ln_gamma_complex`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_importance_sampling_gaussian_compiled" => "Native Gaussian importance sampling using measured target/proposal models; integrand references match their coordinate domain. Output has ordinate units, not ordinate times length; ESS remains dimensionless. Importance-sampling estimate of `∫ f(x) p_target(x) dx`. Draws `n` points from `proposal` and averages `f(x) * p_target(x) / p_proposal(x)`. Because the weight has mean one under the proposal, the estimator is unbiased for the target integral whatever the shift; what the shift costs is variance, visible in the effective sample size. The proposal must be non-degenerate and have at least the support of the target, otherwise the weights are not finite and the estimate is not defined. The error is the sample standard error of the weighted terms, and the effective sample size is `(Σ w)^2 / Σ w^2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_integrate_adaptive_scaled" => "Normalized native integrate::adaptive_quadrature: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Adaptive Quadrature (Adaptive Simpson's Method) Recursively refines the interval until the estimated error satisfies the tolerance or the refinement limit is reached. The integrand must return a consistent result for a given coordinate because refinement reuses the parent panel's samples. A panel with consecutive floating-point bounds uses a trapezoid through its two finite endpoint samples, or returns `NaN` if either sample is nonfinite. ## Arguments * `f` - Function to integrate. * `range` - Interval `(a, b)`. * `tolerance` - Error tolerance (e.g., 1e-6). ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::adaptive_quadrature; let f = |x: f64| x.sin(); let res = adaptive_quadrature(f, (0.0, std::f64::consts::PI), 1e-6); assert!((res - 2.0).abs() < 1e-6); ```\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_integrate_gauss_kronrod_any_scaled" => "Normalized native integrate::gauss_kronrod_any: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Adaptive Gauss–Kronrod quadrature over an interval that may be infinite at either end, by mapping it to a finite one.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_integrate_gauss_kronrod_scaled" => "Normalized native integrate::gauss_kronrod: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Globally adaptive Gauss–Kronrod quadrature of `f` over the finite interval `[a, b]`. The panel with the largest error estimate is bisected until the total estimate drops below `tolerance` or `max_panels` panels exist. The returned [`Quadrature::error`] is the sum of the panel estimates, so a caller can tell a converged result from an exhausted budget. Reversing the limits negates the integral. Non-finite integrand values are propagated to the result rather than treated as zero.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_integrate_gauss_legendre_scaled" => "Normalized native integrate::gauss_legendre_quadrature: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Gauss-Legendre Quadrature Uses standard weights and nodes for n = 5 (hardcoded for now as it's efficient for general purpose). Can be extended to arbitrary n in the future. ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::gauss_legendre_quadrature; let f = |x: f64| x.powi(3); let res = gauss_legendre_quadrature(f, (0.0, 1.0)); assert!((res - 0.25).abs() < 1e-10); ```\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_integrate_gk_parallel" => "True native parallel Gauss-Kronrod quadrature using physical coherent bounds and compiled reference scales; rule0=G7K15,1=G10K21. [`integrate_gk`] with the nodes of each panel evaluated by [`gk_panel_par`]. For a pure callback the partition, arithmetic and result are bit-identical to the serial driver, regardless of the number of Rayon workers.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_integrate_romberg_scaled" => "Normalized native integrate::romberg_integration: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Romberg Integration Uses Richardson extrapolation on the Trapezoidal rule to improve accuracy. ## Arguments * `f` - Function to integrate. * `range` - Interval. * `max_steps` - Order of extrapolation (e.g., 5-10). ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::romberg_integration; let f = |x: f64| x.exp(); let res = romberg_integration(f, (0.0, 1.0), 6); assert!((res - (std::f64::consts::E - 1.0)).abs() < 1e-10); ```\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_integrate_simpson_scaled" => "Normalized native integrate::simpson_rule: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Pure Numerical Simpson's Rule Performs numerical integration using Simpson's 1/3 rule. ## Arguments * `f` - The function to integrate. * `range` - Interval `(a, b)`. * `n_steps` - Number of steps (must be usually even, but we handle it). ## Returns `Result<f64, String>` ## Errors Returns an error if the number of steps is zero or if an internal calculation fails. ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::simpson_rule; let f = |x: f64| x * x; let res = simpson_rule(f, (0.0, 1.0), 10).unwrap(); assert!((res - 1.0 / 3.0).abs() < 1e-10); ```\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_integrate_trapezoidal_scaled" => "Normalized native integrate::trapezoidal_rule: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Pure Numerical Trapezoidal Rule Performs numerical integration using the trapezoidal rule for a given function. ## Arguments * `f` - The function to integrate, as a closure. * `range` - A tuple `(a, b)` representing the integration interval. * `n_steps` - The number of steps to use. ## Returns The approximate value of the definite integral. ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::trapezoidal_rule; let f = |x: f64| x * x; let res = trapezoidal_rule(f, (0.0, 1.0), 1000); assert!((res - 1.0 / 3.0).abs() < 1e-4); ```\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_integrate_triangle_compiled" => "Native adaptive triangle quadrature on three physical planar vertices; compiled function has matching coordinate references and absolute tolerance has area times ordinate units. Adaptive integration over a planar triangle. The panel is the seven-point Dunavant rule of degree five; its error is estimated against the embedded three-point midpoint rule of degree two, and the region with the largest estimate is bisected into four congruent triangles until the summed estimate is below `max(abs_tol, rel_tol * |I|)` or `max_evals` integrand evaluations have been spent. The estimate is a heuristic, not a rigorous bound — the same contract as the box cubature in this module. The vertices may be given in either orientation; the affine map uses the absolute area, so the result is the integral over the triangle, not a signed one. A degenerate triangle (zero area) integrates to zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_band_pattern" => "Actual native normalized band sparsity pattern with checked dimension/bandwidth budgets. Pattern of the band `-kl <= j - i <= ku` of an `n x n` matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_block_triplets" => "Actual native stage-block assembly in normalized coordinates. cm/cj are immutable two-index compiled coefficient programs with dimensionless input/output; mass is None or a normalized matrix handle. n*stages<=128 bounds output triplets before allocation. Assembles the triplets of the block system `sum over blocks (bi, bj) of cm(bi, bj) M + cj(bi, bj) J` in stage-major numbering for `stages` blocks of size `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_color_columns" => "Actual native graph coloring retained with its original sparsity pattern; prevents mismatched or forged colored finite-difference descriptors. Greedy colouring of the columns of a sparsity pattern: columns that share a row get different colours. Returns the colour of each column and the number of colours.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_coloring_data" => "Inspect the actual native color assignment/count retained with its sparsity pattern.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_dense" => "Retain a native normalized dense Jacobian/mass matrix. Measured quantities require explicit prior normalization; RHS-derived Jacobians retain their physical reference frame separately.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_factor" => "Actual native dense/banded/sparse stage-block factorization. n*stages<=512; solver Dense,Banded,Sparse; all triplets are checked before native indexing. Factors the system with the given triplets in stage-major numbering (`stages` blocks of size `n`) according to `structure`; `band` is the `(kl, ku)` of one block (used for banded structure only).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_factor_solve" => "Actual native factor solve in its stage-major normalized coordinate layout. Solves the factored system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_for_each" => "Genuine serial native matrix visitor on normalized coefficients. Ordinary native handle is authenticated by its session bank and never enters the callback. Visitor receives exact row/column Int and Float value,returns an ignored Bool acknowledgement. Shared evaluation budget and original source errors stop subsequent source invocations; native iteration order is preserved. Calls `g(i, j, value)` for every stored entry.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_matrix_data" => "Inspect normalized native matrix data and actual evaluation count. RHS-derived Jacobians additionally expose their original time/state reference quantities; normalized coefficients are never mislabeled as one common physical matrix domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_provider_eval" => "Actual native provider evaluation on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; updated caller evaluation count and physical frame remain with the returned matrix. Evaluates the Jacobian at `(t, y)` with `f0 = f(t, y)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_provider_eval_with" => "Actual native provider evaluation with explicit real serial/parallel FD mode; normalized frame contracts and actual updated nfev are preserved. As [`JacobianProvider::eval`] with the finite-difference routines of `fd`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_provider_new" => "Actual native JacobianProvider builder over an owned RHS tape. Structure Dense,Banded,Sparse; analytic0 finite differences,1 exact native tape dense AD,2 exact native tape sparse AD. Sparse structure requires its normalized matrix pattern. Provider borrows are reconstructed per invocation to retain safe owned lifetimes; every evaluation uses the real native provider. Prepares the provider for a system of dimension `n`. A sparse callback with `Dense` structure makes the structure sparse. Returns `None` if a banded/sparse pattern does not fit `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_jacobian_sparse" => "Retain checked native normalized CSR data from exact row/column triplets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_kkt_residual_scaled" => "Actual native KKT stationarity residual in the normalized coordinate/objective metric. Physical gradient E/D,constraint Jacobians C/D,G/D,multipliers E/C,E/G are normalized by four positive references; result is dimensionless. Infinity norm of the gradient of the Lagrangian, i.e. the KKT stationarity residual for the multipliers `lambda` (on `h`) and `mu` (on `g`, with `L = f + lambda . h + mu . g`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_levin_adaptive_compiled" => "Actual native adaptive Levin collocation with positive absolute physical tolerance and bounded collocation/evaluation work. [`levin`] with the number of points doubled until two successive results agree to `tol` (absolute, or relative to the result when that is larger), starting from 8 points and stopping at `max_points`. # Errors A message when no pair of successive collocations agrees: typically a stationary point of the phase, or an integrand that is itself oscillatory.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_levin_compiled" => "Actual native Levin collocation with independent physical amplitude,phase,and derivative programs. Absolute reference tolerance is checked for interface consistency; fixed collocation supplies no error estimate. `integral from a to b of f(x) exp(i w g(x)) dx` by Levin collocation at `n` Chebyshev points (`n >= 2`); `dg` is the derivative of `g`. # Errors A message when `n < 2`, the data is not finite, or the collocation system cannot be solved.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_levin_plan_integrate" => "Integrates a compiled real amplitude with the prepared weights. The complex result has amplitude units times coordinate units. Each call evaluates only the amplitude program. Integrates an amplitude sampled once at each prepared node. # Errors A non-finite sample, an unrepresentable result, or cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_levin_plan_integrate_samples" => "Integrates one real amplitude sample per ordered plan node. The result has amplitude units times coordinate units. No compiled function is required for this call. Integrates amplitude values already sampled at [`Self::nodes`]. # Errors The wrong sample count, a non-finite sample, an unrepresentable result, or cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_levin_plan_new" => "Prepares repeated integration on one interval at fixed frequency. Supply compiled scalar phase and phase derivative, with derivative units equal to phase units divided by the coordinate dimension. Frequency is inverse phase. The handle retains only the prepared nodes and weights.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_levin_plan_nodes" => "Returns the measured quadrature nodes in the order required by the sampled amplitude operator. An empty interval has no nodes. The physical nodes, in the order required by [`Self::integrate_samples`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_linear_regression_new" => "Construct an actual native regression CostFunction with independently measured predictor/response domains and positive reference quantities; coefficients retain intercept E and slope E/D units. Builds a least-squares problem from predictors and responses. # Errors Returns an error when the row counts differ, the design is empty, or a value is not finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_lm_scaled" => "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Levenberg-Marquardt for nonlinear least squares `min |f(x)|^2` with a finite-difference Jacobian. Works for `m >= n` residuals. # Errors [`RootError::NoConvergence`] when work is exhausted or steps stagnate; [`RootError::Invalid`] for invalid input or malformed residuals and [`RootError::Singular`] if the augmented decomposition fails.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_lsq_result_converged" => "Actual native normalized least-squares report convergence predicate. All original report fields/status are supplied explicitly; this does not synthesize convergence from an iteration count. Whether the reported termination passed a convergence test.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_lsq_status_converged" => "Actual native least-squares status predicate; budget/stagnation statuses never imply convergence. Whether an explicit residual or first-order convergence test passed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mass_band" => "Actual native normalized mass-matrix band. Half bandwidths `(kl, ku)` of the stored nonzeros.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mass_cols" => "Actual native normalized mass-matrix cols. Dimension (columns) of the matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mass_for_each" => "Genuine serial native matrix visitor on normalized coefficients. Ordinary native handle is authenticated by its session bank and never enters the callback. Visitor receives exact row/column Int and Float value,returns an ignored Bool acknowledgement. Shared evaluation budget and original source errors stop subsequent source invocations; native iteration order is preserved. Calls `g(i, j, value)` for every nonzero entry.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_mass_matvec" => "Actual native mass-matrix action in explicitly normalized coordinates; vector length is checked before native indexing. Matrix-vector product `M x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mass_rows" => "Actual native normalized mass-matrix rows. Dimension (rows) of the matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_acceleration_new" => "Owned position-only acceleration tape. Positive time reference and each coordinate reference restore acceleration coordinate/time², preventing hidden dependence on velocity or time in the MechanicalSystem trait.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_backward_euler_linear" => "Actual native generic backward Euler on the retained constant matrix with physical time/state reconstruction and bounded allocation. Solves a linear ODE system y' = Ay using the backward (implicit) Euler method. This method is A-stable, making it excellent for stiff equations. The update rule is y_{n+1} = (I - dt*A)^-1 * `y_n`. # Returns A `Result` containing the solution path, or an error string if matrix inversion fails. Solves a linear ODE system `y' = Ay` using the backward (implicit) Euler method. This method is A-stable, making it excellent for stiff equations where explicit methods would require prohibitively small time steps. The update rule is `y_{n+1} = (I - dt*A)^-1 * y_n`. # Arguments * `system` - The linear ODE system to solve, implementing the `LinearOdeSystem` trait. * `y0` - The initial state vector. * `t_span` - The time interval `(t_start, t_end)`. # Returns A `Result` containing the solution path as `Vec<(f64, Vec<f64>)>`. # Errors This function will return an error if the matrix `(I - dt*A)` is not invertible, which can occur if the system is singular for the given time step `dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_eval" => "Actual native MechanicalSystem acceleration evaluation with physical coordinate/time² result components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_linear_dim" => "Actual native LinearOdeSystem dimension.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_linear_matrix" => "Actual native LinearOdeSystem matrix in its declared normalized reference coordinate metric; mixed physical row/column domains remain on the retained model.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_linear_new" => "Declare a constant linear ODE from a normalized native dense Jacobian carrying its physical RHS frame. The resulting coefficient matrix is the caller's explicit constant linear model, not a linearity inference from sampled values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_semi_implicit_euler" => "Actual native mechanical semi-implicit Euler. State contains independently measured positions then corresponding velocities; source tape depends only on position. Solves a second-order ODE system (typically mechanical systems) using the semi-implicit Euler method. This method is often used in mechanics for its energy conservation properties (it is symplectic). It is particularly well-suited for systems where the state vector can be partitioned into positions and velocities, and accelerations depend only on positions. It assumes the state vector `y` is ordered as `[x0, x1, ..., v0, v1, ...]`. # Arguments * `system` - The mechanical system to solve, implementing the `MechanicalSystem` trait. * `y0` - The initial state `[initial_positions..., initial_velocities...]`. * `dt` - The fixed time step. # Returns A `Vec` of tuples `(time, state_vector)` representing the solution path. # Errors This function will return an error if the length of the initial state vector `y0` is not twice the spatial dimension of the system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_spatial_dim" => "Actual instantiated native MechanicalSystem spatial dimension.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_symplectic_trajectory" => "Actual native symplectic_trajectory with physical position, momentum, mass and time. The mass itself defines the positive mass reference, so native mass1is a checked normalization and physical momentum is restored with m*coordinate/time. No numerical update is implemented in glue. Integrates a separable Hamiltonian system for `steps` steps with the symplectic method of the given `order` (2: Verlet, 4: Yoshida), returning the states `(q, p)` after every step.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_verlet_step" => "Actual native verlet_step with physical position, momentum, mass and time. The mass itself defines the positive mass reference, so native mass1is a checked normalization and physical momentum is restored with m*coordinate/time. No numerical update is implemented in glue. One step of the Störmer–Verlet (leapfrog) method for `q'' = a(q)` (`a` the acceleration, `-∇V/m`): kick, drift, kick.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_mechanics_yoshida4_step" => "Actual native yoshida4_step with physical position, momentum, mass and time. The mass itself defines the positive mass reference, so native mass1is a checked normalization and physical momentum is restored with m*coordinate/time. No numerical update is implemented in glue. One step of the fourth-order Forest–Ruth (Yoshida) composition of Verlet steps with weights `w1, w0, w1`, `w1 = 1/(2 - 2^(1/3))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_meijer_meijer_g" => "Native meijer meijer_g; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Value of `G^{m,n}_{p,q}(z | a; b)`, the Meijer G-function, at complex parameters and complex `z` (principal branch; see the module documentation). `p = a.len()` and `q = b.len()`. Returns `None` for invalid orders (`m > q` or `n > p`), non-finite inputs, `z = 0` where the limit does not exist, or when a finite value cannot be resolved. The single-pole exponential reduction uses its closed form. Residue series use the Mellin-Barnes integral as an independent cross-check where it applies.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_meijer_meijer_g_real" => "Native meijer meijer_g_real; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Value of `G^{m,n}_{p,q}(z | a; b)` for real parameters and real `z`. Returns `None` when the value is complex (a branch cut was crossed), when the imaginary part is not negligible, or under the conditions of [`meijer_g`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_monte_carlo_antithetic_compiled" => "Native antithetic Monte Carlo with physical compiled-function references and bounded pair count. Antithetic-variate Monte Carlo integral over `[lo, hi]`. Each of the `pairs` iterations draws one uniform point `u` and evaluates the integrand at both `u` and its reflection `1 - u` in every axis; the estimator is the mean of the `pairs` pair averages, so it costs `2 * pairs` evaluations. Both points are marginally uniform, hence the estimator is unbiased whatever the correlation between them. For an integrand that is monotone along the sampled axes the two evaluations are negatively correlated and the variance is below that of [`monte_carlo`] with the same number of evaluations; for an integrand that is symmetric about the centre of the box the correlation is positive and the variance can be slightly worse. The reported error is the standard error of the pair averages, which is the correct standard error of this estimator and already accounts for the correlation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_monte_carlo_compiled" => "Native serial Monte Carlo over physical bounds using an owned immutable compiled program; same-unit coordinates, arbitrary output units, actual native error/status and explicit sample/evaluation limits. Plain Monte Carlo integral over `[lo, hi]` with a seeded generator; the error is the standard error of the mean.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_monte_carlo_parallel" => "True native parallel Monte Carlo over physical bounds using an owned immutable compiled program; same-unit coordinates, arbitrary output units, actual native error/status and explicit sample/evaluation limits. Parallel [`monte_carlo`]: every block of 4096 samples has its own generator stream (seeded from `seed` and the block index), the block sums are combined in block order. Deterministic for a given `seed` and `n` whatever the thread count; the samples differ from the serial single-stream function.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_monte_carlo_stratified_compiled" => "Native stratified Monte Carlo with checked exponential grid/sample cost and independent physical output units. Stratified Monte Carlo integral over `[lo, hi]`. Every axis is cut into `strata_per_axis` equal cells, so the box has `strata_per_axis^dim` strata and each stratum receives `per_stratum` uniform samples. The estimator is the volume-weighted sum of the stratum means and the error is the root of the sum of the squared stratum standard errors, which is unbiased and never assumes that the strata are equally variable. Stratification removes the between-stratum part of the variance, so it pays when the integrand is smooth on the scale of a cell (the within-cell variance falls like the square of the cell width) and buys nothing when the integrand varies inside every cell, as a discontinuity finer than the grid does. The cost is exponential in the dimension: `strata^dim * per_stratum` evaluations. Arguments below one are treated as one. Returns `converged = false` with a `NaN` value and no evaluations when `strata_per_axis^dim * per_stratum` does not fit in a `usize`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_accelerations" => "Returns a three-component gravitational acceleration per body in the original input order. The output carries length per time squared. Reading the accelerations leaves the chain state and time intact. Accelerations per body in the original input order. The returned vectors are Cartesian accelerations. In states mutated after construction, coincidences retain the gravitational singularity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_angular_momentum" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Angular momentum about the centre of mass.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_cartesian" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Cartesian positions and velocities (original body order, absolute frame) at the current time.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_energy" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Total energy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_evolve_until" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Advances to the physical time `t_end` with adaptive fictitious steps (initial `ds0`) and local error tolerance `tol`; the chain is re-selected before every step and the last step is solved for so that the final time is hit to round-off. Returns the number of accepted steps. # Errors Fails if the step size collapses or the final-time solve does not converge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_gbs_step" => "Actual native GBS step. Its returned error is explicitly in the native normalized state metric, while fictitious time has physical energy*time units. One Gragg-Bulirsch-Stoer extrapolated step of size `ds` with `kmax` columns (order `2 kmax`) at fixed chain. Returns the relative error estimate.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_kinetic" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Kinetic energy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_leapfrog" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. One second-order time-transformed leapfrog step of size `h` in `s` (chain coordinates).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_new" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Builds the chain system from Cartesian data. # Errors Fails for fewer than two bodies, length mismatch, non-positive or non-finite masses, non-finite inputs, or an unrepresentable initial chain or energy. The unsoftened interaction is singular at coincidence.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_potential" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Potential energy (negative for attractive gravity).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_chain_rechain" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Re-selects the chain from the current configuration; the new chain vectors are exact sums of the old ones, so no precision is lost.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_eih_accelerations" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Full 1PN (EIH) accelerations (Newtonian part included) of all particles, given the Newtonian accelerations `acc_newton` of the sources.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_eih_energy" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Conserved 1PN energy of the EIH N-body Lagrangian (Landau-Lifshitz section 106).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_hermite_add_binary" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Registers particles `a` and `b` (indices into the *current* particle arrays) as a KS-regularised binary: they are replaced by one centre-of-mass particle (stored at index `a`; index `b` becomes a massless passive placeholder that is excluded from all sums). # Errors Fails with the sixth-order scheme, PN, equal indices or out-of-range indices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_hermite_advance" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Runs complete blocks until the next block would end after `t_end`. To query a state between block times, use [`Self::state_at`]. # Errors Rejects a non-finite end time and propagates [`step`](Self::step) errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_hermite_enable_post_newtonian" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Enables the 1PN EIH corrections with speed of light `c` and re-initialises. # Errors Fails with the sixth-order scheme, with softening, or with binaries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_hermite_energy" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Total energy at the current system time (Newtonian, or the 1PN energy with PN enabled). # Errors Propagates binary propagation errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_hermite_new" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Creates a system (all particles at time `t0`) and evaluates the initial derivatives. # Errors Fails for mismatched array lengths, fewer than two particles, a non-finite initial time, invalid step parameters, or a block interval with no power of two.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_hermite_next_time" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Time at which the next block ends.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_hermite_state_at" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Positions and velocities of all particles synchronised to time `t` (binary components are reported at the position of their regularised internal state; the placeholder particle of a binary reports its second component). # Errors Propagates binary propagation errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_nbody_hermite_step" => "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Advances the system by one block: the particles that are due at the earliest end time. # Errors Rejects invalid step bounds and clocks that cannot advance to a finite time. Propagates binary propagation errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_newton_bracketed_scaled" => "Normalized native rootfind::newton_bracketed: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then2 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Safeguarded Newton on a sign-changing bracket `[a, b]`. Newton steps must lie in the central half of the current bracket; otherwise bisection guarantees progress. Convergence uses bracket width, not the Newton step size, which can be tiny far from a root. `tol` is a relative/absolute width tolerance scaled by `1 + |x|`. # Errors [`RootError::NotBracketed`] / [`RootError::NoConvergence`]. [`RootError::Invalid`] for non-finite inputs, function or derivative values, or a non-positive tolerance.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_newton_cg_scaled" => "Genuine matrix-free trust-region Newton-CG: physical point,positive coordinate/objective references,explicit normalized-coordinate metric options,shared callback evaluation limit,then normalized objective/gradient/Hessian-vector callbacks. Exact derivative actions come from the supplied callbacks; no finite-difference substitution. Returns physical x,value,gradient norm plus actual native status and work counters. Minimize a smooth objective using only vector actions. `gradient(x, out)` and `hessian_vector(x, direction, out)` must fill every output element. Buffers are reset to NaN before each callback, so missing writes are detected. Non-finite trial objectives/gradients are rejected; the last accepted point is retained. No parallel callback calls occur. # Errors Returns an error for invalid options/input, a non-finite initial value or gradient, or a non-finite Hessian action/CG intermediate.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_newton_cg_tape" => "Actual native Tape Newton-CG. Tape input, parameter and output references preserve mixed physical coordinate units. Initial and parameter vectors may hold separately measured Value cells. Options use the normalized coordinate metric. The normalized gradient norm is dimensionless. Iteration, CG-action and instruction work is bounded before the solve. Minimize one tape output using analytic reverse gradients and HVPs. Parameters are constant. The objective tape must have matching input arity. No finite-difference Hessian is substituted when a second-order rule is absent. # Errors Returns the same errors as [`minimize`], or `InvalidInput` for an invalid output index/arity. Opaque second-order rules report `NonFiniteModel`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_newton_cg_tape_plan_minimize" => "Solve a prepared tape objective from a new measured initial point and parameters, using the plan's retained buffers. Each solve has its own normalized options and work budget. Results and counters have the same units and meaning as the one-shot tape solver. Minimize a tape output with reusable Newton and CG buffers. # Errors Returns the same errors as [`minimize_tape`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_newton_cg_tape_plan_new" => "Retain an independent snapshot of a native tape and select one objective output. The plan also holds the Newton and CG buffers used by repeated solves. Releasing the input tape does not invalidate the plan.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_newton_fd_scaled" => "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Newton's method for `f(x) = 0` with a backtracking (Armijo) line search on `|f|^2`. Pass `jac = None` to use finite differences. # Errors [`RootError::Singular`] on a singular Jacobian, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] when the iteration limit is hit.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_newton_jacobian_scaled" => "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Newton's method for `f(x) = 0` with a backtracking (Armijo) line search on `|f|^2`. Pass `jac = None` to use finite differences. # Errors [`RootError::Singular`] on a singular Jacobian, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] when the iteration limit is hit.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_numerical_jacobian_scaled" => "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Forward-difference Jacobian of `f: R^n -> R^m` at `x`. This compatibility function returns a same-shaped NaN matrix when a callback is malformed or a derivative cannot be represented. Prefer [`try_numerical_jacobian`] when the failure reason is needed.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_ode_adaptive" => "Native ode::solve_adaptive: this legacy driver supports rtol/atol/max_steps; h0/hmin must be zero and hmax must cover the whole span, with unsupported requests rejected. independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` over `t_span` with the adaptive Dormand–Prince 5(4) method, keeping the local error below `atol + rtol * |y|`. # Errors Returns an error when the step size underflows, `max_steps` accepted or rejected steps are exhausted, or the state stops being finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_bdf_events" => "Adaptive BDF with finite-difference Jacobians and event detection. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. Each event supplies program, terminal and direction (-1, 0 or 1). The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. Variable-order BDF with event detection on its backward-difference interpolant. # Errors As [`bdf`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_bdf_ex" => "Legacy adaptive BDF with the Jacobian of the retained RHS tape and event detection. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. Each event supplies program, terminal and direction (-1, 0 or 1). The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. Variable-order BDF with dense output and event detection; superseded by [`bdf_with`]. # Errors As [`bdf_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_bdf_jac" => "Adaptive BDF with the Jacobian of the retained RHS tape. Pass an empty events list. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. Variable-order BDF with a user-supplied Jacobian `jac(t, y)`. The solution is advanced in the fixed-leading-coefficient form on a backward-difference array; the order (capped at `max_order`, `1..=5`) and the step size are chosen after every `order + 1` equal steps by comparing the error estimates of the neighbouring orders. The Jacobian and its LU factorisation are reused while the Newton iteration converges. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_bdf_parallel" => "Adaptive BDF with dense output and event detection. Set controls[1] to 1 to use the Jacobian of the retained RHS tape. An omitted or other value uses finite differences, evaluated concurrently for systems with at least 64 state components. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. Each event supplies program, terminal and direction (-1, 0 or 1). The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. [`bdf_with`] with concurrently evaluated finite-difference Jacobians. The right-hand side must be `Sync`; the solution is bit-identical. It pays from `n` of a few hundred when one evaluation of `f` is expensive (measured 1.35 at `n = 100` to 1.56 at `n = 400`); below `STIFF_PAR_MIN_N` it uses the serial Jacobian. The Radau solver gains nothing (Newton / LU dominate: 1.08, 0.99, 0.92), so there is no `radau_with_par`. # Errors As [`bdf_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_bdf_with" => "Adaptive BDF with dense output and event detection. Set controls[1] to 1 to use the Jacobian of the retained RHS tape. An omitted or other value uses finite differences. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. Each event supplies program, terminal and direction (-1, 0 or 1). The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. Variable-order BDF with dense output, event detection and structured Jacobians. Identical to [`bdf_jac`] (the Jacobian is optional) but additionally locates `bo.events` on the backward-difference interpolating polynomial of the last step (direction-aware; a terminal event ends the run), uses a banded or sparse LU when [`BdfOptions::structure`] says so, and [`OdeSolution::eval`] interpolates anywhere in the integrated range. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_bvp_fd" => "Actual native finite-difference BVP y''=f(x,y,y') with physical coordinateD/stateE references. f inputs have domains D,E,E/D and output E/D²; guess is D->E. Boundary values, true native samples and explicit evaluation budget retain those domains. Finite-difference BVP solver for `y'' = f(x, y, y')` with Dirichlet ends. Second-order finite-difference solution of the BVP `y'' = f(x, y, y')`, `y(a) = ya`, `y(b) = yb` on `n` interior points, by Newton iteration on the discrete system. Returns `(x, y)` including the end points; `guess` gives the initial profile. # Errors [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if Newton fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_data" => "Physical accepted samples and native event/order statistics. Fixed endpoint-only solvers return only their actual initial/final states.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_etdrk4" => "Actual native matrix ETDRK4 with independent physical state/time reference frame. Linear matrix coefficients are explicitly normalized in that same metric; RHS tape is the actual nonlinear term. ETDRK4 for `u' = L u + N(t, u)` with a dense matrix `L`. Takes `steps` equal steps from `t0` to `t1` and returns the states at the `steps + 1` grid points. Fourth-order accurate for any `L`, independent of its stiffness. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for mismatched dimensions or `steps == 0`; [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if the matrix functions cannot be formed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_etdrk4_diag" => "Use real exponential Runge–Kutta weights for an independent linear rate per state component. The diagonal has inverse-time units. The retained tape encodes the nonlinear part of the RHS. The trajectory includes the initial and each accepted fixed-step state, with each component restored to its physical units. The evaluation budget bounds the four tape evaluations per step. Integrates `u_i' = lambda_i u_i + N_i(t, u)` by diagonal ETDRK4. Returns the states at the `steps + 1` equally spaced grid points, including the initial state. Real scalar weights use a Taylor expansion near zero and reciprocal-power formulas for large linear rates. The nonlinear callback writes the rate vector and may couple the state components. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for an empty state, mismatched dimensions, zero steps or non-finite inputs. [`OdeError::NonFinite`] if the step or an updated state is non-finite. [`OdeError::StepLimit`] if the trajectory cannot be reserved.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_eval" => "Actual native dense output; no invented interpolant for a discrete trajectory. Outside the solved interval returns None. Evaluates the continuous extension at `t` (available for [`dopri5`], [`rosenbrock23`], the Radau and the BDF integrators); `None` outside the integrated range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_exp_matrix" => "Actual native matrix exponential on explicitly dimensionless normalized matrix coefficients. Physical-rate Jacobians must first be multiplied by a matching physical time in the declared metric. Matrix exponential helper re-exported for the exponential integrators. # Errors [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if the matrix exponential cannot be computed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_exprb32" => "Actual native adaptive exponential Rosenbrock3/2 with exact native tape Jacobian, physical mixed state reconstruction and explicit attempts/evaluation budgets. Adaptive exponential Rosenbrock method `exprb32` (order 3 with an embedded order-2 estimate) for `y' = f(t, y)`. Each step linearises `f` at the current point (Jacobian `jac(t, y)` or a finite-difference one; time dependence is handled by augmenting the state with `t`), evaluates `phi_1` and `phi_3` of `h J` and applies `y+ = y + h phi_1(hJ) f + 2 h phi_3(hJ) D` with `D = N(U) - N(y)`, `U = y + h phi_1(hJ) f`, `N(y) = f(y) - J y`. Linear problems are integrated exactly. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`], [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`] or [`OdeError::SolveFailed`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_fixed" => "Native ode::solve_fixed; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` from `t_span.0` to `t_span.1` in `steps` equal steps. # Errors Rejects zero steps, non-finite inputs, unrepresentable steps, or a trajectory that cannot be reserved. Returns an error when the state stops being finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_fixed_events" => "Native ode::solve_fixed_events; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates in `steps` equal steps and locates events. # How an event is located without a dense output A fixed-step method has no interpolant, so there is nothing to Brent-iterate on. It is a *one-step* method, however, which means the state at any intermediate time is just another application of the same step from the same start: the locator bisects the step length, re-integrating the partial step each time, until the bracket is below `1e-13`. That is exact for the method — the located state is a point of the method's own trajectory, not of an interpolant — and it costs about fifty evaluations per event, which is the right trade for a method that is cheap by construction. A `terminal` event stops the integration; the trajectory then ends at the event point. # Errors As [`solve_fixed`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_fixed_final" => "Returns only the final measured state of a retained RHS tape, preserving each component's dimension. Steps are equal; method 0 is Euler, 1 is Heun and 2 is classical Runge–Kutta. maxeval bounds RHS evaluations before integration. Intermediate samples are not retained. Integrates on the same fixed grid as [`solve_fixed`] and returns its final state using scratch space proportional to the state dimension. # Errors Rejects zero steps, non-finite inputs, unrepresentable steps, non-finite states, or cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_gauss_step" => "Actual native gauss_legendre_step. Physical time/state/rate references are retained by the RHS tape; no alternate method is substituted. stages selects the native1/2/3-stage Gauss-Legendre method where applicable. Advance one implicit Gauss–Legendre collocation step. One, two and three stages give orders two, four and six, respectively. The method is symmetric, A-stable and B-stable. In exact arithmetic it is symplectic for canonical Hamiltonian systems and preserves quadratic invariants. The returned count is the number of right-hand-side evaluations. When fixed-point iteration stalls, Newton's method linearizes the right-hand side independently at each collocation node. # Errors Returns an error for invalid or non-finite data, a callback dimension mismatch, a singular stage Jacobian, or failure of the implicit iteration.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_geometric_adaptive" => "Actual tolerance-controlled native geometric driver; method-specific full/two-half-step error control, physical step bounds, native events and explicit attempt/evaluation budgets. Integrate the selected RK4 or geometric method forward using step doubling. The finite interval uses `start <= end`. The accepted state is two half steps of the requested method, without extrapolation or method substitution. Both the endpoint estimate and the midpoint defect of its cubic Hermite extension control acceptance. A trial needs a representable time strictly between its endpoints. A bracketed event between adjacent times is reported at the later timestamp. Adaptive state-dependent step selection does not retain fixed-step symplecticity. Gauss–Legendre trials reuse stage storage for the duration of the solve. # Errors Invalid inputs or callback outputs, exhausted attempts/evaluations, a tolerance requiring a smaller step than `h_min`, an unsplittable time grid, or failed implicit stages.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_geometric_fixed" => "Actual fixed geometric driver with physical state components, native Hermite event localization, explicit method/steps/optional stages controls and budgets. Integrate a fixed geometric ODE method with Hermite event localization. # Errors Invalid state, time interval, callback dimensions, or failed implicit iteration.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_geometric_sample" => "Actual native fixed-geometric requested-time sampler. Times have the RHS time dimension; mixed physical state components retain independent references. Sample a requested fixed method with `steps` substeps per output interval. # Errors Invalid grids, callback values, or geometric-stage convergence failures.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_hermite_segment" => "Native Hermite segment; endpoints are physical state vectors and endpoint rates have each state dimension divided by the RHS time dimension. Evaluate one checked cubic Hermite segment in linear state-vector work. # Errors Empty or mismatched vectors, nonfinite inputs, a query outside the segment, or arithmetic overflow during the evaluation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_imex_rk" => "Actual native imex_rk on explicit/implicit physical RHS tapes sharing one declared metric, with all3native schemes, exact implicit tape Jacobian, checked steps and shared evaluation budget. [`imex_rk_with`] with the options passed as separate arguments. # Errors See [`imex_rk_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_imex_rk_with" => "Actual native imex_rk_with on explicit/implicit physical RHS tapes sharing one declared metric, with all3native schemes, exact implicit tape Jacobian, checked steps and shared evaluation budget. Additive IMEX Runge-Kutta integration of `y' = f_ex(t, y) + f_im(t, y)` with `steps` equal steps. `f_ex` (non-stiff) is treated explicitly, `f_im` (stiff) implicitly: each stage solves `Y - h gamma f_im(t, Y) = rhs` by Newton iteration with the Jacobian `jac_im(t, y)` of `f_im` (finite differences when `None`), factored once per step. Returns the states on the `steps + 1` grid points. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_integrate_hamiltonian" => "Actual general native Hamiltonian integrator with all8native methods. dq/dt and dp/dt tapes receive(time,q,p); q/p components carry independent physical references and rates preserve each component/time. Returned object retains actual native Hermite trajectory. Integrates a canonical Hamiltonian or partitioned system `dq/dt = dq_dt(t, q, p)`, `dp/dt = dp_dt(t, q, p)` over `span = (t0, t1)` using `steps` steps of `method`. Explicit splitting methods (`StormerVerlet`, `Ruth3`, `Ruth4`, `Yoshida6`, `Yoshida8`) are symplectic for separable Hamiltonians `H(t, q, p) = T(t, p) + V(t, q)`. Implicit Gauss–Legendre Runge–Kutta methods (`ImplicitMidpoint`, `GaussLegendre4`, `GaussLegendre6`) are symplectic for **arbitrary** (including non-separable) canonical Hamiltonian systems and preserve all quadratic invariants to machine precision. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for non-finite inputs or `t1 < t0`, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] when `q0.len() != p0.len()`, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] when implicit Newton iteration fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_integrate_symplectic" => "Actual native separable symplectic integrator. Acceleration tape receives(time,position) and outputs position/time²; velocity references are derived from position/time. All8native methods and original native dense data are preserved. Integrates a second-order separable system `q' = p`, `p' = accel(t, q)` using a symplectic or geometric integrator over `span = (t0, t1)` with `steps` steps. For autonomous acceleration `accel(q)` with splitting methods (`StormerVerlet`, `Ruth4`, `Yoshida6`, `Yoshida8`), this uses the exact velocity-Verlet composition matching `rssn::kernels::ode_ext::symplectic_split` (`Rssn_Numeric::ode_symplectic`), extended to support time-dependent forces, `Ruth3`, and implicit Gauss–Legendre methods (`ImplicitMidpoint`, `GaussLegendre4`, `GaussLegendre6`). # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for non-finite inputs or invalid span, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] when `q0.len() != p0.len()` or `accel` changes length, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if an implicit stage fails to converge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_last" => "Actual native trajectory last; only valid on a native discrete trajectory. The final state, or an empty slice for an empty trajectory.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_leapfrog" => "Actual native leapfrog over a pure-position acceleration tape with physical position/velocity/time references and explicit evaluation budget. Velocity-Verlet (leapfrog) for `q'' = acc(q)`; returns `(q, p)` after `steps` steps of size `dt` (unit mass, `p = q'`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_max_energy_drift" => "Actual native maximum absolute energy drift on accepted phase nodes. Hamiltonian tape receives(q,p); positive energy reference must match every q_i*p_i/time domain. Native reverse/finite-difference substitution is not involved. Maximum absolute Hamiltonian drift `max_k |H(q_k, p_k) - H(q_0, p_0)|` along the trajectory.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_phase_eval" => "Actual native symplectic trajectory Hermite evaluation. Independent position/momentum or velocity units are preserved; out-of-span queries remain None. Continuous cubic Hermite evaluation of `(q(t), p(t))` at any `t` in the span.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_radau" => "Actual legacy native variable-stage Radau entry, including its true collocation dense output and event handling. Physical frames and explicit bounds match the modern Radau adapter. Variable-order Radau IIA integration; superseded by [`radau_with`]. # Errors As [`radau_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_radau5_fixed" => "Native ode_stiff::radau5_fixed; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Constant-step Radau IIA(5): `n_steps` equal steps from `t0` to `t1`. The Jacobian is a finite-difference one refreshed every step and the Newton iteration is fully converged. Returns the final state; used to measure the order of convergence (5). # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_radau_events" => "Native ode_stiff::radau5_events; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Radau IIA with event detection on its collocation polynomial. The extension is the collocation polynomial through the internal stages, so an event time is located to the order of the method, not to the step size. # Errors As [`radau5`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_radau_fixed" => "Native ode_stiff::radau_fixed; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Constant-step Radau IIA with `stages` (3, 5 or 7) stages, i.e. order `2 stages - 1` (5, 9, 13). # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_radau_jac" => "Native ode_stiff::radau5_jac; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Radau IIA(5) integration with a user-supplied Jacobian `jac(t, y)`. The method is L-stable, stiffly accurate and of order 5. The stage system is solved by simplified Newton iteration with a single LU factorisation reused across iterations and across steps whose step size is (nearly) unchanged; the Jacobian is refreshed only when the Newton iteration fails to converge. The error estimate is the embedded third-order formula of Hairer and Wanner. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_radau_with" => "Native ode_stiff::radau_with; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Variable-order Radau IIA integration (orders 5, 9 and 13) of `M y' = f(t, y)` with dense output (the collocation polynomial), event detection, structured Jacobians and index-1, 2 and 3 DAEs. The number of stages is raised while the simplified Newton iteration converges very fast (smooth, tightly toleranced problems profit from the high orders) and lowered when it converges slowly; the accepted orders are reported in [`OdeSolution::orders`]. Events are handled exactly as in [`crate::kernels::ode_adaptive::dopri5`]: direction-aware, located on the collocation polynomial, terminal events end the run. With a singular mass matrix the system is a DAE; the initial values must be consistent, and unless `opts.h0` is given the first step is `1e-5 |t1 - t0|`. For index 2 or 3 give the variable blocks in [`RadauOptions::index`] (see the module documentation). # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`] (also for unsupported stage counts, mismatched mass/pattern/index sizes).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_rhs_new" => "Build an owned native tape RHS: inputs(time,state components),outputs corresponding rates. Each state may have independent physical units; tape references define normalized solver coordinates. Parameter quantities retain their own domains.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_rk4_step" => "Actual native rk4_step. Physical time/state/rate references are retained by the RHS tape; no alternate method is substituted. stages selects the native1/2/3-stage Gauss-Legendre method where applicable. A classical fourth-order Runge-Kutta step. # Errors Callback dimension or nonfinite-value errors, or an unrepresentable result.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_rosenbrock23_events" => "Actual native Rosenbrock2/3 with native event localization and dense output in independently scaled physical state coordinates. The same integrator, locating events on its Hermite extension. The extension is third order, so an event time is located to the accuracy of the step rather than to the step size; the bracket is refined by Brent iteration to `1e-14`. # Errors As [`rosenbrock23`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_ruth4_step" => "Actual native partitioned_ruth4_step. Physical time/state/rate references are retained by the RHS tape; no alternate method is substituted. stages selects the native1/2/3-stage Gauss-Legendre method where applicable. Partitioned 4th-order Ruth (Forest–Ruth / Yoshida) step on a flat state `y = [q, p]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_sdirk" => "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` from `t0` to `t1` with the adaptive SDIRK3(2) method, using the analytic Jacobian `jac(t, y)`. The step size is controlled by the embedded second-order estimate with exponent `1/3`; steps whose Newton iteration fails are rejected and retried with half the step. The cubic Hermite continuous extension of the accepted steps is exposed by [`SdirkSolution::eval`]. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for an empty state, `t0 == t1` or a Jacobian of the wrong shape; [`OdeError::NonFinite`] when the state or the estimate stops being finite; [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] when Newton iteration does not converge at the smallest step; [`OdeError::StepLimit`] when [`SdirkOptions::max_steps`] or the step-size floor is reached.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_sdirk_events" => "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3_events; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Adaptive SDIRK3(2) with event detection on its cubic Hermite dense output. Events follow the conventions of the other integrators: a sign change of `g(t, y)` is located by Brent iteration on the continuous extension, `direction` selects rising, falling or both crossings, and a `terminal` event ends the run at the event point, which is recorded as the last sample of [`OdeSolution::t`]. Non-terminal events are recorded in [`OdeSolution::events`] and integration continues to `t1`. The located event time inherits the `O(h³)` accuracy of the interpolation (see the module limitations), not the `1e-14` bracket tolerance of the root finder. # Errors As [`sdirk3`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_sdirk_fd" => "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3_fd; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. The same integrator with a forward-difference Jacobian (`n + 1` right-hand-side evaluations per Newton iterate). # Errors As [`sdirk3`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_sdirk_fixed" => "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3_fixed; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` from `t0` to `t1` in `steps` equal steps with no step-size control, returning the final state. This is the entry point for convergence studies: with `steps = 2^k` the error of a third-order method falls by `8` per refinement of the step. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for an empty state or `steps == 0`, and the errors of [`sdirk3`] otherwise.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_sdirk_fixed_events" => "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3_fixed_events; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Fixed-step SDIRK3(2) with event detection on the cubic Hermite dense output. The `steps` equal steps are the same as in [`sdirk3_fixed`]; events follow the conventions of [`sdirk3_events`]. Returns the full solution (accepted samples, located events and dense output), so [`OdeSolution::eval`] also works. The right-end slope of every step is the last stage of the method, so the extension adds no right-hand-side evaluations. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for an empty state or `steps == 0`, and the errors of [`sdirk3_fixed`] otherwise.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_shoot_initial" => "Actual native damped shooting-parameter solve. Positive independent parameter/residual references define the normalized Newton metric; returned parameters retain their own physical units. Original tape failures take precedence over native nonconvergence. Initial state solving a boundary residual by damped finite-difference Newton. The caller supplies an integration-backed residual. At most 60 Newton trials, each with at most 30 backtracking residuals, preserve the legacy work bound. # Errors Original residual errors or a malformed, singular, nonconverged iteration.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_shooting" => "Actual native ODE shooting with true initial-state and terminal-residual tapes, independently measured parameters/residuals and original errors. RHS uses its mixed physical state frame; config controls inner native integration rather than an unrelated substitute. General shooting method: `params -> initial state` and `final state -> residual` define the boundary conditions; the parameters are adjusted by Newton iteration so the residual vanishes. # Errors [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if the parameter iteration or an inner integration fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_symplectic_split" => "Actual native symplectic split with pure-position acceleration; state contains physical positions then velocities. SplitMethod is explicit Verlet/TripleJump/Yoshida6/Yoshida8 and triple-jump order is bounded2..10. Integrates `q' = p`, `p' = accel(q)` (unit masses; for `H = |p|^2/2 + V(q)` the acceleration is `-grad V`) with a symplectic composition method and `steps` equal steps from `t0` to `t1`. Each Verlet sub-step of the composition is time-reversible and the whole map is symplectic. Returns the final `(q, p)`. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for mismatched lengths, `steps == 0` or an odd or too large order in [`SplitMethod::TripleJump`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_variational_lobatto" => "Actual native Galerkin-Lobatto variational integrator with physical mass matrix, position and momentum. A native tape reverse gradient supplies the potential force; explicit positive M/L/T references preserve energy/force units and stage/work budgets. [`variational_lobatto_with`] with the options passed as separate arguments. # Errors See [`variational_lobatto_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_variational_lobatto_with" => "Actual native Galerkin-Lobatto variational integrator with physical mass matrix, position and momentum. A native tape reverse gradient supplies the potential force; explicit positive M/L/T references preserve energy/force units and stage/work budgets. Galerkin variational integrator with `stages >= 2` Gauss-Lobatto nodes for `L(q, q') = q'^T M q' / 2 - V(q)`. The path inside each step is the degree-`stages - 1` polynomial through the node values; the discrete action is the Lobatto quadrature of the Lagrangian. The discrete Euler-Lagrange equations are solved by Newton's method (finite-difference Jacobian) for the interior values and the next position; momenta follow from the discrete Legendre transforms. Order `2 stages - 2`; symplectic and momentum-preserving by construction. Returns the final `(q, p)`. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for bad dimensions or `stages < 2`; [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if Newton's iteration does not converge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_verlet_step" => "Actual native partitioned_verlet_step. Physical time/state/rate references are retained by the RHS tape; no alternate method is substituted. stages selects the native1/2/3-stage Gauss-Legendre method where applicable. Partitioned Störmer–Verlet step on a flat state `y = [q, p]` (or implicit midpoint if `y.len()` is odd).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_view" => "Actual native named solution view; native component naming/padding is preserved while samples and events retain physical dimensions. This solution as a structured result, naming each state component. The integrator works with anonymous columns; a model knows what they are. Passing the names here is what turns `y[3][1]` into `velocity`, and it is the only place the two views meet. A name list shorter than the state is padded with `y{k}`, and one longer is truncated: a mismatch is a caller's slip, not a reason to lose the solution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_ode_yoshida4" => "Actual native fourth-order Yoshida over a pure-position acceleration tape, preserving physical phase/time references and original native evaluation errors. Fourth-order symplectic Yoshida (triple-jump) composition of leapfrog.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_optimization_analyze" => "Native analyzer of retained optimizer state; native labels use normalized objective thresholds and are heuristics,not convergence certificates. Labels the convergence quality of `result`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_optimization_gradient_scaled" => "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Central-difference gradient of `f` at `x`.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_optimization_print" => "Print native normalized optimizer-state diagnostics. Particle populations project their actual best position,cost,iteration and evaluation counts to the vector State required by the native printer. Prints a short report for `result`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_optimization_result" => "Physical best point/objective and actual native termination from retained optimizer state. Missing native best values remain None; mixed coordinate domains are preserved per component.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_oscillatory2d_compiled" => "Native general-phase2D oscillatory cubature; owned phase program references match physical coordinates and omega has inverse phase dimension. Evaluation limit applies separately to each compiled program. General-phase fallback for `integral f(x, y) exp(i omega phi(x, y)) dx dy` over the box `[lo, hi]`. [`filon2d`] is moment-corrected only for a linear phase. For a nonlinear phase this fallback integrates the real and imaginary oscillating integrands directly with [`cubature_genz_malik`]: deterministic and reliable while the adaptive partition resolves the oscillation, but its cost grows with `|omega|` (there is no frequency-independent 2-D Levin collocation). The tolerance is used as both absolute and relative tolerance of the two cubatures, with an integrand budget of `OSC2D_MAX_EVALS` evaluations per component. A non-finite phase at the box corners, edge midpoints or centre, or any other invalid argument, returns `NaN` with `converged == false`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_advection_1d" => "Native advection_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t + a u_x = 0` on the periodic domain `[x0, x1)` with `n` nodes (`x1` is identified with `x0`). Stable for `|a| dt / dx <= 1`, checked up front. # Errors [`PdeError::Unstable`], [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] or [`PdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_advection_2d" => "Native advection_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t + a_x u_x + a_y u_y = 0` on the periodic rectangle `[x0, x1) x [y0, y1)` (the upper ends are identified with the lower ones). # Errors [`PdeError::Unstable`] when a directional Courant number exceeds one, [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] or [`PdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_advection_3d" => "Native advection_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t + a . grad(u) = 0` on the periodic box `[x0, x1) x [y0, y1) x [z0, z1)`. # Errors As [`advection_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_dirichlet" => "Retain an immutable compiled two-coordinate physical boundary program, with lifetime evaluation budget and dependency-tracked program storage. Dirichlet condition with a position-dependent value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_dirichlet_const" => "Constant measured Dirichlet data in the retained grid reference frame. Constant Dirichlet value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_field1_max_error" => "Native maximum nodal error against an owned physical exact-solution program; returns measured ordinate error. Maximum absolute difference to `exact(x)` over all nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_field2_at" => "Checked native2D field sample retains measured ordinate units. Value at node `(i, j)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_field2_max_error" => "Native maximum nodal error against an owned physical exact-solution program; returns measured ordinate error. Maximum absolute difference to `exact(x, y)` over all nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_field2_point" => "Checked native2D field node coordinates retain measured length units. Coordinates of node `(i, j)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_field3_at" => "Checked native3D field sample with physical field units.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_field3_max_error" => "Actual native3D maximum nodal error against an immutable physical exact-solution program, with original evaluation failures preserved. Maximum absolute difference to `exact(x, y, z)` over all nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_field3_point" => "Checked actual native3D node coordinates in physical length units. Coordinates of node `(i, j, k)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_field_data" => "Retrieve native field samples,shape,and physical snapshot time with preserved measurement units.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid" => "Construct an actual native1D/2D/3D rectangular grid from physical bounds and explicit positive length/time/value references. Shape product is bounded before native allocations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid1_dx" => "Physical native grid spacing in the retained length domain. Grid spacing for a bounded grid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid1_nodes" => "Native1D grid nodes restored to measured coordinates. Node coordinates of a bounded grid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid2_dx" => "Physical native grid spacing in the retained length domain. Spacing in `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid2_dy" => "Physical native grid spacing in the retained length domain. Spacing in `y`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid3_dx" => "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference. Spacing in `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid3_dy" => "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference. Spacing in `y`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid3_dz" => "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference. Spacing in `z`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid3_is_empty" => "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid3_len" => "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid_unit_cube" => "Native unit-cube grid interpreted through explicit physical references, bounded before native allocation. Unit cube with `n` nodes per axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_grid_unit_square" => "Native unit-square grid interpreted through explicit physical references. Unit square with `n x n` nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_heat_1d" => "Native heat_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t = alpha u_xx` on `grid` with Crank-Nicolson time stepping (`steps` equal steps up to `t_end`); `bc = [left, right]`. The scheme is second-order in space and time and unconditionally stable. # Errors [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] for a bad grid, step count or `alpha <= 0`; [`PdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`PdeError::NonFinite`] otherwise.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_heat_2d" => "Native heat_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t = alpha (u_xx + u_yy)` with Crank-Nicolson; `bc = [left, right, bottom, top]`. # Errors As [`heat_1d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_heat_3d" => "Native heat_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t = alpha (u_xx + u_yy + u_zz)` with Crank-Nicolson; `bc = [x-, x+, y-, y+, z-, z+]`. # Errors As [`heat_1d`](super::heat_1d).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_laplace_1d" => "Native laplace_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_xx = 0` on a 1D grid. # Errors As [`poisson_1d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_laplace_2d" => "Native laplace_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `Laplace(u) = 0` (see [`poisson_2d`]). # Errors As [`poisson_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_laplace_3d" => "Native laplace_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `Laplace(u) = 0` on a 3D grid. # Errors As [`poisson_3d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_method_of_lines_1d" => "Native stiff1D method of lines. Compiled RHS inputs have domains(time,length,value,value/length,value/length^2),output value/time. Initial program is length->value. Method Radau5 or Bdf1..Bdf5; physical atol and all step bounds retain the grid time/value domains; explicit attempted-step and per-program evaluation budgets. field_data exposes native accepted steps/evaluations. Method of lines for `u_t = F(t, x, u, u_x, u_xx)` on a 1D grid with a stiff ODE solver. Semi-discretises with second-order central differences (ghost nodes for Neumann ends, Dirichlet nodes held fixed) and integrates the ODE system to `t_end`. Each boundary callback is evaluated once at `(endpoint, 0)` and its spatial value is used throughout the solve. # Errors [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] for a bad grid or `t_end`, [`PdeError::Ode`] if the integrator fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_method_of_lines_1d_timed" => "Stiff 1D method of lines with two time-dependent compiled boundary programs in left-to-right order. Set each kind to Dirichlet for a measured field value or Neumann for a measured outward derivative. Each boundary program takes a measured Time. The returned field retains physical samples and exact prescribed Dirichlet endpoint values at the requested final time. Integrates a 1D method-of-lines problem with time-dependent endpoint data. The endpoint callbacks receive each RHS evaluation time, including internal integrator stages. Dirichlet endpoints are excluded from the ODE unknowns. Neumann data are outward normal derivatives. The returned field includes the prescribed Dirichlet values at `t_end`. # Errors Returns [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] for an invalid grid or final time, [`PdeError::NonFinite`] for non-finite boundary data, or [`PdeError::Ode`] when the integrator fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_neumann" => "Retain an immutable compiled outward-gradient boundary program; field/length output and physical tangential coordinates are checked. Neumann condition with a position-dependent outward flux.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_neumann_const" => "Constant measured outward normal gradient has field/length dimension. Constant Neumann flux (`0.0` for an insulated / reflecting wall).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_poisson_1d" => "Native poisson_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `-u_xx = f` on a 1D grid; `bc = [left, right]`. # Errors As [`poisson_2d`](super::poisson_2d).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_poisson_2d" => "Native poisson_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `-(u_xx + u_yy) = f` with the 5-point stencil; `bc = [left, right, bottom, top]`. A problem with Neumann conditions on all four sides is singular and is rejected with [`PdeError::Unsupported`], unless `solver` is [`PoissonSolver::MeanFree`], which solves it in the zero-mean subspace when the data are compatible. # Errors [`PdeError::InvalidInput`], [`PdeError::Unsupported`] (pure Neumann, or CG with a Neumann side), [`PdeError::SolveFailed`] (singular matrix or CG not converged), [`PdeError::Incompatible`] or [`PdeError::NotConverged`] ([`PoissonSolver::MeanFree`]).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_poisson_3d" => "Native poisson_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `-(u_xx + u_yy + u_zz) = f` with the 7-point stencil; `bc = [x-, x+, y-, y+, z-, z+]`. # Errors As [`poisson_2d`](super::poisson_2d).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_solver" => "Native PdeProblem dispatch. Kind0..2heat1D..3D,3..5wave,6..8Poisson,12..14advection; use dedicated Laplace endpoints for9..11. Reference and compiled-function contracts match dedicated native solvers. Solves a [`PdeProblem`] with the matching finite-difference scheme. # Errors Any [`PdeError`] of the underlying solver.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_wave_1d" => "Native wave_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_tt = c^2 u_xx` with the explicit leapfrog scheme; `v0` is the initial velocity `u_t(x, 0)`. The scheme is second-order and stable for the Courant number `c dt / dx <= 1`, which is checked up front. # Errors [`PdeError::Unstable`] when the CFL condition fails, or the errors of [`heat_1d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_wave_2d" => "Native wave_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_tt = c^2 (u_xx + u_yy)` with leapfrog; stable for `c dt sqrt(1/dx^2 + 1/dy^2) <= 1`. # Errors As [`wave_1d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pde_wave_3d" => "Native wave_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_tt = c^2 (u_xx + u_yy + u_zz)` with leapfrog; stable for `c dt sqrt(1/dx^2 + 1/dy^2 + 1/dz^2) <= 1`. # Errors As [`wave_1d`](super::wave_1d).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_backward_euler_step" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. One backward Euler step: `y1 = y0 + h f(t + h, y1)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_bogacki" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE system using the Bogacki-Shampine 2(3) method.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_cash_karp" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE system using the Cash-Karp 4(5) method.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_dormand" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE system forward in time using the Dormand-Prince 5(4) method. The output includes the initial state and every accepted step. The last time is exactly the requested endpoint when it is reached. Invalid controls, non-finite rates, or a step that cannot advance return the accepted prefix. Tolerances are nonnegative and at least one must be positive.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_dormand_new" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Creates a new Dormand-Prince 5(4) solver.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_explicit_euler" => "Evolves a native measured ODE tape using Forward, Midpoint or Heun with a signed time step. The last interval is shortened to reach the requested endpoint. Returns time and mixed physical state for each accepted grid point. Forward needs one tape evaluation per step and Midpoint and Heun need two. Solves an ODE with a fixed Euler formula, ending exactly at the requested time. The sign of `dt` must agree with the direction of travel. Times are located on the nominal grid from the start, without accumulating rounding drift. State updates use the represented interval between successive grid points. The last step ends at `t_span.1`, shortening the interval if necessary. A zero-length span returns the initial state without evaluating the RHS. # Errors Returns an error for invalid times, a zero or oppositely signed step, mismatched dimensions, non-finite states or right-hand sides, unrepresentable progress, or a history whose size cannot be represented or reserved.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_forward_euler" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE with first-order forward Euler and a complete step at each sample. For an endpoint between grid points the final sample uses a full step. Use [`solve_explicit_euler`] for a checked solve ending at the requested time.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_heun_euler" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE with second-order Heun and complete steps. Use [`solve_explicit_euler`] to terminate exactly at the requested time.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_implicit_midpoint_step" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. One implicit midpoint step: `y1 = y0 + h f(t + h/2, (y0 + y1)/2)` (symplectic and A-stable, order 2).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_midpoint_euler" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE with second-order explicit midpoint and complete steps. Use [`solve_explicit_euler`] to terminate exactly at the requested time.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_rk4" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE system using the classic 4th-order Runge-Kutta method with a fixed step size. This method is a widely used, robust, and relatively accurate explicit method for approximating the solutions of ordinary differential equations. # Arguments * `system` - The ODE system to solve, implementing the `OdeSystem` trait. * `y0` - The initial state vector. * `t_span` - A tuple `(t_start, t_end)` specifying the time interval. * `dt` - The fixed time step. # Returns A `Vec` of tuples `(time, state_vector)` representing the solution at each time step.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_rosenbrock_solve" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` from `t0` to `t1` with the adaptive Rosenbrock method ROS2 (L-stable, order 2; `γ = 1 + 1/√2`), the error estimated against the linearly implicit Euler step. Returns the accepted `(t, y)` pairs, or `None` when a linear system is singular or the step size underflows.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_simulate_damped_oscillator_scenario" => "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Simulates a damped harmonic oscillator.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_simulate_gravity_semi_implicit_euler_scenario" => "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Solves a 2D orbital mechanics problem using the energy-conserving semi-implicit Euler method. This scenario simulates the orbit of a celestial body around a much more massive star, demonstrating the semi-implicit Euler method's ability to preserve energy and produce stable, realistic orbits over long simulation times. # Returns A `Vec` of tuples `(time, state_vector)` representing the solution path. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_semi_implicit_euler` function encounters an error, e.g., if the initial state vector dimensions are incorrect.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_simulate_lorenz_attractor_scenario" => "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Simulates the Lorenz attractor system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_simulate_lotka_volterra_scenario" => "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Simulates the Lotka-Volterra predator-prey system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_simulate_oscillator_forward_euler_scenario" => "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Solves the damped harmonic oscillator with the less stable forward Euler method. This scenario demonstrates the numerical energy gain over time that can occur with the forward Euler method when solving oscillatory systems, highlighting its limitations for such problems. # Returns A `Vec` of tuples `(time, state_vector)` representing the solution path.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_simulate_stiff_decay_scenario" => "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Solves a stiff ODE system, demonstrating the stability of the backward Euler method. This scenario highlights how the backward Euler method can handle stiff equations with relatively large time steps, whereas a forward method would become unstable. # Returns A `Result` containing the solution path as `Vec<(f64, Vec<f64>)>`. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_backward_euler_linear` function encounters an error, e.g., if matrix inversion fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_simulate_vanderpol_scenario" => "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Simulates the Van der Pol oscillator.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_system_dim" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_physics_system_eval" => "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_assignment_deposit" => "Uses the prepared positions and weights with one measured charge per particle. Returns a periodic mesh density in charge per unit length. Deposits one charge per prepared particle as density per unit length. # Errors The charge array length differs from the prepared particle count.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_assignment_gather" => "Interpolates one measured value per cell at the prepared particle positions. A positive field reference with the same dimension specifies the normalization. Results keep the field dimension and original particle order. Evaluates the prepared stencils on a mesh field. # Errors The field does not have one entry per grid cell.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_assignment_new" => "Prepares the periodic grid stencils of fixed measured positions in particle order. Select Linear (or CIC) or Cubic to share the same interpolation weights between deposition and gathering. Prepares the periodic weights for repeated transfers at fixed positions. # Errors A position is non-finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_config_new" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Validates a configuration. # Errors If the mesh or the box length is invalid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_deposit" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Deposits `q` at `pos` and returns the charge density per unit length. Positions are wrapped periodically. The two slices are zipped, so a trailing element of the longer slice is ignored.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_field" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. The electric field at the cell centres from the last half step.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_gather" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Gathers a mesh quantity to the particles with the same shape weights. # Errors If `field` does not have one entry per cell.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_grid" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. The mesh.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_grid_cells" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Number of cells.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_grid_length" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Box length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_grid_new" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Creates a periodic mesh with `cells` cells on a box of length `length`. # Errors If `cells` is not a power of two of at least 8, `length` is not finite and positive, or the spacing rounds to zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_grid_spacing" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Cell size `length / cells`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_new" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Creates a system from equal-length particle arrays. # Errors If the configuration is invalid, the arrays differ in length, there are no particles, or a mass is not finite and positive.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_positions" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Particle positions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_shape" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. The shape function.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_shape_cells" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Number of mesh cells a particle contributes to (the stencil width).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_shape_order" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Order of the shape's interpolation error (the field error at particles).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_solve" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Solves `-d^2 phi / dx^2 = rho` and returns `phi` and `E = -d phi / dx`. The zero mode is dropped, i.e. a uniform neutralising background is assumed. The `CIC` structure factor is divided out of the linear mesh density; the cubic kernel reproduces cubics, so it needs no correction. # Errors If `rho` does not have one entry per cell.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_step" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. One leapfrog step: half kick, drift, field solve, half kick. # Errors If `dt` is not finite and positive, or the mesh solve fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_total_charge" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Total charge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_total_momentum" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Total momentum `sum m v`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_pic_velocities" => "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q²T²/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Particle velocities.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_poly_eval_complex_scaled" => "Native normalized polynomial value and exact polynomial derivative, restored with independent positive coordinate and output references. Evaluates a polynomial (coefficients in ascending order) and its derivative at a complex point with Horner's scheme.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_polynomial_antidifference_difference" => "Evaluate F(to) minus F(from) using a divided difference and the measured interval width. Both endpoints share the prepared coordinate dimension. The result keeps the polynomial coefficient dimension. Evaluate `F(to) - F(from)` without subtracting two rounded values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_polynomial_antidifference_eval" => "Evaluate the prepared polynomial antidifference at a measured coordinate. Its result keeps the polynomial coefficient dimension. Evaluate the antidifference at one point.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_polynomial_antidifference_new" => "Prepare the discrete antidifference for up to 128 ascending polynomial coefficients. Use normalized coordinate u = (x - origin) / step. The origin and positive step have the same dimension, and all coefficients have the common value dimension. The prepared function F is zero at the origin and F(x + step) - F(x) is the polynomial evaluated at u. Compile the antidifference of the given ascending power coefficients.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_qp_active_set_scaled" => "Native active-set QP with positive coordinate,objective,equality and inequality references. H has E/D^2,g E/D,Aeq C/D,beq C,Aineq G/D,bineq G; result step D and multipliers E/C,E/G. Solves `min 0.5 d^T H d + c^T d` subject to `A_eq d = b_eq` and `A_ineq d <= b_ineq` by a primal active-set method started from `d = 0`, which must be feasible (`b_ineq >= 0`). The KKT system of the working set is solved exactly at every step, so the returned multipliers are those of the Lagrangian `0.5 d^T H d + c^T d + lambda . (A_eq d - b_eq) + mu . (A_ineq d - b_ineq)`. Returns `None` if a KKT system stays singular after regularisation or the working set fails to settle within the iteration budget.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_quadrature_exp_sinh_scaled" => "Normalized native quadrature::exp_sinh: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Exp-sinh quadrature on `[a, inf)`; suitable for algebraic or exponential decay and endpoint singularities at `a`.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_quadrature_filon_scaled" => "Normalized native quadrature::filon: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length2, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Filon quadrature for `integral_a^b f(x) cos(w x) dx` (`cosine = true`) or `sin(w x)` (`cosine = false`), using `2 * panels` sub-intervals. Exact when `f` is quadratic on each double panel, so the accuracy does not degrade as the frequency `w` grows.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_quadrature_gk_breakpoints_scaled" => "Integrates a normalized callback with a shared tolerance and panel budget across known interior breakpoints. The Float parameters are `[a, b, abs_tol, rel_tol, ...breakpoints]` and the integer controls are `[rule, max_panels]` with rule 0 for G7K15 or 1 for G10K21. Pass positive coordinate and integrand reference scales and a callback evaluation budget. The result value and error have the product of the two reference dimensions. Adaptive Gauss–Kronrod integration split at known interior breakpoints. Supply breakpoints in increasing coordinate order even for reversed limits. Kronrod samples each subinterval's interior, avoiding the breakpoints for ordinary representable intervals. The panel budget and the tolerance `max(abs_tol, rel_tol * |value|)` apply to the entire partition. Exhausting the refinement budget returns an estimate with `converged = false` if the error is still above tolerance. # Errors Returns an error for invalid endpoints, tolerances or breakpoints, an insufficient initial panel budget, a nonfinite estimate, or cancellation.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_quadrature_gk_panel_scaled" => "Normalized native quadrature::gk_panel: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. One Gauss-Kronrod panel on `[a, b]` returning `(kronrod, error, evals)`. The error is the QUADPACK heuristic built from the Gauss/Kronrod difference and the integral of `|f - mean|`.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_quadrature_integrate_scaled" => "Normalized native quadrature::integrate: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Adaptive G7K15 integration with a default panel budget (2000). `tol` is the relative tolerance (absolute is `1e-3 * tol`).\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_quadrature_sinh_sinh_scaled" => "Normalized native quadrature::sinh_sinh: parameters Float vector length1, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Sinh-sinh quadrature on `(-inf, inf)` for rapidly decaying integrands.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_quadrature_tanh_sinh_scaled" => "Normalized native quadrature::tanh_sinh: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Tanh-sinh quadrature on `[a, b]`. Handles integrable endpoint singularities (`1/sqrt(x)`, `log x`, ...) because the nodes cluster double-exponentially at the end points and the integrand is never evaluated at them. `tol` is the relative tolerance.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_quasi_monte_carlo_compiled" => "Native serial Halton/Sobol cubature with checked physical compiled-function references; sequence0=Halton,1=Sobol. Quasi-Monte Carlo integral of `f` over the box `[lo, hi]`. The error estimate is the difference to the estimate that uses only the first half of the points. Matching bounds may describe a reversed axis. Returns an undefined integral without evaluating `f` for mismatched bounds, non-finite axis widths, unsupported dimensions, or a zero sample count.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_quasi_monte_carlo_parallel" => "True native parallel Halton/Sobol cubature with checked physical compiled-function references; sequence0=Halton,1=Sobol. Parallel [`quasi_monte_carlo`] with a fixed summation order. Each block starts at its exact sequence index and then advances locally. Blocks of 4096 points are summed concurrently, and their sums are added in block order. Different thread counts give the same result. The serial implementation adds the same samples with a different association.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_regression_cost" => "Evaluate the retained native regression CostFunction with measured intercept/slope coefficients; returns physical half mean-squared residual units.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_regression_value_and_gradient" => "Return the half mean-squared residual and its coefficient gradient using the same residual pass. If predictor units are D and response units are E, coefficients use E then E/D, value uses E squared, and the gradient uses E then E times D. The gradient preserves the intercept-first coefficient order. Half the mean squared residual and its coefficient gradient. The intercept is the first coefficient, followed by one slope per column of the design. Both outputs come from the same residual pass. # Errors Returns an error when the design is empty, the row counts differ, or the coefficient count is not `columns + 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_root_near_scaled" => "Normalized native rootfind::root_near: parameters Float vector length1, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. A root of `f` near `guess`, by Newton's method with a bisection fallback. If no sign change is found, a local minimum of the residual magnitude can identify a touching root. A callback that rounds residuals to zero limits positional accuracy. Positive offsets below the local resolution can be indistinguishable from a touching root. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for a non-finite guess or a non-finite value inside a bracket. [`RootError::NoConvergence`] when no sign change or resolved touching root is found.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_rootfind_homotopy_univariate_scaled" => "Native homotopy_univariate on normalized polynomial coefficients with a positive physical coordinate reference; returned complex roots restore its units. Degree and iteration work are bounded before native calls. Homotopy continuation for the roots of a real-coefficient polynomial. Homotopy continuation for a polynomial with real coefficients: tracks the roots of `H(z, t) = (1-t) * gamma * (z^n - 1) + t * p(z)` from the roots of unity at `t = 0` to those of `p` at `t = 1` with predictor (Euler) and Newton corrector steps and adaptive step size. This is the one-variable instance of total-degree homotopy continuation; the random complex `gamma` is fixed (deterministic) so generic paths do not cross. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if a path fails to be tracked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_rootfind_polynomial_roots_companion_scaled" => "Native polynomial_roots_companion on normalized polynomial coefficients with a positive physical coordinate reference; returned complex roots restore its units. Degree and iteration work are bounded before native calls. Roots of a polynomial as eigenvalues of its companion matrix. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if the QR iteration fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_rootfind_polynomial_roots_jenkins_traub_complex_scaled" => "Native polynomial_roots_jenkins_traub_complex on normalized polynomial coefficients with a positive physical coordinate reference; returned complex roots restore its units. Degree and iteration work are bounded before native calls. All complex roots of a complex polynomial by Jenkins-Traub. All complex roots of a polynomial with complex coefficients (`(re, im)` pairs, ascending order) by the three-stage Jenkins-Traub algorithm (no-shift, fixed-shift and variable-shift stages with shifts on a circle just inside the smallest root modulus), polished by Newton iteration against the original polynomial. Roots are found in order of increasing modulus and deflated forward, which is the numerically stable direction. Roots at zero are detected from vanishing low-order coefficients. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if the shift iteration fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_rootfind_polynomial_roots_jenkins_traub_scaled" => "Native polynomial_roots_jenkins_traub on normalized polynomial coefficients with a positive physical coordinate reference; returned complex roots restore its units. Degree and iteration work are bounded before native calls. All complex roots of a real-coefficient polynomial (ascending order) by the Jenkins-Traub algorithm; see [`polynomial_roots_jenkins_traub_complex`]. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if the shift iteration fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_adaptive" => "Actual native sde_adaptive with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. [`sde_adaptive_with`] with the options passed as separate arguments. # Errors See [`sde_adaptive_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_adaptive_noncommutative_stratonovich_with" => "Actual native sde_adaptive_noncommutative_stratonovich_with with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. Adaptive-step integration of a **Stratonovich** SDE with **non-commutative** noise on `[t0, t1]`, by exact conversion to Ito form. As [`sde_adaptive_stratonovich_with`], with the controller of [`sde_adaptive_noncommutative_with`] (fine cell grid, Chen's relation); the returned path is the controlled Ito-Milstein solution of the equivalent Ito SDE, which converges to the Stratonovich solution. The Brownian path is the same as for the Ito solver ([`noncommutative_noise_grid`]). # Errors As [`sde_adaptive_noncommutative_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_adaptive_noncommutative_with" => "Actual native sde_adaptive_noncommutative_with with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. Adaptive-step Milstein integration of an Ito SDE with **non-commutative** noise on `[t0, t1]`. The Brownian path is fixed in advance on a grid of `opts.cells` cells, each with its increment and its Levy areas ([`levy_areas`]); a step is a union of cells, whose increment and areas are combined *exactly* by Chen's relation `A_{0,2h} = A_1 + A_2 + (W_1 x W_2 - W_2 x W_1) / 2`. Splitting a rejected step is therefore merging fewer of the same cells: the realised path is the same for every step sequence, the errors of the controller cannot bias the noise, and a fixed-step reference on the cells sees exactly the path the adaptive solver used ([`noncommutative_noise_grid`]). The error control is that of [`sde_adaptive_with`], with the Milstein correction built from the areas (for commutative noise the correction is the same function of the increments, so the estimate and the controller agree with it in law). A step of a single cell that is still rejected ends the run with [`SdeError::StepLimit`]: use more cells. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`], [`SdeError::NonFinite`] or [`SdeError::StepLimit`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_adaptive_stratonovich_with" => "Actual native sde_adaptive_stratonovich_with with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. Adaptive-step integration of a **Stratonovich** SDE `dX = a dt + g o dW` with commutative noise on `[t0, t1]`, by exact conversion to Ito form. The drift is replaced by the Ito-equivalent drift ([`stratonovich_to_ito_drift`]) and [`sde_adaptive_with`] runs on the Ito SDE `dX = a~ dt + g dW`, which has the same solution process. The returned path is therefore the *controlled Ito-Milstein solution of the equivalent Ito SDE*; it converges to the Stratonovich solution of the original coefficients (for `dX = sigma X o dW` to `X_0 exp(sigma W_T)`), with the error control of [`sde_adaptive_with`] applied to the converted drift. The conversion uses finite-difference derivatives of `g`, which costs `2m + 1` extra diffusion evaluations per drift evaluation. # Errors As [`sde_adaptive_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_adaptive_with" => "Actual native sde_adaptive_with with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. Adaptive-step integration of an Ito SDE with commutative noise on `[t0, t1]`. Each step computes the Euler-Maruyama and the Milstein update from the same Brownian increment; the Milstein correction plus the trapezoidal drift correction `h |a(y_EM) - a(y)| / 2` estimate the local error of the former (so additive noise and strongly varying drifts both control the step). A step is accepted when the scaled error `max_i |delta_i| / (atol + rtol |y_i|)` is at most one (the tolerance bounds the local, not the global, pathwise error; the global error accumulates like a random walk). Rejected increments are split by Brownian bridges and re-used, so the realised path is a genuine Brownian sample independent of the step sequence. The returned solution is the Milstein one. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`], [`SdeError::NonFinite`] or [`SdeError::StepLimit`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_aggregate_noise" => "Actual native Chen aggregation retains the same physical Brownian path and iterated integrals. Merges groups of `group` consecutive steps. The increments add up and the iterated integrals combine exactly as `I_jl = sum_k I_jl^k + sum_{k<k'} dW_j^k dW_l^{k'}`. Used to compare a coarse scheme with a fine reference on the same Brownian path.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_brownian_increments" => "Actual native Brownian increments in physical sqrt(time) units. h must be strictly positive; shape and allocation budgets are checked. Generates `steps` Brownian increments (`m` components each, variance `h`) from `rng`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_drift_implicit_core" => "Native drift-implicit path over retained Brownian increments. All five scheme variants use physical time and state references and share a checked evaluation budget. Fixed-step drift-implicit integration over prescribed Wiener increments. Rejects inconsistent shapes, invalid timesteps, non-finite coefficients and nonconvergent implicit equations. Milstein includes all commutative cross terms.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_drift_implicit_sampled" => "Runs all drift-implicit steps over the retained Brownian increments and returns the initial state, every state at a positive multiple of the stride, and the final state once. State units and callback evaluation limits match the full path. Runs every fixed drift-implicit step while retaining selected states. The result starts at `y0`, includes the state after every `save_every` increments, and ends at the final state without duplicating it. A path with no increments returns only `y0`. Saved states use the same arithmetic and callback evaluations as [`drift_implicit_core`]. No randomness is drawn internally. `t0` and `dt` are times, `increments` are Wiener increments, and the states retain the coordinate units of `y0`. # Errors Rejects zero retention stride, inconsistent shapes, a non-advancing grid, non-finite coefficients or states, and nonconvergent implicit equations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_drift_implicit_step_areas" => "Native drift-implicit single step using the specified retained conditional Levy-area sample and its iterated integrals. One drift-implicit step with prescribed Brownian increments and iterated Itô integrals. Noncommutative Milstein uses the supplied full iterated-integral matrix; Euler/theta schemes do not need those integrals. Invalid shapes and nonconvergent Newton iterations return errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_ensemble" => "Actual parallel native ensemble on immutable owned drift/diffusion tapes. The explicit global evaluation budget is shared atomically across real parallel members; original failures are preserved. Returned final states retain independent physical units. Simulates `paths` independent sample paths of `dX = a dt + g dW` with `steps` fixed steps of size `h` and returns the end state of each, in path order. Path `i` draws its Brownian increments from `Rng::new(ensemble_seed(seed, i))`, so the result is independent of the thread count and equals running the paths one after the other. The first error (in path order) is returned. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`] from a member.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_ensemble_seed" => "Actual deterministic native ensemble member seed. Seed of member `index` of an ensemble with base `seed`: a function of the pair only, so the members do not depend on how many threads run them or in which order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_error_invalid" => "Construct the actual native invalid-parameter error object; text is bounded and preserved. Convenience constructor for [`Self::Invalid`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_error_message" => "Inspect the retained native SDE extension error text.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_euler_maruyama" => "Actual native euler_maruyama over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Euler-Maruyama path with explicit increments; `dws[k]` is the increment over `[t0 + k h, t0 + (k + 1) h]`. Returns the `dws.len() + 1` states. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_heun_stratonovich" => "Actual native heun_stratonovich over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Heun predictor-corrector for the *Stratonovich* interpretation `dX = a dt + g o dW` (strong order 1 for commutative noise, converges to the Stratonovich solution). # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_ito_drift_eval" => "Evaluate the actual native converted Ito drift. Each result component has its state unit divided by the time unit, with a checked shared evaluation budget.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_levy_areas" => "Actual native conditional Levy areas. Supplied increments have sqrt(time) units and output flattened m by m Ito integrals have time units. Samples the iterated Ito integrals `I_jl` of one step of length `h`. The integrals are drawn conditionally on the increments `dw` by the Fourier expansion of the Brownian bridge (Kloeden, Platen and Wright) with the Gaussian approximation of the truncated tail that reproduces its full conditional covariance (Wiktorsson's improvement): with `xi = dw / sqrt(h)`, `I_jl / h = xi_j xi_l / 2 + A_jl + sqrt(rho) (mu_j xi_l - mu_l xi_j) + sqrt(rho) nu_jl` for `j < l`, where `A` is the Fourier sum over `terms` modes, `rho` the tail sum divided by `2 pi^2`, and `mu`, `nu` standard normal. The remainder error decays like `1 / terms^{3/2}` in distribution; a few terms suffice for strong order one. `dw` contains the `m` supplied increments. The returned matrix is flat and row-major. Pair evaluation can run in parallel, with the same RNG draws and floating-point summation order for every worker count.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_milstein" => "Actual native milstein over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Milstein scheme (strong order 1) for commutative noise; arguments as in [`euler_maruyama`]. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_milstein_noncommutative" => "Actual native milstein_noncommutative over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Milstein scheme (strong order 1) for **non-commutative** noise. The correction uses the sampled iterated integrals of `noise` instead of the symmetric products `(dW_j dW_k - delta_jk h) / 2`. For commutative noise it is equivalent to [`milstein`] in law. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_milstein_noncommutative_stratonovich" => "Actual native milstein_noncommutative_stratonovich over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Milstein scheme (strong order 1) for **non-commutative** noise in the *Stratonovich* interpretation `dX = a dt + g o dW`. The correction is `sum_{j,l} (D_j g)_{.l} J_jl` with the Stratonovich iterated integrals `J_jl = I_jl + delta_jl h / 2 = dW_j dW_l / 2 + A_jl` (`A` the Levy area), i.e. the same sampled areas as [`milstein_noncommutative`] with the diagonal shifted by `h / 2`. The drift is the Stratonovich drift, unchanged. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_milstein_stratonovich" => "Actual native milstein_stratonovich over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Milstein scheme (strong order 1) for the *Stratonovich* interpretation `dX = a dt + g o dW` of the same coefficients; arguments as in [`euler_maruyama`]. It differs from the Ito [`milstein`] only in the iterated integral of the correction, `I_jk = dW_j dW_k / 2` instead of `(dW_j dW_k - delta_jk h) / 2`: for scalar noise the correction is `g g' dW^2 / 2` rather than `g g' (dW^2 - h) / 2`, so the two differ by the Ito-Stratonovich drift correction `g g' h / 2` and converge to different solutions. For `dX = sigma X dW` they are `X_0 exp(sigma W_T)` (here) and `X_0 exp(sigma W_T - sigma^2 T / 2)` ([`milstein`]). # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_model_new" => "Owned native drift/diffusion tapes. Diffusion outputs are row-major n by m and have component dimensions state/sqrt(time); all input/output reference scales are explicit and may differ from the drift tape frame.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_noise_data" => "Physical Brownian/Levy arrays from a retained sample; increments have sqrt(time) units and areas have time units.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_noncommutative_noise_grid" => "Actual native reproducible noncommutative grid. The normalized [0,1] reference frame is explicitly a physical time reference equal to the requested positive span; state-independent noise retains genuine conditional areas. The fine-cell Brownian path of [`sde_adaptive_noncommutative_with`] as a [`NoiseSample`]. Step `h = (t1 - t0) / cells` on `[t0, t1]`; use it to compare the adaptive solution with a fine fixed-step [`milstein_noncommutative`] reference on the *same* path.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_path_data" => "Physical native accepted samples and original rejection/seed statistics; no interpolant is invented for stochastic paths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_sample_alpha_stable_with" => "Native callback-based alpha-stable sampler using the retained RNG. Scale and location have the same physical domain; alpha/beta are dimensionless. Samples an alpha-stable random variable `S_alpha(scale, beta, location)` via Chambers-Mallows-Stuck (1976). At `alpha = 2` this uses one standard normal draw. Otherwise it consumes two uniform draws from `[0, 1)` and uses the S1 scale and location parametrization, including its logarithmic correction at `alpha = 1`. # Errors Invalid parameters, invalid uniform draws, or a non-finite sample.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_sample_noise" => "Actual native conditional Brownian increments/Levy areas. Positive time reference defines normalized stochastic time; physical h has the same dimension. RNG state advances exactly through the native routine. Samples `steps` Brownian increments and their iterated integrals ([`levy_areas`]) for `m` Wiener processes and step size `h`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_sample_poisson_with" => "Native callback-based Poisson sampler instantiated with the retained native RNG's actual unit/normal streams. Exact Poisson sampling: Knuth multiplication for small means and Hoermann transformed rejection (PTRS) for large means. The normal callback is retained for source compatibility and is unused.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_srk_noncommutative" => "Actual native srk_noncommutative over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Derivative-free stochastic Runge-Kutta scheme of strong order 1 for **non-commutative** Ito noise (Kloeden-Platen, eq. 11.1.7). With the supporting values `U^j = Y + a h + g^j sqrt(h)` (`g^j` the `j`-th column of `g`, one per noise channel), `Y' = Y + a h + sum_j g^j dW_j + h^{-1/2} sum_{j,l} (g^l(U^j) - g^l(Y)) I_jl`, where `I_jl` are the sampled iterated Ito integrals of `noise` ([`sample_noise`]). It needs `m` extra diffusion evaluations per step and no derivative of `g`. On commutative noise the Levy-area part cancels and it agrees with [`srk_platen`] up to the `O(h)` of the supporting values. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_srk_noncommutative_stratonovich" => "Actual native srk_noncommutative_stratonovich over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. The Stratonovich counterpart of [`srk_noncommutative`]. After Kloeden-Platen, eq. 11.1.10: with the Stratonovich iterated integrals `J_jl = I_jl + delta_jl h / 2` and the Stratonovich drift it converges to the solution of `dX = a dt + g o dW` with strong order 1. Because `J_jj` has mean `h / 2` (unlike `I_jj`), the difference quotient must be accurate to `O(h)`: the scheme uses the *central* supporting values `U^j_{+-} = Y +- g^j sqrt(h)` and `Y' = Y + a h + sum_j g^j dW_j + (2 sqrt(h))^{-1} sum_{j,l} (g^l(U^j_+) - g^l(U^j_-)) J_jl` (a forward quotient would leave a systematic `O(sqrt(h))` error and only strong order 1/2). It costs `2m` extra diffusion evaluations per step. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_srk_platen" => "Actual native srk_platen over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Platen's derivative-free stochastic Runge-Kutta scheme for commutative Itô noise, with strong order 1. Each supplied Brownian increment has `m` components. The scheme replaces the Milstein term with differences of the diffusion at supporting values `x + a h + g_j sqrt(abs(h))`. Corrections at extreme scales retain an out-of-range intermediate product in scaled form when the weighted result is representable. An empty increment sequence returns just `y0` without evaluating either callback. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sde_stratonovich_to_ito_drift" => "Owned native Stratonovich-to-Ito drift recipe. Evaluation calls the actual native derivative-based conversion over the immutable physical reference frame, retaining no source callback lifetime. The Ito-equivalent drift of a Stratonovich SDE `dX = a dt + g o dW`: `a~_i = a_i + (1/2) sum_j sum_k g_{kj} d_k g_{ij}`. The correction is `(1/2) sum_j (D_j g)_{.j}` with the directional derivative of column `j` along itself, with the same scaled probes as the Milstein term. The ordinary step is `eps = 1e-6 max(1, |y|_inf) / |g^j|`. A probe uses one side at the floating-point range boundary when only that side is finite. The closure writes `a~` into its output, so any Ito integrator applied to `(a~, g)` converges to the Stratonovich solution of `(a, g)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sdirk_eval" => "Actual native SDIRK Hermite dense output in physical state coordinates. Evaluates the cubic Hermite continuous extension at `t`. Within an accepted step `[t_n, t_{n+1}]` the interpolant is the cubic Hermite polynomial matching `y_n`, `y_{n+1}` and the endpoint slopes `f(t_n, y_n)`, `f(t_{n+1}, y_{n+1})`. Returns `None` outside the integrated range or when no step was accepted.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_series_sum_infinity_scaled" => "Normalized native series::sum_to_infinity: parameters Float vector length1, exact nonnegative integer controls length2, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Sum of `term(k)` for `k = from, from + 1, ...` to infinity. The integer index advances through `i64::MAX`. See [`sum_terms_to_infinity`] for the stopping rules. Use that entry point for recurrences or to keep indices exact beyond the integer precision of the `f64` callback.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_series_sum_range_scaled" => "Normalized native series::sum_range: parameters Float vector length0, exact nonnegative integer controls length2, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_series_sum_terms" => "Estimates an infinite sum from a finite measured prefix, stopping at the requested absolute tolerance, the end of the prefix, or the term budget. The record's value and heuristic error have the term dimension. Missing estimates are None, and terms reports the number consumed. Estimates an infinite sum from terms supplied in order. This accepts an unbounded generator or a finite prefix. At each doubling checkpoint it compares Wynn's epsilon algorithm on the most recent partial sums, Richardson extrapolation on the doubling checkpoints, and the Levin `u` transform of the first terms. A final partial block may still contribute Wynn or Levin estimates. The calculation stops on the requested tolerance, input exhaustion, or after evaluating `max_terms` terms. The reported error is a change between estimates and is heuristic. When extrapolation has insufficient data, the result is the available partial sum with infinite error. A non-finite term or partial sum yields `NaN` and infinite error, counting the triggering term in `terms`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_signal_fft" => "Native signal fft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the one-dimensional discrete Fourier Transform. This function uses the `rustfft` library to perform the FFT. # Arguments * `input` - A slice of complex samples. Its contents are preserved. # Returns A vector of complex numbers representing the FFT of the input. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::signal::fft; use rustfft::num_complex::Complex; let mut input = vec![ Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), ]; let output = fft(&mut input); assert!((output[0].re - 4.0).abs() < 1e-9); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_signal_ifft" => "Native signal ifft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the one-dimensional inverse discrete Fourier Transform. This is the positive-sign transform followed by the `1/n` normalisation, so `ifft(fft(x)) == x` (up to round-off) for every length. # Arguments * `input` - A mutable slice of complex frequency-domain samples. # Returns A vector of complex numbers representing the inverse transform. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::signal::fft; use rssn::kernels::signal::ifft; use rustfft::num_complex::Complex; let mut input = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 0.5), Complex::new(-2.0, 0.0)]; let spectrum = fft(&mut input); let back = ifft(&mut spectrum.clone()); assert!((back[0] - input[0]).norm() < 1e-12); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_signal_stft" => "Native signal stft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Short-time Fourier transform of complete frames with starts `hop` samples apart. Returns full unnormalised complex spectra. The number of bins per frame is the window length. Incomplete trailing frames are discarded. Empty windows, zero hops and signals shorter than a window yield an empty result.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_simplex_scaled" => "Native simplex LP with normalized coefficient conversion using positive coordinate/objective/constraint references; preserves Optimal,Infeasible,Unbounded without fabricated coordinates or objective. Two-phase simplex: minimise `c . x` subject to rows `a_i . x (rel_i) b_i` and `x >= 0`, with steepest-edge pricing and the Bland anti-cycling fallback; see [`simplex_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_simplex_with_scaled" => "Native simplex LP with actual selectable Dantzig,SteepestEdge,Bland pricing and retained pivot statistics; independent positive coordinate/objective/constraint references. Two-phase tableau simplex with a selectable pricing rule. For [`Pricing::Dantzig`] and [`Pricing::SteepestEdge`] the solver monitors degeneracy: after a run of consecutive pivots that do not improve the objective it switches to Bland's rule (smallest-index entering variable, ties in the ratio test broken by smallest basic index) until a strictly improving pivot occurs, which guarantees termination on cycling examples such as Beale's. The leaving-variable tie-break is always Bland's.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sobol_new" => "Create a real native Sobol sequence in1..8 normalized dimensions; rejects unsupported dimensions instead of clamping. Creates a generator for `dim` (clamped to 1..=8) dimensions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sobol_next" => "Advance the retained native Sobol sequence, returning normalized unit-cube coordinates. Next point in `[0, 1)^dim`, starting at index one. After the last `u32` index the sequence continues from index zero, the origin.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sobol_point_at" => "Returns the indexed normalized unit-cube point without advancing the sequence. Index zero is the origin. The index must fit in an unsigned 32-bit integer. Returns the indexed point without changing the sequence's position. Index zero returns the origin.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_sobol_seek" => "Sets a retained sequence position by its unsigned 32-bit index. The next draw is at index plus one, wrapping the largest index to the origin. Sets the position so that [`Self::next_point`] returns index `index + 1`, modulo `2^32`. Seeking to zero restarts generation from the first nonzero point.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_solve_nonlinear_scaled" => "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Solves the square nonlinear system `f(x) = 0` by Newton's method with a finite-difference Jacobian. `f` writes the residuals for the point given as its first argument into its second argument; both have the length of `start`. # Errors Returns an error when a Jacobian is singular, a residual is not finite, or the iteration does not converge within `max_iter` steps.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_solve_root_bisection_scaled" => "Normalized native solve::solve_root_bisection: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Finds a root of `f` in `interval` by bisection. Endpoints may be in either order. A finite endpoint or midpoint whose residual is zero or has magnitude less than `tolerance` is accepted. Otherwise the absolute interval width is compared with the tolerance. Zero tolerance disables approximate stopping. The two endpoint evaluations are followed by at most `max_iter` midpoint evaluations. At the budget or when no representable point remains inside the bracket, returns its midpoint. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite endpoints or function values, a negative or non-finite tolerance, or endpoints with the same sign outside the tolerance.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_solve_root_newton_scaled" => "Normalized native solve::solve_root_newton: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then2 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Finds a root of `f` by Newton's method, using `df` for the derivative. # Errors Returns an error when the derivative vanishes or the iteration does not converge.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_solve_root_scaled" => "Normalized native solve::solve_root: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Finds a root of `f` near `guess`: Newton's method with a central difference derivative from several starting points, then bisection over growing brackets. A non-finite sample discards the current attempt. # Errors Returns an error for a non-finite guess or a negative or non-finite tolerance, or when every strategy fails.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_solve_with_bfgs_tape" => "Actual native basin solve_with_bfgs instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Minimises `problem` with BFGS. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_solve_with_gradient_descent_tape" => "Actual native basin solve_with_gradient_descent instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Minimises `problem` with steepest descent. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_solve_with_pso_tape" => "Actual native basin solve_with_pso instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Minimises `problem` with a global-best particle swarm. `bounds` is `(lower, upper)`; the swarm needs a finite box. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_special_complex_bessel_i_complex" => "Native special_complex bessel_i_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The modified Bessel function of the first kind `I_ν(z)` for real order `ν` and complex `z` (principal branch).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_special_complex_bessel_j_complex" => "Native special_complex bessel_j_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The Bessel function of the first kind `J_ν(z)` for real order `ν` and complex `z` (principal branch of `z^ν`, cut along the negative real axis).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_special_complex_digamma_complex" => "Native special_complex digamma_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The digamma function `ψ(z) = Γ'(z) / Γ(z)`; poles give NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_special_complex_gamma_complex" => "Native special_complex gamma_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `Γ(z)`; poles (non-positive integers) give `+∞`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_special_complex_lambert_w_complex" => "Return the dimensionless complex solution to w exp(w) = z on integer branch k. When the imaginary component is signed zero on the negative real axis, its sign selects the side of the branch cut. The principal branch preserves small real and imaginary components near the origin. The branch `k` of the Lambert W function: the solution of `w e^w = z` in the strip of branch `k`. On the negative real axis, a positive zero imaginary part selects the upper lip and a negative zero selects the lower lip. `W_0(0) = 0` and `W_k(0) = -∞` for `k != 0`; the branch point `-1/e` gives `-1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_special_complex_ln_gamma_complex" => "Native special_complex ln_gamma_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analytic branch of `ln Γ(z)`, defined and continuous on the plane cut along the non-positive real axis, with `ln Γ(z + 1) = ln Γ(z) + ln z`. Non-positive integers (the poles) give `+∞`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_default_terms" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The default number of Fourier modes of the simulated Lévy areas for a step (or Brownian cell) of size `h`: `clamp(ceil(1 / sqrt(h)), 16, 4096)`. The truncation error of the area decays like `h p^(-3/2)` with the kernel's Gaussian tail correction, so this rule keeps it far below the `O(h)` strong error of the scheme at an `O(m^2 / sqrt(h))` sampling cost per step.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_ensemble" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Integrates one path per ensemble member, sequentially and order-preserving. Member `i` uses [`ensemble_seed`] with `(seed, i)` as its own seed, so the result does not depend on execution order or concurrency. The paths run one after another because the Aevia interpreter closures are `!Send`; the kernel's rayon ensemble would need a `Send + Sync` callback, which this build cannot produce. The returned paths are in index order. # Errors The first member error, in index order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_ensemble_seed" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Seed of path `index` of an ensemble with base `seed`. A function of the pair only (the kernel's derivation), so path `i` is the same whichever order or concurrency runs the ensemble; this build runs the paths sequentially because an interpreter closure is `!Send`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_error_capability" => "Actual native optional capability diagnostic. The capability an `E1001` names, when it is one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_error_code" => "Actual native stable diagnostic code. The stable Aevia diagnostic code.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_error_new" => "Native stochastic error builder with exact Invalid/Shape/NonFinite/StepLimit/Unsupported variants; unsupported capability is an explicit closed known capability name.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_events" => "Actual native sign-change event detection on the native piecewise-linear stochastic path; this does not claim to detect unobserved between-node process crossings. Programs receive coherent physical time/state components. Detects sign changes of `g_i(t, y)` along a path. Between two consecutive nodes the path is the chord of [`Path::eval`]; a crossing is a sign change of `g` at the two nodes in an allowed direction (falling `g0 > 0 >= g1`, rising `g0 < 0 <= g1`, the kernel's convention for ODE events), and its time is the root of `g` along the chord, found by bisection. A node where `g` is exactly zero counts once (as the end of the step that reaches it). Only the discretised path is watched: the continuous process may cross and return between two nodes, which no discrete scheme can see. Hits come in time order. If an event is `terminal`, the returned path ends at the first terminal hit (the hit is appended as its last node) and later hits are dropped. The returned path is the input when nothing terminates.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_integrate" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Integrates `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` from `y0`. `drift` returns `n` values; `diffusion` returns `n` values for [`Structure::Diagonal`] (the diagonal entries) or `n × m` values in row-major order for [`Structure::General`]. Fixed/uniform-grid schedules use prescribed increments. Adaptive schedules retain the requested method and use a bounded noise tree with coupled step doubling, a separate drift-error check, and explicit resolution/work errors. See the module documentation for tolerance scaling and finite allocations. # Errors `E0802` for shapes/parameters, `E0806` for a non-finite state, `E0807` for a step collapse. No method/structure/step-policy combination is refused with `E1001` any more.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_integrate_drift_implicit" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Integrates a (potentially stiff) SDE `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` using the specified [`DriftImplicitScheme`]. Supports `BackwardEuler` (`theta = 1`), `Trapezoidal` (`theta = 0.5`), general `Theta(theta)`, `DriftImplicitMilstein` (strong order 1.0), and `SplitStepBackwardEuler` (for nonlinear stiff superlinear drifts). # Errors Returns [`SdeError`] on invalid options, mismatched callback dimensions, non-finite values, or Newton non-convergence.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_jump_compensated" => "Actual native compensated jump diffusion. Poisson intensity has inverse time units; retained marks have independent physical references. Jump tape receives(time,state,mark) and outputs state increments; expected-jump tape receives(time,state) and outputs the exact supplied expected state increment, rather than evaluating at the mean mark. Native arrivals/marks remain fixed before adaptive rejection. Integrate a jump diffusion with an explicit compensator. Integrate a compensated jump diffusion with an explicit expected jump coefficient. `expected_jump(t, y)` must return the expected coefficient `E[c(t, y, Z)]`. For nonlinear coefficients, evaluate the coefficient under the mark distribution before taking the expectation. # Errors Invalid integration inputs, callback shapes, or non-finite expected coefficients.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_jump_data" => "Physical native jump path samples and actual total/per-step jump counts.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_jump_distribution" => "All native JumpDistribution variants over an explicit positive physical mark reference. data1/data2 contain normalized vectors, controls contain normalized distribution parameters. Exact native validation is called before retention.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_jump_mean" => "Actual native optional analytical mean mark; infinite or undefined stable means remain None. Analytical mean mark `E[Z]` when finite and known (used for compensated Poisson jumps).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_jump_sample" => "Actual native jump mark sampling on the retained native stream, returning physical mark units. Samples one jump mark vector `Z` from the distribution using `stream`. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_jump_sample_batch" => "Draws consecutive marks into a rank-two tensor with one row per sample. Columns use the physical mark reference. Successful calls advance the stream in the same order as single draws. Zero samples and rejected batches keep the stream state. Fills caller-owned storage with consecutive jump marks, in sample-major order. Each mark has the same component count as [`Self::sample`]. Fills consume the same stream draws as repeated calls to that method, and an empty output is a no-op. The mark shape is checked once per nonempty batch. # Errors Invalid parameters, output length not divisible by the mark dimension, or a numerical failure while sampling. A numerical failure can leave partial output and advances the stream through the failing draw.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_levy_increments" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Generates `steps` vectors of `dim`-dimensional `\\\\alpha`-stable Lévy increments `\\\\Delta L_k ~ S_\\\\alpha(dt^{1/\\\\alpha}, \\\\beta, 0)` from the deterministic language stream. # Errors Returns [`SdeError::Invalid`] if `dim == 0`, `dt <= 0`, or `\\\\alpha, \\\\beta` are out of range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_method_calculus" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The integral the scheme converges to.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_method_from_name" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The method named `name`, if it is one this build knows.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_method_name" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The spelling used by the language surface and diagnostics.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_mlmc" => "Actual native MLMC with coupled fine/coarse Brownian paths. Payoff compiled program receives physical state components; target RMSE and estimate/standard-error have payoff units and variance has their square. Explicit hierarchy/sample/work caps bound all native levels. Estimate a terminal SDE expectation with multilevel Monte Carlo. Estimates the expected value `E[payoff(X_{t1})]` of a terminal functional of an SDE `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` using Giles' Multilevel Monte Carlo (MLMC) method with coupled fine/coarse Brownian paths (`\\\\Delta W^{(c)}_k = \\\\Delta W^{(f)}_{2k} + \\\\Delta W^{(f)}_{2k+1}`) and optimal sample allocation `N_l \\\\propto \\\\sqrt{V_l / C_l}`. Each (level, sample index) draws from its own deterministic seeded stream. A coupled fine/coarse pair shares that stream. The estimates for a given seed may differ from versions that reused streams across levels. # Errors Returns [`SdeError`] if options or callback dimensions are invalid or if any simulated trajectory becomes non-finite. Returns [`SdeError::StepLimit`] if cancelled at a checkpoint between solves and payoff evaluations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_next_normal" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The next standard normal, Box–Muller (cosine branch).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_next_unit" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The next uniform in `[0, 1)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_options_adaptive" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Adaptive integration on `[t0,t1]` using bounded dyadic coupled noise. `cells=0` selects the default finest resolution for every method.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_options_fixed" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. `steps` fixed steps of `dt` from `t0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_options_fixed_span" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Fixed steps covering `[t0, t1]`: the step count is rounded, and `dt` is adjusted to `(t1 - t0) / steps` so the end time is reached exactly.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_options_grid" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. An explicit uniform grid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_options_with_terms" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Sets the number of Fourier modes for the simulated Lévy areas.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_path_eval" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The state at time `t`, by linear interpolation between the accepted nodes; `None` outside `[t_first, t_last]`. A numerical SDE path is only known at its nodes. Between them the interpolant is the chord, which is continuous and deterministic. It is as accurate as the scheme itself (strong order at most 1, and a Brownian path is only Hölder-1/2 between nodes anyway); it does not add the within-step Brownian-bridge variance, so statistics of `eval` between nodes understate the within-step variation by `O(dt)`. Use the node values (`y`) when exact distributional statistics at the nodes are wanted.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_path_last" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The final state, when the path has one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_path_resample" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The states at the requested times (`saveat`): [`Path::eval`] at each. # Errors `E0802` when a time lies outside the path's span.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_sample_alpha_stable" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Samples a general `\\\\alpha`-stable random variable `S_\\\\alpha(scale, beta, location)` using the exact Chambers–Mallows–Stuck (1976) algorithm. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_sample_poisson" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Samples a `Poisson(mean)` random variable from `stream` using Knuth's exact multiplication algorithm for `mean <= 30` and exact transformed rejection for `mean > 30`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_sde_order" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The strong/weak order of `method` on **commutative** noise (see [`sde_order_for`] for a given structure), as the kernel's documentation states it.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_sde_order_for" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The strong/weak order of `method` on noise of the given `structure`. As [`sde_order`], except that `HeunStratonovich` on non-commutative noise is only strong order 1/2 (it has no Lévy-area term), and that the schemes which dispatch to the Lévy-area kernels on non-commutative noise keep strong order one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_stream_new" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. A stream seeded with `seed` (the same state `rng(seed)` starts from).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_structure_coefficients" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. How many values the diffusion callback returns.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_structure_is_commutative" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Whether the structure is (declared) commutative.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_structure_noise" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The number of noise channels (`m`) for a state of `n` components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_stochastic_wiener" => "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Brownian increments for `steps` steps of `dt`, `m` components each, on the language stream seeded with `seed`. The argument order is the kernel's `sde_wiener(seed, m, steps, h)` so the language surface, the manifest operator and this helper cannot drift: `wiener(seed, m, steps, dt)[k][j]` is the increment of the `j`-th Wiener process over `[t0 + k dt, t0 + (k+1) dt]`, drawn step-major (component order inside a step), which is exactly the layout the kernel's fixed-step solvers consume. A fixed-step integration with the same seed and structure therefore consumes these numbers.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_body_data" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_body_new" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_composition_weights" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Step-size multipliers of a symmetric composition of even `order`. Step-size multipliers `c_i` (summing to 1) of the symmetric composition `S(c_1 h) S(c_2 h) ...` that lifts a symmetric second-order step `S` to the even `order` (Yoshida sequences for 6 and 8, Suzuki triple jumps beyond). # Errors Fails for odd or zero `order`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_free_flow_exact" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. **Exact** free rotation for time `t` of an asymmetric rigid body, in closed form. With `d` the principal axis about which the polhode circulates, the body-frame angular momentum is `L_d = +-A_d dn`, `L_p = A_p cn`, `L_s = A_s sn` (Jacobi functions of `tau = tau_0 + omega t`, parameter `m`; Landau and Lifshitz, section 37). The orientation follows from the Euler angles with the (space-fixed) angular momentum as polar axis: `theta` and `psi` are algebraic in `L`, and the precession angle is `phi(t) = |L| t / I_d + |L| (2T - L^2 / I_d) int_0^t dt' / (L^2 - L_d^2)`, an elliptic integral of the **third kind** ([`crate::sim::elliptic::elliptic_pi`]). Symmetric tops and steady rotations use the exact symmetric-top flow. Valid for any `t` (including negative).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_free_step" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. One symmetric (`NO_SQUISH` / DLM) step of the free rotor, second order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_free_step_composed" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Free rotation for time `h` with the chosen splitting composed to the even `order` (2, 4, 6, 8, ...) by [`composition_weights`]. # Errors Fails for an invalid `order`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_free_step_top" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Second-order symmetric step of the free rotor built on exact **symmetric-top flows**: `exp(h/2 R_a) exp(h/2 R_b) exp(h S) exp(h/2 R_b) exp(h/2 R_a)` where `S` is the symmetric top and `R_a`, `R_b` the single-axis remainders. Exact when the body is itself a symmetric top and much more accurate than [`free_step`](Self::free_step) for nearly symmetric bodies.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_integrate" => "Native Hamiltonian splitting with six schemes, physical coordinate, velocity and time references, and a shared evaluation budget. Integrates `n` steps of signed size `h` with `scheme`, reusing acceleration storage. # Panics Panics if `n` is positive and `p` is shorter than `q`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_integrate_sampled" => "Integrates every step with a separable symplectic scheme. Retains step zero, each positive save_every multiple, and the final step. Each state preserves the physical positions followed by velocities. Step indices are exact and the signed step size uses the acceleration tape's time dimension. Integrates a separable Hamiltonian and samples its phase state by step count. Retains step zero, each multiple of `save_every`, and the final step. `h` may be negative for backward integration. With zero `h` the sampled state stays constant and the acceleration callback is skipped. Otherwise `acc(q, a)` must write every element of `a` at each kick. # Errors Empty or unequal state slices, non-finite inputs, zero cadence, a non-finite state or acceleration, an unavailable snapshot allocation, or cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_kick_space" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Adds `h * torque` (space-frame torque) to the angular momentum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_kinetic_energy" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Rotational kinetic energy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ks_advance" => "Actual native physical-time KS advance with explicit positive gravitational parameter mu(L³/T²), bounded eta and a genuine tape perturbation acceleration. Callback budget failures and incomplete native endpoint errors are preserved. Advances a perturbed two-body problem in KS variables for a given physical time. The gravitational parameter is `mu`, the physical time `dt > 0` (RK4 in the fictitious time, step `ds = eta / omega` with the harmonic frequency `omega` of the regularised oscillator; the last steps are solved for so that the physical time lands exactly on `dt`). `pert(x, v, tau)` is the perturbing relative acceleration at elapsed time `tau`. Returns the relative position and velocity. # Errors Fails for a non-positive `dt`, `mu` or `eta`, or a collision (`r = 0`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ks_from_cartesian" => "Actual native Cartesian-to-KS transform. Cartesian units are L,L/T; regular coordinates have sqrt(L) and du/ds has L^(3/2)/T, with fictitious time ds=T/L. KS map of a regular state: returns `(u, u')` for position `x` and velocity `v` (`u'` is the derivative with respect to the fictitious time `ds = dt / r`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ks_matrix" => "Actual native KS matrix, linear in regular coordinates; input and matrix entries have sqrt(length) units. The KS matrix `L(u)` (4x4, rows give `x1, x2, x3, 0`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ks_propagate" => "Actual native fixed fictitious-time KS propagation. Native central mu=1is normalized by an explicit positive physical mu reference: time reference=sqrt(Lref³/mu). Fictitious step has T/L units, outputs retain regular-coordinate/time/specific-energy units. Propagates a (perturbed) Kepler problem in KS variables with RK4. Propagates a (perturbed) Kepler problem with gravitational parameter 1 in KS variables with RK4 in the fictitious time `s`, for `n` steps of size `ds`. `pert(x, v, t)` is the perturbing acceleration. Without perturbation the equations are a harmonic oscillator of frequency `sqrt(-E/2)`, so near-collision orbits are integrated without singularity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ks_to_cartesian" => "Actual native KS-to-Cartesian transform with physical sqrt(L),L^(3/2)/T regular coordinates and L,L/T outputs. Cartesian position and velocity from a KS state.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_l_space" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Angular momentum in the space frame.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_matrix_to_quat" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Unit quaternion (scalar first) of a proper rotation matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_quat_mul" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Quaternion product `a * b` (scalar first).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_quat_to_matrix" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Rotation matrix (body to space) of a unit quaternion.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_rattle_step" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. One **implicit RATTLE-type** step with a space-frame torque: the discrete rigid-body (Moser-Veselov) free step, in which the rotation increment `F` solves the implicit constraint equation `F J - J F^T = h Pi^` (`J = tr(I)/2 - I` in the principal frame, Newton iteration), sandwiched between half kicks of the torque. The map is symplectic, time reversible and second order; it conserves the space-frame angular momentum exactly in the absence of torque and keeps `R^T R = 1` to round-off. # Errors Fails if the Newton iteration for the rotation increment does not converge (very large steps) or a principal moment is not positive.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_scheme_order" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Order of accuracy of the scheme.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_scheme_stages" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Stage table: pairs `(drift, kick)` applied in sequence (drift first).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_split_step" => "Actual native custom-stage drift/kick step. State contains physical positions then velocities and stage coefficients are explicitly normalized; a pure-position acceleration tape preserves coordinate/time² units. One step of a composition scheme. `acc(q, a)` writes the acceleration. # Panics Panics if a stage is executed and `p` is shorter than `q`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_step" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. One step with a space-frame torque `torque(q)`: half kick, free rotation, half kick.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_step_exact" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Strang step with space-frame torque built on the **exact** free flow ([`free_flow_exact`](Self::free_flow_exact)): half kick, exact free rotation, half kick. Symplectic, time reversible and second order, with no splitting error in the free part.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_symmetric_top_flow" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Exact flow for time `h` of the **symmetric top** obtained by replacing the two inverse moments about the axes other than `ax` with their mean: `L` is constant in space, so `q(h) = q_L(h) * q(0) * q_ax(h)` with `q_L` a rotation about `L` by `|L| h / I_perp` and `q_ax` a rotation about the symmetry axis by `h L_ax (1/I_ax - 1/I_perp)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_triple_jump" => "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Suzuki triple jump: lifts a symmetric scheme of order `p` to order `p + 2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ttl_data" => "Physical retained native TTL time, positions and momenta.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ttl_gbs_step" => "Actual native ttl_gbs_step with a true native tape reverse gradient of the physical negative potential. Binding has energy units and may be negative when initial T+B>0; fictitious step has energy*time units; the returned error is explicitly in the native normalized state metric. One Gragg-Bulirsch-Stoer extrapolated step of the time-transformed leapfrog. One Gragg-Bulirsch-Stoer extrapolated step of the time-transformed leapfrog ([`ttl_step`]) of size `ds` in the fictitious time, using `kmax` columns. Returns the relative error estimate. The scheme has order `2 kmax` in `ds`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ttl_new" => "Build actual native TTL phase state with physical positions, momenta, masses and initial time. The fictitious integration increment has energy*time dimensions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ttl_step" => "Actual native ttl_step with a true native tape reverse gradient of the physical negative potential. Binding has energy units and may be negative when initial T+B>0; fictitious step has energy*time units; the returned error is explicitly in the native normalized state metric. Time-transformed leapfrog for `H = T(p) + V(q)` with `V < 0`. Uses the extended Hamiltonian `K = ln(T + B) - ln(-V)` with `B = -E0` (`E0` the energy to preserve); the fictitious step `h` corresponds to the physical step `h / (T + B)`, which is `h r / (GM)` for Kepler (a Sundman transformation `dt = r ds`). The scheme is symmetric and symplectic in the extended phase space. `mass` holds one mass per coordinate; `potential_grad(q, g)` writes `grad V` and returns `V`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_symplectic_ttl_step_composed" => "Actual native ttl_step_composed with a true native tape reverse gradient of the physical negative potential. Binding has energy units and may be negative when initial T+B>0; fictitious step has energy*time units; the returned error is explicitly in the native normalized state metric. Time-transformed leapfrog composed to an even `order`. Time-transformed leapfrog composed to the even `order` (4, 6, 8, ...): the TTL step is a symmetric second-order integrator of the extended Hamiltonian `ln(T + B) - ln(-V)`, so composition with [`composition_weights`] removes the `O(h^2)` energy error of the plain scheme. # Errors Fails for an invalid `order`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_transforms_fft" => "Native transforms fft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) of a sequence of complex numbers. Works at any length and leaves the length unchanged using a shared, runtime-selected SIMD FFT plan from [`super::fft`]. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `Complex<f64>` representing the input sequence. # Example ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft; let mut data = vec![ Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0), ]; fft(&mut data); assert!((data[0].re - 2.0).abs() < 1e-9); ``` A length that is not a power of two keeps its length: ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft; let mut data = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 0.0); 3]; fft(&mut data); assert_eq!(data.len(), 3); // [3, 0, 0] for a constant signal. assert!((data[0].re - 3.0).abs() < 1e-12); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_transforms_fft_many_par" => "Native transforms fft_many_par; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Forward FFT of every row, concurrently, in row order. Each row runs the serial [`fft`] kernel and only different rows run concurrently, so the result is bit-identical to transforming the rows one at a time and therefore independent of the thread count. Fewer than `grain` rows (or `grain < 2`) run sequentially, so a small batch does not pay for the task spawn. Use the batch entry point instead of a hand-written `rayon` map when the rows are independent (the usual row/column pass of a 2-D transform).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_transforms_fft_slice" => "Native transforms fft_slice; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the Forward FFT for a slice. **Use this for efficient, parallel chunk processing (e.g., with rayon).** Works at any length and operates in place: a power-of-two length uses the scalar radix-2 kernel and other lengths use the shared FFT planner. Bit-identical to [`fft`]. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `Complex<f64>` representing the input sequence. # Example ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft_slice; let mut data = vec![ Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0), ]; fft_slice(&mut data); assert!((data[0].re - 2.0).abs() < 1e-9); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_transforms_ifft" => "Native transforms ifft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the Inverse Fast Fourier Transform (IFFT). Works at any length and leaves the length unchanged, so `ifft(fft(x))` returns `x` at every length. The `1/n` normalisation is included. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable `Vec<Complex<f64>>` representing the input frequency-domain sequence. # Example ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft; use rssn::kernels::transforms::ifft; let mut data = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 2.0), Complex::new(3.0, 4.0)]; let original = data.clone(); fft(&mut data); ifft(&mut data); assert!((data[0].re - original[0].re).abs() < 1e-9); assert!((data[0].im - original[0].im).abs() < 1e-9); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_transforms_ifft_many_par" => "Native transforms ifft_many_par; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. [`fft_many_par`] for the inverse transform.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_transforms_ifft_slice" => "Native transforms ifft_slice; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the Inverse FFT for a slice. **Use this for efficient, parallel chunk processing (e.g., with rayon).** Works at any length and operates in place; the `1/n` normalisation is included. Bit-identical to [`ifft`]. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `Complex<f64>` representing the input frequency-domain sequence. # Example ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft_slice; use rssn::kernels::transforms::ifft_slice; let mut data = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0)]; let original = data.clone(); fft_slice(&mut data); ifft_slice(&mut data); assert!((data[0].re - original[0].re).abs() < 1e-9); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_try_numerical_jacobian_scaled" => "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Validated forward-difference Jacobian, with a backward-side retry at domain boundaries and checked callback length and representability. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] if inputs, samples, shape or a derivative are invalid.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_dwt" => "Native wavelets dwt; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. One level of the periodised discrete wavelet transform: returns `(approximation, detail)`. `a[k] = sum_m h~[m] x[(2k + m) mod n]`, `d[k] = sum_m g~[m] x[(2k + m) mod n]` with the analysis banks. An even signal of length `n` maps to `n/2` coefficients of each kind. For odd lengths or another boundary rule use [`dwt_with`]. # Errors [`WaveletError::OddLength`] for an empty or odd-length signal.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_dwt2" => "Native wavelets dwt2; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. One level of the separable two-dimensional transform, `x` row-major with `rows x cols` entries. The rows are transformed first, then the columns, and the four sub-bands are packed into a `(2*ra) x (2*ca)` row-major array with `ra = w.coeff_len(rows, b)` and `ca = w.coeff_len(cols, b)`: `LL` at rows `0..ra`, columns `0..ca`; `LH` at rows `0..ra`, columns `ca..2ca`; `HL` at rows `ra..2ra`, columns `0..ca`; `HH` at rows `ra..2ra`, columns `ca..2ca`. In each label, the first letter gives the filter within each row (horizontal) and the second the filter within each column (vertical). # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when `rows * cols` does not match `x.len()`, or when a row or column transform fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_dwt_plan_analyze" => "Computes one level of approximation and detail for the plan's input length. The two vectors both retain the physical dimension of the original signal. Writes one level of approximation and detail coefficients in the plan's layout. # Errors Returns [`WaveletError::Shape`] for a length mismatch, without changing the output slices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_dwt_plan_coeff_len" => "Returns the number of entries in each approximation and detail band for this signal length and boundary. Number of entries in each approximation or detail band.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_dwt_plan_new" => "Prepares one-level analysis and synthesis for a fixed nonzero signal length. Use a wavelet such as Haar or Db10 and one of Periodic, Zero, Symmetric or Reflect. Shares filter taps and boundary layout across independent signals. Prepares one level for the given length, wavelet and boundary. # Errors [`WaveletError::OddLength`] for an empty signal and [`WaveletError::Shape`] for a length that cannot fit in a slice of `f64` values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_dwt_plan_signal_len" => "Returns the signal length fixed by the plan and used for synthesis output. Number of samples in the input or reconstructed signal.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_dwt_plan_synthesize" => "Reconstructs the original signal length from two equal-dimension coefficient bands of the plan's required length. The output retains the shared dimension. Reconstructs one level into the given output slice. # Errors Returns [`WaveletError::Shape`] for a length mismatch, without changing the output slice.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_dwt_with" => "Native wavelets dwt_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. One level of the discrete wavelet transform with an explicit [`Boundary`] mode. Returns `(approximation, detail)`, each of the length that [`Wavelet::coeff_len`] reports for `x.len()` and `b`. In [`Boundary::Periodic`] mode this is the circular transform of [`dwt`], extended by one sample for an odd length. Other modes use the documented boundary extension. Use [`DwtPlan`] to reuse the banks and output storage. # Errors [`WaveletError::OddLength`] for an empty signal.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_extend" => "Native wavelets extend; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Sample `i` of the boundary extension of `x` in mode `b`. `Periodic` has period `n`, `Symmetric` period `2n` (mirror with the edge sample repeated) and `Reflect` period `2(n-1)` (mirror without repeating it); `Zero` returns `0.0` outside `[0, n)`. For `n = 1` the nonzero extension modes return `x[0]`, and an empty signal has no samples.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_idwt" => "Native wavelets idwt; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`dwt`] (exact up to rounding). # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the two coefficient lists differ in length or are empty.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_idwt2" => "Native wavelets idwt2; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`dwt2`]: `rows` and `cols` are the shape of the original array. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the packed array does not have the expected `(2*ra) x (2*ca)` size, or when a column or row transform fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_idwt_with" => "Native wavelets idwt_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`dwt_with`]. `n` is the length of the signal that produced the coefficients. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the lists differ in length, are empty, or do not match `w.coeff_len(n, b)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_analysis_high" => "Native wavelets method_analysis_high; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analysis high-pass filter `g~`, the alternating mirror of the synthesis low-pass.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_analysis_low" => "Native wavelets method_analysis_low; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analysis low-pass filter `h~` (the same as [`Wavelet::low_pass`]).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_coeff_len" => "Native wavelets method_coeff_len; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The number of coefficients [`dwt_with`] returns for a signal of length `n`. `ceil(n/2)` in [`Boundary::Periodic`] mode, and `j_hi - j_lo + 1` — about `n/2 + (m_hi - m_lo)/2` — in the extension modes, where `[m_lo, m_hi]` is the support of the synthesis banks (see the module documentation).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_high_pass" => "Native wavelets method_high_pass; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analysis (decomposition) high-pass filter `g~[n] = (-1)^n h[L-1-n]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_is_orthogonal" => "Native wavelets method_is_orthogonal; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `true` when one bank serves both analysis and synthesis (orthonormal filters).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_low_pass" => "Native wavelets method_low_pass; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analysis (decomposition) low-pass filter `h~`, normalised to `sum h~ = sqrt 2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_synthesis_high" => "Native wavelets method_synthesis_high; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The synthesis high-pass filter `g[n] = (-1)^n h~[L-1-n]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_synthesis_low" => "Native wavelets method_synthesis_low; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The synthesis (reconstruction) low-pass filter `h`; equal to [`Wavelet::low_pass`] for the orthogonal families.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_taps" => "Native wavelets method_taps; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Number of filter taps (the length of the banks).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_method_vanishing_moments" => "Native wavelets method_vanishing_moments; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The vanishing moments of the analysis and of the synthesis wavelet, `(Nr, Nd)`. Counted from the filters: the analysis wavelet annihilates polynomials of degree below `Nr`, the synthesis wavelet those below `Nd`. For the orthogonal families the two agree and equal the Daubechies order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_wavedec" => "Native wavelets wavedec; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Multilevel decomposition: `[approximation_L, detail_L, ..., detail_1]`. Requires `2^levels` to divide the length, as before; use [`wavedec_with`] for the general case. # Errors [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when `2^levels` does not divide the length, or the length is shorter than the filter at some level.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_wavedec2" => "Native wavelets wavedec2; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Multilevel separable two-dimensional decomposition: `[LL_L, packed_L, ..., packed_1]`. Every level applies [`dwt2`] to the current `LL` band and keeps the whole packed array (`LL`, `LH`, `HL`, `HH`) for the inverse; the next level then transforms the extracted `LL` band. The arrays therefore follow the [`wavedec`] convention: the first array is the final approximation and the remaining ones are the details of the levels from coarsest to finest. The periodic transform requires the dyadic shape `2^levels | rows` and `2^levels | cols` (the sizes halve exactly at every level); use [`wavedec2_with`] for another [`Boundary`] mode or a non-dyadic shape. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when `rows * cols` does not match `x.len()` or a dimension is zero; [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when `levels` is zero or exceeds the dyadic depth.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_wavedec2_with" => "Native wavelets wavedec2_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Multilevel separable two-dimensional decomposition with an explicit [`Boundary`] mode. The recursion is the same as in [`wavedec2`], but any shape is accepted as long as every level strictly shrinks the approximation band in both dimensions. The extension modes return about `n/2 + (m_hi - m_lo)/2` coefficients, so once the filters reach the whole band the count stops shrinking; that is detected per level and reported as [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] instead of recursing on a band that does not get smaller. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when `rows * cols` does not match `x.len()` or a dimension is zero; [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when `levels` is zero, or a level would not shrink the approximation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_wavedec_with" => "Native wavelets wavedec_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Multilevel decomposition with a [`Boundary`] mode, for any non-empty signal. Every level keeps the number of coefficients that [`Wavelet::coeff_len`] reports for its length and `b`, so the levels stop shrinking once the length is comparable to the filter length (see the module documentation). # Errors [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when `levels` is zero or `2^(levels-1) > x.len()`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_waverec" => "Native wavelets waverec; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`wavedec`]. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] for an inconsistent list.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_waverec2" => "Native wavelets waverec2; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`wavedec2`]: `rows` and `cols` are the shape of the decomposed array. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the coefficient list or one of its shapes does not fit `rows x cols` and the periodic rule.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_waverec2_with" => "Native wavelets waverec2_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`wavedec2_with`]; `rows` and `cols` are the shape of the decomposed array. The shape of every level is recomputed from `rows`, `cols` and the mode, exactly like [`waverec_with`]; the `LL` band of each stored level is overwritten with the reconstruction of the next finer level and [`idwt2`] recovers its input. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the list is empty, one of its arrays has the wrong size, or the shape chain does not fit `rows x cols` and the mode.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_wavelets_waverec_with" => "Native wavelets waverec_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`wavedec_with`]; `n` is the length of the decomposed signal. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the list or one of its lengths does not fit `n` and the mode.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_add_source" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Adds a soft current source.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_ade_dt_limit" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Largest time step for which the coupled ADE scheme is stable, `2 / sqrt(ω0² + a/ε∞ + 4 Σ_a h_a^{-2}/ε∞)` for the stiffest pole and cell; infinite when the medium is non-dispersive. [`Yee::new`] reduces `dt` to this bound.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_config_new" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). PEC level sets may have any scalar output domain normalized by their explicit positive program reference; only signs and scale-invariant fractions are used. Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Vacuum configuration with PEC walls everywhere. # Errors For a bad dimension or sizes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_energy" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Conserved energy: `½ Σ (ε∞ E² + H²) ΔV` and, for a dispersive medium, the oscillator energy `Σ (P_t² + ω0² P²) / (2 a) ΔV` of every pole, both taken at `n - 1/2`. Without poles and without ohmic conduction this is the electromagnetic energy alone; with conduction it excludes the dissipated part, which accumulates in [`Yee::ohmic_loss`]. `E` sits at `n` and `H` at `n + 1/2`, so the estimator itself wobbles at `O(ω dt)` (a few per cent on a coarse grid) even where the discrete scheme conserves energy exactly; the mean over a period is constant.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_field" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Field values of one component.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_field_mut" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Mutable field values of one component (for initial conditions).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_h" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Cell size along an axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_half_step_h" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Sets `H^{-1/2}` from `E^0` assuming `H^0 = 0` (call once after setting `E` for a standing-wave initial condition; not for PML runs).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_idx" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Flat index of cell `(i, j, k)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_n" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Cells per axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_new" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Creates a solver. The time step is `cfl / sqrt(Σ 1/h²)`, reduced to the ADE stability bound ([`Yee::ade_dt_limit`]) when a dispersive pole is present and to keep the subcell PEC scheme stable. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions, inconsistent periodic faces, a CFL number above one, or a PML thicker than half the grid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_ohmic_loss" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Total ohmic energy dissipated so far, `∫ Σ σ E² dV dt` (zero for a lossless medium). It is accumulated with the midpoint rule, so `energy() + ohmic_loss()` is conserved up to the estimator wobble.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_plane_wave_sample" => "Reads electric and magnetic amplitudes in their separate physical units at an auxiliary normal-incidence TF/SF line index. The magnetic field is sampled one half-cell ahead of the electric field. Returns the actual electric and magnetic sample times. No installed source or an out-of-range index returns None. Samples the auxiliary line of the normal-incidence TF/SF source. Returns `None` without an installed source or past the auxiliary endpoint. The sample carries the electric and magnetic times of the actual stored values, including after time-step changes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_pole_drude" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Drude pole with plasma frequency `ω_p`: `χ = -ω_p² / (ω² + i γ ω)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_pole_lorentz" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Lorentz pole with static contribution `Δε`: `χ = Δε ω0² / (ω0² - ω² - i γ ω)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_run" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Runs `steps` steps.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_set_oblique_plane_wave" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Installs an oblique plane wave on an arbitrary TF/SF box. # Errors For a zero direction or polarisation, a polarisation parallel to the direction, or components along inactive axes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_set_oblique_plane_wave_rotated" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Installs an oblique plane wave on a **rotated** box: the box `[lo, hi]` of `w` is rotated about its centre by the orthonormal matrix `rotation` (columns are the images of the axes). The incident wave itself keeps the direction and polarisation given in `w`. # Errors As [`Yee::set_oblique_plane_wave`], and for a matrix that is not orthonormal or that mixes in an inactive axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_set_plane_wave" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Installs the TF/SF plane-wave source. The auxiliary line starts at rest. The first drive sample is taken at the next electric time, after the first magnetic half-step. # Errors The slab is outside the grid, a correction touches the PML along the propagation axis, or the auxiliary line cannot be allocated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_sigma" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Ohmic conductivity at every cell (empty when the medium is lossless).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_size" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Number of cells.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_state" => "Native Yee state. The solver reserves48MiB: up to16MiBgrid/auxiliary/history storage and32MiBfully independent compiled snapshots, including at most4mutable sources. Snapshots remain valid after their original source handles are released.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_step" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. One leapfrog step (`H` then `E`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_analysis_yee_susceptibility" => "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E²L³/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid≤4096and source/work bounds precede execution. Susceptibility `χ(ω)` (time dependence `e^{-iωt}`) as `(re, im)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_angle" => "Angle in radians; zero for a zero vector. Computes the angle between two vectors in radians. Formula: \$\\\\theta = \\\\arccos\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\mathbf{a} \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{b}}{||\\\\mathbf{a}|| ||\\\\mathbf{b}||}\\\\right)\$ # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_approx_bspline_basis" => "Value of the `i`-th B-spline basis function at `x`. Uses the Cox–de Boor recurrence on non-decreasing knots, with right-continuous spans and a closed last span. An invalid index or a non-finite local knot gives NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_approx_bspline_eval" => "Evaluates a B-spline curve `sum c_i B_{i,p}(x)` by de Boor's algorithm. Requires finite, non-decreasing `knots`, `degree < coeffs.len()` and `knots.len() == coeffs.len() + degree + 1`. The coordinate is clamped to the valid range. Returns NaN for an invalid shape or endpoint.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_approx_bspline_interpolate" => "Fits an interpolating B-spline and returns `(knots, coefficients)`. Degree zero places boundaries at the midpoints between consecutive sites. Higher degrees use a clamped knot vector with averaged interior knots. # Errors [`ApproxError::Invalid`] for inconsistent or non-finite samples, an invalid degree, unordered sites, or an unrepresentable final degree-zero interval. [`ApproxError::Singular`] if the collocation system is singular or overflows.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_approx_chebyshev_points" => "Chebyshev points of the second kind (extrema) `cos(pi k / n)` mapped to `[a, b]`, `k = 0..=n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_approx_clenshaw" => "Clenshaw evaluation of `sum c_k T_k(t)` at `t in [-1, 1]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_approx_horner" => "Evaluates a polynomial with ascending coefficients by Horner's scheme.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_approx_pade" => "Pade approximant `[m/n]` from Taylor coefficients `c_0, c_1, ...` (needs `m + n + 1` coefficients). Returns `(numerator, denominator)` with ascending coefficients and `denominator[0] = 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_arity" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_available" => "List valid backend selectors discovered on this host. The first device query can initialize GPU adapters.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_call" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_call_batch" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_call_batch_columns" => "Evaluate a retained scalar program over measured rank-one columns in declared symbol order. Each column has its own physical unit and matches the explicit row count. Returns a single measured vector in input order. The active CPU pool splits sufficiently large work into fixed independent blocks. Cancellation and the program's row and work limits apply.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_call_batch_par" => "Evaluate a retained scalar program over a tensor of session-owned input handles with shape `[arity, rows]`. Columns follow declared symbol order and retain their individual physical dimensions. The executor estimates the work per row from the compiled program and uses the active CPU pool for independent blocks when there is enough work. The measured result preserves row order. Cancellation is checked between blocks, and shape, units, and the program’s row and work limits apply.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_compile" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_compile_current" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy. Compiles `root` with the [current] backend. # Errors See [`BackendError`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_current" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy. The backend numeric kernels compile with: the innermost [`with_backend`] scope, else the process default ([`set_default`]), else the [`Interpreter`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_current_name" => "Return the canonical active backend selector without allocating a handle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_interpreter" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_name" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_named" => "Resolve a named backend into a session-owned handle for measured compilation. An unavailable selector returns Unsupported.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_reference" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_release" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_select_default" => "Set a named process default and return its resolved selector. Use auto to clear the override. Thread-local overrides keep priority.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_set_default" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy. Sets the process-wide default backend (`None` restores the interpreter).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_value" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_backend_with_backend_compile" => "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy. Runs `f` with `backend` as the current backend on this thread.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bessel_i0" => "Modified Bessel I of order zero. Computes the modified Bessel function of the first kind, I₀(x). Power series for |x| ≤ 30 and the asymptotic expansion beyond; relative accuracy about 1e-15.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bessel_i1" => "Modified Bessel I of order one. Computes the modified Bessel function of the first kind, I₁(x) (odd in `x`). Same method and accuracy as [`bessel_i0`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bessel_j0" => "Bessel J of order zero. Computes the Bessel function of the first kind, J₀(x). Accurate to roughly 1e-15 (absolute): power series for |x| < 5, Miller backward recurrence for 5 ≤ |x| ≤ 25 and the Hankel asymptotic expansion beyond.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bessel_j1" => "Bessel J of order one. Computes the Bessel function of the first kind, J₁(x) (odd in `x`). Same method and accuracy as [`bessel_j0`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bessel_k0" => "`K₀(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bessel_k1" => "`K₁(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bessel_y0" => "Bessel Y of order zero. Computes the Bessel function of the second kind, Y₀(x), for x ≥ 0 (`NaN` for x < 0, `-∞` at 0). Series (A&S 9.1.13) for x < 5, the Neumann expansion `Y₀ = (2/π)(ln(x/2) + γ) J₀ - (4/π) Σ (-1)^k J_{2k}/k` (A&S 9.1.88) for 5 ≤ x ≤ 25 and the Hankel expansion beyond; accurate to about 1e-14.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bessel_y1" => "Bessel Y of order one. Computes the Bessel function of the second kind, Y₁(x), for x ≥ 0 (`NaN` for x < 0, `-∞` at 0). Series (A&S 9.1.11) for x < 5; for 5 ≤ x ≤ 25 the derivative of the Neumann expansion of Y₀ (`Y₁ = -Y₀'`, `J₀' = -J₁`, `J_{2k}' = (J_{2k-1} - J_{2k+1})/2`); Hankel expansion beyond. Accurate to about 1e-14.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bfgs" => "Dimensionless serial optimization: initial vector,[lower bounds,upper bounds for lbfgs_b],positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,objective callback,gradient callback. Both callbacks share a strict evaluation budget. Gradient length matches initial vector; L-BFGS methods retain ten correction pairs. Returns x,value,iterations,evaluations,converged; callback failures propagate unchanged. BFGS quasi-Newton method with a weak-Wolfe line search. If `grad` is `None` a central-difference gradient is used.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_bfgs_scaled" => "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. BFGS quasi-Newton method with a weak-Wolfe line search. If `grad` is `None` a central-difference gradient is used.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_binomial" => "Floating-point binomial coefficient; returns zero when k exceeds n. Computes the binomial coefficient C(n, k) = n! / (k! * (n-k)!)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bootstrap" => "Seeded nonparametric bootstrap: sample vector, positive replicate count, nonnegative u64 seed, statistic-evaluation budget, statistic callback. The callback accepts a sample-unit vector and returns a finite scalar with independently declared statistic units; every summary/replicate retains those units. Budget must cover the original estimate and every replicate. Work is capped at 100000000 resampled entries and storage at 8388608 values. Percentile confidence bounds are 95 percent and are statistical estimates, not coverage guarantees. Non-parametric bootstrap resampling with `n_resamples` draws. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`] when `samples` is empty, [`StatsExtError::Domain`] when the resample count is zero or cannot be allocated, a uniform draw is outside `[0, 1)`, or a statistic is non-finite. Callback errors propagate.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_brent" => "Serial bracketed Brent root solver: lower bound, upper bound, positive argument tolerance, positive iteration budget, callback. The root and tolerance have callback-domain dimensions; residual dimensions are independent. Returns root/evaluations without an extra residual evaluation. Endpoint samples count as evaluations, callback failures propagate unchanged, and iteration exhaustion is an explicit error. Brent's method on a bracketing interval `[a, b]`. # Errors [`RootError::NotBracketed`] if the endpoints have the same nonzero sign, [`RootError::Invalid`] for non-finite inputs, non-positive tolerance, or non-finite callback values, and [`RootError::NoConvergence`] at the limit.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_brent_with_bracket" => "Bracketed Brent root solver returning the root, actual evaluation count and final two endpoints in ascending order. The argument tolerance and all returned coordinates use the callback's input dimension. An exact zero repeats its coordinate at both endpoints.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_broyden" => "Dimensionless nonlinear system solver: initial vector,positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,residual callback. Callback input and output retain the initial vector length. Returns x,evaluations; callback failures and iteration exhaustion are explicit errors. Broyden's \"good\" method: finite-difference Jacobian at the start, then rank-one updates of its inverse-applied form (Sherman-Morrison on `B`). # Errors As [`newton_system`].\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_broyden_scaled" => "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. Broyden's \"good\" method: finite-difference Jacobian at the start, then rank-one updates of its inverse-applied form (Sherman-Morrison on `B`). # Errors As [`newton_system`].\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_bspline_build" => "Fit a portable B-spline model. Axis and ordinate units are carried independently by canonical witnesses. Fits an interpolating B-spline of `degree` through `(xs, ys)`. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`], [`InterpError::Domain`], or [`InterpError::Singular`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bspline_query" => "Evaluate a fitted portable B-spline. Validates opaque kind/version, payload bounds and knot structure; query and returned values retain their independent physical units. Evaluates the B-spline at `x`. A degree-zero fit to one sample gives a constant at every finite query. # Errors [`InterpError::Domain`] when the query is nonfinite, or an error from [`bspline_eval`] if the stored spline representation is invalid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bvh_build" => "Build immutable portable BVH defining data from same-unit N x 3 lower/upper corner tensors. Payload kind rssn/bvh-defining-data/v1 stores only canonical finite corner rows, never pointers or internal tree nodes. Queries validate/version-check the payload and reconstruct the native tree; portable reconstruction is limited to one million work-array coefficients. Builds a BVH over `aabbs`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bvh_nearest" => "Nearest AABB on a portable BVH. Query coordinates share the descriptor's coordinate units; index/distance tensors have zero or one entry, with measured distances. An empty definition gives empty results. Returns `(aabb_index, distance)` for the [`Aabb`] closest to `point`. If its distance overflows `f64`, the index remains the nearest and the distance is positive infinity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_bvh_ray" => "Indices of AABBs hit by origin+t*direction, t>=0, on an immutable portable BVH. Origin shares coordinate units; direction is dimensionless and nonzero. Payload kind/version, finite corners and allocation bounds are checked before reconstruction. Returns the indices of all [`Aabb`]s intersected by the ray `origin + t * dir` (`t >= 0`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_cauchy_root_bound" => "Classic Cauchy upper bound from a complex coefficient vector in ascending order. Coefficients may share ordinate units. The polynomial coordinate and the result are dimensionless. Classic Cauchy upper bound on the modulus of all roots of \$p(z) = \\\\sum_{k=0}^n `c_k` z^k\$ (ascending coefficients): \$R = 1 + \\\\max_{0 \\\\le k < n} |`c_k` / `c_n`|\$.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_cauchy_sharp_root_bound" => "Compute the sharp Cauchy radius from ascending complex coefficients with a common ordinate dimension. Returns zero for a nonconstant monomial. The polynomial coordinate and the result are dimensionless. Computes the sharp Cauchy radius, the positive solution of \$|`c_n`| R^n = \\\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} |`c_k`| R^k\$ for a non-monomial polynomial. Coefficients are ascending. A monomial has radius zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_cholesky" => "Lower Cholesky factor of a positive definite matrix. Cholesky factor `L` (lower triangular) with `A = L L^T`. # Errors [`DenseError::Singular`] if `A` is not symmetric positive definite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_blackbody_power" => "Stefan-Boltzmann law: total power radiated by a blackbody. P = σ * A * T⁴ # Arguments * `area` - Surface area (m²) * `temperature` - Temperature (K)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_compton_wavelength" => "Compton wavelength of a particle: `λ_C` = h/(mc)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_coulomb_force" => "Calculates the electric force between two point charges using Coulomb's law. F = k * q1 * q2 / r² # Arguments * `q1`, `q2` - Charges (C) * `r` - Distance between charges (m) # Returns force magnitude (N), positive for repulsion\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_cyclotron_radius" => "Calculates the cyclotron radius (Larmor radius) for a charged particle. r = mv / (|q|B) # Arguments * `mass` - Particle mass (kg) * `velocity` - Particle speed (m/s) * `charge` - Particle charge (C) * `b_field` - Magnetic field magnitude (T)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_de_broglie_wavelength" => "De Broglie wavelength: λ = h/p # Arguments * `momentum` - Particle momentum (kg·m/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_electric_field_point_charge" => "Calculates the electric field magnitude from a point charge. E = k * q / r² # Arguments * `q` - Charge (C) * `r` - Distance from charge (m)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_electric_potential_point_charge" => "Calculates the electric potential from a point charge. V = k * q / r # Arguments * `q` - Charge (C) * `r` - Distance from charge (m)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_heisenberg_position_uncertainty" => "Heisenberg uncertainty principle minimum: Δx·Δp ≥ ħ/2 Returns minimum position uncertainty given momentum uncertainty.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_hydrogen_energy_level" => "Hydrogen atom energy levels (Bohr model). `E_n` = -13.6 eV / n² # Arguments * `n` - Principal quantum number (1, 2, 3, ...) # Returns energy in Joules\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_ideal_gas_pressure" => "Ideal gas law: PV = nRT, solving for pressure. # Arguments * `n` - Amount of substance (mol) * `t` - Temperature (K) * `v` - Volume (m³)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_ideal_gas_temperature" => "Ideal gas law: PV = nRT, solving for temperature.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_ideal_gas_volume" => "Ideal gas law: PV = nRT, solving for volume.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_length_contraction" => "Length contraction: L = L₀ / γ # Arguments * `proper_length` - Proper length (m) * `velocity` - Relative velocity (m/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_lorentz_factor" => "Lorentz factor γ = 1 / √(1 - v²/c²) # Arguments * `velocity` - Velocity (m/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_lorentz_force" => "Calculates the Lorentz force on a charged particle. F = q(E + v × B) - returns force magnitude assuming v ⟂ B # Arguments * `charge` - Particle charge (C) * `velocity` - Particle speed (m/s) * `e_field` - Electric field magnitude (V/m) * `b_field` - Magnetic field magnitude (T)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_magnetic_field_infinite_wire" => "Calculates the magnetic field magnitude at distance r from an infinite wire. B = μ₀ * I / (2π * r) # Arguments * `current` - Current in wire (A) * `r` - Perpendicular distance from wire (m)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_mass_energy" => "Mass-energy equivalence: E = mc²\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_maxwell_boltzmann_mean_speed" => "Mean speed from Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution. ⟨v⟩ = √(8kT/(πm))\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_maxwell_boltzmann_rms_speed" => "RMS speed from Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution. `v_rms` = √(3kT/m)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_maxwell_boltzmann_speed_distribution" => "Maxwell-Boltzmann speed distribution probability density. f(v) = 4π * (m/(2πkT))^(3/2) * v² * exp(-mv²/(2kT)) # Arguments * `v` - Speed (m/s) * `mass` - Particle mass (kg) * `temperature` - Temperature (K)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_photon_energy" => "Photon energy: E = hf = hc/λ # Arguments * `wavelength` - Wavelength (m)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_photon_wavelength" => "Photon wavelength from energy.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_quantum_harmonic_oscillator_energy" => "Quantum harmonic oscillator energy levels. `E_n` = ħω(n + 1/2) # Arguments * `n` - Quantum number (0, 1, 2, ...) * `omega` - Angular frequency (rad/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_relativistic_kinetic_energy" => "Relativistic kinetic energy `KE = (γ - 1)mc²`, accurate also in the low-speed limit. # Arguments * `mass` - Rest mass (kg) * `velocity` - Velocity (m/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_relativistic_momentum" => "Relativistic momentum: p = γmv # Arguments * `mass` - Rest mass (kg) * `velocity` - Velocity (m/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_relativistic_total_energy" => "Total relativistic energy: E = γmc² # Arguments * `mass` - Rest mass (kg) * `velocity` - Velocity (m/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_relativistic_velocity_addition" => "Relativistic velocity addition: u = (v + w) / (1 + vw/c²)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_simple_harmonic_oscillator" => "Simple harmonic oscillator solution. Returns position x(t) = A * cos(ωt + φ) # Arguments * `amplitude` - Amplitude A * `omega` - Angular frequency ω (rad/s) * `phase` - Initial phase φ (radians) * `time` - Time t\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_time_dilation" => "Time dilation: Δt = γ * Δt₀ # Arguments * `proper_time` - Proper time interval (s) * `velocity` - Relative velocity (m/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_classical_wien_displacement_wavelength" => "Wien's displacement law: peak wavelength of blackbody radiation. `λ_max` = b / T, where b ≈ 2.898 × 10⁻³ m·K\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_clenshaw_curtis" => "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. Clenshaw-Curtis quadrature of `f` on `[a, b]` with `n + 1` points.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_combinatorics_bell" => "Computes the Bell number `B(n)` as an `f64`. Counts all partitions of a set of `n` elements, including the one partition of the empty set. Returns positive infinity when the count overflows `f64` (`n >= 219`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_combinatorics_bell_batch" => "Returns a Bell number for each unsigned integer index in input order. Finite values are Some, and counts that exceed Float are None. The batch reuses the same finite-count table as the scalar Bell function. Computes [`bell`] for each index using the shared table of finite counts. Accepts unsorted indices and preserves their order and repetitions. An empty input yields an empty output.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_combinatorics_catalan" => "Computes the nth Catalan number `C_n`. `C_n` = (1 / (n + 1)) * C(2n, n)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_combinatorics_combinations" => "Computes the number of combinations `C(n, k) = n! / (k! * (n-k)!)`. # Arguments * `n` - The total number of items. * `k` - The number of items to choose. # Returns The number of combinations as an `f64`. Returns `0.0` if `k > n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_combinatorics_factorial" => "Computes the factorial of `n` (`n!`) as an `f64`. # Arguments * `n` - The non-negative integer for which to compute the factorial. # Returns The factorial of `n` as an `f64`. Returns `f64::INFINITY` if `n` is too large to fit in `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_combinatorics_falling_factorial" => "Computes the falling factorial (x)_n = x(x-1)...(x-n+1).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_combinatorics_permutations" => "Computes the number of permutations `P(n, k) = n! / (n-k)!`. # Arguments * `n` - The total number of items. * `k` - The number of items to choose. # Returns The number of permutations as an `f64`. Returns `0.0` if `k > n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_combinatorics_rising_factorial" => "Computes the rising factorial (Pochhammer symbol) x^(n) = x(x+1)...(x+n-1).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_combinatorics_solve_recurrence_numerical" => "Numerically solves a linear recurrence relation by unrolling it. The recurrence relation is assumed to be of the form: `a_n = coeffs[0]*a_{n-1} + coeffs[1]*a_{n-2} + ... + coeffs[order-1]*a_{n-order}`. # Arguments * `coeffs` - A slice of `f64` representing the coefficients of the recurrence relation. * `initial_conditions` - A slice of `f64` representing the initial values `a_0, a_1, ..., a_{order-1}`. * `target_n` - The index `n` for which to compute `a_n`. # Returns A `Result` containing the numerical value of `a_n`. # Errors Returns an error if: - The number of initial conditions does not match the order of the recurrence. - The internal storage fails to compute values (e.g., empty result vector).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_combinatorics_stirling_second" => "Computes the Stirling number of the second kind `S(n, k)` as an `f64`. Counts partitions of `n` elements into `k` nonempty subsets. The empty set has one partition into zero subsets. Returns zero for `k > n` and for `k = 0 < n`, and positive infinity when the count overflows `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_complex_cauchy_derivative_scaled" => "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. `f^(n)(z) = n! / (2 pi i) ∮ f(w) / (w - z)^(n+1) dw` over the circle of `radius` around `z` (Cauchy's integral formula); accurate for high orders where finite differences are not.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_complex_circle_integral_scaled" => "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. `∮ f(z) dz` over the circle `|z - center| = radius`, counter-clockwise, with `n` equally spaced nodes.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_complex_contour_integral_scaled" => "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. `∫ f(z) dz` along the polygon through `path`, by Simpson's rule on each segment subdivided `per_segment` times.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_complex_count_zeros_poles_scaled" => "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. The number of zeros minus the number of poles of `f` inside a circle. The circle is `|z - center| = radius` (argument principle), and the count is the winding number of the polygon through the sampled values of `f`. Samples `f` once per node, in counterclockwise order, with at least 64 nodes. Returns `None` if a sampled value is zero or nonfinite or if a sampled change of argument exceeds `pi / 2`. Undersampling can still alias a winding whose changes between nodes are not observed.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_complex_derivative_scaled" => "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. `f'(z)` by the central difference along the real axis (valid for analytic `f`).\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_complex_newton_scaled" => "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. Newton's method for an analytic `f` with derivative `df`, from `z0`. Returns the root once a step is below `tolerance`, or `None` after `max_iterations` or at a vanishing derivative.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_complex_residue_scaled" => "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. The residue of `f` at `z0`: `(1 / 2 pi i) ∮ f dz` over a small circle that must enclose no other singularity.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_complex_winding_number" => "Returns the winding number about zero of the polygon formed by a rank-one tensor of complex vertices with a common physical dimension. The last vertex connects to the first. Counterclockwise winding is positive. Empty paths and paths through zero return a domain error. Winding number about the origin of the closed polygonal path through `points`. Joins the last vertex to the first. Counterclockwise turns count as positive. Returns `None` for an empty path, a nonfinite vertex or an edge through the origin. A single nonzero finite vertex has winding number zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_computer_graphics_degrees_to_radians" => "Converts degrees to radians.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_computer_graphics_dot_product_nd" => "Computes the dot product of two vectors of any dimension. # Errors Returns an error when the slices have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_computer_graphics_radians_to_degrees" => "Converts radians to degrees.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_cond" => "2-norm condition number; infinity for a singular matrix. 2-norm condition number `sigma_max / sigma_min` (infinite when singular). # Errors As [`svd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_control_c2d_zoh" => "Discretise a measured continuous state-space model under a zero-order hold. Accepts positive sample time and returns a portable record with a, b, c, d and dt. Matrix a has inverse-time units before discretisation, and b gains one factor of Time. Zero-order-hold discretisation with sample time `dt`. Computes `exp([A B; 0 0] dt)` by scaling and squaring with a diagonal Pade approximant of order six. The upper-right block is the exactly integrated input matrix, not a Euler approximation. # Errors Returns an error when `dt` is not finite and positive, or when the matrix exponential fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_control_simulate_continuous" => "Simulate a measured continuous state-space model on a strictly increasing time grid. Hold each input row until the next time and return one measured output row per grid point. Simulates a continuous-time model on the strictly increasing time grid `t` from initial state `x0` under piecewise-constant (zero-order-hold) inputs `u`, where `u[k]` is held across `[t[k], t[k + 1])`. Each step uses exact zero-order-hold discretisation via [`StateSpace::c2d_zoh`], caching `(Ad, Bd)` whenever consecutive step sizes match to a relative tolerance of `1e-12`. Uniform grids compute the matrix exponential only once. Returns the output `y[k] = C x(t[k]) + D u[k]` at each grid point. # Errors Returns an error when the model is discrete-time, when `x0` or `u` dimensions are wrong, when `u.len() != t.len()`, when any entry is non-finite, or when `t` is not strictly increasing.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_control_simulate_discrete" => "Simulate a portable discrete state-space model from an initial state and one input row per sample. Return one measured output row per input row, including the initial output and direct feedthrough. Simulates a discrete-time model from `x0` under the input sequence `u`. The recursion `x[k+1] = A x[k] + B u[k]`, `y[k] = C x[k] + D u[k]` is applied directly. For continuous-time models, use [`StateSpace::simulate_continuous`] (with an explicit time grid) or [`StateSpace::simulate_with_dt`] (with a uniform sample time). Returns the output at each input sample. # Errors Returns an error for a wrong `x0` or input length, or when the model has no sample time.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_convergence_aitken_acceleration" => "One pass of Aitken's delta-squared process over `sequence`. Entry `i` is `s_i - (s_{i+1} - s_i)^2 / (s_{i+2} - 2 s_{i+1} + s_i)`. Entries whose second difference is below `1e-9` in magnitude (already converged, or numerically meaningless) are skipped, so the result can be shorter than `len - 2`; fewer than three inputs give an empty vector.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_convergence_richardson_extrapolation" => "Richardson extrapolation of approximations `A(h), A(h/2), A(h/4), ...`. For errors that expand in powers of `h^2` (Romberg integration, central differences), the standard factors are `4^j`. The result is the diagonal of the extrapolation table, with the highest order estimate last.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_convergence_richardson_extrapolation_with" => "Richardson extrapolation with a configurable error-term growth factor. Column `j` of the table eliminates one error term by the factor `growth^j`: `growth = 4` for errors in `h^2, h^4, ...` as `h` halves, `growth = 2` for errors in `1/n, 1/n^2, ...` as the number of terms `n` doubles. Returns the diagonal and retains only one working column. For finite `growth > 1`, a change of scale avoids overflow in intermediate powers or products when the estimate is finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_convergence_wynn_epsilon" => "Wynn's epsilon algorithm (Shanks transformation) on `sequence`. Returns the accelerated estimates: the first entry is the last element of the sequence itself, and each further entry is the last element of the next even epsilon column, so later entries are higher-order estimates of the limit. Estimates that are not finite are dropped, and the process stops when two consecutive values coincide (exact convergence).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_correlation" => "Pearson correlation of equally sized samples. Computes the Pearson correlation coefficient of two slices of data. # Arguments * `data1` - The first slice of `f64` data points. * `data2` - The second slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The Pearson correlation coefficient as an `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_cosh_integral" => "The hyperbolic cosine integral `Chi(x) = γ + ln x + ∫_0^x (cosh t - 1)/t dt` for `x > 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_cosine_integral" => "The cosine integral `Ci(x) = γ + ln x + ∫_0^x (cos t - 1)/t dt` for `x > 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_cosine_similarity" => "Cosine similarity; zero vectors are invalid. Computes the cosine similarity between two vectors. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths or if any vector is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_covariance" => "Sample covariance of equally sized samples. Computes the sample covariance of two slices of data. # Arguments * `data1` - The first slice of `f64` data points. * `data2` - The second slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The covariance of the two data sets, or NaN when the slices differ in length or hold fewer than two points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_cross_product" => "Computes the cross product of two 3D vectors. # Arguments * `v1` - First 3D vector. * `v2` - Second 3D vector. # Returns * `Ok(Vec<f64>)` - The 3D cross product vector. # Errors Returns an error if the input vectors are not 3D.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_amg_general_solve" => "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Solves `A x = b` by repeated cycles. # Panics Panics if the right-hand side or supplied initial iterate has the wrong length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_amg_solve" => "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Solves `A x = b` by repeated cycles; returns the solution, the relative residual history and whether `tol` was reached.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_bicgstab" => "BiCGSTAB with identity preconditioning. Convergence reports the independently recomputed relative residual. Preconditioned `BiCGSTAB` for general nonsymmetric operators. Uses the same finite-input, complete linear-operator, zero-RHS, and verified convergence contract as [`cg`]. Coefficient ratios and preconditioned directions retain their scales to avoid spurious arithmetic breakdown.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_cg" => "Conjugate gradients with identity preconditioning; the matrix must be symmetric positive definite. Convergence reports the independently recomputed relative residual. Preconditioned conjugate gradients for SPD operators. `apply(x, y)` computes `y = A x`; `precond(r, z)` computes `z = M^-1 r`. Both operators must be linear and overwrite every output entry. Inputs, output entries, and tolerance must be finite; tolerance is nonnegative and an initial guess must have the RHS length. Invalid inputs or incomplete operator outputs produce a nonconverged result rather than false success. The solver normalizes the initial residual correction and search directions; dot products and coefficient ratios retain binary exponents until vector updates. Convergence is checked against the original system, not just the recursive residual. Residual correction is refined under one shared iteration budget when normalization loses tiny components. A zero iteration budget still checks the initial guess.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_cg_amg" => "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Preconditioner action `z = M^{-1} r` (one cycle from zero), symmetric positive definite for symmetric `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_eigs" => "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Implicitly restarted Arnoldi for the `k` wanted eigenpairs of a general real operator. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k >= n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if the small dense eigenproblem fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_eigs_shift_invert" => "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Sparse-LU shift-invert eigensolve with verified residuals in the original problem. Duplicate CSR entries are accumulated, and the shift is subtracted once per row. # Errors Invalid CSR data, singular shifted factorization, or failed eigensolve.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_eigs_sym" => "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Thick-restart Lanczos for the `k` wanted eigenpairs of a symmetric operator on `R^n`. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if the projected eigenproblem fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_gmres" => "Restarted GMRES with identity preconditioning. Convergence reports the independently recomputed relative residual. Restarted GMRES(`restart`) with right preconditioning. Uses the same finite-input and complete-output contract as [`cg`]. The preconditioned basis vectors are retained, and Arnoldi uses reorthogonalized modified Gram-Schmidt. A zero restart is clamped to one, as before. Final convergence uses the original system residual.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_minres" => "MINRES for symmetric indefinite systems, identity preconditioning; reports independently recomputed original-system residual. MINRES for a symmetric (indefinite allowed) operator with an SPD preconditioner `precond`. The reported `residual` is the true relative residual `|b - A x| / |b|` of the returned iterate; the iteration stops when the recurrence estimate (preconditioned residual norm) drops below `tol` relative to its initial value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_amd" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Approximate minimum degree ordering (quotient graph with element absorption, supervariables and mass elimination); see [`crate::kernels::ordering::amd_order`]. Returns `perm[new] = old`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_bandwidth" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Half bandwidth `max |i - j|` over the stored entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_diagonal" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Diagonal of the matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_from_triplets" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Builds a matrix from `(row, col, value)` triplets; duplicates are summed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_jacobi_inverse" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Jacobi preconditioner apply function data: the inverse diagonal.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_matvec" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Matrix-vector product `y = A x`. Every row is an independent sequential dot product, so large matrices are processed in parallel blocks of rows without changing a bit of the result.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_min_degree" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Minimum-degree ordering on the elimination graph of the symmetrised pattern: repeatedly eliminates a vertex of smallest current degree, turning its neighbourhood into a clique (exact degrees, lazily maintained heap). Returns `perm[new] = old`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_nested_dissection" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Nested dissection ordering; see [`crate::kernels::ordering::nested_dissection_order`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_permute" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Symmetric permutation `B[i][j] = A[perm[i]][perm[j]]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_csr_model_rcm" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Reverse Cuthill-McKee ordering of the symmetrised pattern, with a George-Liu pseudo-peripheral start vertex per connected component. Uses the leading `min(rows, cols)` square, with every stored off-diagonal entry contributing an edge regardless of its value. Components, including isolated vertices, are processed in ascending order of their smallest vertex, then the complete order is reversed. Returns `perm` with `perm[new] = old`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_cv" => "Coefficient of variation. Computes the coefficient of variation (CV = `std_dev` / mean). Useful for comparing variability across datasets with different means.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dae_implicit_adaptive" => "Dimension-aware residual DAE BDF1/BDF2 with genuine step-doubling control: t0,t1,y0,yp0,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,newton_tolerance,max_newton_iterations,residual. State has arbitrary E dimensions, derivatives E/D, residuals independent R; Newton tolerance carries R. Algebraic initial values must be consistent. Returns times/states/rates/evaluations/rejected. Solves a fully implicit Differential-Algebraic Equation `F(t, y, y') = 0` over `span = (t0, t1)` using variable-step BDF1/BDF2 with Newton iteration. Refines differential derivatives when possible; algebraic initial states must already be consistent. Step doubling controls the BDF local error; Newton failure alone is never treated as an accuracy estimate. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Empty`] if `y0` is empty, [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for invalid inputs, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if Newton iteration or step control fails.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_dae_index1_adaptive" => "Dimension-aware semi-explicit DAE with explicit nondimensionalization: t0,t1,y0,z0,time_scale,state_scale,algebraic_scale,constraint_scale,normalized_atol,rtol,normalized_h0,normalized_h_min,normalized_h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,normalized_newton_tolerance,max_newton_iterations,rhs,constraint. Four positive physical reference scales make mixed state/constraint Newton blocks dimensionless. Index1 constraint receives t,y,z; index2 receives t,y. Returns physical times,states,algebraic,rates and actual work counts. Adaptive semi-explicit DAE solver with bounded callback work. # Errors Invalid controls, malformed/nonfinite callbacks, failed consistency or stage solves, exhausted budgets, or an unsatisfied tolerance at minimum step.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_dae_index2_adaptive" => "Dimension-aware semi-explicit DAE with explicit nondimensionalization: t0,t1,y0,z0,time_scale,state_scale,algebraic_scale,constraint_scale,normalized_atol,rtol,normalized_h0,normalized_h_min,normalized_h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,normalized_newton_tolerance,max_newton_iterations,rhs,constraint. Four positive physical reference scales make mixed state/constraint Newton blocks dimensionless. Index1 constraint receives t,y,z; index2 receives t,y. Returns physical times,states,algebraic,rates and actual work counts. Adaptive semi-explicit DAE solver with bounded callback work. # Errors Invalid controls, malformed/nonfinite callbacks, failed consistency or stage solves, exhausted budgets, or an unsatisfied tolerance at minimum step.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_dde_constant_adaptive" => "Dimension-aware constant-delay method-of-steps DP5(4): t0,t1,y0,positive_delays,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,rhs,history. RHS receives t,y,delay-by-state matrix and returns state/time. History receives time and returns state. Strict total RHS/prehistory callback budget; no fabricated intra-step history. Returns times/states/Hermite rates/evaluations/rejected. Integrate a constant-delay differential equation. Solves a constant-delay Delay Differential Equation (DDE) system: `y'(t) = rhs(t, y(t), &[y(t - \\\\tau_1), ..., y(t - \\\\tau_k)])` over `span = (t0, t1)` given a pre-initial history function `history(t)` for `t <= t0`. Uses the **Method of Steps** with an adaptive Dormand–Prince 5(4) pair, continuous cubic Hermite history interpolation, and automatic breakpoint tracking at integer combinations of the delays `t0 + \\\\sum m_j \\\\tau_j` so that derivative discontinuities at `t0` do not degrade high-order convergence. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Empty`] if `y0` is empty, [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] if any delay is non-positive or inputs are non-finite, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] if `rhs` or `history` returns a vector of length `!= y0.len()`, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if the step size collapses or `max_steps` is exceeded.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_dense_cholesky_solve" => "Solves `A x = b` for symmetric positive definite `A` using Cholesky. # Errors As [`cholesky`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_det_units" => "Determinant with runtime matrix-order dimension exponent. The Value result retains entry-units raised to matrix order; only square matrices are accepted. Determinant of the factored matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_lstsq_svd" => "Minimum-norm least-squares solution through the SVD (handles rank deficiency).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_lu_model_build" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Computes the LU factorisation of a square matrix. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] for non-square input or incompatible matrix storage, [`DenseError::Singular`] when a pivot is exactly zero, or [`DenseError::NonFinite`] for NaN or infinite entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_lu_model_det" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Determinant of the factored matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_lu_model_inverse" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Inverse of the factored matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_lu_model_solve" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Solves `A x = b`, returning a new solution vector. Returns an empty vector for a short right-hand side and ignores any trailing entries. Use [`Lu::solve_into`] to check shape and finiteness.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_null_space" => "Orthonormal basis of the null space `{x : A x = 0}` as the columns of the result (`cols x (cols - rank)`), from the SVD.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_orth" => "Orthonormal basis of the column space (range) of `A` as the columns of the result (`rows x rank`), from the SVD; `tol` as in [`null_space`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_pinv_tol" => "Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse with a relative singular-value threshold. # Errors Returns non-finite input for invalid relative tolerance, or SVD errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_schur" => "Real Schur decomposition `A = Q T Q^T` with orthogonal `Q` and quasi-upper-triangular `T`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_solve" => "Solves the square system `A x = b` by LU. # Errors Propagates the errors of [`lu_factor`] and [`Lu::solve_into`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_solve_spd_tridiagonal" => "Solves a symmetric positive-definite tridiagonal linear system in `O(n)` time via `LDL^T` factorisation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dense_solve_tridiagonal" => "Solves a general tridiagonal linear system in `O(n)` time via the Thomas algorithm: `sub[i - 1] * x[i - 1] + diag[i] * x[i] + sup[i] * x[i + 1] = rhs[i]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_corr" => "The Pearson correlation coefficient of two equally long samples. Population and sample normalization give the same coefficient. # Errors Returns [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] for different lengths, [`NumericError::Empty`] for empty samples, and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values or a sample with no spread.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_cov" => "The sample covariance of two equally long samples. Use `population` to divide by the sample length. Otherwise, divide by one less than the length for samples with two or more observations. The covariance of a pair of singleton samples is zero. # Errors Returns [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] for different lengths, [`NumericError::Empty`] for empty samples, and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values or a result outside the finite range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_cov_exact" => "The covariance of two exact samples. # Errors [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] when the lengths differ, and [`NumericError::Empty`] when there are no elements.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_histogram" => "A uniform histogram with `bins` buckets. Returns `(counts, edges)`. For a nonconstant sample, the `bins + 1` edges span the sample range. An interior edge belongs to the bucket above it, and the highest edge belongs to the last bucket. Edges can coincide when a bucket is narrower than the representable spacing. Constant samples use unit-width buckets starting at the value minus 0.5, with all observations in the first bucket. Large samples with a bounded number of buckets can be counted in parallel. The counts and edges are independent of the active Rayon pool size. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values, a zero bucket count, or buckets that cannot be allocated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_histogram_edges" => "Histogram counts for caller-supplied, possibly nonuniform bucket edges. Returns one count per adjacent pair of edges. Every bucket includes its lower edge, and the last bucket also includes its upper edge. Every sample appears in one bucket, including observations exactly on the outer edges. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample. [`NumericError::Domain`] if the edges contain fewer than two values, fail to increase strictly, or would require unallocatable counter storage. Non-finite observations or edges and observations outside the supplied range also give a domain error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_kurtosis" => "The excess kurtosis `m4 / m2^2 - 3`. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample, and [`NumericError::Domain`] for a constant sample or non-finite values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_mean_exact" => "The arithmetic mean of an exact sample. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_median" => "Median with type-7 interpolation; sorting occurs in adapter-owned storage, preserving the caller's vector. The median of a float sample (runs in `O(n)` average time via selection). # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_median_exact" => "Exact rational median; sorting occurs in local storage and no float conversion is performed. The median of an exact sample. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_mode" => "The most frequent value of a float sample; ties go to the smallest. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_mode_exact" => "The most frequent value of an exact sample. Ties go to the smallest value, so the answer does not depend on the order of the input. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_quantile" => "The `q`-quantile of a float sample, `0 ≤ q ≤ 1`. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values or a `q` outside `[0, 1]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_quantile_exact" => "The `q`-quantile of a sorted exact sample, `0 ≤ q ≤ 1`. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] when `q` is outside `[0, 1]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_quantiles" => "Type-7 quantiles of a finite sample, in the supplied probability order. A single query selects the two adjacent ranks in linear time. Multiple queries share one ordered copy of the sample. # Errors Returns [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite observations or a probability outside `[0, 1]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_skewness" => "The skewness `m3 / m2^{3/2}`. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample, and [`NumericError::Domain`] for a constant sample or non-finite values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_descriptive_var_exact" => "The variance of an exact sample. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_bc_fixed" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_bcs" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_coord" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Physical coordinates of a node.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_data" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_elements" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Number of elements.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_integral" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Integral of a function of the state over the domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_l2_error" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Quadrature `L2` norm of `q_var - exact`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_mesh" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_nodes_per_element" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Nodes per element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_pressure" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Pressure of a conserved state.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg3_value" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Value of a variable at a node.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_advection" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_advection_prepare1d" => "Prepares periodic scalar DG advection on a one-dimensional mesh at a fixed measured velocity. The plan retains the nodal differentiation and surface weights. Prepares the Legendre–Gauss–Lobatto operator for a fixed mesh and velocity. # Errors Returns an error for invalid one-dimensional geometry, degree, velocity or work size.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_advection_step" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Advance periodic scalar DG advection from its nodal state by one SSPRK3 step. The caller controls the physical step and must respect the DG CFL limit; this restart interface reuses the existing DG volume/surface operator. # Errors Invalid mesh/state/time, a non-finite speed, or a step above the DG CFL limit.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_advection_step_nd" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Advance periodic scalar DG advection in one or two dimensions by SSPRK3. # Errors Invalid mesh, velocity/state dimensions, time controls, or multidimensional CFL.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_advection_step_prepared1d" => "Advances the prepared periodic DG plan by one positive measured time interval within its CFL bound. The input and output are element-major nodal vectors with the mesh field dimension. Advances a nodal state by one SSPRK3 step and returns the new state. # Errors Returns the same validation and numerical errors as [`Self::step_into`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_bc_custom" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_bc_fixed" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_bcs" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_burgers" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_coord" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Physical coordinates of a node.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_data" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_elements" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Number of elements.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_entropy" => "Native mathematical Euler entropy in dimensionless reference coordinates. The logarithm uses normalized pressure and density; changing physical reference scales intentionally changes this entropy coordinate. Specific thermodynamic entropy `s = ln(p / ρ^γ)` of an Euler state with `nvel` velocity components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_entropy_stable_flux" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_euler" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_flux" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_from_characteristic" => "Native characteristic transformation in the explicitly normalized conserved coordinate basis. Eigenvectors mix physical component domains, so its coordinates are dimensionless relative to the law references; inverse uses the identical basis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_has_characteristics" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_integral" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Integral of a variable over the domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_l2_error" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Quadrature `L2` norm of `q_var - exact`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_lgl" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Legendre–Gauss–Lobatto nodes and weights with `p + 1` points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_lgl_diff" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Differentiation matrix on the LGL nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_max_speed" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_mesh_line" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. One-dimensional mesh of `ne` elements on `[lo, hi]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_mesh_rect" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Two-dimensional mesh.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_nodes_per_element" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Nodes per element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_numerical_flux" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_nvar" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_rho_p" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_to_characteristic" => "Native characteristic transformation in the explicitly normalized conserved coordinate basis. Eigenvectors mix physical component domains, so its coordinates are dimensionless relative to the law references; inverse uses the identical basis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_two_point_flux" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_value" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Value of a variable.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dg_wall_state" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_digamma" => "Numerical digamma function. Computes the digamma function, ψ(x) = d/dx ln(Γ(x)) = Γ'(x)/Γ(x).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_disk_to_uhp" => "Maps a dimensionless complex point in the open Poincare unit disk to the open upper half-plane. An input on or outside the unit circle or a nonfinite result produces a domain error. Inverse Cayley transform mapping the unit disk `{ |w| < 1 }` to the upper half-plane: `z = i * (1 + w) / (1 - w)`. Returns `None` at `w = 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distance" => "Euclidean distance between vectors. Computes the Euclidean distance between two vectors without allocating an intermediate vector. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distributions_dirichlet_log_pdf" => "Dirichlet log density on the unit simplex, with positive finite concentrations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distributions_dirichlet_pdf" => "Dirichlet density on the unit simplex, with positive finite concentrations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distributions_dirichlet_sample" => "One Dirichlet sample from a deterministic seed and positive finite concentrations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distributions_multivariate_normal_log_pdf" => "Dimensionless multivariate normal log density with a row-major covariance matrix. Rejects non-finite data, mismatched dimensions and non-positive-definite covariance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distributions_multivariate_normal_log_pdfs" => "Dimensionless multivariate normal log densities in row-major query order. A single covariance factor is reused by every query.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distributions_multivariate_normal_pdf" => "Dimensionless multivariate normal density; covariance dimensions must match the mean.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distributions_multivariate_normal_sample" => "One dimensionless multivariate normal sample from a deterministic seed. Rejects mismatched dimensions and invalid covariance matrices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distributions_weibull_inverse_survival" => "Inverts the Weibull survival probability `P(X > x)` directly. A survival probability of zero gives positive infinity, and a probability of one gives zero. Tiny positive probabilities retain the upper tail. # Errors Returns [`DistError::Domain`] for invalid parameters or survival probability.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_distributions_weibull_quantiles" => "Evaluates Weibull lower-tail quantiles using one validated shape and scale. Probabilities are in `[0, 1]`. Order and repetitions are preserved, and an empty probability slice yields an empty vector. The endpoints are `0` and positive infinity, as in [`Distribution::quantile`]. # Errors Returns [`DistError::Domain`] for invalid parameters or probabilities.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dogleg" => "Dimensionless nonlinear system solver: initial vector,positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,residual callback. Callback input and output retain the initial vector length. Returns x,evaluations; callback failures and iteration exhaustion are explicit errors. Powell's trust-region dogleg solver for nonlinear systems `f(x) = 0`. # Errors As [`newton_system`].\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_dogleg_scaled" => "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. Powell's trust-region dogleg solver for nonlinear systems `f(x) = 0`. # Errors As [`newton_system`].\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_dot" => "Dot product of equal-length vectors. Computes the dot product of two vectors. Formula: \$\\\\mathbf{a} \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{b} = \\\\sum_{i=1}^n `a_i` `b_i`\$ # Arguments * `v1` - The first vector. * `v2` - The second vector. # Returns * `Ok(f64)` - The dot product. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_abs" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Absolute value `|self|`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_acos" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Inverse cosine `acos(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_add" => "Native normalized dual arithmetic; tangent propagation stays in the RSSN Dual implementation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_asin" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Inverse sine `asin(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_atan" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Inverse tangent `atan(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_constant" => "A dimensionless normalized dual constant; used inside exact forward-AD callbacks. Creates a constant dual number with derivative `0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_cos" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Cosine of `self`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_cosh" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Hyperbolic cosine `cosh(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_div" => "Native normalized dual arithmetic; tangent propagation stays in the RSSN Dual implementation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_exp" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Natural exponential `exp(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_forward_derivative_scaled" => "Exact native AD with normalized callback variables and independent physical references: point,positive coordinate_scale,positive value_scale,max_evaluations,callback. Forward callback scalar duals are {val,deriv}; vector duals are {val:vector,deriv:vector}; all dual arithmetic is dimensionless and uses native num_dual_* operators. Reverse callbacks use checked NativeAdVar handles on an ephemeral session tape; native operations enforce same-tape identity and a65536-node limit. Output derivatives restore E/D; no numerical differencing or opaque-callback finite-difference fallback. Exact first derivative `(f(x), f'(x))` of `f(Dual) -> Dual` via forward-mode AD.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_dual_forward_gradient_scaled" => "Exact native AD with normalized callback variables and independent physical references: point,positive coordinate_scale,positive value_scale,max_evaluations,callback. Forward callback scalar duals are {val,deriv}; vector duals are {val:vector,deriv:vector}; all dual arithmetic is dimensionless and uses native num_dual_* operators. Reverse callbacks use checked NativeAdVar handles on an ephemeral session tape; native operations enforce same-tape identity and a65536-node limit. Output derivatives restore E/D; no numerical differencing or opaque-callback finite-difference fallback. Exact gradient of `f(&[Dual]) -> Dual` at `x` via forward-mode AD. # Errors [`AdError::Empty`] when `x` is empty.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_dual_forward_jacobian_scaled" => "Exact native AD with normalized callback variables and independent physical references: point,positive coordinate_scale,positive value_scale,max_evaluations,callback. Forward callback scalar duals are {val,deriv}; vector duals are {val:vector,deriv:vector}; all dual arithmetic is dimensionless and uses native num_dual_* operators. Reverse callbacks use checked NativeAdVar handles on an ephemeral session tape; native operations enforce same-tape identity and a65536-node limit. Output derivatives restore E/D; no numerical differencing or opaque-callback finite-difference fallback. Exact Jacobian of `f(&[Dual]) -> Vec<Dual>` at `x` via forward-mode AD. Returns a row-major `m × n` Jacobian. # Errors [`AdError::Empty`] when `x` is empty or `f` returns an empty vector, and [`AdError::ShapeMismatch`] if the output length varies across passes.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_dual_ln" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Natural logarithm `ln(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_mul" => "Native normalized dual arithmetic; tangent propagation stays in the RSSN Dual implementation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_new" => "Construct a normalized dual from finite primal and tangent values. Creates a dual number with explicit value and derivative.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_pow" => "Native normalized dual floating power with a dimensionless exponent. Floating-point power `self^p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_powi" => "Native normalized dual integer power. Integer power `self^n`. For a finite nonzero base and finite tangent, a representable tangent is retained even when the primal or an unscaled power overflows or underflows.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_powi_with_second_derivative" => "Evaluate the normalized integer power along val + t * deriv at t = 0. Returns the primal, first derivative and second directional derivative. The exponent is an Int in the signed 32-bit range. Nonfinite results are rejected. Evaluates `self^n` and both derivatives along `self.deriv`. Treats the input as `self.val + t * self.deriv` and differentiates at `t = 0`. The returned dual holds the value and first derivative; the second component is the second derivative with respect to `t`. The powers zero and one have zero second derivative.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_sin" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Sine of `self`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_sinh" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Hyperbolic sine `sinh(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_sqrt" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Square root `sqrt(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_sub" => "Native normalized dual arithmetic; tangent propagation stays in the RSSN Dual implementation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_tan" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Tangent of `self`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_tanh" => "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Hyperbolic tangent `tanh(self)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_dual_variable" => "A normalized dual variable with tangent one. Creates an independent variable dual number with derivative `1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_eigen_symmetric" => "Real eigenvalues and orthonormal eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix. Eigen-decomposition of a symmetric matrix by cyclic Jacobi rotations. Returns eigenvalues in ascending order and the matrix whose columns are the corresponding orthonormal eigenvectors. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] for non-square input, [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] after 100 sweeps.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_enestrom_kakeya_bounds" => "Root annulus for at least two positive, finite real coefficients in ascending order. Coefficients may share ordinate units. The lower and upper root radii are dimensionless. Eneström–Kakeya annulus bounds \$(r_{\\\\min}, r_{\\\\max})\$ for a polynomial with strictly positive real ascending coefficients \$`a_0`, \\\\dots, `a_n` > 0\$.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_entropy" => "Shannon entropy of the supplied probabilities. Computes the Shannon entropy of a discrete probability distribution. H(X) = -Σ p(x) * log2(p(x))\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_erf" => "Numerical error function. Computes the error function, erf(x) = (2/√π) ∫₀ˣ e^(-t²) dt.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_erfc" => "Numerical complementary error function. Computes the complementary error function, erfc(x) = 1 - erf(x).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_erfi" => "The imaginary error function `erfi(x) = -i erf(ix) = (2/√π) ∫_0^x e^{t²} dt`, from its everywhere-convergent power series (all terms of one sign, so no cancellation).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_bch_decode" => "Decodes a codeword produced by [`bch_encode`], correcting up to `t` bit errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_bch_encode" => "Encodes data bits with a binary BCH code correcting `t` errors. The code is the shortened narrow-sense primitive BCH code of the smallest block length `2^m - 1` that can carry `data.len()` bits (see [`Bch`]); the result is the systematic codeword `data ++ parity`. Use [`Bch::encode`] for explicit control of `(n, k)` and for error details. # Arguments * `data` - The data bits (`0` or `1`). * `t` - The error correction capability (corrects up to `t` bit errors). # Returns The encoded codeword, or an empty vector if the arguments are invalid (empty or non-binary data, `t == 0`, or no supported code is large enough).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_calculate_syndromes" => "Calculates the syndromes of a received codeword. The syndrome `S_i` is the polynomial evaluated at α^i. Requests beyond 255 return the 255 distinct evaluations, since subsequent roots repeat. Use [`reed_solomon_check`] to validate the block shape and syndromes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_chien_search" => "Finds the roots of the error locator polynomial to determine error locations. Uses Chien search to efficiently evaluate the polynomial at all field elements. # Errors Returns an error if the field element inversion fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_code_rate" => "Computes the code rate (number of information bits per coded bit). # Arguments * `k` - Number of information bits. * `n` - Total codeword length. # Returns The code rate as a floating-point number.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_convolutional_encode" => "Encodes data using a simple rate-1/2 convolutional code. Uses generators G1 = 1+D^2 (0b101) and G2 = 1+D+D^2 (0b111). # Arguments * `data` - The binary data to encode (each byte is 0 or 1). # Returns The encoded data (twice the length of input).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_crc16_compute" => "Computes the CRC-16 checksum of the given data. Uses the IBM/ANSI polynomial (0xA001 in reflected form). # Arguments * `data` - The data to compute the checksum for. # Returns The 16-bit CRC checksum. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::crc16_compute; let data = b\"123456789\"; let checksum = crc16_compute(data); assert_eq!(checksum, 0xBB3D); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_crc32_compute_numerical" => "Computes the CRC-32 checksum of the given data. Uses the standard IEEE 802.3 polynomial (0xEDB88320 in reflected form). # Arguments * `data` - The data to compute the checksum for. # Returns The 32-bit CRC checksum. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::crc32_compute_numerical; let data = b\"Hello, World!\"; let checksum = crc32_compute_numerical(data); assert_eq!(checksum, 0xEC4AC3D0); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_crc32_finalize_numerical" => "Finalizes a CRC-32 computation started with `crc32_update_numerical`. # Arguments * `crc` - The running CRC value. # Returns The final CRC-32 checksum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_crc32_update_numerical" => "Updates an existing CRC-32 with additional data (for streaming). # Arguments * `crc` - Current CRC value (use 0xFFFFFFFF for initial call). * `data` - Additional data to process. # Returns Updated CRC value (call `crc32_finalize_numerical` to get final CRC).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_crc32_verify_numerical" => "Verifies the CRC-32 checksum of data. # Arguments * `data` - The data to verify. * `expected_crc` - The expected CRC-32 checksum. # Returns `true` if the computed CRC matches the expected value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_crc8_compute" => "Computes the CRC-8 checksum of the given data. Uses the ITU polynomial (0x07). # Arguments * `data` - The data to compute the checksum for. # Returns The 8-bit CRC checksum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_deinterleave" => "De-interleaves data (reverses the interleave operation). # Arguments * `data` - The interleaved data. * `depth` - The interleaving depth used during interleaving. # Returns The de-interleaved data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_error_correction_capability" => "Estimates the error correction capability from the minimum distance. A code with minimum distance d can correct up to floor((d-1)/2) errors. # Arguments * `min_distance` - The minimum distance of the code. # Returns The number of errors that can be corrected.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_error_detection_capability" => "Estimates the error detection capability from the minimum distance. A code with minimum distance d can detect up to d-1 errors. # Arguments * `min_distance` - The minimum distance of the code. # Returns The number of errors that can be detected.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_forney_algorithm" => "Computes error magnitudes using Forney's algorithm. # Arguments * `omega` - The error evaluator polynomial. * `sigma` - The error locator polynomial. * `error_locs` - The positions of errors in the codeword. # Returns A vector of error magnitudes for each error location. # Errors Returns an error if division by zero occurs during magnitude calculation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_hamming_check_numerical" => "Checks if a Hamming(7,4) codeword is valid. # Arguments * `codeword` - A 7-bit codeword # Returns `true` if the codeword is valid (no errors), `false` otherwise.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_hamming_decode_numerical" => "Decodes a 7-bit Hamming(7,4) codeword, correcting a single-bit error if found. # Arguments * `codeword` - A slice of 7 bytes, each representing a bit (0 or 1). # Returns A `Result` containing: - `Ok((data, error_pos))` where `data` is the 4-bit corrected data and `error_pos` is `Some(index)` if an error was corrected at the given 1-based index, or `None` if no error was found. - `Err(String)` if the input length is not 7. # Errors Returns an error if the input codeword length is not exactly 7. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_decode_numerical; use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_encode_numerical; let data = vec![1, 0, 1, 1]; let mut codeword = hamming_encode_numerical(&data).unwrap(); codeword[2] ^= 1; // Introduce error at position 3 (1-indexed) let (decoded, error_pos) = hamming_decode_numerical(&codeword).unwrap(); assert_eq!(decoded, data); assert_eq!(error_pos, Some(3)); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_hamming_distance_numerical" => "Computes the Hamming distance between two byte slices. The Hamming distance is the number of positions at which the corresponding bits differ. # Arguments * `a` - First byte slice * `b` - Second byte slice # Returns The Hamming distance, or `None` if slices have different lengths. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_distance_numerical; let a = vec![0, 1, 1, 0]; let b = vec![1, 1, 0, 0]; assert_eq!(hamming_distance_numerical(&a, &b), Some(2)); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_hamming_encode_numerical" => "Encodes a 4-bit data block into a 7-bit Hamming(7,4) codeword. Hamming(7,4) is a single-error correcting code. It takes 4 data bits and adds 3 parity bits to create a 7-bit codeword. # Arguments * `data` - A slice of 4 bytes, each representing a bit (0 or 1). # Returns A `Some(Vec<u8>)` of 7 bits representing the codeword, or `None` if the input length is not 4. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_encode_numerical; let data = vec![1, 0, 1, 1]; let codeword = hamming_encode_numerical(&data).unwrap(); assert_eq!(codeword.len(), 7); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_hamming_weight_numerical" => "Computes the Hamming weight (number of 1s) of a byte slice. For binary data where each byte is 0 or 1, this counts the number of 1s. # Arguments * `data` - Byte slice where each byte represents a bit (0 or 1) # Returns The count of non-zero bytes. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_weight_numerical; let data = vec![1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1]; assert_eq!(hamming_weight_numerical(&data), 4); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_interleave" => "Interleaves data to spread burst errors across multiple codewords. This technique helps in correcting burst errors by distributing consecutive symbols across different codewords. # Arguments * `data` - The data to interleave. * `depth` - The interleaving depth (number of codewords to spread across). # Returns The interleaved data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_minimum_distance" => "Computes the minimum distance of a code given a set of codewords. # Arguments * `codewords` - A slice of codewords. # Returns The minimum Hamming distance between any two distinct codewords.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_reed_solomon_check" => "Checks if a Reed-Solomon codeword is valid. # Arguments * `codeword` - The codeword to check. * `n_parity` - The number of parity symbols. # Returns `true` if the block has at most 255 symbols, contains at least the requested parity count, and has all-zero syndromes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_reed_solomon_decode" => "Decode/correct a finite GF256 Reed-Solomon block, returning a new corrected block. The source's mutable buffer is local to this adapter; inputs are unchanged. Decodes a Reed-Solomon codeword, correcting errors. This implementation uses the Berlekamp-Massey algorithm to find the error locator polynomial, Chien search to find error locations, and Forney's algorithm to find error magnitudes. It corrects up to `n_parity / 2` altered symbols in place. Beyond that correction radius, the received block may decode to another codeword. An error leaves `codeword` unchanged. # Arguments * `codeword` - A mutable slice of bytes representing the received codeword. * `n_parity` - The number of parity symbols in the original encoding. # Returns A `Result` indicating success or an error if decoding fails (e.g., too many errors). # Errors Returns an error if: - The block exceeds 255 symbols or contains fewer symbols than its parity count. - The decoder cannot verify a correction within its error budget. - No valid error locations can be found. - The Berlekamp-Massey or Forney algorithms fail to converge. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::reed_solomon_decode; use rssn::kernels::error_correction::reed_solomon_encode; let message = vec![0x01, 0x02, 0x03]; let mut codeword = reed_solomon_encode(&message, 4).unwrap(); codeword[0] ^= 0xFF; // Introduce error reed_solomon_decode(&mut codeword, 4).unwrap(); assert_eq!(&codeword[..3], &message); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_error_correction_reed_solomon_encode" => "Encodes a message using Reed-Solomon codes over GF(2^8). This function implements a systematic encoding scheme for Reed-Solomon codes. It appends `n_parity` parity symbols to the message. The parity symbols are computed by dividing the message polynomial (shifted by `n_parity` positions) by the generator polynomial and taking the remainder. Use [`ReedSolomonEncoder`] to encode multiple messages with the same parity. # Arguments * `message` - A slice of bytes representing the message. * `n_parity` - The number of parity symbols to add. # Returns A `Result` containing the full codeword (message + parity symbols), or an error if the total length exceeds the field size. # Errors Returns an error if: - The total length (message + parity) exceeds 255. - The number of parity symbols is invalid. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::reed_solomon_encode; let message = vec![0x01, 0x02, 0x03]; let codeword = reed_solomon_encode(&message, 4).unwrap(); assert_eq!(codeword.len(), 7); // 3 data + 4 parity ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_exp_integral_e1" => "`E1(x) = ∫_x^∞ e^(-t)/t dt` for `x > 0`: the power series for `x <= 1`, Lentz's continued fraction beyond.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_exp_integral_ei" => "The exponential integral `Ei(x) = -PV ∫_(-x)^∞ e^(-t)/t dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_factorial" => "Floating-point factorial of a nonnegative u64 integer. Computes the factorial n!\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_fft" => "The unnormalised forward transform.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_fft2" => "Forward two-dimensional FFT of a row-major matrix. # Errors Returns an error if the shape overflows or does not match the input length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_fftfreq" => "The sample frequencies of a length-`n` transform with spacing `d`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_fftn" => "Forward FFT over every tensor axis. # Errors Returns an error if the shape overflows or does not match the input length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_fftshift" => "Center the zero frequency in a one-dimensional spectrum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_ifft" => "The inverse transform, scaled by `1/N`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_ifft2" => "Inverse two-dimensional FFT of a row-major matrix. # Errors Returns an error if the shape overflows or does not match the input length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_ifftshift" => "Undo a centered one-dimensional spectrum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_irfft" => "Rebuilds a real signal of length `n` from the half spectrum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_irfft_axes" => "Rebuilds a real tensor from the half spectrum of [`rfft_axes`]. `shape` is the half-spectrum shape; `n_last` the length of the last listed axis in the result (`2 * (m - 1)` when the original was even).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_rfft" => "The transform of a real signal: the non-redundant half of the spectrum, `n/2 + 1` bins.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_rfft_axes" => "The half-spectrum transform of a real tensor over `axes`: the last listed axis keeps `n/2 + 1` bins. Returns the data and its shape.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_rfftfreq" => "The non-negative sample frequencies of a length-`n` real transform, `n/2 + 1` of them.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fft_shift_permutation" => "Where each element goes under `fftshift` (`inverse = false`) or `ifftshift` of a row-major tensor over `axes`: result element `i` is input element `permutation[i]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fftconv_autocorrelate" => "Linear autocorrelation at nonnegative lags, from zero through `samples.len() - 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fftconv_circular_convolve" => "Circular convolution of two real sequences of period `max(a.len(), b.len())`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fftconv_convolve" => "Linear convolution of two real sequences (`len = a.len() + b.len() - 1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fftconv_convolve_overlap_save" => "Full linear convolution using block FFTs and a reused filter spectrum. Uses zero extension and independent overlap-save blocks. The result has `a.len() + taps.len() - 1` samples, or zero samples if either input is empty. This is especially useful for a recording much longer than its filter. Use [`OverlapSavePlan`] when applying the same filter to several recordings. # Errors Errors on non-finite inputs or output, or an unallocatable extent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fftconv_correlate" => "Cross-correlation `c[k] = sum_i a[i + k - (len(b) - 1)] * b[i]` (full mode, length `a.len() + b.len() - 1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fftconv_poly_mul_i64" => "Multiplies two integer polynomials (ascending coefficients) exactly. Each output is the corresponding integer coefficient modulo `2^64`, interpreted as an `i64`. Use [`poly_mul_i64_checked`] to detect overflow.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fftconv_poly_mul_i64_checked" => "Multiplies two integer polynomials (ascending coefficients) exactly. Returns `None` if any output coefficient is outside the range of `i64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_batch_inverse_mod" => "`num_finite_field_batch_inverse_mod(values, modulus)` returns every modular inverse using a single inversion and pooled storage. The modulus must be at least 2 and every element must be invertible. Computes the modular inverses of all elements in `values` modulo `modulus` using Montgomery's batch-inversion trick. Returns `None` if `modulus < 2` or any element is not coprime to `modulus`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_batch_inverse_skip_zeros" => "`num_finite_field_batch_inverse_skip_zeros(values, modulus)` returns each inverse, with 0 in the position of any noninvertible element. The modulus must be at least 2.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_dot_mod" => "`num_finite_field_dot_mod(a, b, modulus)` returns the exact modular dot product of two equal-length vectors. The modulus must be at least 2.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_gf256_add" => "Performs addition in GF(2^8). In GF(2^8), addition is equivalent to a bitwise XOR operation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_gf256_div" => "Performs division in GF(2^8).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_gf256_inv" => "Computes the multiplicative inverse in GF(2^8).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_gf256_mul" => "Performs multiplication in GF(2^8) using lookup tables.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_gf256_mul_add" => "Returns destination XOR coefficient times source over GF(256) with polynomial 0x11d. Supply equal-length byte vectors and an exact byte coefficient. Adds the scaled source into the destination using field addition (XOR). # Errors Returns an error and leaves the destination unchanged when the lengths differ.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_gf256_pow" => "Performs exponentiation in GF(2^8).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_gf256_scale" => "Multiplies every byte by the supplied coefficient in GF(256) with polynomial 0x11d. Inputs and results are exact integers from 0 through 255. Returns the products in input order. Multiplies each byte of the slice by the coefficient in place.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_poly_mul_ntt_mod" => "Exact coefficient convolution modulo an odd prime. Inputs use the same coefficient order. For a nonempty product the next power of two of the output length must divide p-1. An empty input gives an empty product. Output uses the native tensor size limit.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_primitive_root_prime" => "Smallest positive primitive root modulo a prime. Accepts 2 and the odd primes representable in u64. Finds the smallest positive primitive root modulo a prime `modulus`. The root and Montgomery context for an odd prime are cached by modulus. Returns `None` if the modulus is composite, zero or one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_slice_add_mod" => "`num_finite_field_slice_add_mod(a, b, modulus)` adds two equal-length vectors of exact u64 integers, returning residues in [0, modulus). The modulus must be at least 2.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_slice_axpy_mod" => "`num_finite_field_slice_axpy_mod(alpha, x, y, modulus)` returns (alpha*x + y) reduced elementwise. The vectors must have equal lengths and the modulus must be at least 2.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_slice_scale_mod" => "`num_finite_field_slice_scale_mod(values, scalar, modulus)` multiplies an exact u64 vector by a scalar modulo an integer of at least 2.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_finite_field_slice_sub_mod" => "`num_finite_field_slice_sub_mod(a, b, modulus)` subtracts two equal-length vectors of exact u64 integers, returning residues in [0, modulus). The modulus must be at least 2.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_flow_inflow_velocity" => "Actual native unit-height inflow profile evaluated at normalized y; amplitude is normalized by the mechanical L/T reference and result retains velocity units. Axial velocity at height `y` in `[0, 1]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_flow_run_channel" => "Native square unit-domain channel with original free-slip walls and uniform unit inflow; normalized dt uses the explicit Time reference, output velocities and pressure have mechanical units. The native rectangular rejection is preserved. Solves the 2D Navier-Stokes equations for channel flow with an obstacle. Free-slip walls and a uniform unit inflow; see [`run_channel_flow_with`] for no-slip walls and a Poiseuille inflow, and for the boundary treatment. # Arguments * `nx` - Grid width. * `ny` - Grid height. * `re` - Reynolds number. * `dt` - Time step. * `n_iter` - Number of iterations. * `obstacle_mask` - A boolean mask where true indicates an obstacle (u=0). # Returns Tuple of (u, v, p) arrays. The solver works on a square `nx` x `nx` grid (`ny` must equal `nx`). # Errors Returns an error if the grid is smaller than 3 points, not square, or if the obstacle mask does not have shape `(ny, nx)`; also if the pressure solver fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_flow_run_channel_with" => "Actual native configurable channel model on a square unit domain; inflow, no-slip/slip wall, obstacle mask, Reynolds number and normalized time step are passed unchanged. Physical outputs retain independent velocity/pressure references. Solves the 2D incompressible Navier-Stokes equations in the unit square with an optional no-slip obstacle, by Chorin's projection method. Each step 1. advances an intermediate velocity explicitly (first-order upwind advection, central diffusion) at every fluid point; solid points and boundary points are then set by the boundary conditions, 2. imposes the boundary conditions: the inflow profile on the left, zero-gradient outflow on the right, the [`WallCondition`] on top and bottom, and *no slip* (`u = v = 0`) on every point of the obstacle mask (so fluid points next to the obstacle see zero velocity in their diffusion and advection stencils), 3. solves the pressure Poisson equation `lap(p) = div(u*) / dt` over the fluid points only, with `dp/dn = 0` on the inflow, the walls and all obstacle faces and `p = 0` at the outflow, by preconditioned conjugate gradients, and 4. corrects `u = u* - dt grad(p)` at fluid points, where a gradient across an obstacle face uses the Neumann (mirrored) pressure. The explicit scheme needs `dt <= h^2 re / 4` and `dt <= h / |u|`. # Errors Returns an error for invalid grid size, mask shape, time step, Reynolds number or inflow, non-finite fields, incompatible flux in a closed fluid region, or an unconverged pressure solve.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_flow_run_lid_cavity" => "Actual native legacy staggered cavity on a square unit domain. Lid velocity and time are normalized by explicit mechanical references. Native pressure/multigrid behavior is retained and work is bounded before invocation. Main solver for the 2D lid-driven cavity problem. Chorin's projection method on a staggered (MAC) grid: `p[j][i]` lives at cell centres, `u[j][i]` on the left face of cell `(j, i)` and `v[j][i]` on its bottom face. Each step 1. advances the intermediate velocity explicitly with first-order upwind advection and central diffusion with `nu = 1 / re`, 2. solves `lap(phi) = div(u*) / dt` with the multigrid solver (the same solver and zero-Dirichlet closure as [`run_channel_flow`]), and 3. corrects `u = u* - dt grad(phi)` and sets `p = phi`. The top row of `u` is the moving lid (`lid_velocity`), the bottom row and the side faces are at rest. The explicit scheme needs `dt <= h^2 re / 4` (diffusion) and `dt <= h / |u|` (advection) to stay stable. # Errors Returns an error if the grid is smaller than 3 points, if the cells are not square (`nx != ny`), or if the multigrid solver fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_flow_save_lid_cavity_scenario" => "Runs the actual native fixed 65x65, 200-step cavity scenario and writes its three native NPY files to the explicitly provided output directory. This legacy routine reports failures itself; the result only acknowledges return from the routine, not successful file creation. An example scenario for the lid-driven cavity simulation. The fields are written as `cavity_u_velocity.npy`, `cavity_v_velocity.npy` and `cavity_pressure.npy` inside `output_dir`, which is created if missing.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_barnsley_fern_ifs" => "Creates the Barnsley fern IFS.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_box_counting_dimension" => "Box-counting dimension of a finite N x 2 point tensor; both coordinate axes share units. Scale/work counts are bounded before native execution. Estimates the box-counting (Minkowski) dimension from a set of 2D points. The box-counting dimension is estimated by: D = lim(ε→0) [log(N(ε)) / log(1/ε)] where N(ε) is the number of boxes of side ε needed to cover the set. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of 2D points. * `num_scales` - Number of different box sizes to use. # Returns An estimate of the box-counting dimension.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_correlation_dimension" => "Correlation dimension of a finite N x 2 coordinate tensor. Pair allocation and radius-work counts are bounded. Estimates the correlation dimension from the log-log slope of the fraction of pairs closer than each of `num_radii` logarithmically spaced radii. Returns zero when fewer than two scales can determine a slope, and NaN for non-finite coordinates or distances outside the range of `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_correlation_dimension_with_radii" => "Correlation dimension from an N x 2 point tensor and explicit positive finite radii. Radii have the same units as the coordinates. Returns None when fewer than two radii determine a slope. Estimates correlation dimension from caller-supplied positive, finite radii. Radii can be supplied in any order. At radius `r`, only pairs strictly closer than `r` contribute. At least two distinct radii with close pairs are needed for a fit. Returns `None` for non-finite coordinates or when the radii cannot determine a slope.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_burning_ship" => "Generates the Burning Ship fractal. Similar to the Mandelbrot set but uses absolute values: `z = (|Re(z)| + i|Im(z)|)^2 + c` The resulting fractal resembles a burning ship. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. # Returns A 2D vector where each element is the escape time.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_henon_map" => "Native Henon map in normalized dimensionless coordinates. Returns an N x 2 point tensor with a checked finite allocation budget. Generates the Henon map trajectory. The Henon map is a discrete-time dynamical system that exhibits chaotic behavior. The map is: - x_{n+1} = 1 - a*`x_n^2` + `y_n` - y_{n+1} = b*`x_n` Classic parameter values: a = 1.4, b = 0.3 # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y)` coordinates. * `num_steps` - The number of iterations. * `a`, `b` - The Henon map parameters. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_ifs_fractal" => "Runs the affine chaos game in dimensionless coordinates with historical seed 12345. Provide N x 6 rows (a,b,c,d,e,f), N finite nonnegative weights including a positive weight, and the starting coordinates. Returns one two-coordinate row per retained step after burn-in. Generates an IFS fractal using the chaos game algorithm. # Arguments * `transforms` - The affine transformations. * `probabilities` - Probabilities for selecting each transformation. * `start_point` - Initial point. * `num_points` - Number of points to generate. * `skip` - Number of initial points to skip (for convergence). # Returns A vector of 2D points on the fractal. Invalid input or a nonfinite orbit yields an empty vector. Use [`AffineIfs`] to reuse preparation or choose a seed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_julia_set" => "Generates the data for a Julia set image. The Julia set is defined by iterating `z = z*z + c` for a fixed c parameter. Unlike the Mandelbrot set, c is constant and z varies. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `c` - The constant c parameter `(real, imaginary)`. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. # Returns A 2D vector where each element is the escape time. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::fractal_geometry_and_chaos::generate_julia_set; // Julia set for c = -0.4 + 0.6i let data = generate_julia_set(100, 100, (-2.0, 2.0), (-2.0, 2.0), (-0.4, 0.6), 50); assert_eq!(data.len(), 100); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_lorenz_attractor" => "Generates the points for a Lorenz attractor simulation. The Lorenz attractor is a set of chaotic solutions for a simplified model of atmospheric convection. This function numerically integrates the Lorenz system of differential equations to produce a sequence of points that trace out the attractor. The system is defined by: - dx/dt = σ(y - x) - dy/dt = x(ρ - z) - y - dz/dt = xy - βz Uses standard parameters: σ = 10, ρ = 28, β = 8/3 # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y, z)` coordinates. * `dt` - The time step for the integration. * `num_steps` - The number of integration steps to perform. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory of the attractor. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::fractal_geometry_and_chaos::generate_lorenz_attractor; let points = generate_lorenz_attractor((0.1, 0.0, 0.0), 0.01, 1000); assert_eq!(points.len(), 1000); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_lorenz_attractor_custom" => "Generates a Lorenz attractor with custom parameters. # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y, z)` coordinates. * `dt` - The time step for the integration. * `num_steps` - The number of integration steps. * `sigma`, `rho`, `beta` - The Lorenz system parameters. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_mandelbrot_set" => "Generates the data for a Mandelbrot set image. The Mandelbrot set is a fractal defined by the iteration `z = z*z + c`. Points `c` for which the iteration remains bounded form the set. This function computes the escape time for each point in a given region. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. # Returns A 2D vector where each element is the number of iterations it took for that point to escape. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::fractal_geometry_and_chaos::generate_mandelbrot_set; let data = generate_mandelbrot_set(100, 100, (-2.5, 1.0), (-1.5, 1.5), 50); assert_eq!(data.len(), 100); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_multibrot" => "Generates the Multibrot set with exponent d. The Multibrot set is a generalization where `z = z^d + c`. d=2 gives the standard Mandelbrot set. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `d` - The exponent (power). * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. # Returns A 2D vector where each element is the escape time.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_newton_fractal" => "Generates a Newton fractal for `z^3 - 1 = 0`. Newton's method applied to finding roots of `f(z) = z^3 - 1` produces a fractal pattern. Each pixel is colored based on which root it converges to. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. * `tolerance` - The convergence tolerance. # Returns A 2D vector where each element indicates which root (0, 1, or 2) was reached, or 3 if no convergence.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_rossler_attractor" => "Generates the Rossler attractor. The Rossler attractor is a system of three non-linear ordinary differential equations that exhibits chaotic dynamics. It is simpler than the Lorenz system. The system is defined by: - dx/dt = -y - z - dy/dt = x + ay - dz/dt = b + z(x - c) Common parameter values: a = 0.2, b = 0.2, c = 5.7 # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y, z)` coordinates. * `dt` - The time step for the integration. * `num_steps` - The number of integration steps. * `a`, `b`, `c` - The Rossler system parameters. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_generate_tinkerbell_map" => "Native Tinkerbell map in normalized dimensionless coordinates. Returns an N x 2 tensor under a finite path budget. Generates the Tinkerbell map trajectory. The Tinkerbell map is a two-dimensional iterated map: - x_{n+1} = `x_n^2` - `y_n^2` + `ax_n` + `by_n` - y_{n+1} = `2x_n`*`y_n` + `cx_n` + `dy_n` Classic parameter values: a = 0.9, b = -0.6013, c = 2.0, d = 0.5 # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y)` coordinates. * `num_steps` - The number of iterations. * `a`, `b`, `c`, `d` - The map parameters. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_julia_escape_time" => "Computes the escape time for a single point in a Julia set.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_logistic_bifurcation" => "Logistic bifurcation samples in normalized coordinates; transient and retained iterations share a finite work budget. Returns columns r and x. Generates data for a bifurcation diagram of the logistic map. For each value of r, the map is iterated and the final values after discarding transients are recorded. # Arguments * `r_range` - The range of r values `(r_min, r_max)`. * `num_r_values` - Number of r values to sample. * `transient` - Number of initial iterations to discard. * `num_points` - Number of points to record after transient. * `x0` - Initial x value. # Returns A `Vec` of `(r, x)` pairs for plotting the bifurcation diagram.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_logistic_map_iterate" => "Iterates the logistic map. The logistic map is defined by: x_{n+1} = r * `x_n` * (1 - `x_n`) It is a classic example of how complex behavior can arise from simple nonlinear dynamical equations. For r between 0 and 4, the dynamics range from stable fixed points to period doubling to chaos. # Arguments * `x0` - Initial value (should be between 0 and 1). * `r` - The growth rate parameter. * `num_steps` - The number of iterations. # Returns A `Vec` of x values representing the orbit.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_lyapunov_exponent_logistic" => "Computes the Lyapunov exponent for the logistic map. The Lyapunov exponent λ measures the rate of separation of infinitesimally close trajectories. For the logistic map: λ = lim (1/n) * sum(ln|r(1-2x_i)|) Positive λ indicates chaos. # Arguments * `r` - The growth rate parameter. * `x0` - Initial value. * `transient` - Number of iterations to discard. * `num_iterations` - Number of iterations for averaging. # Returns The estimated Lyapunov exponent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_lyapunov_exponent_lorenz" => "Computes the largest Lyapunov exponent for a 3D flow (e.g., Lorenz system). Uses the method of tracking the evolution of a small perturbation. # Arguments * `start_point` - Initial point `(x, y, z)`. * `dt` - Time step. * `num_steps` - Number of integration steps. * `sigma`, `rho`, `beta` - Lorenz system parameters. # Returns The estimated largest Lyapunov exponent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_mandelbrot_escape_time" => "Computes the escape time for a single point in the Mandelbrot set. # Arguments * `c_real` - Real part of the complex number c * `c_imag` - Imaginary part of the complex number c * `max_iter` - Maximum iterations # Returns The number of iterations before escape, or `max_iter` if the point is in the set.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_orbit_density" => "Normalized phase-space point histogram. N x 2 input points and bounded bin counts produce CSR integer rows (offsets/indices); entries are counts, preserving empty rows without padding. Computes the density distribution of orbit points in a 2D histogram. # Arguments * `points` - The orbit trajectory. * `x_bins` - Number of bins in x direction. * `y_bins` - Number of bins in y direction. * `x_range` - Range for x axis. * `y_range` - Range for y axis. # Returns A 2D vector of counts for each bin.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_orbit_entropy" => "Computes the entropy of an orbit from its density distribution. Uses Shannon entropy: H = -`sum(p_i` * `log(p_i)`) # Arguments * `density` - A 2D density histogram from `orbit_density`. # Returns The Shannon entropy of the distribution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_sierpinski_triangle_ifs" => "Creates the Sierpinski triangle IFS.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_c" => "Fresnel cosine integral `C(x) = ∫_0^x cos(π t² / 2) dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_erf_complex" => "Error function of a complex argument. Maclaurin series for `|z| < 1.5`, otherwise `1 - exp(-z²) w(i z)` after folding to `Re z >= 0` with `erf(-z) = -erf(z)`. The exponential is scaled with the Faddeeva factor near the floating-point range limits.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_faddeeva_w" => "The Faddeeva function `w(z) = exp(-z²) erfc(-i z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_fresnel_f" => "Auxiliary function `f(x)` of DLMF 7.2.10 (`≈ 1/(π x)` for large `x`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_fresnel_fg" => "Auxiliary functions `(f(x), g(x))` of DLMF 7.2.10: `f = (1/2 - S) cos θ - (1/2 - C) sin θ`, `g = (1/2 - C) cos θ + (1/2 - S) sin θ`, `θ = π x² / 2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_fresnel_g" => "Auxiliary function `g(x)` of DLMF 7.2.10 (`≈ 1/(π² x³)` for large `x`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_fresnel_sc" => "Fresnel integrals `(S(x), C(x))` together.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_fresnel_sc_complex" => "`(S(z), C(z))` for a complex argument `z = re + i·im`, returned as `((S.re, S.im), (C.re, C.im))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_s" => "Fresnel sine integral `S(x) = ∫_0^x sin(π t² / 2) dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_sc_interval" => "Returns the dimensionless Fresnel sine and cosine integrals from a to b as item0 and item1. Narrow intervals and far-field tails use direct differences. Infinite bounds give improper integrals. Reversing the bounds negates both results. Normalised Fresnel sine and cosine integrals `(S, C)` over `[a, b]`. Both integrands use the phase `π t² / 2`. Narrow intervals are integrated directly, and far from the origin the common endpoint limit cancels before rounding, so differences can survive even when the values from [`fresnel_sc`] round to the same number. Reversing the limits negates both results. Infinite limits are interpreted as improper integrals. Equal limits give zero, including equal infinities. A NaN limit gives two NaNs. ``` use rssn::kernels::fresnel::fresnel_sc_interval; let (s, c) = fresnel_sc_interval(1.0, 1.0_f64.next_up()); assert_eq!(s, f64::EPSILON); assert!(c < 0.0); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fresnel_sin_cos_half_pi_square" => "`(sin(π x² / 2), cos(π x² / 2))` with the phase reduced exactly.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fujiwara_root_bound" => "Fujiwara upper bound from ascending complex coefficients with a common ordinate dimension. The polynomial coordinate and the result are dimensionless. Fujiwara upper bound on the modulus of all polynomial roots: \$R = 2 \\\\max_{1 \\\\le k \\\\le n} \\\\left|\\\\frac{c_{n-k}}{c_n}\\\\right|^{1/k}\$ with factor \$1/2\$ on \$k=n\$.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_gram_schmidt" => "Performs the Gram-Schmidt process to orthogonalize a set of functions. All functions must have the same sampling points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_gram_schmidt_orthonormal" => "Performs the Gram-Schmidt process to orthonormalize a set of functions. # Errors Returns an error if any pair of input functions has mismatched sample points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_infinity_norm" => "Calculates the L-infinity norm of a function represented by discrete points. The L-infinity norm (or Chebyshev norm) is defined as `max(|f(x)|)`. For discrete points, it is simply the maximum absolute value among the sampled points. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the function's samples. # Returns The numerical value of the L-infinity norm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_inner_product" => "Calculates the inner product of two functions, `<f, g> = ∫f(x)g(x)dx`. Both functions must be sampled at the same x-coordinates. For discrete points, it is approximated as `Σ f(x_i)g(x_i)Δx_i`. # Arguments * `f_points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the first function's samples. * `g_points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the second function's samples. # Returns A `Result` containing the numerical value of the inner product. # Errors Returns an error if the input functions have different numbers of sample points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_l1_norm" => "Calculates the L1 norm of a function represented by discrete points. The L1 norm (or Manhattan norm) is defined as `∫|f(x)|dx`. For discrete points, it is approximated as `Σ|y_i|Δx_i`. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the function's samples. # Returns The numerical value of the L1 norm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_l2_grid_new" => "Prepares trapezoidal L2 weights for finite nondecreasing coordinates. The result can be reused for independent sampled fields on the same axis. Repeated coordinates contribute zero panel width. Prepares the weights for finite, nondecreasing coordinates. # Errors Returns an error for a nonfinite coordinate or a decrease.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_l2_grid_norm" => "Returns the sampled L2 norm, with dimension equal to the ordinates' dimension times the square root of the axis dimension. Returns a domain error if the norm overflows Float. Computes the sampled L2 norm using the prepared weights. # Errors Returns an error for the wrong number of ordinates or a nonfinite one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_l2_grid_norm_and_normalize" => "Returns {norm, normalized} from one reduction. The measured norm is None if it overflows Float. Normalized ordinates carry the inverse square root of the axis dimension and are None for zero measure. Normalization uses the unrounded scale, including when the norm underflows or overflows. Returns the L2 norm and normalized ordinates from one reduction. Zero measure leaves the ordinates as supplied. If the rounded norm overflows or underflows, normalization still uses its internal scale. # Errors Returns an error for the wrong number of ordinates or a nonfinite one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_l2_norm" => "Calculates the L2 norm of a function represented by discrete points. The L2 norm is `sqrt(∫|f(x)|²dx)`. For samples, the integral is the trapezoidal rule applied to the squared ordinates. On a finite nondecreasing grid, repeated coordinates give zero-width panels. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the function's samples. # Returns The numerical value of the L2 norm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_normalize" => "Normalizes a function relative to its L2 norm. Zero measure leaves the samples as supplied. Normalization uses the norm's internal scale even if the rounded norm is zero or infinite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_functional_project" => "Computes the projection of function `f` onto function `g`. Both functions must be sampled at the same x-coordinates.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_amr_cells_per_level" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Number of cells per level.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_amr_composite_l1_error" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. `L1` norm of `component(prim) - exact(x)` over the composite grid (every cell counted at its finest level).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_amr_config" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Default AMR parameters around a scheme: two levels, regrid every 4 steps, threshold 0.05, refluxing on.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_amr_primitive_on_level" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Primitive variables on the uniform grid of level `level` (coarser data are injected piecewise constantly).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_amr_totals" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Integrals of the conserved variables over the composite grid (every cell counted at its finest level).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_config_euler" => "Creates a ideal-gas Euler configuration with the dimensionless adiabatic exponent. The options record contains cfl, reconstruction and riemann. Riemann solver names are Hllc, Hlle, Roe and Rusanov. Hlle uses two Einfeldt wave speeds. Euler solver with all faces transmissive, HLLC + WENO5, CFL 0.4.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_config_shallow_water" => "Creates a shallow-water configuration with gravity in the frame’s acceleration reference. The options record contains cfl, reconstruction and riemann. Riemann solver names are Hllc, Hlle, Roe and Rusanov. Hlle uses two Einfeldt wave speeds. Shallow-water solver with all faces transmissive, HLLC + MUSCL(minmod).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_grid_cell_volume" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Cell volume.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_grid_centre" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Cell centre.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_grid_h" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Cell width along `axis`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_grid_ijk" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Cell multi-index.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_grid_index" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Flat index of cell `(i, j, k)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_grid_is_empty" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Whether there are no cells.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_grid_len" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Number of cells.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_grid_new" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Creates a grid. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid dimension, extent, spacing or storage size.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_l1_error" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Discrete L1 norm of `component(prim) - exact(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_primitive" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Primitive variables of cell `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_solve" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Runs the solver from primitive initial data to `t_end`. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid grid, an invalid time or CFL number, inconsistent periodic faces or inadmissible initial data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_solve_amr" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Runs the AMR solver from primitive initial data (evaluated at the cell centres of every level) to `t_end`. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions, a CFL number outside `(0, 1)`, an invalid grid or refinement extent, a non-finite or negative final time, non-admissible base initial data, or a step that cannot advance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_solve_with_sources" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Runs the solver with the source terms of [`Sources`]. Gravity (Euler) uses the well-balanced interface pressure described in the module documentation; a bed (shallow water) uses the hydrostatic reconstruction, which is well-balanced for the lake at rest. # Errors As [`solve`], and for gravity with shallow water, a bed or Manning friction with Euler, or a bed or Manning friction with shallow water in 3D.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_totals" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Integrals of the conserved variables over the domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_with_axis" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Sets both faces of an axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_fv_with_face" => "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Sets one face (`2 * axis + is_max`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_gamma" => "Numerical gamma function. Computes the gamma function, Γ(x). Γ(n) = (n-1)! for positive integers.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_gauss_legendre" => "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. `n`-point Gauss-Legendre quadrature of `f` on `[a, b]`.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_geometric_mean" => "Geometric mean of a sample. Computes the geometric mean of a slice of positive data. GM = (x1 * x2 * ... * xn)^(1/n)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_convex_hull_2d" => "The 2D convex hull of `points` in counter-clockwise order, via Andrew's monotone chain algorithm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_convex_hull_3d" => "The 3D convex hull of `points`, returned as outward-oriented triangular faces (indices into `points`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_delaunay_2d" => "2D Delaunay triangulation of `points`, returning triangles as `[usize; 3]` vertex indices in counter-clockwise order, via the Bowyer–Watson algorithm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_distance" => "The Euclidean distance between two points of the same dimension. # Errors [`GeometryError::ShapeMismatch`] when the dimensions differ.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_point_in_polygon" => "Returns `true` if `point` lies inside or on the boundary of `polygon`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_polygon_area" => "The area of a polygon given its vertices, by the shoelace formula. `points` is `[x0, y0, x1, y1, …]`; the result is non-negative and does not depend on the winding direction. Fewer than three vertices give zero. Ill-conditioned and extreme-scale inputs use the exact represented coordinates.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_ray_segment_intersection_2d" => "Intersection of a 2D ray `origin + t * dir` (`t >= 0`) with segment `[b1, b2]`. Returns `Some((t, point))` if they intersect.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_ray_triangle_intersection_3d" => "Intersection of a 3D ray `origin + t * dir` (`t >= 0`) with triangle `(v0, v1, v2)`, via the Möller–Trumbore algorithm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_segment_intersection_2d" => "Intersection point of two 2D line segments `[a1, a2]` and `[b1, b2]`, if any.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_segment_triangle_intersection_3d" => "Intersection of a 3D line segment `[p0, p1]` with triangle `(v0, v1, v2)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_geometry_tetrahedron_volume" => "The non-negative volume of a tetrahedron, from four vertices in `R^3`. Ill-conditioned and extreme-scale inputs use an exact determinant of the represented coordinates. Fewer than twelve coordinates give zero. Only the first twelve coordinates are used.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_config" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Rigid-wall configuration with unit density. # Errors For a bad dimension or sizes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_energy" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Acoustic energy `½ Σ (p²/K + ρ|v|²) ΔV`, the invariant of the conservative scheme (`K = ρc²` in the cells, `ρ` the face density).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_fields" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_index" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Flat cell index.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_n" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Cells per axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_new" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Creates the solver. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions or a PML that is too thick.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_rest" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Initialises `v^{-1/2}` from `p^0` for a start from rest (velocity zero at `t = 0`); call once after [`Acoustics::set_pressure`]. Not for PML runs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_run" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Runs `steps` steps.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_set_medium" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Sets a spatially varying medium from `x ↦ (c, ρ)` sampled at the cell centres, and re-derives [`Acoustics::dt`] and the PML from the result. The density on a face is the harmonic mean of the two cells it separates and the bulk modulus of a cell is `ρc²`, which is what keeps the scheme second order across an interface (an interface is exact when it lies on a face). Call this before stepping: the fields, the clock and the sources are left alone. # Errors A non-positive or non-finite `c` or `ρ` at a cell centre.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_set_pressure" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Sets the pressure from a function of the cell-centre position (velocity zero).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_source" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Adds a soft source `s(t)` to the pressure equation of a cell.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_acoustics_step" => "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. One leapfrog step (velocity, then pressure).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_apply_boundary_conditions_penalty" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Applies boundary conditions to a global stiffness matrix and force vector. Uses the penalty method for prescribed DOFs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_assemble_1d_stiffness_matrix" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. 1D global stiffness assembly (one DOF per node); alias of [`assemble_global_stiffness_matrix`] named like its 2D/3D siblings.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_assemble_2d_stiffness_matrix" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Assembles a 2D global stiffness matrix from triangular elements.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_assemble_3d_stiffness_matrix" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Assembles a 3D global stiffness matrix from tetrahedral elements (12 DOFs each, ordered `(u, v, w)` per node).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_assemble_global_stiffness_matrix" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Assembles the global stiffness matrix for a 1D structure composed of linear elements. This function takes a list of local stiffness matrices and their connectivity to construct the global stiffness matrix for the entire structure. # Arguments * `num_nodes` - The total number of nodes in the structure. * `elements` - A vector of tuples `(element_matrix, node1_idx, node2_idx)`, where `element_matrix` is the local stiffness matrix and `node1_idx`, `node2_idx` are the global indices of the nodes connected by this element. # Returns A `Matrix<f64>` representing the global stiffness matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_assemble_stiffness_matrix" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Assembles a global stiffness matrix from local matrices and DOF maps. `dof_map[i]` is the global DOF of local DOF `i`): the dimension-generic form of [`assemble_global_stiffness_matrix`] (1D bars, 2 DOFs), [`assemble_2d_stiffness_matrix`] (triangles, 6 DOFs) and [`assemble_3d_stiffness_matrix`] (tetrahedra, 12 DOFs). # Panics Panics if a local matrix is smaller than its DOF map or a DOF is out of range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_beam_global_stiffness_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the global stiffness matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_beam_local_stiffness_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the local stiffness matrix in local coordinates (6x6). DOFs: [u1, v1, θ1, u2, v2, θ2]\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_beam_mass_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the consistent mass matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_beam_new" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new beam element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_beam_transformation" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the transformation matrix from global to local coordinates.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_aca" => "Solves the interior Laplace problem on a closed mesh using ACA-compressed constant panels and GMRES. Supply one Potential or Flux coefficient per triangle, normalized by the frame's field or field-per-length reference. A pure-flux interior problem requires zero net outward flux and returns a zero area-weighted mean boundary potential. The mesh cap is 512 panels. Restart and iteration lengths are positive and capped at 128 and 4096. The positive tolerance, at most one, controls both ACA and GMRES. Invalid controls, incompatible fluxes and nonconvergence produce errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_compression" => "Returns the fraction of matrix entries stored by ACA relative to dense storage. One means that storing compressed blocks saved no entries. Requires a retained ACA solution. Stored fraction relative to dense storage.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_cylinder" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Scenario for 2D BEM: Simulates potential flow around a cylinder. This function sets up a circular boundary and applies boundary conditions corresponding to a uniform flow in the x-direction. It then uses the BEM solver to calculate the potential and flux on the cylinder's surface. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(u, q)` of potentials and fluxes on the cylinder surface, or an error string if the BEM system cannot be solved. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_laplace_bem_2d` function encounters an error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_element_new" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Creates a new 2D boundary element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_eval2" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Evaluates the potential at an internal point in the domain after solving the boundary. # Arguments * `point` - The `(x, y)` coordinate of the internal point. * `elements` - The boundary elements. * `u` - The solved boundary potentials. * `q` - The solved boundary fluxes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_eval3" => "Evaluates a retained interior constant-panel Laplace solution. Supply an interior point as three coordinates normalized by the frame's Length reference. The result is a measured potential in the field dimension. Accuracy decreases near the boundary. Exterior and vertex-coefficient solutions require different evaluators and are rejected.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_eval3_exterior" => "Evaluates a retained exterior constant-panel Laplace solution. Supply a point outside the closed body as three coordinates normalized by the frame's Length reference. The result is a measured potential in the field dimension. Accuracy decreases near the boundary. Interior and vertex-coefficient solutions require different evaluators and are rejected.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_exterior" => "Solves the exterior Laplace problem with decay at infinity using ACA-compressed constant panels and GMRES. Supply one Potential or Flux coefficient per triangle, normalized by the frame's field or field-per-length reference. Flux follows the body's outward normal into the exterior. Dirichlet, Neumann and mixed data are accepted. The mesh cap is 512 panels. Restart and iteration lengths are positive and capped at 128 and 4096. The positive tolerance, at most one, controls both ACA and GMRES. Invalid controls and nonconvergence produce errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_area" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Area of triangle `t`, if it exists.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_centroid" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Centroid of triangle `t` (the collocation point), if it exists.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_corners" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. The three corners of triangle `t`, or `None` if out of range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_cube" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. An axis-aligned cube of edge length `side` centred at `center`, each face split into `n x n` squares of two triangles (`12 n^2` triangles in total), with outward orientation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_icosphere" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. A geodesic sphere: the icosahedron with every triangle split into four `subdivisions` times (`20 * 4^subdivisions` triangles), vertices projected onto the sphere.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_new" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Checks that the mesh is a closed, consistently oriented surface with non-degenerate triangles and positive enclosed volume. # Errors Describes the first defect found.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_normal" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Outward unit normal of triangle `t`, if it exists and is not degenerate.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_total_area" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Total surface area.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_validate" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Checks that the mesh is a closed, consistently oriented surface with non-degenerate triangles and positive enclosed volume. # Errors Describes the first defect found.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_mesh_volume" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Enclosed volume (divergence theorem; positive for outward orientation).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_p1" => "Solves the interior or exterior Laplace problem with linear elements. Each vertex has one Potential or Flux coefficient in the supplied frame's field or field-per-length reference. Use the boundary-field accessor for the resulting vertex coefficients. Solves the interior Laplace problem with **continuous piecewise-linear** potential and flux on the triangulated surface, by collocation at the vertices (dense LU). `bcs` gives the data per **vertex**: the potential or the derivative along the outward normal. Single-layer integrals use adaptive subdivision away from the collocation point and a Duffy transformation for the elements that contain it; the double-layer kernel vanishes on those (planar) elements, and the free-term/diagonal is fixed by the rigid-body identity `Σ_j H_ij = 0`. Exterior problems: pass `exterior = true` (orientation reversed and the flux sign convention of [`solve_laplace_bem_3d_exterior`]). # Errors For an invalid mesh, wrong data length, or a singular system (pure Neumann interior problems are singular).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_p1_aca" => "Solves the interior or exterior Laplace problem with ACA-compressed linear elements and GMRES. Each vertex has one Potential or Flux coefficient in the supplied frame's field or field-per-length reference. The positive tolerance, at most one, controls both ACA and GMRES. Solves the Laplace problem with **linear (P1) elements** like [`solve_laplace_bem_3d_p1`], but with hierarchical-matrix compression. The vertex collocation matrices `G` and `H` are assembled lazily from the element integrals (adaptive subdivision away from the collocation point, Duffy transformation on elements containing it), far blocks of the vertex cluster tree are compressed by adaptive cross approximation, the rigid-body diagonal of `H` follows from one compressed matrix-vector product with the vector of ones, and the system is solved by Jacobi-preconditioned GMRES. # Errors For an invalid mesh, wrong data length, non-finite data, a pure Neumann interior problem (singular) or GMRES failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_plan_panel_count" => "Returns the number of boundary values required by a prepared three-dimensional Laplace BEM plan. Number of panels and prescribed values in each solve.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_prepare3" => "Prepares an interior three-dimensional Laplace boundary-element solve and stores the constant-panel collocation operator and LU factors. Supply Potential or Flux for each triangle in mesh order. The plan retains the mesh's reference frame. Dense storage is quadratic in the panel count. Factors the system for the supplied trianglewise boundary kinds. # Errors Rejects invalid meshes, a mismatched number of kinds, singular systems and non-finite boundary operators. Reports cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_selfcheck3" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Self-check of the 3D BEM: solves the Dirichlet problem `u = x` on a 320-triangle unit icosphere with [`solve_laplace_bem_3d_mesh`] and verifies the recovered flux against the exact `q = n_x`. This is the former argument-less entry point; the general solver is [`solve_laplace_bem_3d_mesh`]. # Errors Returns an error if the solve fails or the flux error exceeds 5 %.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_solution_fields" => "Returns normalized potential and flux arrays in panel order for constant elements or vertex order for linear elements. The record carries a measured field reference for potential and a field-per-length reference for flux.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_solve2" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Solves a 2D Laplace problem (e.g., potential flow, steady-state heat conduction) using the Boundary Element Method (BEM) with constant elements. This function discretizes the boundary of the domain into elements and applies boundary conditions to solve for unknown potentials or fluxes on the boundary. # Arguments * `points` - A `Vec` of `(x, y)` tuples defining the vertices of the boundary polygon. * `bcs` - A `Vec` of `BoundaryCondition` for each element, specifying known potential or flux. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(u, q)`, where `u` is a `Vec<f64>` of potentials and `q` is a `Vec<f64>` of normal fluxes on each element. Returns an `Err` string if the system is ill-posed or has no unique solution. # Errors This function will return an error if the number of points and boundary conditions do not match, or if the BEM system has no unique solution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_solve3" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Solves the 3D Laplace equation inside a closed triangulated surface by the collocation Boundary Element Method with constant elements. The potential `u` and flux `q = du/dn` (outward normal) are piecewise constant on the triangles and collocated at their centroids. Per element, `bcs` prescribes either the potential (Dirichlet) or the flux (Neumann); the other quantity is solved for. The discretised boundary integral equation is `sum_j Hhat_ij u_j = sum_j G_ij q_j`, with the single-layer kernel `G = 1 / (4 pi r)` integrated over each triangle by adaptive subdivision with a 7-point Gauss rule (the singular self term analytically), the double-layer coefficients `H_ij = -Omega_ij / (4 pi)` given exactly by the solid angle subtended by element `j` at the collocation point `i`, and the free-term diagonal fixed by the rigid-body-motion identity `sum_j Hhat_ij = 0`. With Neumann data on every element the potential is determined only up to an additive constant; it is then fixed by the condition that the area-weighted mean of `u` vanishes, and the fluxes must satisfy the compatibility condition `sum_j q_j A_j = 0` to within `1e-6` of the flux scale. For repeated values with the same mesh and boundary kinds, use [`PreparedLaplaceBem3d`]. # Errors Fails for an invalid mesh (see [`SurfaceMesh3D::validate`]), a number of boundary conditions different from the number of triangles, non-finite data, an incompatible pure-Neumann problem, or a singular system. Reports cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_solve3_prepared" => "Solves for new per-triangle boundary values using prepared Laplace operators. Potentials are expressed in the mesh's field reference and fluxes in field per length. A pure-flux solve requires zero net outward flux and uses a zero area-weighted mean boundary potential. The returned solution works with the ordinary field and interior evaluation operators. Solves for new values in mesh triangle order, using the fixed boundary kinds. # Errors Rejects a mismatched number of values, non-finite data or an incompatible pure-Neumann problem. Reports cancellation or a non-finite solution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_vector2_new" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Creates a new 2D vector.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_vector2_norm" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Calculates the norm of the vector.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_vector3_new" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Creates a new 3D vector.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_bem_vector3_norm" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Calculates the norm of the vector.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_boundary_from_datum" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_boundary_is_dirichlet" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Whether this is a Dirichlet condition.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_boundary_is_periodic" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Whether this is periodic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_boundary_kind" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_cheb_diff_matrix" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Chebyshev differentiation matrix on `x_i = -cos(π i / n)` scaled to an interval of length `len`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_chebyshev_coord" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Coordinates of flat index `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_chebyshev_heat" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Integrates `u_t = α Δu + s(t, x)` with the θ-method (`θ` in `[1/2, 1]`). # Errors For unsupported boundary types or a singular system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_chebyshev_max_error" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Largest error against an exact function.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_chebyshev_new" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Creates a grid with the given degrees and bounds. # Errors For bad dimensions or degrees below 2.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_chebyshev_node" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Node coordinate along an axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_chebyshev_sample" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Samples a function at all nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_chebyshev_size" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Number of unknowns.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_chebyshev_solve_helmholtz" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Solves `-Δu + σ u = f` with the given boundary conditions. # Errors For unsupported boundary types or a singular system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_cnm_heat_nd" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Crank–Nicolson (θ = 1/2) heat equation on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. Any face conditions of [`crate::sim::boundary`] are allowed (Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, periodic, time-dependent data); data are stored with `x` fastest. # Errors For inconsistent sizes or boundary descriptions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_cnm_schrodinger_1d" => "Native nondimensional Crank-Nicolson Schrodinger equation i*psi_t=-Delta(psi)/2+V*psi. The explicit frame restores kinetic coefficient L²/(2T), potential frequency 1/T and complex amplitude units; it does not silently choose SI hbar or mass. Native spacing, method, layout and work bounds are retained. Solves the 1D Schrödinger equation i * `psi_t` = -0.5 * `psi_xx` + V * psi using the Crank-Nicolson method. # Arguments * `psi_initial` - The initial wave function (complex). * `v` - The potential energy distribution (real). * `dx` - The spatial step size. * `dt` - The time step. * `steps` - The number of time steps.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_cnm_schrodinger_2d" => "Native nondimensional Crank-Nicolson Schrodinger equation i*psi_t=-Delta(psi)/2+V*psi. The explicit frame restores kinetic coefficient L²/(2T), potential frequency 1/T and complex amplitude units; it does not silently choose SI hbar or mass. Native spacing, method, layout and work bounds are retained. 2D Schrodinger equation `i psi_t = -1/2 Laplacian psi + V psi`. Zero Dirichlet, by the Cayley/Crank-Nicolson split of [`solve_schrodinger_cn_nd`]; `x` fastest. 2D counterpart of [`solve_schrodinger_1d_cn`]; conserves the norm. # Errors For inconsistent lengths or a non-positive step.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_cnm_schrodinger_3d" => "Native nondimensional Crank-Nicolson Schrodinger equation i*psi_t=-Delta(psi)/2+V*psi. The explicit frame restores kinetic coefficient L²/(2T), potential frequency 1/T and complex amplitude units; it does not silently choose SI hbar or mass. Native spacing, method, layout and work bounds are retained. 3D Schrodinger equation by the Cayley/Crank-Nicolson split; see [`solve_schrodinger_2d_cn`]. # Errors As [`solve_schrodinger_2d_cn`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_cnm_solve_heat_equation_2d_cn_adi" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Solves the 2D heat equation `u_t` = D * (`u_xx` + `u_yy`) using the ADI method. Uses homogeneous Dirichlet boundaries from the first half-step. With zero steps, returns the initial values verbatim. Grid data is x-fastest. # Panics Panics for a positive-step nonempty grid whose extents overflow or exceed the supplied data length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_cnm_solve_heat_equation_2d_cn_adi_fixed_boundary" => "Advances a 2D heat field with Crank–Nicolson ADI. The x-fastest initial field provides all four fixed Dirichlet edges, which retain their values throughout the solve. Supply spacing in units of length_reference, diffusivity in length_reference^2/time_reference, the time step in time_reference, and temperature in value_reference. The returned field keeps its grid references. Evolves a two-dimensional heat field with fixed Dirichlet boundaries. The x-fastest initial field supplies the four boundary edges. They remain fixed during both ADI half-steps. The grid has `nx * ny` nodes, including the boundaries. Spacings must be positive, and diffusivity and the time step must be nonnegative. A grid without interior nodes is stationary. # Errors Returns an error for shape mismatches, non-finite data or results, invalid physical parameters, unrepresentable coefficients, or cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_cnm_solve_heat_equation_3d_cn_adi" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. 3D heat equation `u_t = D (u_xx + u_yy + u_zz)` by Crank-Nicolson. Uses approximate factorisation (ADI): three tridiagonal sweeps per step, unconditionally stable, second order, zero Dirichlet boundaries. `x` is the fastest index. 3D counterpart of [`solve_heat_equation_2d_cn_adi`]. # Errors For a wrong array length or non-positive step sizes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_compute_element_strain" => "Native normalized strain-coordinate contraction; B and displacement columns use one explicitly normalized coordinate basis, returning dimensionless strain coordinates. Shapes and work are bounded. Calculates element strains from nodal displacements.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_create_rectangular_mesh" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Creates a simple rectangular mesh of triangular elements. Returns (nodes, elements) where elements are node indices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_datum_constant" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Constant datum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_datum_eval" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Evaluates the datum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_datum_space" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Space-dependent datum `g(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_datum_spacetime" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Space-time datum `g(t, x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_datum_time" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Time-dependent datum `g(t)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_dimensional_advance_transport_nd" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Like [`solve_transport_nd`] but returns only the final state (no snapshot history, so memory stays `O(n)`). # Errors As [`solve_transport_nd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_dimensional_heat_adi" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Crank-Nicolson heat equation `u_t = D Δu` with zero Dirichlet boundaries on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid, advanced `steps` steps of size `dt`. The step is the factorised (Beam-Warming) approximate-factorisation form `Π_a (I - ρ_a δ²_a) uⁿ⁺¹ = Π_a (I + ρ_a δ²_a) uⁿ` with `ρ_a = D dt / (2 h_a²)`: unconditionally stable, second order in space and time, one tridiagonal solve per line and axis. For data that is a product of functions of the single axes it equals the product of the 1D Crank-Nicolson solutions. Boundary entries of `u0` are ignored (treated as zero). # Errors For inconsistent shapes, non-positive `dt`, negative diffusivity, or an unrepresentable diffusion coefficient at the given spacing and time step. A cancelled computation also returns an error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_dimensional_poisson" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Solves `∇²u = f` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid by a stationary iteration. `u0` is the initial guess; its boundary nodes are the (fixed) Dirichlet data. Iteration stops when the largest update is below `tolerance` or after `max_iter` sweeps. With a 1D grid this is the scalar tridiagonal iteration, with `dims = [nx, ny]` it reproduces [`crate::sim::physics_cfd::solve_poisson_2d_jacobi`] and friends. # Errors For inconsistent shapes, spacings or lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_dimensional_schrodinger" => "Native nondimensional Crank-Nicolson Schrodinger equation i*psi_t=-Delta(psi)/2+V*psi. The explicit frame restores kinetic coefficient L²/(2T), potential frequency 1/T and complex amplitude units; it does not silently choose SI hbar or mass. Native spacing, method, layout and work bounds are retained. Schrodinger equation `i ψ_t = -½ Δψ + V ψ` (ħ = m = 1) with zero Dirichlet boundaries on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. One step is the symmetric splitting `e^{-iV dt/2} · Π_a C_a · e^{-iV dt/2}` where `C_a = (I + i dt/2 K_a)⁻¹ (I - i dt/2 K_a)` is the Crank-Nicolson (Cayley) step of the one-dimensional kinetic operator `K_a = -½ δ²_a`. The kinetic factors commute, every factor is unitary, so the scheme is second order in space and time and conserves the norm to round-off. For `V = 0` and separable data it equals the product of 1D Crank-Nicolson solutions. # Errors For inconsistent shapes, non-positive `dt`, or cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_dimensional_solve_transport_nd" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Advances a [`Transport`] equation `steps` explicit Euler steps on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid and returns all `steps + 1` snapshots (the first is `u0`). The scheme is only conditionally stable: keep `Σ|c_a| dt/h_a <= 1` and `2 α dt Σ 1/h_a² <= 1`. # Errors For inconsistent shapes, a velocity list of the wrong length, invalid time controls, too many snapshots, or cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_dimensional_solve_transport_sampled_nd" => "Advance the retained transport equation and record step 0, positive multiples of save_every, and the final step. Data, spacings, and time steps are normalized in the equation's Length/Time/field frame. Returns x-fastest fields as rows with their physical reference. Work and retained history are bounded independently. Advances a [`Transport`] equation and retains snapshots at a fixed step cadence. The result holds step 0, every positive multiple of `save_every` through `steps`, and the final step if it is off cadence. The initial and final state share one snapshot when `steps` is zero. Boundary and stability conventions are those of [`solve_transport_nd`]. # Errors For an inconsistent grid or equation, zero cadence, or an unrepresentable snapshot count, or cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_dimensional_wave" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Explicit leapfrog for `u_tt = c² Δu` with zero Dirichlet boundaries on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. Returns the `steps + 1` snapshots (`u0`, `u1`, ...), the first step using the initial velocity `ut0` (Taylor start). # Errors For inconsistent shapes, an unrepresentable snapshot count, a nonfinite speed or invalid time step, cancellation, or a violated CFL condition `|c| dt sqrt(Σ 1/h_a²) <= 1` (with a `10⁻¹²` rounding allowance).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_dirichlet" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Homogeneous or constant Dirichlet condition.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_bcs" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_clamped" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Fully clamped boundary.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_constitutive" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Elasticity matrix in Voigt notation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_displacement" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Displacement vector at dof `d`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_eval" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Displacement at a point (point location by search).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_roller" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Constant normal-pressure traction `σ n = -p n` is built by the caller from `n`; this helper fixes only one displacement component to zero (a roller).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_solve" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Solves the static problem. # Errors If the mesh dimension does not match the model, a displacement-free body (singular system) or an unsupported boundary type is met.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_solve_heterogeneous" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Solves the static problem with a spatially varying material. `E(x)` and `ν(x)` are evaluated at every quadrature point; the per-cell stress recovery ([`ElasticSolution::stress_at_cell`]) uses the constitutive matrix at the cell centroid. A constant material reproduces [`solve`] to round-off. # Errors As [`solve`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_stress" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Stress (Voigt) at the centroid of a cell, using that cell's constitutive matrix (heterogeneous material) or the homogeneous one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_traction" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Constant traction.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_elastic_von_mises" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Von Mises equivalent of a Voigt stress (2D: plane-stress form with `σ_zz = 0`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_etdrk4" => "Native ETDRK4 with a genuinely coupled compiled nonlinear map: two scalar programs per complex mode (real then imaginary), each taking coherent [time,re0,im0,...]. At most31 modes fit the compiled ABI's63 inputs. Linear symbols are normalized inverse-time coefficients; nonlinear outputs must have field/time units. Native x-fastest spectral extent ordering and exact ETDRK4 stepping are preserved. Fourth-order ETDRK4 time stepping of `u_t = L u + N(u, t)` in the spectral domain. Integrates `u_t = L u + N(u, t)` in the spectral domain with the fourth-order exponential time differencing Runge–Kutta scheme of Cox and Matthews (contour-integral coefficients of Kassam and Trefethen). `lin[p]` is the (diagonal) linear symbol; `nonlinear(t, uh)` returns the spectral transform of `N`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_face_axis" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Axis index 0, 1 or 2.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_face_index" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Storage index `2 * axis + is_max`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_face_is_max" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Whether this is the upper face of its axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_face_of_axis" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Face of `axis` (lower or upper).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_faces_axis_periodic" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Whether `axis` is periodic (both faces must agree). # Errors If `axis` is out of range or only one face of the axis is periodic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_faces_dirichlet_zero" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Homogeneous Dirichlet on every face.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_faces_get" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Condition on a face.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_faces_homogeneous" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. The same description with all boundary data set to zero (used for error / residual equations in multigrid).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_faces_uniform" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. The same condition on every face (periodic is allowed).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_faces_with" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Sets the condition on one face.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_faces_with_axis" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Sets the condition on both faces of an axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm1_boundary_periodic" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Periodic boundary conditions on `[0, L)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm1_boundary_uniform" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Uniform boundary condition on both ends.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm1_is_empty" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. True if `nx == 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm1_len" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Number of grid points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm2_boundary_periodic" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Doubly periodic boundary conditions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm2_boundary_uniform" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Same boundary condition on all four faces.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm2_is_empty" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. True when the grid has no points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm2_len" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. The number of points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm3_boundary_periodic" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Triply periodic boundary conditions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm3_boundary_uniform" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Same boundary condition on all six faces.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm3_field_at" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Returns the value at grid node `(x, y, z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm3_field_new" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm3_index" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Flat index `(z * ny + y) * nx + x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm3_is_empty" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. True if empty.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm3_len" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Total number of grid points `nx * ny * nz`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_adaptive_evolve" => "Actual generic native adaptive controller over caller-provided compiled step/stability/event functions. Each scalar step output takes coherent [time,step,state...] and has field units; stability takes [time,state...] and returns Time, optional event returns dimensionless. Controls/state arrays are normalized in the explicit frame; order is the supplied stepper's actual order. Native step doubling, rejection/work budgets and terminal-event localization are preserved. Adapt any restartable PDE step kernel with a step-doubling error estimate. `order` is the selected method's temporal order. `absolute_tolerances` has one positive entry per state coefficient. `stability` returns the maximum stable step for the current state, or positive infinity for an implicit scheme. Temporal control cannot certify spatial discretization error. An accepted endpoint on the event uses its existing validated state. # Errors Invalid controls or state shape, exhausted budgets, underflow, a failed step kernel, or a non-finite event. Tolerance violations are never force-accepted.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_condition" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_faces_new" => "Native per-face constant/profile FDM boundary construction from exactly2/4/6 scalar condition capabilities. Every condition must use the same physical reference frame; solver calls validate profile face lengths and native paired periodicity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_field_data" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_grid" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_history_at" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_history_data" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_history_len" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_advection" => "Original native solve_advection: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves advection with the first-order upwind scheme. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] when the field does not fit, and [`FdmError::Domain`] when the CFL condition fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_advection_1d" => "Original native solve_advection_1d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves 1D linear advection `u_t + v u_x = 0` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if `|v| dt / h > 1`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len() != grid.nx`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_advection_3d" => "Original native solve_advection_3d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves 3D linear advection `u_t + v·∇u = 0` on `grid` with velocity `(vx, vy, vz)` and boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if `(|vx| + |vy| + |vz|) dt / h > 1`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len() != grid.len()`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_advection_with_bc" => "Original native solve_advection_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves 2D linear advection `u_t + v·∇u = 0` with general boundary conditions `bc` (including `Periodic`, `Dirichlet`, and outflow `Neumann`). # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if `(|vx| + |vy|) dt / h > 1`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `spec.initial.len() != spec.grid.len()`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_heat" => "Original native solve_heat: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the heat equation with an explicit scheme, returning every snapshot including the initial one. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] when the initial field does not fit, and [`FdmError::Domain`] when `α dt / h²` exceeds the stability limit.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_heat_1d" => "Original native solve_heat_1d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D heat equation `u_t = \\\\alpha u_{xx}` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc` and time-stepping `stepping` (`Explicit`, `BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, or `Adi`). Implicit schemes (`BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, `Adi`) are unconditionally stable for any `\\\\Delta t > 0`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid parameters or unstable explicit step, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len() != grid.nx`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_heat_3d" => "Original native solve_heat_3d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solve the three-dimensional heat equation with prescribed face data. Solves the 3D heat equation `u_t = \\\\alpha ∇²u` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc` and time-stepping `stepping` (`Explicit`, `BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, or `Adi` Douglas–Gunn 3D Alternating Direction Implicit). # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid parameters or unstable explicit step (`r > 1/6`), [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len() != grid.len()`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_heat_with_bc" => "Original native solve_heat_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solve the two-dimensional heat equation with prescribed face data. Solves the 2D heat equation `u_t = \\\\alpha ∇²u` with general boundary conditions `bc` and time-stepping `stepping` (`Explicit`, `BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, or `Adi` Peaceman–Rachford Alternating Direction Implicit). `BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, and `Adi` are unconditionally stable for arbitrarily large diffusion Courant numbers `\\\\alpha \\\\Delta t / h^2`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid, BCs, or unstable explicit CFL, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `spec.initial.len() != spec.grid.len()`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_poisson" => "Original native solve_poisson: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves `∇²u = f` by successive over-relaxation. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] when the source does not fit the grid, and [`FdmError::NotConverged`] when the sweep limit is reached.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_poisson_1d" => "Original native solve_poisson_1d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D Poisson equation `u_{xx} = source` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc` using direct `O(N)` tridiagonal / cyclic-tridiagonal elimination. Note: Pure Neumann or Periodic problems with zero reaction have a constant nullspace; in that case the first node is pinned to zero mean. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or boundary conditions, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `source.len() != grid.nx`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_poisson_3d" => "Original native solve_poisson_3d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 3D Poisson equation `∇²u = source` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc` using Successive Over-Relaxation (SOR). # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or BCs, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `source.len() != grid.len()`, and [`FdmError::NotConverged`] if `iterations` is exceeded.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_poisson_with_bc" => "Original native solve_poisson_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 2D Poisson equation `∇²u = spec.source` with general boundary conditions `bc` (Dirichlet, non-zero Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, or Periodic). # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or BCs, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `spec.source.len() != spec.grid.len()`, and [`FdmError::NotConverged`] if SOR does not converge within `spec.iterations`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_wave" => "Original native solve_wave: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the wave equation by leapfrog with zero Dirichlet boundaries. Returns the initial field with zeroed boundaries and each of the `steps` subsequent fields. Initial velocities on the boundary are ignored. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] when a field does not fit, and [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or evolution parameters, including a CFL violation or an unrepresentable snapshot count.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_wave_1d" => "Original native solve_wave_1d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D wave equation `u_{tt} = c^2 u_{xx}` using the leapfrog scheme with boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if CFL `|c| dt / h > 1`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial` or `velocity` does not match `grid.nx`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_wave_3d" => "Original native solve_wave_3d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 3D wave equation `u_{tt} = c^2 ∇²u` on `grid` using the leapfrog scheme. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if CFL `|c| dt / h > 1/\\\\sqrt{3}`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial` or `velocity` does not match `grid.len()`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_wave_3d_with_bc" => "Original native solve_wave_3d_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Three-dimensional leapfrog wave solver with Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, or periodic faces. # Errors Invalid finite inputs, incompatible field dimensions, or unstable Courant number.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_solve_wave_with_bc" => "Original native solve_wave_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 2D wave equation `u_{tt} = c^2 ∇²u` with general boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for an invalid time step, CFL `|c| dt / h > 1/\\\\sqrt{2}`, or too many snapshots. [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial` or `velocity` does not match `spec.grid.len()`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_zero_dirichlet" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Homogeneous Dirichlet boundary condition (`u = 0`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fdm_zero_neumann" => "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Homogeneous Neumann (insulating / reflective) boundary condition (`\\\\partial u / \\\\partial n = 0`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fea_mesh_data" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_cartesian_nodal_shape" => "Returns the nodal shape, including the boundary, in x, y and optional z order. Nodal shape in x, y and optional z order, including the boundary.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_cartesian_prepare" => "Prepares the tensor-product Q1 finite-element Poisson operator on a two- or three-dimensional unit grid. Element counts may differ by axis. The physical domain uses the frame's length reference and all boundary values are zero. Prepares a grid with two or three positive element counts. Coordinates and output storage use x-fast order on the unit domain. # Errors Rejects invalid extents and grids too large for addressable storage.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_cartesian_solve_prepared" => "Solves the prepared Poisson equation for a new compiled source. Each quadrature point needs one evaluation from measured Length coordinates to the field divided by Length squared. Result data are normalized by value_reference and use x-fast order. Solves for a new source. The callback runs once per element quadrature point. # Errors Reports non-finite source values, cancellation and unrepresentable results.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_constant_problem" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_error_indicators" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Residual error indicators `η_T`, one per cell, of a P1 or P2 solution. `η_T² = h_T² ∫_T (f(t,·) - c u_h + ∇·(k ∇u_h))² dx + Σ_{e ⊂ ∂T} ½ h_e ∫_e [k ∂_n u_h]² ds`, with `h_T` and `h_e` the diameters of the cell and of its faces. The face sum runs over interior faces only, so the estimator measures the discretisation error of the differential equation, not the approximation of non-homogeneous boundary data. For P1 the element term is `f - c u_h` (`∇·(k ∇u_h) = 0`); for P2 the constant `k Δu_h` is included. In 1D the face term is empty (the jump lives on a set of measure zero).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_error_indicators_var" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. [`error_indicators`] for spatially varying `k(x)`, `c(x)`: `η_T² = h_T² ∫_T (f - c(x) u_h + ∇·(k ∇u_h))² + Σ_e ½ h_e ∫_e [k(x) ∂_n u_h]²`, with `∇·(k ∇u_h) = k Δu_h + ∇k · ∇u_h` (the gradient of `k` by central differences of the closure at the quadrature points, nominal step `10⁻⁶ h_T`) and `k` evaluated at the quadrature points of the face in the jump. At floating- point limits the difference uses distinct finite representable coordinates and their actual spacing.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_eval" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Evaluates a finite element function at `x` (brute-force point location).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_heat_step" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Advance nodal finite-element heat data by one theta-method step. Uses the existing consistent mass/stiffness assembly and boundary load machinery at both physical times; no reinterpolation of the state occurs. # Errors Invalid state/control data, incompatible boundary conditions, or failed linear solve.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_heat_step_nodal_source" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Advance FEM heat data with additional nodal source values at both times. The source is interpolated in the existing finite-element basis and mapped to a load by the consistent mass matrix, preserving the theta method. # Errors Invalid nodal source/state data, controls, boundary data, or linear solve.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_interpolate" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Interpolates a function at the dofs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_l2_error" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. `L2` error against an exact function.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_legacy_scenario1" => "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Example scenario for the 1D FEM Poisson solver. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_poisson_1d` function encounters an error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_legacy_scenario2" => "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Example scenario for the 2D FEM Poisson solver. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_poisson_2d` function encounters an error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_legacy_scenario3" => "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Example scenario for the 3D FEM Poisson solver. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_poisson_3d` function encounters an error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_legacy_simplex" => "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Poisson/reaction–diffusion `-Δu + c u = f` on interval, triangle or tetrahedron meshes with P1/P2 elements and marker boundary conditions. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions or a singular system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_legacy_solve1" => "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Solves the 1D Poisson equation: -d^2u/dx^2 = f(x) with Dirichlet boundary conditions u(0) = 0, u(L) = 0. # Arguments * `n_elements` - Number of linear elements. * `domain_length` - The length L of the domain. * `force_fn` - The forcing function f(x). # Returns A vector representing the solution u at each node. # Errors This function will return an error if `n_elements` is 0, or if the underlying linear system solver fails to converge or encounters numerical instability.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_legacy_solve2" => "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Solves the 2D Poisson equation on a unit square with zero Dirichlet boundaries. Uses the same tensor-product Q1 stiffness assembled from bilinear or trilinear elements, diagonalized by the discrete sine basis. # Errors Reports invalid extents, non-finite source values, cancellation or an unrepresentable result.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_legacy_solve3" => "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Solves the 3D Poisson equation on a unit cube with zero Dirichlet boundaries. Uses the same tensor-product Q1 stiffness assembled from bilinear or trilinear elements, diagonalized by the discrete sine basis. # Errors Reports invalid extents, non-finite source values, cancellation or an unrepresentable result.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_residual_estimator_indicators" => "Evaluates normalized cell residual indicators using prepared geometry and a constant-coefficient FEM problem in the same reference frame. Time and solution values are normalized; the indicator reference is U L^((d+2)/2) / T for mesh dimension d. Values follow the source space DOF order. Computes the cell indicators for constant coefficients. # Errors Returns an error when the solution length differs from the space size.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_residual_estimator_indicators_var" => "Evaluates normalized cell residual indicators using prepared geometry and a variable-coefficient FEM problem in the same reference frame. Diffusivity must be positive at every sampled coordinate. The indicator reference is U L^((d+2)/2) / T for mesh dimension d. Computes the cell indicators for spatially varying coefficients. # Errors Returns an error when the solution length differs from the space size.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_residual_estimator_new" => "Prepares residual error estimation on a fixed P1 or P2 simplex space. The immutable estimator retains its own cell geometry, interior face adjacency and reference quadrature. The source space can be released after preparation. Prepares cell geometry and interior face adjacency. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid space, degenerate cells, or geometry whose edge differences, diameters or barycentric gradients cannot be represented in f64.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_solve_adaptive" => "Actual native spatial Dörfler refinement, independently from temporal adaptivity. Positive Length/Time/field references determine coefficient/source units and residual-indicator reference. A conservative worst-case refinement/DOF bound is checked before native allocation; actual returned space, field and estimator history are retained. Adaptive solution of the static problem: solve, estimate, mark and refine. Starting from `mesh`, the loop solves `-∇·(k ∇u) + c u = f(t, x)` with [`solve_static`], estimates the error with [`error_indicators`], marks the smallest set of cells whose indicators carry the fraction `theta` of `Σ η_T²` (Dörfler marking), refines them with [`SimplexMesh::refine_marked`] and repeats. It stops when the total estimate is at most `tolerance` or after `max_steps` refinements (so at most `max_steps + 1` solves). `degree` selects P1 or P2. # Errors If `degree` is not 1 or 2, `theta` is outside `(0, 1]`, `max_steps` is zero, or one of the solves fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_solve_adaptive_var" => "Actual native spatial Dörfler refinement, independently from temporal adaptivity. Positive Length/Time/field references determine coefficient/source units and residual-indicator reference. A conservative worst-case refinement/DOF bound is checked before native allocation; actual returned space, field and estimator history are retained. [`solve_adaptive`] with spatially varying `k(x)`, `c(x)` ([`error_indicators_var`]). # Errors As [`solve_adaptive`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_solve_heat" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Integrates `u_t - ∇·(k ∇u) + c u = f` with the θ-method (`θ` in `[1/2, 1]`). # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions, a singular system or an invalid `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_solve_heat_var" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. [`solve_heat`] with spatially varying `k(x)` and `c(x)`. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions, a singular system or an invalid `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_solve_static" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Solves the static problem at time `t` (the source and boundary data are evaluated at `t`). # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions or a singular system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_solve_static_var" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. [`solve_static`] with spatially varying `k(x)` and `c(x)`. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions or a singular system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_space_new" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Builds the space. # Errors For a degree other than 1 or 2.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_space_size" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Number of degrees of freedom.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fem_variable_problem" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fft_nd" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. In-place N-dimensional FFT of row-major (x fastest) data with extents `n` (each a power of two). The inverse includes the `1/N` normalisation. # Errors If an extent is not a power of two or the length does not match.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fluid_faces_get" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Condition of a face.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fluid_faces_uniform" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. The same condition on every face.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fluid_faces_with" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Sets one face.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fluid_faces_with_axis" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Sets both faces of an axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fluid_inflow" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Inflow with constant velocity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fluid_kind" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fluid_moving_wall" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Wall moving with a constant velocity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fluid_outflow" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Outflow at zero pressure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fluid_wall" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Fixed no-slip wall.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_coord" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Point coordinates (`x = i h`, origin at the corner).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_dealias_mask" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. 2/3-rule dealiasing mask (`true` = keep).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_deriv" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Spectral derivative `∂/∂x_axis` (the Nyquist mode is dropped).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_h" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Grid spacing along an axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_helmholtz" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Solves `-Δu + σ u = f` spectrally with periodic boundaries. For `σ = 0` the forcing must have zero mean up to transform roundoff, and the solution has zero mean. For repeated or batched sources use [`Self::helmholtz_plan`]. # Errors Invalid grid, shift or forcing, incompatible forcing in a singular mode, or a nonrepresentable symbol, transform or solution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_helmholtz_batch" => "Solves (-Laplace + sigma) u = f on a periodic Fourier grid for one or more forcing rows in input order. Give measured forcing values in field/length^2 and sigma in inverse length squared. Each row has exactly grid.size points with the x axis fastest. The result reports the batch size followed by the Fourier grid extents, flattened solutions divided by value_reference, and the grid length, time, and field references. Singular compatible Fourier modes use zero amplitude. Solves several real periodic forcings in input order. Larger batches use independent workers under the active execution policy. # Errors The first failing source in input order, as for [`Self::solve`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_k2" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. `|k|²` of flat mode index `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_kvec" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Wavenumber vector of flat mode index `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_new" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Creates a grid. # Errors For bad sizes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_size" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Total number of points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_to_phys" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Real part of the inverse transform.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_to_phys_c" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Complex inverse transform.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_to_spec" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Transform of real data to the spectral domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_to_spec_c" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Transform of complex data to the spectral domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_fourier_wavenumber" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Wavenumber of mode index `j` along `axis` (negative frequencies wrapped).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_frame_new" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_frame_references" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_gas_exact" => "Native ideal-gas method in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric primitives are normalized density Mass/Length^3, velocity Length/Time and pressure Mass/(Length Time^2); numeric times and coordinates use the frame references. Distinct units remain separate in returned records. Native timesteps and callback evaluations have explicit budgets. The exact solution of the Riemann problem `l | r` at `ξ = x/t`. Use [`ExactRiemann`] to sample the same initial states at several points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_gas_hllc" => "Native ideal-gas method in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric primitives are normalized density Mass/Length^3, velocity Length/Time and pressure Mass/(Length Time^2); numeric times and coordinates use the frame references. Distinct units remain separate in returned records. Native timesteps and callback evaluations have explicit budgets. The HLLC numerical flux between the states `l` and `r`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_gas_prepare_riemann" => "Prepares the exact ideal-gas Riemann solution for repeated sampling. Each normalized input vector is [density, velocity, pressure], and gas density and pressure must be positive. The mechanical frame fixes the physical references of all three quantities. Solves the wave pattern for initial states separated by a discontinuity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_gas_sample_riemann" => "Samples a prepared exact Riemann solution at a measured self-similar velocity x/t. Returns the measured density, velocity and pressure. Within a vacuum, density and pressure are zero and velocity is x/t. Samples at the self-similar velocity `xi = x/t`. At a contact this returns the left star state, and at a shock it returns the undisturbed state. In a vacuum, including its fan tails, density and pressure are zero and velocity is continued as `xi`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_gas_sample_riemann_batch" => "Samples up to 65536 measured self-similar velocities x/t. The record contains measured density, velocity and pressure vectors in the input order. Independent samples can use the active CPU pool. Samples a series of self-similar velocities in their input order. Large queries share the active CPU pool without altering any state value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_gas_sod" => "Native ideal-gas method in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric primitives are normalized density Mass/Length^3, velocity Length/Time and pressure Mass/(Length Time^2); numeric times and coordinates use the frame references. Distinct units remain separate in returned records. Native timesteps and callback evaluations have explicit budgets. Sod's shock tube on `[0, 1]` at `t`: numerical and exact densities at the cell centres.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_gas_solve" => "Native ideal-gas method in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric primitives are normalized density Mass/Length^3, velocity Length/Time and pressure Mass/(Length Time^2); numeric times and coordinates use the frame references. Distinct units remain separate in returned records. Native timesteps and callback evaluations have explicit budgets. Solves the 1D Euler equations (HLLC, MUSCL, SSP-RK3). The domain is `[x0, x1]` with `cells` cells, the initial data `initial(x)`, the final time `t_end`, the CFL number `cfl` and the reconstruction `limiter`. Returns the cell centres and the primitive states. This entry point delegates to the dimension-generic engine [`crate::sim::euler_nd`] (1D case); grids with fewer than six cells or a CFL number outside `(0, 1)` use the compact reference implementation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_heat_adaptive" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Crank–Nicolson heat integration with step-doubling error control. # Errors On solver failure or when `max_steps` is exceeded.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_heat_fixed" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Integrates the heat equation with the θ-method (`θ = 1` backward Euler, `θ = 1/2` Crank–Nicolson) using `steps` equal steps. # Errors On solver failure or an invalid `theta` / absorbing boundary.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_heat_variable_adaptive" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Crank–Nicolson integration of the variable-coefficient heat equation with step-doubling error control. # Errors On solver failure, a bad `alpha` or when `max_steps` is exceeded.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_heat_variable_fixed" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Integrates the variable-coefficient heat equation with the θ-method (`θ = 1` backward Euler, `θ = 1/2` Crank–Nicolson) using `steps` equal steps. # Errors On solver failure, a bad `alpha` or an invalid `theta` / absorbing boundary.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_helmholtz_incident_field" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Incident field at a point.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_helmholtz_scattered_field" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Scattered pressure at a point away from the surface.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_helmholtz_solve" => "Native exterior Helmholtz solver. Wave number is normalized by inverse frame length, coupling by frame length, direction dimensionless. Unit incident amplitude corresponds to the frame potential reference; returned fields retain it. Mesh, dense work, ACA iterations and storage are bounded. Solves the exterior scattering problem. `coupling` is the Burton–Miller parameter `α` (`None` for the recommended `i / k`, `Some(0)` for the conventional equation, which is singular at the irregular frequencies). # Errors For an invalid mesh, `k <= 0` or a singular system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_helmholtz_solve_aca" => "Native exterior Helmholtz solver. Wave number is normalized by inverse frame length, coupling by frame length, direction dimensionless. Unit incident amplitude corresponds to the frame potential reference; returned fields retain it. Mesh, dense work, ACA iterations and storage are bounded. [`solve_helmholtz_exterior`] with a hierarchical matrix: complex ACA for the admissible blocks and complex GMRES; no dense `n x n` matrix is formed. Returns the solution and storage statistics. # Errors As [`solve_helmholtz_exterior`]; also when GMRES does not converge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_helmholtz_stats" => "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_contact" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_dynamic" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Integrates `M a + f_int(u) = f_ext(t)` from the given initial state with fixed step `dt`. The external load is the body force and the nominal tractions of `prob` (constant in time, applied suddenly at `t = 0`); displacement conditions are evaluated at the new time. `observer` is called after every step with the new state. # Errors If an element inverts, Newton fails or the effective matrix is singular.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_grid_hyper_kinetic" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Kinetic energy `½ vᵀ M v` with the consistent mass matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_lame" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Lamé parameters `(λ, μ)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_material" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_piola" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. First Piola–Kirchhoff stress `P(F)`. # Errors If the deformation or stress is invalid. See [`HyperelasticPoint::new`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_point_energy_density" => "Returns the strain energy per reference volume of a prepared material point. The measured result has pressure units. Stored strain energy per unit reference volume. # Errors Returns an error if the energy density is outside the finite range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_point_piola" => "Returns the measured 3 by 3 first Piola stress of a prepared material point. First Piola–Kirchhoff stress `P(F)`. # Errors Returns an error if a stress component is outside the finite range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_point_tangent" => "Returns the measured 3 by 3 by 3 by 3 material tangent with indices (i, J, k, L) for the derivative of P[i, J] with respect to F[k, L]. Material tangent `∂P[i][J] / ∂F[k][L]`, indexed as `a[i][J][k][L]`. # Errors Returns an error if a tangent component is outside the finite range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_point_tangent_action" => "Applies the prepared material tangent to a dimensionless gradient increment. Returns the 3 by 3 measured stress increment without constructing a rank-four tensor. Applies the material tangent to a dimensionless gradient increment. Returns the stress increment `dP(F)[h]` without forming the fourth-order tangent. The result has the same units as the stress. # Errors Returns an error for a non-finite direction or stress increment.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_prepare_point" => "Prepares one hyperelastic material point at a dimensionless 3 by 3 deformation gradient. Stress, stiffness and energy density share its constitutive state and retain the material pressure units. Prepares a material with a dimensionless deformation gradient `f`. # Errors Returns an error for invalid Lamé parameters, non-finite gradients, `det F <= 1e-12`, or an unrepresentable inverse needed by neo-Hooke.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_problem" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_rest" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Initial state at rest with the consistent initial acceleration `a = M⁻¹ (f_ext - f_int(u))`. # Errors If the mass matrix is singular or an element is inverted.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_state_fields" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_static" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Static solution by load stepping and Newton iterations. The load is applied in `steps` equal increments (the displacement data, tractions and body force are scaled by `s / steps`) with Newton iterations (tolerance on the free-dof residual relative to the largest external or internal force norm). # Errors If an element inverts, Newton fails to converge or the system is singular.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_strain" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Strain energy `∫ W dV` of the displacement field `u`. # Errors If the field length is wrong or the deformation or energy is invalid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_hyper_tangent" => "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Material tangent `A_{iJkL} = ∂P_{iJ}/∂F_{kL}` stored as `a[i][J][k][L]`. # Errors If the deformation or tangent is invalid. See [`HyperelasticPoint::new`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_laplacian_apply" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. `out = L u` at free nodes, `0` at Dirichlet nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_laplacian_dirichlet_count" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Number of Dirichlet nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_laplacian_forcing" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Boundary-datum contribution to `Δu` for Neumann and Robin faces at time `t`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_laplacian_has_absorbing" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Whether any face carries an absorbing (wave-only) condition.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_laplacian_is_dirichlet" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Whether node `p` is a Dirichlet node.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_laplacian_new" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Assembles the operator for the given boundary conditions. # Errors If a Robin condition is nonfinite or degenerate (`alpha = beta = 0`), or the boundary periodicity differs from the grid's.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_laplacian_set_dirichlet" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Overwrites Dirichlet nodes of `u` with `g(t, x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_laplacian_system" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Matrix `shift I + scale L` with identity rows at Dirichlet nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_apply_dirichlet_bc" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Applies Dirichlet boundary conditions to a 2D field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_apply_neumann_bc" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Applies Neumann boundary conditions (zero gradient) to a 2D field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_burgers_1" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Solves the 1D viscous Burgers' equation: `∂u/∂t + u·∂u/∂x = ν·∂²u/∂x²` This nonlinear equation models shock formation and viscous dissipation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_burgers_2" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. 2D viscous Burgers `u_t + u (u_x + u_y) = ν Δu` (upwinded); 2D counterpart of [`solve_burgers_1d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_burgers_3" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. 3D viscous Burgers `u_t + u (u_x + u_y + u_z) = ν Δu`; see [`solve_burgers_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_cavity_mac" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Second-order MAC projection solver for the lid-driven cavity. 2D, or 3D with `dim = 3`, at Reynolds number `re`, run to `t_end`; see [`crate::sim::ns_mac`] for obstacles, inflow/outflow and periodic faces. # Errors For invalid sizes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_cavity_simple" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Simplified lid-driven cavity flow solver using stream function-vorticity formulation. # Arguments * `nx`, `ny` - Grid dimensions * `re` - Reynolds number * `lid_velocity` - Top lid velocity * `num_steps` - Number of time steps * `dt` - Time step # Returns (stream function, vorticity)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_compute_divergence" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the divergence of a 2D velocity field. div(V) = ∂u/∂x + ∂v/∂y\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_compute_gradient" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the gradient of a scalar field. Returns (∂p/∂x, ∂p/∂y)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_compute_laplacian" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the Laplacian of a scalar field with centered second differences. The row axis uses `dx`, the column axis uses `dy`, and the outer layer is zero. Grids without interior cells therefore return an all-zero result. Spacing signs have no effect. Zero or non-finite spacing uses IEEE division.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_compute_stream_function" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the stream function from vorticity using Poisson equation. ∇²ψ = -ω\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_compute_vorticity" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the vorticity ω = ∂v/∂x - ∂u/∂y from velocity field. # Arguments * `u` - x-component of velocity (Matrix) * `v` - y-component of velocity (Matrix) * `dx`, `dy` - Grid spacing\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_as_mut_slice" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Returns a mutable slice to the underlying data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_as_slice" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Returns a slice to the underlying data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_dimensions" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Returns the dimensions of the grid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_from_data" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Creates a new grid from existing data and dimensions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_heat_bc" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Heat equation `u_t = α Δu` on the grid of `initial` (1D, 2D or 3D). Face conditions `bcs` may be Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, periodic or time dependent; the Crank–Nicolson method of [`crate::sim::structured`] is used. # Errors For inconsistent sizes or boundary descriptions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_is_empty" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Is the grid empty.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_len" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Size of the grid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_new" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Creates a new grid with the given dimensions, initialized with a default value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_poisson_2d" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves Poisson equation `∇²u = f` using Successive Over-Relaxation (SOR). # Panics Panics if the `source` grid's dimensions do not match the `width` and `height` provided. Panics if internal indexing operations go out of bounds due to incorrect `FdmGrid` usage, though typical usage within this function should prevent this.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_poisson_bc" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Poisson/Helmholtz equation `-Δu + σ u = f` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid with face conditions `bcs`, solved by geometric multigrid. # Errors For inconsistent sizes or boundary descriptions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_poisson_nd" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves `Δu = f` with zero Dirichlet boundaries on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid by SOR. # Errors For inconsistent shapes or lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_scenario_heat" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Example scenario: Simulates heat conduction on a 100x100 plate.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_scenario_wave" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Example scenario: Simulates wave propagation on a 120x120 grid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_solve_heat_equation_2d" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves a 2D heat equation `u_t = alpha * ∇²u` using the finite difference method.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_solve_wave_dirichlet_sampled" => "Advances a 2D or 3D homogeneous Dirichlet wave from a measured displacement grid and a flat rate tensor in displacement/time. Give measured length spacings, a speed, a signed time step, a step count and a positive save cadence. Samples contain step zero, cadence multiples and the final step. Each row of data is a measured displacement field with x fastest. grid_shape gives the field extents and steps gives the completed step indices. Evolves a two- or three-dimensional Dirichlet wave and retains step samples. Initial displacement and velocity share a grid. Faces are zero from the initial sample onward. Retains step zero, every multiple of `save_every`, and the last step. Time advances by the signed step `dt`. # Errors Invalid shapes, spacing, speed, time step or cadence, a violated leapfrog CFL bound, non-finite data or states, allocation limits, or cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_solve_wave_equation_2d" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves the two-dimensional wave equation with zero initial velocity. Positive step counts impose homogeneous Dirichlet values from the initial time. With zero steps the function returns the raw initial samples. Use time steps satisfying `(c dt / dx)^2 + (c dt / dy)^2 <= 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_solve_wave_equation_3d" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves the three-dimensional wave equation with zero initial velocity. Uses a second-order start and homogeneous Dirichlet faces for positive step counts. With zero steps the function returns the raw initial samples. Use time steps satisfying `Σ_a (c dt / h_a)^2 <= 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_wave_bc" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Wave equation `u_tt = c² Δu` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid with face conditions `bcs` (including first-order absorbing faces) by leapfrog. # Errors If the CFL condition is violated or the description is inconsistent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fdm_with_value" => "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Creates a new grid with the given dimensions, initialized with a specified value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fluid_air" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Air at 20°C and 1 atm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fluid_kinematic_viscosity" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Kinematic viscosity ν = μ/ρ (m²/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fluid_new" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Creates new fluid properties.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fluid_prandtl" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Prandtl number Pr = ν/α = μ·Cp/k\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fluid_thermal_diffusivity" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Thermal diffusivity α = k/(ρ·Cp) (m²/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fluid_water" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Water at 20°C.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_advection1" => "Advances a retained 1D finite-volume mesh with an upwind flux. Supply a one-entry measured Length/Time velocity, a positive measured Time step, an integer step count, and the left and right ghost-cell values in the mesh field dimension. Both ghost values are validated. CFL must be at most one. The upwind face uses the ghost value on the side from which the flow enters. Step count and cell-step work are bounded. The measured field is returned in increasing cell order and replaces the retained field on success. An invalid input or unrepresentable output leaves the previous field intact.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_advection2" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Solves the 2D linear advection equation using the Finite Volume Method.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_advection3" => "Advances the retained 3D finite-volume mesh using measured velocity and timestep. The Boolean mask is flat in x-fastest order or shaped [depth, height, width]. True cells are set to zero on each step. Unmarked exterior cells use zero inflow and free outflow. Returns a flat measured field in x-fastest order. CFL and work bounds are checked. Advances the 3D linear advection equation with first-order upwind fluxes. The callback marks cells to set to zero on each time step. All other cells use a zero exterior state at an inflow face and their own state at an outflow face. The returned values use the mesh's x-fastest storage order. For stability, choose `dt` so that the sum of the three directional Courant numbers `dt * |velocity| / spacing` is at most one. # Panics Panics if a mesh advanced for at least one step has inconsistent extents and cell storage.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_burgers1" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Solves the 1D Burgers' equation `u_t + (u^2/2)_x = 0` using FVM and Lax-Friedrichs flux.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_lax_friedrichs_flux" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Lax-Friedrichs numerical flux for a general conservation law `u_t` + f(u)_x = 0.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_mesh1_new" => "Creates a retained one-dimensional finite-volume mesh directly from sampled cell averages. Supply a one-entry cell-count vector with at least three cells and a one-entry measured Length vector for the total domain extent. Supply one value in the frame's field dimension per cell in increasing coordinate order. The adapter uses those samples directly and stores a uniform cell spacing from the total extent and cell count. Invalid shapes, dimensions, samples and nonpositive or unrepresentable spacing produce errors. The mesh handle can be reused for advection and data retrieval.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_mesh2_new" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Creates a new 2D mesh.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_mesh3_new" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Creates a new 3D mesh.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_mesh_data" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_num_cells" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Returns the number of cells in the mesh.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_scenario_advection1" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Example scenario: Simulates the advection of a square wave (top-hat profile).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_scenario_advection2" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Example scenario: Simulates the advection of a 2D Gaussian blob.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_scenario_advection3" => "Runs the built-in 3D Gaussian advection example on a 30-by-30-by-30 unit cube. The outer layer is set to zero each step. The returned dimensionless field is flattened in x-fastest order. Example scenario: Simulates the advection of a 3D Gaussian blob.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_shallow_water1" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Solves 1D Shallow Water Equations using FVM and Lax-Friedrichs flux.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_shallow_water2" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. 2D shallow water equations `h_t + (hu)_x + (hv)_y = 0`, ... On a grid of `dims = [nx, ny]` cells (flat, `x` fastest) with the Lax-Friedrichs flux and zero-gradient boundaries. 2D counterpart of [`solve_shallow_water_1d`]; the numerical viscosity is shared between the two axes (unsplit scheme), so it smears slightly more than the 1D routine on y-independent data. Stable for `(|u| + sqrt(g h)) dt (1/dx + 1/dy) <= 1` (roughly). # Errors For a length mismatch or non-positive step sizes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_fvm_shallow_water3" => "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. 3D barotropic shallow-water system `h_t + ∇·(h u) = 0`, `(h u)_t + ∇·(h u ⊗ u) + ∇(g h²/2) = 0`. Euler with `γ = 2`, `p = g h²/2` and no energy equation: the 3D analogue of the planar shallow-water equations. On a grid of `dims = [nx, ny, nz]` cells (flat, `x` fastest) with the Lax-Friedrichs flux and zero-gradient boundaries. 3D counterpart of [`solve_shallow_water_2d`]; the numerical viscosity is shared between the three axes (unsplit scheme). Stable for `(|u| + sqrt(g h)) dt (1/dx + 1/dy + 1/dz) <= 1` (roughly). # Errors For a length mismatch or non-positive step sizes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_l2_norm" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the L2 norm of a field for convergence checking.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_max_abs" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the maximum absolute value in a field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_max_velocity_magnitude" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the maximum velocity magnitude from a 2D velocity field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_poisson_gauss_seidel" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Solves the 2D Poisson equation using Gauss-Seidel iteration. Generally converges faster than Jacobi.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_poisson_jacobi" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Solves the 2D Poisson equation `∇²u = f` using Jacobi iteration. This function implements the Jacobi iterative method to solve the Poisson equation on a 2D grid with Dirichlet boundary conditions (implicitly handled by the iteration not updating boundary points). It is suitable for steady-state problems. # Arguments * `f` - Source term (2D grid) as a `Matrix<f64>`. * `u0` - Initial guess for `u` (2D grid) as a `Matrix<f64>`. * `dx`, `dy` - Grid spacing in x and y directions. * `max_iter` - Maximum number of iterations. * `tolerance` - Convergence tolerance for the maximum difference between successive iterations. # Returns A `Matrix<f64>` representing the solution `u`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_poisson_sor" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Solves the 2D Poisson equation using Successive Over-Relaxation (SOR). # Arguments * `omega` - Relaxation parameter (1 < ω < 2 for over-relaxation)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_legacy_velocity_from_stream_function" => "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes velocity field from stream function. u = ∂ψ/∂y, v = -∂ψ/∂x\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_line_element_new" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_line_local_stiffness_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the local stiffness matrix for a 1D linear element. The local stiffness matrix relates the forces and displacements at the nodes of a single element. For a 1D linear element, it is given by: `[EA/L -EA/L]` `[-EA/L EA/L]` # Returns A `Matrix<f64>` representing the local stiffness matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_linear_system_new" => "Native PDE in an explicit reference frame. Callback coordinates and outputs retain their physical domain; normalized numeric coefficients are checked. Native step/factor budgets, boundary errors and callback errors propagate. Prepares `a` for repeated solves. # Errors Invalid/non-finite CSR storage or tolerance, or a singular direct system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_linear_system_solve" => "Native PDE in an explicit reference frame. Callback coordinates and outputs retain their physical domain; normalized numeric coefficients are checked. Native step/factor budgets, boundary errors and callback errors propagate. Solves `A x = b` (`x0` is the Krylov initial guess). # Errors Invalid/non-finite RHS or initial data, unrepresentable output, or an iterative solve that misses its tolerance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_marker_get" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Condition of a marker, or the default.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_marker_new" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Empty assignment without default.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_marker_set" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Assigns a condition to a marker.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_marker_with_default" => "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Assignment where unlisted markers use `default`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_material_aluminum" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates aluminum material with typical properties.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_material_bulk_modulus" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Bulk modulus K = E / (3(1-2ν))\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_material_copper" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates copper material with typical properties.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_material_new" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new material with specified properties.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_material_shear_modulus" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Shear modulus G = E / (2(1+ν))\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_material_steel" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates steel material with typical properties.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_max_shear_stress" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the maximum shear stress from principal stresses.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_mechanical_references" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_mechanical_system_data" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_mechanical_system_new" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_multigrid_convergence_factor" => "Native residual reduction factor, without replacing the kernel's stopping report. Geometric-mean residual reduction per cycle. Returns zero if there was no cycle or the initial residual was zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_multigrid_new" => "Actual native structured multigrid hierarchy with explicit Length/Time/field references and original V/W/Fmg controls. Nonzero/mixed/periodic native boundary conditions are retained; sources are normalized by field/length². Builds the hierarchy for `-Δu + σ u = f`. # Errors On invalid coefficients, boundary data or a singular coarsest problem.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_multigrid_num_levels" => "Actual number of native continuum multigrid levels. Number of levels.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_multigrid_result" => "Native continuum multigrid solution with explicit physical field reference and original normalized residual history/status.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_multigrid_solve" => "Native continuum multigrid solve, preserving normalized residual history and convergence status. Source arrays use field/length² reference and time is normalized by the retained Time reference; output field retains its physical amplitude. Solves with source `f` at the nodes and boundary data evaluated at time `t`. # Errors If the source length is wrong, the time or active data are nonfinite, or the residual, solution or workspace cannot be represented.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_neumann" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Homogeneous or constant Neumann condition.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_node2_distance" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Distance to another node.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_node2_new" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new 2D node.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_node3_new" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new 3D node.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_nonlinear_diffusion" => "Native PDE in an explicit reference frame. Callback coordinates and outputs retain their physical domain; normalized numeric coefficients are checked. Native step/factor budgets, boundary errors and callback errors propagate. Fixed-step implicit integration (θ-method) of nonlinear diffusion by Newton–Krylov. # Errors If Newton fails to converge or `theta` is outside `[1/2, 1]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_nonlinear_diffusion_adaptive" => "Native PDE in an explicit reference frame. Callback coordinates and outputs retain their physical domain; normalized numeric coefficients are checked. Native step/factor budgets, boundary errors and callback errors propagate. Adaptive (step-doubling, Crank–Nicolson) Newton–Krylov integration of nonlinear diffusion. # Errors If Newton fails to converge or `max_steps` is exceeded.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_open_fraction" => "Returns the dimensionless open fraction along one, two, or three distinct zero-based axes. Normalize the three origin and length slots by the frame's Length reference and supply positive lengths on the active axes. The compiled field takes three coherent Length coordinates and returns Length. Positive signed distances are open. One active axis uses segment root refinement. Two or three active axes use adaptive subdivision and simplex clipping. The depth cap is 12 and depth times the number of axes is at most 18. The independent callback budget accepts 1 through 8,388,608 evaluations. Invalid geometry, callback units or arity, nonfinite samples and budget exhaustion produce errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_open_length_fraction" => "Returns the dimensionless open fraction of an axis-aligned segment. Axis indices are zero based. Normalize the three origin coordinates and positive segment length by the frame's Length reference. The compiled field takes three coherent Length coordinates and returns Length. Positive distances are open. The kernel samples the segment, then bisects intervals where its sampled sign changes. The callback budget accepts 1 through 8,388,608 evaluations, including refinement. Invalid geometry, callback units or arity, nonfinite samples, and exhausted evaluation budgets produce errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_advection_1d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves the 1D advection equation `du/dt + c * du/dx = 0` using an explicit finite difference scheme. This function implements a simple first-order upwind scheme for stability. # Arguments * `u0` - Initial condition (vector of `u` values at `t=0`). * `c` - Advection speed. * `dx` - Spatial step size. * `dt` - Time step size. * `num_steps` - Number of time steps to simulate. # Returns A `Vec<Vec<f64>>` where each inner `Vec` is the solution `u` at a given time step.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_advection_2d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D upwind advection `u_t + c·∇u = 0` (periodic with period `n - 1` per axis); 2D counterpart of [`solve_advection_1d`]. Returns all snapshots. # Errors For inconsistent shapes or a non-positive `dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_advection_3d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D upwind advection; see [`solve_advection_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_advection_diffusion_1d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves the 1D advection-diffusion equation: `∂u/∂t + c·∂u/∂x = α·∂²u/∂x²`\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_advection_diffusion_2d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D advection-diffusion `u_t + c·∇u = α Δu`; 2D counterpart of [`solve_advection_diffusion_1d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_advection_diffusion_3d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D advection-diffusion; see [`solve_advection_diffusion_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_diffusion_1d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves the 1D diffusion equation `du/dt = alpha * d2u/dx2` using an explicit finite difference scheme. # Arguments * `u0` - Initial condition. * `alpha` - Diffusion coefficient. * `dx` - Spatial step size. * `dt` - Time step size. * `num_steps` - Number of time steps. # Returns A `Vec<Vec<f64>>` where each inner `Vec` is the solution `u` at a given time step.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_diffusion_2d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D explicit diffusion `u_t = α Δu` with frozen boundary values; 2D counterpart of [`solve_diffusion_1d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_diffusion_3d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D explicit diffusion; see [`solve_diffusion_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_solve_poisson_1d_gauss_seidel" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 1D Poisson equation by Gauss-Seidel iteration; see [`solve_poisson_1d_jacobi`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_1d_jacobi`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_solve_poisson_1d_jacobi" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 1D Poisson equation `u'' = f` by Jacobi iteration (Dirichlet values are the end values of `u0`). 1D counterpart of [`solve_poisson_2d_jacobi`]. # Errors For inconsistent lengths or a non-positive spacing.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_solve_poisson_1d_sor" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 1D Poisson equation by SOR with relaxation factor `omega`; see [`solve_poisson_1d_jacobi`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_1d_jacobi`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_solve_poisson_3d_gauss_seidel" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Poisson equation by Gauss-Seidel iteration; see [`solve_poisson_3d_jacobi`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_3d_jacobi`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_solve_poisson_3d_jacobi" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Poisson equation `Δu = f` by Jacobi iteration on `dims = [nx, ny, nz]` (flat, `x` fastest). 3D counterpart of [`solve_poisson_2d_jacobi`]. # Errors For inconsistent lengths or non-positive spacings.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cfd_solve_poisson_3d_sor" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Poisson equation by SOR; see [`solve_poisson_3d_jacobi`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_3d_jacobi`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cnm_simulate_1d_heat_conduction_cn_scenario" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Scenario for the 1D Crank-Nicolson solver.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cnm_simulate_2d_heat_conduction_cn_adi_scenario" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Scenario for the 2D Crank-Nicolson ADI solver.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cnm_solve_heat_equation_1d_cn" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves the 1D heat equation `u_t` = D * `u_xx` using the Crank-Nicolson method. # Arguments * `initial_condition` - The initial temperature distribution. * `dx` - The spatial step size. * `dt` - The time step. * `d_coeff` - The diffusion coefficient D. * `steps` - The number of time steps to simulate. # Returns The final temperature distribution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_cnm_solve_heat_equation_1d_cn_tridiagonal" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Direct tridiagonal Crank–Nicolson reference (zero Dirichlet ends).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fdm_advection_diffusion_1d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves a 1D advection-diffusion equation `u_t + c*u_x = d*u_xx` using FDM.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fdm_advection_diffusion_2d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D advection-diffusion `u_t + c·∇u = d Δu` (upwind + central, frozen boundaries); returns the final state. 2D counterpart of [`solve_advection_diffusion_1d`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fdm_advection_diffusion_3d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D advection-diffusion; see [`solve_advection_diffusion_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fdm_burgers_1d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves 1D Burgers' equation `u_t + u*u_x = nu*u_xx`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fdm_burgers_2d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D Burgers' equation `u_t + u (u_x + u_y) = nu Δu` with central convection differences and frozen boundaries; returns the state after `steps` steps. 2D counterpart of [`solve_burgers_1d`]. # Errors For inconsistent shapes or a non-positive `dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fdm_burgers_3d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Burgers' equation; see [`solve_burgers_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fdm_solve_poisson_1d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 1D Poisson equation `u'' = f`, zero Dirichlet ends, SOR. 1D counterpart of [`solve_poisson_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_nd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fdm_solve_poisson_3d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Poisson equation `Δu = f`, zero Dirichlet boundaries, SOR. 3D counterpart of [`solve_poisson_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_nd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fvm_burgers_2d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D Burgers' equation; 2D counterpart of [`solve_burgers_1d`]. See [`solve_burgers_nd`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_nd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fvm_burgers_3d" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Burgers' equation; see [`solve_burgers_nd`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_nd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fvm_burgers_nd" => "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Burgers' equation `u_t + Σ_a (u²/2)_{x_a} = 0` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. The cells are stored flat, with x fastest. Uses the multi-dimensional Lax–Friedrichs flux with equal numerical-viscosity shares per axis and zero-gradient outflow boundaries. The one-dimensional stencil agrees with [`solve_burgers_1d`] up to arithmetic roundoff. See [`solve_burgers_nd_with_boundaries`] for the stability condition. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_nd_with_boundaries`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_fvm_burgers_nd_with_boundaries" => "Solves the multidimensional Burgers equation by conservative Lax–Friedrichs fluxes. Use a velocity-valued frame. The flat initial field is in units of value_reference with x fastest, spacing in units of length_reference, and dt in units of time_reference. Supply Periodic or Outflow for both faces of each axis. The CFL bound holds separately on each axis. The returned field keeps all three references. Advances Burgers' equation with a boundary choice for each active axis. Uses the same Lax–Friedrichs flux on both sides of an interface. A fully periodic grid conserves the sum of cell averages up to roundoff. Zero-gradient faces allow the boundary physical flux to leave or enter the grid. For the equal viscosity shares used here, a sufficient monotonicity condition is `D * dt * max|u| <= h[a]` on each active axis, where `D` is the number of axes. At fixed grid spacing, the one-step limit as `dt` tends to zero retains Lax–Friedrichs averaging. # Errors An invalid grid, boundary count, initial field, or step size, or a flux or state that cannot be represented.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_sm_simulate_1d_advection_diffusion_scenario" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Example scenario for the 1D spectral solver.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_sm_simulate_2d_advection_diffusion_scenario" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Example scenario for the 2D spectrclsal solver.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_physics_sm_simulate_3d_advection_diffusion_scenario" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Example scenario for the 3D spectral solver.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_principal_stresses" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Converts stress to principal stresses. Returns (sigma1, sigma2, angle) where sigma1 >= sigma2. # Panics Panics if the stress vector length is not 3.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_quad_cantilever" => "Execute the actual fixed native cantilever scenario (105 nodes,80 elements, CG maximum5000 iterations), writing beam_original.csv and beam_deformed.csv to the requested directory. Native educational coefficients are explicitly normalized; no measured inputs are accepted. An example scenario for a cantilever beam under a point load. This function sets up a mesh for a 2D cantilever beam, defines fixed boundary conditions at one end and applies a point load at the free end. It then runs the elasticity simulation and saves the original and deformed node positions to CSV files for visualization. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `run_elasticity_simulation` fails or if it cannot create or write to the output CSV files. `beam_original.csv` and `beam_deformed.csv` are written into `output_dir`, which is created if it does not exist.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_quad_element" => "Computes the plane-stress stiffness matrix of one bilinear quadrilateral. Supply four counterclockwise [x,y] rows scaled by the mechanical Length reference, Young's modulus scaled by the stress reference, and a dimensionless Poisson ratio. The record contains rows, cols, the 64 normalized entries in row-major order, and an entry_reference in Force per Length. Model thickness is one Length reference. Invalid material, malformed vertices, and nonpositive or nonfinite quadrature Jacobians produce errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_quad_solve" => "Solves a bilinear quadrilateral plane-stress mesh. Supply [x,y] node rows and four zero-based node indices per counterclockwise element, followed by Young's modulus, the dimensionless Poisson ratio, fixed node indices and [node,fx,fy] load rows. Normalize coordinates, Young's modulus and loads by the mechanical Length, stress and Force references. Thickness is one Length reference. Both displacement components of a fixed node are zero. The return record contains interleaved normalized x/y displacements and their measured Length reference. The adapter bounds node count, load rows and estimated solve work. It rejects invalid connectivity, geometry, material and nonconvergent solves.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_refine_mesh" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Refines a triangular mesh by subdividing each triangle into 4 smaller triangles.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_robin" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Robin condition with constant data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_safety_factor_von_mises" => "Native dimensionless safety factor from three normalized plane stresses and yield strength in the same stress reference; zero equivalent stress preserves the native positive-infinite result. Safety factor based on von Mises criterion.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_simplex_bary_gradients" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Constant gradients of the barycentric coordinates of a cell.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_simplex_boxed" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Tetrahedral mesh of a box (six tetrahedra per cube, Kuhn subdivision), markers 0-5.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_simplex_interval" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Uniform interval mesh with `n` cells on `[lo, hi]` (markers 0 and 1).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_simplex_measure" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Cell measure (length, area or volume).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_simplex_mesh_data" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_simplex_positive_fraction" => "Native positive fraction of a segment/triangle/tetrahedron in dimensionless level-set coordinates; common positive rescaling does not change the fraction. Fraction of a point, segment, triangle or tetrahedron where the linearly interpolated vertex values are positive. Returns NaN for any other vertex count or a nonfinite value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_simplex_rectangle" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Triangulated rectangle (two triangles per quad), markers 0-3.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_simplex_refine_marked" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Refines the cells flagged in `marked` by conforming edge bisection. Every marked tetrahedron is split at the midpoints of its six edges into eight tetrahedra. The split is carried out as recursive bisection: a cell is cut along its longest split edge (ties broken by vertex indices) and the two halves are cut again until no split edge is left. Only midpoints of edges of marked cells are inserted, so an unmarked neighbour is cut along exactly the edges it shares with marked cells (**red–green closure**) and a cell without a split edge is kept. Both cells sharing a face cut that face from the same edge midpoints to the opposite vertex of the face, hence there are no hanging nodes. Boundary facets inherit the marker of the parent facet containing them. The refinement level of every produced cell is one more than the level of its parent; cells that are not touched keep their level. In 1D and 2D the same scheme splits a marked cell into 2 or 4 cells. Entries of `marked` past the last cell are ignored.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_simplex_rule" => "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Grundmann-Moeller quadrature on the `n`-simplex. Grundmann–Möller quadrature on the `n`-simplex, exact for polynomials of degree `2s + 1`. Returns barycentric points (`n + 1` coordinates) and weights that sum to one (multiply by the simplex measure).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_sm_advance_advection_diffusion_3d_modes" => "Advance existing unnormalized-forward 3D FFT coefficients with spectral Forward Euler. Data uses x-fastest order and the supplied frame's field reference. Spacing, velocity, diffusivity and time are normalized in the supplied frame. Zero steps or a zero time step preserves the coefficients. Advances an existing complex Fourier spectrum by the configured Euler steps. The spectrum uses the unnormalized forward transform of [`fft3d`] and the same axis order, with x varying fastest, then y, then z. [`ifft3d`] divides by the grid size on returning to physical space. A zero-step or zero-time-step call keeps all validated coefficients unchanged. # Errors Rejects zero or overflowing extents, mismatched shape, nonfinite data, invalid spacing or periods, invalid diffusivity or time step, and nonfinite computed output. When a call fails, the spectrum stays unchanged.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_sm_advection_diffusion_exact_nd" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Exact-in-time Fourier solution of `u_t + c·∇u = D Δu` on a periodic box of 1D, 2D or 3D (power-of-two points per axis, data with `x` fastest): every mode is multiplied by `exp((-i c·k - D |k|²) t)`. # Errors For sizes that are not powers of two or inconsistent data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_sm_fft2d" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_sm_fft3d" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_sm_ifft2d" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_sm_ifft3d" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_sm_solve_advection_diffusion_1d" => "Original Fourier spectral Forward Euler stepping, preserving conditional stability and the native method; this is distinct from sm_advection_diffusion_exact_nd. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivity and steps use explicit L/T/field reference normalization. Odd/even FFT sizes, native x-fastest layout and work bounds are checked. Solves the 1D advection-diffusion equation (`u_t` + c*`u_x` = D*`u_xx`) using a Fourier spectral method.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_sm_solve_advection_diffusion_2d" => "Original Fourier spectral Forward Euler stepping, preserving conditional stability and the native method; this is distinct from sm_advection_diffusion_exact_nd. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivity and steps use explicit L/T/field reference normalization. Odd/even FFT sizes, native x-fastest layout and work bounds are checked. Solves the 2D advection-diffusion equation using a Fourier spectral method.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_sm_solve_advection_diffusion_3d" => "Original Fourier spectral Forward Euler stepping, preserving conditional stability and the native method; this is distinct from sm_advection_diffusion_exact_nd. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivity and steps use explicit L/T/field reference normalization. Odd/even FFT sizes, native x-fastest layout and work bounds are checked. Solves the 3D advection-diffusion equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_solve_helmholtz" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Solves `-Δu + σ u = f` on the grid with the given boundary conditions (`σ` may be negative for the indefinite Helmholtz equation). # Errors On a singular problem (e.g. pure Neumann with `σ = 0`), a solver failure or an absorbing boundary.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_solve_helmholtz_multigrid" => "Actual native continuum multigrid free solver with coherent compiled spatial source of field/length² units, retained physical boundary data, and original cycle controls. Convenience wrapper: solves `-Δu + σu = f(x)` with multigrid. # Errors See [`MultigridSolver::new`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_solve_poisson_multigrid_nd" => "Actual native ND multigrid wrapper on a zero-origin rectangular grid. Extents and positive spacings are normalized by the retained Length reference; source uses field/length². Original V/W/Fmg controls, boundary conditions, residuals and convergence status are preserved. Geometric multigrid for `-Δu + σu = f` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. Face conditions may be Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin or periodic; V, W or full multigrid cycles. Data are stored with `x` fastest. # Errors For inconsistent sizes or boundary descriptions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_solve_static_structural" => "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Solves a static structural problem for displacements. This function takes the global stiffness matrix, applied forces, and boundary conditions to solve for the nodal displacements. It modifies the global stiffness matrix and force vector to incorporate Dirichlet boundary conditions. # Arguments * `global_k` - The global stiffness matrix. * `forces` - The global force vector. * `fixed_dofs` - A vector of tuples `(node_idx, prescribed_displacement)` for fixed degrees of freedom. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<f64>` of nodal displacements, or an error string if the system is singular. # Errors Returns an error if the force vector dimension mismatches the stiffness matrix, or if the resulting system is singular.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_solve_variable_helmholtz" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Solves `-∇·(α(x)∇u) + σ u = f` with a node-sampled diffusivity and the given boundary conditions (`σ` may be negative for the indefinite Helmholtz equation). The face values of `α` use `averaging`. # Errors On a singular problem (e.g. pure Neumann with `σ = 0`), a solver failure, an absorbing boundary or a bad `alpha`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_spectrum_data" => "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_coord" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Coordinates of node `p` (inactive axes are `0`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_h" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Spacing along `axis`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_h_min" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Smallest spacing over the active axes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_ijk" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Node multi-index of a flat index.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_index" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Flat index of node `(i, j, k)` (x fastest).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_is_empty" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Whether the grid has no nodes (never true for a valid grid).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_l2_error" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Discrete L2 norm of the error (`sqrt(h^d Σ e²)`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_len" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Number of nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_max_error" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Largest absolute difference between `u` and `exact` at the nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_new" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Builds a grid; periodicity is read from `bcs`. # Errors For an invalid axis count or spacing, too few nodes, an unrepresentable grid size, or one-sided periodicity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_sample" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Samples `f` at every node.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_structured_unit" => "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Unit hypercube `[0,1]^dim` with `n` nodes per axis. # Errors See [`Grid::new`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_tetrahedron_b_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Strain-displacement matrix `B` (6 x 12).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_tetrahedron_dof_map" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Global DOF numbers of the 12 local DOFs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_tetrahedron_elasticity_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Isotropic elasticity matrix in Voigt order `(xx, yy, zz, xy, yz, zx)` (engineering shear strains).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_tetrahedron_local_stiffness_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Local stiffness matrix `V Bt D B` (12 x 12).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_tetrahedron_new" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a tetrahedral element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_tetrahedron_shape_gradients" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Shape-function gradients (one row per node). A degenerate (zero volume) element yields non-finite entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_tetrahedron_signed_volume" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Signed volume `det[1 x y z] / 6` (positive for a right-handed node order).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_tetrahedron_volume" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Element volume (absolute value).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_thermal_line_conductivity_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the conductivity (stiffness) matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_thermal_line_new" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new 1D thermal element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_thermal_triangle_area" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Calculates the area of the triangle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_thermal_triangle_conductivity_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the conductivity matrix (3x3).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_thermal_triangle_new" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new 2D triangular thermal element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_transport_equation" => "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_triangle_area" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Calculates the area of the triangle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_triangle_b_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the strain-displacement (B) matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_triangle_compute_stress" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes element stresses given nodal displacements. # Panics Panics if the number of displacements is not 6.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_triangle_constitutive_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the constitutive (D) matrix for plane stress or plane strain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_triangle_local_stiffness_matrix" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the local stiffness matrix (6x6).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_triangle_new" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new triangular element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_triangle_von_mises" => "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes von Mises stress from stress components [σx, σy, τxy]. # Panics Panics if the stress vector length is not 3.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_variable_diffusion_apply" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. `out = L_α u` at free nodes, `0` at Dirichlet nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_variable_diffusion_dirichlet_count" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Number of Dirichlet nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_variable_diffusion_forcing" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Boundary-datum contribution to `∇·(α∇u)` for Neumann and Robin faces at time `t`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_variable_diffusion_has_absorbing" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Whether any face carries an absorbing (wave-only) condition.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_variable_diffusion_is_dirichlet" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Whether node `p` is a Dirichlet node.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_variable_diffusion_new" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Assembles the operator for a node-sampled diffusivity. # Errors If `alpha` does not have one value per node, is negative or not finite, if a Robin condition is nonfinite or degenerate, or if the boundary periodicity differs from the grid's.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_variable_diffusion_set_dirichlet" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Overwrites Dirichlet nodes of `u` with `g(t, x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_variable_diffusion_system" => "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Matrix `shift I + scale L_α` with identity rows at Dirichlet nodes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_grid_wave_leapfrog" => "Native PDE in an explicit reference frame. Callback coordinates and outputs retain their physical domain; normalized numeric coefficients are checked. Native step/factor budgets, boundary errors and callback errors propagate. Leapfrog integration of the wave equation to `t_end` with `steps` steps. # Errors If the CFL condition `c dt <= h_min / sqrt(dim)` is violated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_halley" => "Dimensionless Halley root solver: initial guess,positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,first derivative,second derivative. The evaluation budget is shared across all three callbacks. Returns root,evaluations; callback failures propagate unchanged. Halley's method (cubic convergence) using `f`, `f'` and `f''`. # Errors [`RootError::NoConvergence`] / [`RootError::Singular`].\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_harmonic" => "`num_harmonic(n)` returns the floating-point harmonic number H_n for a nonnegative u64 integer. H_0 is zero. The `n`-th harmonic number `sum_{k=1..n} 1/k`, with `H_0 = 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_harmonic_mean" => "Harmonic mean of a sample. Computes the harmonic mean of a slice of positive data. HM = n / (1/x1 + 1/x2 + ... + 1/xn)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_abelianization" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The abelianisation of a presented group: `Z^rank + torsion`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_betti_list" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The Betti numbers `b_0 ..= b_top` of a complex.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_bottleneck_distance" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The bottleneck distance of two persistence diagrams (lists of `(birth, death)`). Infinite bars are matched among themselves; if their numbers differ the distance is infinite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_chain_betti_mod_p" => "Return the Betti numbers of a session-owned chain complex in ascending degree order over a prime field. The second argument is an exact prime in the unsigned 64-bit range. Each boundary matrix is reduced once, with packed binary elimination for characteristic two. Betti numbers in chain degree order over the field with prime modulus `p`. Each boundary matrix is reduced once.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_chain_cohomology" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. `H^k(C; Z)` by the universal coefficient theorem: the free part of `H_k` and the torsion of `H_{k-1}`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_chain_complex" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The chain complex with basis `basis[k]` in degree `k` (lists of `k`-simplices). Faces missing from `basis[k-1]` are dropped, which gives the chain complex of a quotient.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_chain_homology" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. `H_k(C; Z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_chain_homology_mod_p" => "Return the dimension of one homology group over a prime field. Pass a session-owned chain complex, a nonnegative degree and a prime in the unsigned 64-bit range. `dim H_k(C; Z/p)` for a prime `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
