    "num_homology_join" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The join `K * L` (the vertices of `L` are shifted past those of `K`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_klein_bottle" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The triangulated Klein bottle from an `m x n` grid (`m, n >= 3`): the columns are identified directly, the rows with a reflection.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_matrix_rank" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The rational matrix as `(rows, columns)` of entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_mayer_vietoris" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. Checks the Mayer-Vietoris sequence of `A ∪ B` at the level of ranks. the Euler characteristics add up, and there are nonnegative ranks of the connecting maps that make every stretch of the long exact sequence exact. Returns the ranks of the connecting maps `H_k(A ∪ B) -> H_{k-1}(A ∩ B)` for `k = 1 ..` when the check passes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_persistence_bars" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. Persistent homology over `Z/2` of a filtered complex. The input is `(value, simplex)` pairs (any order; every face of a simplex must be present with a value no larger). Bars of dimension up to `max_dim` with positive length are returned, sorted by dimension, birth, death. This is the standard algorithm: the columns of the boundary matrix, ordered by (value, dimension, lexicographic), are reduced from left to right until their lowest ones are distinct.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_pi1_presentation" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The edge-path presentation of the fundamental group of the component of the smallest vertex: the generators are the edges outside a spanning tree, the relators the boundaries of the triangles.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_presentation_export" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_product" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The triangulated product `K x L` (ordered product of simplices: the vertex `(v, w)` is numbered `index(v) * |L| + index(w)`), or `None` when it would exceed `limit` simplices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_projective_plane" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. A triangulation of the real projective plane: for `k = 0` the minimal one with 6 vertices; for `k >= 3` a disc with `2k` boundary edges whose antipodal boundary points are identified (word `a a`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_rank_mod_p" => "Return the rank of a rectangular integer matrix of at most 128 by 128 over a prime field. The modulus is an exact prime in the unsigned 64-bit range. Negative entries and characteristic two are supported. The rank of a rectangular integer matrix over the field with prime modulus `p`. Binary elimination packs 64 columns into each machine word. # Panics Panics for ragged rows or a modulus below two. The caller must supply a prime.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_reduced_homology" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The reduced homology: `H_0` loses one free summand when the complex is nonempty.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_relative_chains" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The chain complex of the pair `(K, L)`: simplices of `K` not in `L`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_rips_filtration" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The Vietoris-Rips filtration of a finite metric space. The space is given by its distance matrix: every clique of the graph of edges of length at most `max_eps`, up to dimension `max_simplex_dim`, with value its diameter. `None` when more than `limit` simplices would be needed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_simplify_presentation" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. Tietze simplification: removes trivial relators and eliminates generators that occur once in some relator or that a one-letter relator kills. The result is presented with generators `1..=count`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_smith_invariants" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The nonzero invariant factors `d_1 | d_2 | ...` of an integer matrix (its Smith normal form), exactly. Their number is the rank.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_sphere" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The `n`-sphere: the boundary of the `(n+1)`-simplex.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_suspension" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The suspension: two cones over the complex glued along it.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_union" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The union of two complexes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_homology_wedge" => "Native exact algebraic topology with session-owned, kind/version checked complex, chain and presentation handles. Complex inputs/exports use CSR offsets/indices. Handles are invalid in other sessions and after release; all objects own their data. Finite construction bounds: 4096 simplices, 12 vertices per simplex, 65536 face/storage words, 256 generators per chain group and 128 x 128 exact matrix elimination. Presentation simplification accepts sixteen generators and 256 input word coefficients with the native 4000-word intermediate limit; product frontiers are bounded to 1048576 coefficient slots. Cubical chains accept 64 pixels; direct cubical Betti accepts 4096. Filtrations carry a canonical value_unit witness verified against stored radius dimensions. Bars use explicit essential flags and zero death placeholders; bottleneck inputs are births/deaths/essential records and unequal essential counts give positive infinity. Products return None on their native simplex limit; malformed inputs and adapter budget exhaustion are errors. Pure source algorithms are invoked in RSSN kernels; opaque payloads contain only session handles, never serialized pointers. The wedge sum, gluing vertex `vk` of `K` to vertex `vl` of `L`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_integrate" => "Serial adaptive Gauss-Kronrod quadrature: a, b, absolute tolerance, relative tolerance, max panels, callback. Returns value/error/evaluations/converged. Bounds match the callback domain; value/error/absolute tolerance carry domain times codomain dimensions; zero absolute tolerance is polymorphic. Callback errors propagate unchanged, and the estimate is heuristic, not a rigorous enclosure. Adaptive Gauss-Kronrod integration of `f` over `[a, b]`. The interval with the largest error estimate is bisected until the total error is below `max(abs_tol, rel_tol * |value|)` or `max_panels` panels exist. An unconverged refinement may use Wynn extrapolation of recent totals. Cancellation is checked between panels and stops refinement with `converged = false`. The first panel is evaluated even if `max_panels` is zero.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_integrate_infinite" => "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. Integral over `(-inf, inf)` using `x = t/(1-t^2)`, `t in (-1, 1)`.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_integrate_left_infinite" => "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. Integral over `[a, inf)` using `x = a + t/(1-t)`, `t in [0, 1)`.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_integrate_semi_infinite" => "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. Integral over `[a, inf)` using `x = a + t/(1-t)`, `t in [0, 1)`.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_interp_ext_akima" => "Akima 1D cubic spline interpolation. [Akima's local slope weights](https://doi.org/10.1145/321607.321609) reduce oscillation near sharp corners. Queries outside the sampled interval extend the nearest segment's polynomial. One sample defines a constant and two samples define a line. # Errors Unequal sample lengths, empty samples, non-finite inputs, non-increasing knots, or local arithmetic outside the `f64` range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_akima_many" => "Evaluates an Akima interpolant at multiple coordinates, preserving query order. Uses the same knot and extrapolation rules as [`akima`]. Successive queries in the same segment reuse its coefficients. Duplicate queries are allowed. An empty query list returns an empty vector after validating the samples. # Errors Unequal sample lengths, empty samples, non-finite inputs, non-increasing knots, local arithmetic outside the `f64` range, or an unavailable output allocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_bicubic" => "Bicubic interpolation on a rectilinear 2D grid. `grid` is row-major of length `ys.len() * xs.len()` where `grid[j * xs.len() + i]` is the value at `(xs[i], ys[j])`. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`], [`InterpError::Empty`], or [`InterpError::Domain`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_bspline_eval" => "Evaluates a B-spline of given `degree` at `x` using de Boor's algorithm. Requires `knots.len() == coeffs.len() + degree + 1`, non-decreasing `knots`, and positive-width support. Queries outside the support are clamped to its endpoints. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] or [`InterpError::Domain`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_chebyshev_fit" => "The monomial coefficients of the polynomial interpolating `(xs, ys)` at `degree + 1` Chebyshev nodes of the sample's range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_interp1" => "Linear interpolation of `(xs, ys)` at `x`. Outside the range the nearest endpoint is extended linearly from its own segment, which is what a piecewise-linear interpolant does at its ends. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] when the two samples differ in length, and [`InterpError::Empty`] when there are no points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_interp2" => "Bilinear interpolation on a rectilinear grid. `grid` is `ny × nx` in row-major order, with `xs` varying fastest. Finite coordinates outside the grid are clamped to its edges. Use [`BilinearGrid`] to reuse validation across a series of queries. # Errors Rejects empty or invalid axes, mismatched grid storage, and non-finite grid values or queries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_interpn" => "N-dimensional multilinear interpolation on a rectilinear grid. `axes[d]` gives the strictly increasing coordinates along axis `d`, `grid` is row-major of length `prod(axes[d].len())`, and `point` has length `axes.len()`. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`], [`InterpError::Empty`], or [`InterpError::Domain`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_pade" => "Computes the `[m / n]` Padé approximant `P(x) / Q(x)` from Taylor series coefficients `coeffs[0..=m+n]` (ascending power order).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_pchip" => "Piecewise Cubic Hermite Interpolating Polynomial (PCHIP, Fritsch–Carlson). Preserves monotonicity of the data without overshoot between knots. Uses [Fritsch–Butland's weighted harmonic mean](https://doi.org/10.1137/0905021) for interior tangents and clipped one-sided formulas at the endpoints. Queries outside the sampled interval extend the nearest segment's polynomial. One sample defines a constant and two samples define a line. # Errors Unequal sample lengths, empty samples, non-finite inputs, non-increasing knots, or local arithmetic outside the `f64` range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_pchip_many" => "Evaluates a PCHIP interpolant at multiple coordinates, preserving query order. Uses the same knot and extrapolation rules as [`pchip`]. Successive queries in the same segment reuse its coefficients. Duplicate queries are allowed. An empty query list returns an empty vector after validating the samples. # Errors Unequal sample lengths, empty samples, non-finite inputs, non-increasing knots, local arithmetic outside the `f64` range, or an unavailable output allocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_polyfit" => "The coefficients of the least-squares polynomial of `degree`, ascending. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] when the samples differ in length, and [`InterpError::Domain`] when there are fewer points than coefficients.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_polyval" => "Evaluates a polynomial given its coefficients in ascending order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_rational_eval" => "Evaluates a rational function `P(x) / Q(x)` with ascending-power coefficients. # Errors [`InterpError::Domain`] when `Q(x) == 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_shepard_idw" => "Shepard inverse-distance weighting (IDW) scattered-data interpolation. Scales weights by the nearest distance when direct weights exceed the Float range. An exact match uses the first sample at that coordinate. # Errors [`InterpError::Empty`] for no samples, [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] for inconsistent lengths, or [`InterpError::Domain`] for nonfinite data or an invalid power.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interp_ext_spline" => "Natural cubic spline interpolation at `x`. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] when the samples differ in length, and [`InterpError::Domain`] when fewer than three points are given (a natural spline needs a second derivative to pin down).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interpolate_b_spline" => "Evaluates a point on a B-spline curve. B-splines are generalizations of Bézier curves, offering more flexibility and local control. They are defined by control points, a degree, and a knot vector. This function uses the Cox-de Boor recursion formula to evaluate the curve at a given parameter `t`. # Arguments * `control_points` - The control points. * `degree` - The degree of the spline, `p`. * `knots` - The knot vector, `U`. * `t` - The parameter. # Returns The coordinates of the point on the curve. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::interpolate::b_spline; let control_points = vec![vec![0.0], vec![1.0], vec![2.0]]; let knots = vec![0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0]; let p = b_spline(&control_points, 2, &knots, 0.5); assert!((p.unwrap()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-9); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interpolate_bezier_curve" => "Evaluates a point on a Bézier curve defined by a set of control points at parameter `t`. This function uses De Casteljau's algorithm, a numerically stable and efficient method for evaluating Bézier curves. The curve passes through the first and last control points and is tangent to the segments connecting the first two and last two control points. # Arguments * `control_points` - A slice of points, where each point is a slice of `f64` coordinates. * `t` - The parameter, typically in the range `[0, 1]`. # Returns The coordinates of the point on the curve. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::interpolate::bezier_curve; let control_points = vec![vec![0.0, 0.0], vec![1.0, 2.0], vec![2.0, 0.0]]; let p = bezier_curve(&control_points, 0.5); assert!((p[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-9); assert!((p[1] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-9); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interpolate_bilinear_interpolation" => "Bilinear interpolation on the grid `xs x ys` (`values[i + nx * j]`); the 2D case of [`multilinear_interpolation`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interpolate_multilinear_interpolation" => "Multilinear (linear / bilinear / trilinear / ...) interpolation on a rectilinear grid; the dimension is the number of axes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interpolate_tensor_cubic_spline_interpolation" => "Tensor-product cubic spline interpolation on a rectilinear grid (1D, 2D, 3D, ...) with not-a-knot end conditions along every axis.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_interpolate_trilinear_interpolation" => "Trilinear interpolation on the grid `xs x ys x zs` (`values[i + nx * (j + ny * k)]`); the 3D case of [`multilinear_interpolation`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_inverse_beta_reg" => "`num_inverse_beta_reg(a, b, p)` returns the regularized beta quantile at a probability in [0, 1]. Shape parameters must be finite and positive. The general inversion uses a bounded search and reports nonrepresentable forward evaluations as domain errors. The inverse of the regularized incomplete beta function. Returns the `x` in `[0, 1]` such that `I_x(a, b) = p`. Endpoints and the one-unit-shape cases use their analytic values. Other cases bracket the quantile in binary64. Reflection gives the upper-tail probability directly from the forward kernel when `x > 0.5`. # Errors The shapes must be finite and positive, and the probability must lie in `[0, 1]`. A nonrepresentable forward evaluation returns an error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_inverse_erf" => "Computes the inverse error function, erf⁻¹(x). Starts from Winitzki's closed-form approximation and polishes with Halley iterations on `erfc(y) = 1 - x` (for x ≥ 0.5, where `1 - x` is exact and the tail keeps full relative precision) or on `erf(y) = x` otherwise.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_inverse_erfc" => "Returns `y` such that `erfc(y) = p` for `0 < p < 2`. The support endpoints return signed infinities. Inputs outside `[0, 2]` return NaN. Refinement in the far tail uses a logarithmic residual.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_iqr" => "Computes the Interquartile Range (IQR) = Q3 - Q1.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_jackknife" => "Leave-one-out jackknife: sample vector, statistic-evaluation budget, statistic callback. The callback domain must accept variable vector lengths; its output units independently determine estimate/bias/standard-error/replicate units. Budget covers the original estimate plus every leave-one-out sample. Work/storage caps match bootstrap. Callback errors propagate unchanged and stop further host calls. Leave-one-out jackknife resampling for bias and standard error estimation. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Domain`] for fewer than two observations or a non-finite statistic. Callback errors propagate.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_kdtree_build" => "Validate a rectangular point tensor and store it as a portable k-d-tree descriptor. Coordinates retain units. Query operators construct the native tree from the defining points. Builds a k-d tree from `points`. # Errors * [`GeometryError::Empty`] if `points` is empty or 0-dimensional. * [`GeometryError::ShapeMismatch`] if points have differing dimensions. * [`GeometryError::Domain`] if a coordinate is non-finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_kdtree_nearest" => "Exact k-nearest queries on a portable k-d-tree descriptor. Returns index and distance tensors; query coordinates share descriptor units. Finds up to `k` nearest points to `query`, sorted by ascending distance. # Errors As in [`Self::nearest`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_kdtree_nearest_batch" => "Finds one nearest point per query row in a portable k-d tree, reconstructing the tree once. Indices follow query order and distances retain the descriptor's coordinate dimension. An empty query matrix returns empty index and distance vectors. Finds the nearest point for every query in input order, using the same selection rule as [`Self::nearest`]. Large batches use the execution policy for parallel mapping. A query does not allocate its own buffer. # Errors The error from the first invalid query, as in [`Self::nearest`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_kdtree_radius" => "Radius queries with coordinate and radius units checked against the descriptor. Returns indices and measured distances. Finds all points within Euclidean `radius` of `query`, sorted by ascending distance. A negative radius returns an empty result. Positive infinity includes every point. # Errors As in [`Self::nearest`]. A NaN radius returns [`GeometryError::Domain`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_kdtree_single_nearest" => "Nearest query on the portable native k-d tree, preserving coordinate units. A portable tree requires at least one point. Finds the nearest point to `query`, returning `(point_index, euclidean_distance)`. Distance is infinite if the mathematical distance exceeds the Float range. # Errors [`GeometryError::ShapeMismatch`] if `query.len()` does not match the tree dimension. [`GeometryError::Domain`] if a query coordinate is non-finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_koebe_distortion_bounds" => "Koebe lower and upper bounds on |f'(z)| for normalized univalent functions at |z|=r in [0,1). Koebe distortion theorem bounds for \$f \\\\in \\\\mathcal{S}\$ at \$|z| = r \\\\in [0, 1)\$: \$\\\\frac{1-r}{(1+r)^3} \\\\le |f'(z)| \\\\le \\\\frac{1+r}{(1-r)^3}\$.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_koebe_growth_bounds" => "Koebe lower and upper bounds on |f(z)| for normalized univalent functions at |z|=r in [0,1). Koebe growth theorem bounds for \$f \\\\in \\\\mathcal{S}\$ at \$|z| = r \\\\in [0, 1)\$: \$\\\\frac{r}{(1+r)^2} \\\\le |f(z)| \\\\le \\\\frac{r}{(1-r)^2}\$.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_koebe_rotation_bound" => "Goluzin's sharp upper bound in radians on the absolute continuous argument of f'(z) for |z|=r in [0,1). Returns Goluzin’s sharp rotation bound on the circle of radius `r`. Goluzin's sharp rotation theorem bound on \$|\\\\arg f'(z)|\$ for \$f \\\\in \\\\mathcal{S}\$ at \$|z| = r \\\\in [0, 1)\$: \$4 \\\\arcsin(r)\$ for \$r \\\\le 1/\\\\sqrt{2}\$, and \$\\\\pi + \\\\ln\\\\frac{r^2}{1 - r^2}\$ for \$1/\\\\sqrt{2} < r < 1\$.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_kurtosis" => "Computes the unbiased sample excess kurtosis `G2` (the estimator used by Excel's `KURT` and `scipy.stats.kurtosis(fisher=True, bias=False)`); a normal distribution has expected value 0.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_l1_norm" => "Manhattan vector norm. Computes the Manhattan norm (\$`L_1`\$ norm) of a vector. Formula: \$||\\\\mathbf{v}||_1 = \\\\sum_{i=1}^n |`v_i`|\$\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_lbfgs" => "Dimensionless serial optimization: initial vector,[lower bounds,upper bounds for lbfgs_b],positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,objective callback,gradient callback. Both callbacks share a strict evaluation budget. Gradient length matches initial vector; L-BFGS methods retain ten correction pairs. Returns x,value,iterations,evaluations,converged; callback failures propagate unchanged. Limited-memory BFGS with history `m` (two-loop recursion).\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_lbfgs_b" => "Dimensionless serial optimization: initial vector,[lower bounds,upper bounds for lbfgs_b],positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,objective callback,gradient callback. Both callbacks share a strict evaluation budget. Gradient length matches initial vector; L-BFGS methods retain ten correction pairs. Returns x,value,iterations,evaluations,converged; callback failures propagate unchanged. Box-constrained L-BFGS (`lower <= x <= upper`) using projected gradient steps and active-bound clamping.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_lbfgs_b_scaled" => "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. Box-constrained L-BFGS (`lower <= x <= upper`) using projected gradient steps and active-bound clamping.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_lbfgs_scaled" => "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. Limited-memory BFGS with history `m` (two-loop recursion).\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_least_squares" => "Damped nonlinear least squares with a scoped vector residual callback. Initial parameters and residuals may have independent dimensions. Returns the best point, residual norm, work counters, convergence and explicit stopping status; callback failures propagate. Minimise one half of the squared residual norm using a damped, column-scaled augmented SVD. The Jacobian may be analytic or safely differenced. The reported scaled gradient is formed from `J[:,j]/max(abs(J[:,j]))` and `f/max(abs(f))`; it is a dimensionless first-order test, not a root proof. Trial steps are accepted only on positive actual and predicted decrease. Residual length must remain constant on every evaluation. # Errors Invalid input, malformed callback results, or a failed decomposition return a [`LeastSquaresError`]. Nonconvergence is reported as a result status.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_linear_amg_build" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Builds the hierarchy for a symmetric positive definite matrix. # Errors [`AmgError::BadMatrix`] for a non-square matrix or a non-positive diagonal entry.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_general_build" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Builds the hierarchy. # Errors [`AmgError::BadMatrix`] for a non-square matrix, a non-positive diagonal entry, or near-null-space vectors of the wrong length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_general_num_levels" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Number of levels including the coarsest.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_general_operator_complexity" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Sum of the nonzeros of all level operators divided by the fine one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_general_precondition" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Preconditioner action `z = M^{-1} r` (one cycle from zero). # Panics Panics if either vector length differs from the order of the fine operator.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_general_precondition_many" => "Applies the general AMG preconditioner from zero to each row of a rank-two RHS array. Rows share one hierarchy and one reusable work buffer. The result keeps the input shape and order, with reference scale and dimension equal to the RHS divided by the operator. Applies the preconditioner from zero with a caller-owned workspace. # Errors Returns [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] before modifying `z` or `workspace` if either vector has the wrong length or the workspace is too short.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_general_solve" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Solves `A x = b` by repeated cycles. # Panics Panics if the right-hand side or supplied initial iterate has the wrong length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_general_vcycle" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. One cycle for `A x = b` starting from `x`. # Panics Panics if either vector length differs from the order of the fine operator.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_general_workspace_len" => "Reports the number of reusable Float slots needed by one cycle of the general AMG hierarchy. A terminal-only hierarchy requires zero slots. Number of `f64` entries needed by the caller-supplied cycle workspace. Storage is one finest-level vector shared by all levels and two vectors sized for each coarser level. A hierarchy consisting of only the terminal level needs no workspace.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_num_levels" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Number of levels including the coarsest.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_operator_complexity" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Sum of the nonzeros of all level operators divided by the fine one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_precondition" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Preconditioner action `z = M^{-1} r` (one cycle from zero), symmetric positive definite for symmetric `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_solve" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Solves `A x = b` by repeated cycles; returns the solution, the relative residual history and whether `tol` was reached.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_amg_vcycle" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. One cycle for `A x = b` starting from `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_anderson" => "Actual native low-rank/operator method. Matrices and state arrays retain explicit physical references; orthonormal factors are dimensionless. Shapes, iterations, callbacks and allocations are bounded. Solves `x = g(x)` by Anderson acceleration with a window of `window` previous iterates. Each step mixes the last iterates so that the linearised residual is smallest in the least-squares sense; with `window = 0` it is the plain iteration `x <- g(x)`. `damping` in `(0, 1]` scales the step (`1` is the undamped method). A step whose least-squares problem is degenerate, or that produces non-finite numbers, falls back to the plain iterate and restarts the window. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] when `g` changes the length of the vector or the start is not finite; [`LowRankError::NoConvergence`] when the residual is still above `tol` after `max_evaluations`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_arnoldi" => "Builds a dimensionless Krylov basis and the Hessenberg matrix for the normalized linear operator. The result contains basis, hessenberg, beta and the measured operator_reference. Multiply the Hessenberg coefficients and residual beta by that reference to restore physical units. Computes a matrix-free Arnoldi factorisation with up to `m` directions. The operator must overwrite its output slice with `A x`. The return value `(V, H, beta)` satisfies `A V_m ≈ V_m H + beta v_{m+1} e_m^T`. The first vector in `V` is the normalised `v0` and the last of its `m + 1` vectors is the normalised residual direction. `H` is `m x m` and upper Hessenberg. Iteration stops when the residual is at most `1e-14` times the norm of the current operator image or when the ambient dimension is exhausted. Unused vectors and columns are zero-padded, with `beta = 0` after this breakdown. A zero or empty start gives all zeros and skips the operator. A non-finite start or operator image, or overflow in a projected coefficient or residual norm, is signalled by NaN in `beta` and on the failing diagonal of `H`, if present. Earlier completed columns and basis vectors are retained.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_array_data" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_array_new" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Converts back to an `ndarray::ArrayD`. # Errors Returns an error if the shape and data are inconsistent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_array_norm" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Computes the Frobenius norm of a tensor.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_as_preconditioner_apply" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Borrows the selected factors as a [`Preconditioner`] for [`cg_with`], [`gmres_with`] and [`bicgstab_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_auto_apply" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Writes `M^-1 r` into `z`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_auto_kind" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. The preconditioner that [`AutoPreconditioner::new`] selected.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_auto_new" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Builds the strongest incomplete factorisation that succeeds; the chain always terminates with Jacobi, so this never fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_auto_preconditioner" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Builds an [`AutoPreconditioner`] for `a`; see [`AutoPreconditioner::new`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_b_is_positive_definite" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Whether the symmetric operator `apply_b` is numerically positive definite: its smallest eigenvalue (Lanczos) exceeds `1e-10` times its spectral radius. A failed estimate counts as not positive definite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_berlekamp_massey" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. Berlekamp-Massey: the shortest linear recurrence of a sequence over `GF(p)`, as the monic connection polynomial `c` (lowest degree first) with `sum_k c[k] * s[i + k] = 0` for every `i`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bicgstab_l" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. `BiCGSTAB`(`l`) with right preconditioning (`l >= 1`). `l = 1` is classical `BiCGSTAB`; `l = 2` or more smooths the residual polynomial with a degree-`l` minimal-residual step, which avoids the breakdown of `BiCGSTAB` on matrices with strongly complex spectra. The iteration count is the number of outer cycles started. Each complete cycle uses `2 l` matrix-vector products, plus the initial and final verification for the solve. Convergence is verified against the original system using the requested nonnegative, finite tolerance. With a zero right-hand side, the reported relative residual is zero for an exact solution and infinite otherwise. Both callbacks must overwrite every output entry with finite values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bicgstab_with" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. `BiCGSTAB` with an explicit [`Preconditioner`]; see [`bicgstab`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bilu0_apply" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. `z = (L U)^-1 r`: block forward then backward substitution. # Panics A slice shorter than the matrix extent can panic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_block_jacobi_apply" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. `z = M^-1 r`, block by block. Blocks are independent, so large systems invert them in parallel without changing a bit: every output block keeps its own arithmetic order. # Panics A slice shorter than the matrix extent can panic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_block_jacobi_block_size" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. The block size.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_bilu0" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Block ILU(0): incomplete block LU keeping the block sparsity pattern. # Errors Non-square or malformed input, an unrepresentable allocation, [`BsrError::MissingDiagonal`] for an absent block, or [`BsrError::SingularBlock`] for a singular pivot.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_block_cols" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Number of block columns.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_block_count" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Number of stored blocks.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_block_jacobi" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Block-Jacobi preconditioner: the inverse of every diagonal block. # Errors Non-square or malformed input, an unrepresentable allocation, or [`BsrError::SingularBlock`] for the first absent or singular diagonal.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_block_rows" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Number of block rows.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_diagonal_blocks" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. The square diagonal blocks, one dense row-major `b x b` block per block row (all zero when the block is not stored). # Errors Non-square or malformed input, or an unrepresentable allocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_fill_ratio" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Stored scalars divided by true nonzeros (`1.0` for an empty matrix). `1.0` means the blocks are completely dense; the larger the value the more padding zeros are stored.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_from_csr" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Converts a CSR matrix with one entry per coordinate. A block is stored when it contains a nonzero, and zeros inside the block are padding. # Errors An incompatible block shape, malformed CSR arrays, or an unrepresentable allocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_from_triplets" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Builds a matrix from `(row, col, value)` triplets. Out-of-range triplets are ignored, duplicate coordinates are summed in input order, and canceling blocks are omitted. # Errors An incompatible block shape or an unrepresentable allocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_matmul" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. The product `A B`; block shape `(self.br, other.bc)`. Blocks that cancel to exactly zero are omitted. Large products share independent ranges of block rows across workers while keeping the accumulation order within each output block fixed. # Errors Different inner extents or block sizes, malformed sparse arrays, or an unrepresentable or unavailable sparse allocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_matmul_dense" => "Multiplies a block sparse matrix by a rank-two array of right-hand sides. The result keeps the product of the operands' reference scales and physical dimensions. Multiplies a row-major dense matrix of shape `(ncols, rhs_cols)` by this BSR matrix on the left. Returns row-major `(nrows, rhs_cols)`. Each output is rounded once from the exact dot product, so running [`Self::matvec`] on each RHS column gives the same bits. # Errors A malformed BSR matrix, a dense operand that disagrees with its given width, or an unrepresentable or unavailable output allocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_matvec" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Matrix-vector product `y = A x`. Every entry of `y` is the correctly rounded dot product of its row, so the result equals [`Csr::matvec`] of [`Bsr::to_csr`] bit for bit, whatever the block shape; block rows run in parallel for large matrices without changing a bit. Only the first `min(y.len(), nrows)` output entries are written. # Panics A malformed matrix or an input missing a referenced entry can panic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_matvec_transpose" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Transposed product `y = A^T x` (`y` has `ncols` entries). Accumulates block by block in plain `f64`, which can round differently from the exact dot products in `transpose().matvec`. # Panics A malformed matrix or slices shorter than the corresponding extents can panic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_nnz" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Number of nonzero scalars.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_stored_len" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Number of stored scalars (`block_count * br * bc`, padding included).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_to_csr" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Converts to CSR, dropping the zeros inside blocks. # Panics A malformed matrix or a scalar row count too large for the CSR row-pointer array can panic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_transpose" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. The transpose, with block shape `(bc, br)`. # Panics A malformed matrix or a block-column count too large for the transposed row-pointer array can panic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_validate" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Checks the representation invariants. # Errors A description of the first violated invariant.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_bsr_zeros" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. An all-zero matrix with no stored blocks. # Errors A zero or incompatible block extent, or an unrepresentable allocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cg_with" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. Conjugate gradients with an explicit [`Preconditioner`]. [`Preconditioner::None`] dispatches to [`cg`] with [`identity_precond`], so selection and direct invocation use the same scale-safe algorithm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_choose_block_size" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Picks the block shape for `csr` among `candidates`. A candidate qualifies when it divides the matrix shape and its fill ratio (stored scalars per true nonzero, see [`Bsr::fill_ratio`]) is at most [`MAX_FILL_RATIO`]. The largest qualifying block wins, ties going to the lower fill ratio. The result is `(1, 1)` for malformed CSR arrays or when no candidate qualifies.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_complex_schur" => "Native complex Schur decomposition of normalized coefficients with a positive physical entry reference; q is dimensionless and t uses the retained entry reference. Complex Schur decomposition `A = Q T Q^H` (`T` upper triangular, `Q` unitary) of a dense square matrix by Householder reduction to Hessenberg form and Wilkinson-shifted QR iterations with deflation. Returns `None` if the iteration does not converge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_contract" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Contracts a single tensor along two specified axes (a generalised trace): the result has rank `ndim - 2` and entries `sum_t a[..., t, ..., t, ...]`, the remaining axes in their original order. # Errors Returns an error if the axes are equal, out of range, or of different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_contract_rectangular" => "Native generic contraction specialized to f64. The compiled scalar callback receives row, inner index, column, has_previous, previous in that order; index/presence inputs are dimensionless and previous/output use the explicit reference dimension. Indices are zero based and coherent physical values are normalized only at the callback boundary. Compute a rectangular product in row-major, first-product accumulation order. `accumulate(row, index, column, previous)` performs one scalar product and, when present, adds it to `previous`. This supports exact, complex and physical host scalars without importing their runtime representation into the kernel. Zero-length contractions obtain each additive identity from `zero`. # Errors Returns [`ContractionError::ShapeOverflow`] when the output size is invalid or the allocation fails, or [`ContractionError::Element`] for the first scalar failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cp_als" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain. CP (CANDECOMP/PARAFAC) decomposition by alternating least squares. Factorises a dense tensor as `T ~= sum_r lambda_r a_r o b_r o c_r` for `options.rank` components, by solving one matricised least-squares problem per mode per sweep (the standard ALS update on the mode-`k` unfolding against the Khatri-Rao product of the other factors). Factors are initialised uniformly at random from `options.seed` and normalised after every update, with the removed norms kept in [`CpDecomposition::weights`]. A rank-`R` tensor whose factors span a full-rank problem is recovered to rounding error, but the factors themselves are only determined up to permutation and scaling: compare reconstructions, not factors. CP is ill-posed, so the result says what happened rather than assuming success. `converged` means the residual reached `tol`; `stagnated` means sweeps stopped improving while the residual stayed above `tol`; `diverged` means a weight blew up, which is how a degenerate problem (border rank below rank) shows itself; `rank_deficient` means the least-squares system was numerically rank deficient in its direct SVD solve. # Errors Returns an error for a tensor of rank below 2, an empty dimension, non-finite entries/options, a zero rank, zero sweeps, or rescaling overflow. Updates solve the Khatri-Rao least-squares system directly by SVD, once per mode, avoiding normal equations and squared condition numbers. Input normalization protects uniformly very small or very large tensors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cp_diagnostics" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cp_factors" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cp_options" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cp_options_new" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain. Options for rank `rank` with `500` sweeps, `tol = 1e-12`, seed `0` and a divergence limit of `1e6`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cp_reconstruct" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain. The dense tensor `sum_r weights[r] * a_r o b_r o ..` of a CP model, with `factors[k]` of size `n_k x rank`. # Errors Returns an error if there are fewer than two factors, a factor does not have one column per weight, or an extent is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cp_reconstruction" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cp_weights" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_cg" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Solves a sparse linear system `Ax=b` using the Conjugate Gradient method. This method is suitable for symmetric, positive-definite matrices. It is an iterative algorithm that converges to the exact solution in at most `n` iterations (where `n` is the matrix dimension) in exact arithmetic, but is typically stopped earlier based on a tolerance. The tolerance is an absolute bound on the Euclidean norm of the residual `b - A x`, checked before the first iteration and after every update. If the iteration limit is reached without meeting it, the current iterate is returned anyway. faer 0.24 does ship a conjugate gradient (`faer::matrix_free::conjugate_gradient`), but it tests a *relative* residual, reports `NonConvergence` as an error and requires a positive definite operator, so using it would change all three behaviours; the loop is kept, iterating over the faer storage. # Arguments * `a` - The sparse matrix `A` ([`SparseMat`]). * `b` - The vector `b` (`Array1<f64>`). * `x0` - An initial guess for the solution `x`. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations. * `tolerance` - The desired tolerance for the residual norm. # Returns A `Result` containing the solution vector `x`, or an error string. # Errors Returns an error if the matrix and vector dimensions are incompatible.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_frobenius_norm" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Computes the Frobenius norm from the live CSC entries. Extreme magnitudes are rescaled when the direct sum of squares leaves the normal range. An empty matrix has norm zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_from_csr_data" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Converts back to a [`SparseMat`] sparse matrix. # Panics Panics if `indptr`, `indices` and `data` are not a consistent CSR representation of a `rows` by `cols` matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_from_dense" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Converts a dense `ndarray::Array` to a sparse matrix. Zero entries are dropped, so the result holds one stored entry per non-zero. # Arguments * `arr` - The dense `ndarray::Array` to convert. # Returns A [`SparseMat`] representing the sparse matrix. # Panics Panics if the input array is not 2D.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_from_triplets" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Creates a sparse matrix from a list of triplets. The historical name is kept for API stability; faer stores the result in CSC form regardless of the name. Duplicate `(row, col)` entries are summed, as they were before. # Arguments * `rows` - The number of rows in the matrix. * `cols` - The number of columns in the matrix. * `triplets` - A slice of `(row_index, col_index, value)` tuples representing the non-zero entries. # Returns A [`SparseMat`] representing the sparse matrix. # Panics Panics if any triplet index is out of bounds for the given shape.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_is_diagonal" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Checks if the sparse matrix is diagonal.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_is_symmetric" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Checks if the sparse matrix is symmetric (\$A = A^T\$).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_l1_norm" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Computes the maximum absolute column sum from the live CSC entries. An empty matrix has norm zero. Column sums containing `NaN` are skipped.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_linf_norm" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Computes the maximum absolute row sum from the live CSC entries. An empty matrix has norm zero. Row sums containing `NaN` are skipped.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_matvec" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Performs sparse matrix-vector multiplication for a sparse matrix and a standard `Vec`. The matrix is stored in CSC, so the loop walks columns: every stored entry contributes `val * vector[column]` to `result[row]`. This is the same arithmetic as `y[i] = sum_j A[i][j] * x[j]`, but it never needs the transpose of the matrix. # Arguments * `matrix` - The sparse matrix. * `vector` - The dense vector. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<f64>` representing the result of the multiplication. # Errors Returns an error if matrix and vector dimensions are not compatible.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_matvec_into" => "Writes A times x to an existing native vector. The destination has one value per matrix row and the product dimension. Its reference can differ from the product reference. An error leaves the destination unchanged. Computes `A x` into caller-owned storage, replacing every output element. # Errors The input needs one value per column and the output one per row. On a shape error the output is untouched.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_norms" => "Returns the Frobenius, induced 1-norm and induced infinity norm from the live CSC entries. Each field carries the matrix entry dimension and physical reference. An empty matrix returns three zeros. Computes the Frobenius, induced 1- and induced infinity norms together. Ordinary inputs use one traversal of the live entries. Extreme scales may need additional passes. Empty matrices produce three zeros.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_rank" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Computes the rank of a sparse matrix by converting to dense and performing RREF. Note: This is inefficient for large sparse matrices and should only be used for small matrices. # Arguments * `matrix` - The sparse matrix. # Returns The rank of the matrix as a `usize`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_to_dense" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Converts a sparse matrix to a dense `ndarray::Array2`. # Arguments * `matrix` - The sparse matrix. # Returns An `ndarray::Array2<f64>` representing the dense matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_trace" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Computes the trace of a square sparse matrix. # Errors Returns an error if the matrix is not square.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_transpose" => "Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns. Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work and allocations are bounded. Transposes a sparse matrix. The transpose is a column-major view of a row-major matrix, so faer converts it without re-sorting the entries. # Panics Panics if the matrix cannot be converted to column-major format.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_transpose_matvec" => "Computes A transpose times x directly from the CSC columns. The input has one value per matrix row. The output has one value per matrix column, with the product of the input dimensions and reference scales. Computes `Aᵀ x` by taking a dot product over each original column. Each dot product uses the stored order of the live entries. # Errors The vector length differs from the number of rows of `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csc_transpose_matvec_into" => "Writes A transpose times x to an existing native vector without constructing a transpose. The destination has one value per column and the product dimension. An error leaves the destination unchanged. Computes `Aᵀ x` into caller-owned storage, replacing every output element. # Errors The input needs one value per row and the output one per column. On a shape error the output is untouched.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_amd_ordering" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Approximate minimum degree ordering (quotient graph with element absorption, supervariables and mass elimination); see [`crate::kernels::ordering::amd_order`]. Returns `perm[new] = old`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_bandwidth" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Half bandwidth `max |i - j|` over the stored entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_diagonal" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Diagonal of the matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_ic0" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. IC(0) factorisation `A ≈ L D L^T` (incomplete Cholesky without fill). The lower triangular factor uses the strictly lower pattern of `A`; the upper triangle of `A` is ignored, so the matrix must be symmetric and positive definite for `M = L D L^T` to be a valid CG preconditioner. On a symmetric matrix this is the same operator as [`Csr::ilu0`] written as `L D L^T` instead of `L U`. # Returns `None` for malformed CSR, a missing diagonal, a nonpositive pivot, or unrepresentable lower-triangular factor entries. The factor stores only the strict lower triangle, its transpose and the pivots.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_ilu0" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. ILU(0) factorisation (incomplete LU keeping the sparsity pattern). The factors are stored in the pattern of `A`: the strictly lower entries of `L` (unit diagonal) and the entries of `U` in the same arrays. No fill is created and no pivoting is performed. # Returns `None` when the matrix has no entry on the diagonal of some row or when a zero pivot is met (structurally or after elimination). The factorisation never returns `NaN`: on `None` the caller keeps the unpreconditioned path or falls back to [`Csr::jacobi_inverse`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_ilut" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Compute threshold incomplete LU with deterministic bounded row storage. # Errors Returns [`IlutError`] for malformed input, non-finite data, unsafe pivots or unrepresentable factorisation arithmetic. No pivoting occurs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_jacobi_inverse" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Jacobi preconditioner apply function data: the inverse diagonal.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_matvec" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Matrix-vector product `y = A x`. Every row is an independent sequential dot product, so large matrices are processed in parallel blocks of rows without changing a bit of the result.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_min_degree_ordering" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Minimum-degree ordering on the elimination graph of the symmetrised pattern: repeatedly eliminates a vertex of smallest current degree, turning its neighbourhood into a clique (exact degrees, lazily maintained heap). Returns `perm[new] = old`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_nested_dissection_ordering" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Nested dissection ordering; see [`crate::kernels::ordering::nested_dissection_order`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_new" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Builds a matrix from `(row, col, value)` triplets; duplicates are summed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_norm1" => "Native sparse matrix 1-norm preserving the matrix entry reference. Maximum absolute column sum of a sparse matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_permute_symmetric" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Symmetric permutation `B[i][j] = A[perm[i]][perm[j]]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_rcm_ordering" => "Native sparse matrix operation with retained entry reference and physical unit domain. Indices are zero based; normalized array storage, capability kind/session and size/work budgets are checked. Reverse Cuthill-McKee ordering of the symmetrised pattern, with a George-Liu pseudo-peripheral start vertex per connected component. Uses the leading `min(rows, cols)` square, with every stored off-diagonal entry contributing an edge regardless of its value. Components, including isolated vertices, are processed in ascending order of their smallest vertex, then the complete order is reversed. Returns `perm` with `perm[new] = old`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_sparse_lu" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Sparse LU factorisation with a fill-reducing symmetric ordering and threshold partial pivoting (left-looking Gilbert-Peierls with a depth-first symbolic reach); see [`SparseLu`]. # Errors [`SparseLuError::NotSquare`] or [`SparseLuError::Singular`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_csr_sparse_lu_with" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. As [`Csr::sparse_lu`] with an explicit pivot threshold in `(0, 1]`: the diagonal entry is kept as pivot when it is at least `threshold` times the largest candidate (`1.0` is classical partial pivoting, small values favour sparsity). # Errors [`SparseLuError::NotSquare`] or [`SparseLuError::Singular`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cubic_spline_interpolation" => "Native interpolation over normalized samples with explicit coordinate and ordinate references. Physical queries and outputs are checked against retained domains; point/order/allocation bounds are enforced. Creates a cubic spline interpolator for a given set of points. Cubic spline interpolation constructs a piecewise cubic polynomial that passes through each data point, ensuring smoothness (continuity of first and second derivatives) at the interior points. The input points must be sorted by their x-coordinates. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples. Must be sorted by x. # Returns A `Result` containing a closure `Arc<dyn Fn(f64) -> f64>` that can be used to evaluate the spline at any point. # Errors Returns an error if fewer than two points are provided, or if the x-coordinates are not strictly increasing. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::interpolate::cubic_spline_interpolation; let points = vec![(0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 1.0), (2.0, 0.0)]; let spline = cubic_spline_interpolation(&points).unwrap(); // Check that interpolated value is reasonable (between the y values) let val = spline(0.5); assert!((spline(0.5) - 0.6875).abs() < 1e-9); assert!(val > 0.0 && val < 1.0); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_cubic_spline_with" => "Native interpolation over normalized samples with explicit coordinate and ordinate references. Physical queries and outputs are checked against retained domains; point/order/allocation bounds are enforced. Cubic spline interpolation with a selectable end condition. Fourth order in the node spacing when the end condition matches the data — clamped with the exact end slopes, or not-a-knot — and second order for the natural condition, whose zero end curvature a general smooth function violates. Values outside the node range are extrapolated with the first or the last cubic. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples with strictly increasing `x`; at least three of them, four for [`SplineEnd::NotAKnot`]. * `ends` - The end condition. # Returns A `Result` containing a closure `Arc<dyn Fn(f64) -> f64>` evaluating the spline. # Errors Returns an error for too few points, non-increasing abscissae, or a singular system. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::interpolate::SplineEnd; use rssn::kernels::interpolate::cubic_spline_with; let points = [(0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 1.0), (2.0, 0.0), (3.0, 1.0)]; let spline = cubic_spline_with(&points, SplineEnd::NotAKnot).unwrap(); assert!((spline(1.0) - 1.0).abs() < 1e-12); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_curve_eval" => "Native interpolation over normalized samples with explicit coordinate and ordinate references. Physical queries and outputs are checked against retained domains; point/order/allocation bounds are enforced.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_delaunay_linear_2d" => "Native normalized-only convenience API: coordinates and ordinates are strictly dimensionless. Corresponding physical adapters accept explicit independent coordinate and ordinate references. Point/order/allocation bounds are enforced. 2D scattered-data linear interpolation over the Delaunay triangulation of `points`. Uses barycentric coordinates inside the containing Delaunay triangle; if `query` lies outside the convex hull, falls back to Shepard IDW (`power = 2`). # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] or [`InterpError::Domain`] when fewer than 3 points are given.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_delaunay_linear_2d_physical" => "Native Delaunay interpolation in the explicitly scaled coordinate metric: samples are normalized by independent positive X/Y references, queries carry the corresponding axis units, and values use a positive ordinate reference. Changing unequal coordinate reference scales intentionally changes the triangulation metric. 2D scattered-data linear interpolation over the Delaunay triangulation of `points`. Uses barycentric coordinates inside the containing Delaunay triangle; if `query` lies outside the convex hull, falls back to Shepard IDW (`power = 2`). # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] or [`InterpError::Domain`] when fewer than 3 points are given.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_dense_cond_norm" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Condition number in the selected norm; singular matrices have infinite condition number. # Errors Returns shape, non-finite input, or factorization errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_dense_eigen" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a general (non-symmetric) real matrix. The matrix is first balanced by an exact power-of-two diagonal similarity, reduced to upper Hessenberg form by Householder similarities and driven to real Schur form by the implicitly shifted QR algorithm (Francis double shift). A complex-conjugate pair of a real matrix therefore appears as a 2x2 diagonal block and is returned as two complex eigenvalues with complex eigenvectors; the transformation is accumulated, so the vectors belong to the original matrix. Eigenvectors of the quasi-triangular factor are obtained by block back substitution. For a symmetric matrix prefer [`eigen_symmetric`]: it is cheaper and returns real orthonormal vectors. A defective matrix (a Jordan block, for instance) has fewer independent eigenvectors than eigenvalues. This routine returns one vector per eigenvalue, flags the result through [`Eigen::defective`] and leaves the dependent vectors parallel; see the module-level limitations. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] for non-square input, [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] when a Schur eigenvalue needs more than `200 n` QR sweeps.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_dense_eigen_generalized_symmetric" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Dense symmetric generalised eigenproblem `A x = lambda B x` (`A`, `B` symmetric). For positive definite `B` (Cholesky `B = L L^T` succeeds) the problem is reduced to the symmetric `L^{-1} A L^{-T}`; the eigenvalues are real and the eigenvectors `B`-orthonormal. For **indefinite** `B` the Cholesky reduction does not exist, and the eigenvalues are those of `B^{-1} A` (LU solve, then the general QR algorithm), which may be complex pairs; no eigenvectors are returned then. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] for mismatched or non-square input, [`DenseError::Singular`] when `B` is indefinite and singular, [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] from the QR iteration.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_dense_eigenvalues" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Eigenvalues `(re, im)` of a general real square matrix (Hessenberg reduction by elimination and the shifted QR algorithm of EISPACK `hqr`). # Errors [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] when a root needs more than 60 iterations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_dense_norm" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Matrix norm with checked shape and finite entries. # Errors Returns shape, non-finite input, or SVD convergence errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_echelon_odd_permutation" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The sign of the permutation that brings the pivots to the diagonal (`true` for odd), for a square matrix of full rank.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_echelon_rank" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The rank over the pivotable columns.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Implicitly restarted Arnoldi for the `k` wanted eigenpairs of a general real operator. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k >= n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if the small dense eigenproblem fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_gen_shift_invert" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. General generalised eigenproblem `A x = lambda B x`. `A` is an arbitrary real matrix and `B` real and nonsingular: the `k` eigenvalues closest to `opts.sigma` by Krylov-Schur on `(A - sigma B)^{-1} B`. Eigenvalues may be complex. The residuals are the absolute 2-norms in the original pencil, as in [`eigs_gen_sym_shift_invert`]. # Errors As [`eigs_krylov_schur`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_gen_sym_shift_invert" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Symmetric generalised eigenproblem `A x = lambda B x` (`A` symmetric, `B` symmetric, positive definite for the Lanczos path): the `k` eigenvalues closest to `opts.sigma`. `solve(x, y)` writes `(A - sigma B)^{-1} x` into `y`. Block Lanczos runs in the `B` inner product on `(A - sigma B)^{-1} B`, which is self-adjoint there; `theta` maps back to `lambda = sigma + 1/theta`. The returned eigenvectors are `B`-orthonormal and `residuals` are the true norms `||A x - lambda B x||₂`. Convergence uses the Ritz residuals of the transformed operator. The callbacks write the full action of a linear map. For a purely real eigenvector the residual requires one application of each operator. **Indefinite `B`.** The `B` inner product only exists for positive definite `B`. The solver first estimates the extreme eigenvalues of `B` by Lanczos ([`b_is_positive_definite`]); when `B` is not (numerically) positive definite it falls back to [`eigs_gen_shift_invert`] (Krylov-Schur on `(A - sigma B)^{-1} B`, any nonsingular `B`), whose eigenvalues may be complex pairs and whose eigenvectors are 2-normalised, not `B`-orthonormal. # Errors As [`eigs_sym_block`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_jd" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Jacobi-Davidson for the `k` eigenvalues of a general **non-symmetric** real operator closest to the (complex) `target`, interior eigenvalues included. The method works in complex arithmetic with a *partial Schur* deflation: the search space stays orthogonal to the locked Schur vectors `Q` (`A Q = Q R`, `R` upper triangular), harmonic Ritz pairs of the pencil `(W^H V, W^H W)`, `W = (I - Q Q^H)(A - target) V`, are extracted with the complex Schur decomposition [`complex_schur`], and the correction equation `(I - Q~ Q~^H)(A - theta)(I - Q~ Q~^H) t = -r` (`Q~ = [Q, u]`) is solved by complex GMRES, optionally right-preconditioned by `precond` (`K^{-1}` for a real `K ~ A - theta`, applied to the real and imaginary parts). Eigenvalues are the diagonal of `R` and the eigenvectors are `Q y` with `y` the eigenvectors of `R`; `residuals` are true residuals `|A x - lambda x|`. As in [`eigs_jd_sym`], iteration continues after `k` locks until no unlocked candidate is closer to the target. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if a projected problem fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_jd_sym" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Jacobi-Davidson for the `k` eigenvalues of a symmetric operator closest to `target` (interior eigenvalues included). The search space is expanded with the solution `t` of the projected correction equation `(I - Q Q^T)(A - theta I)(I - Q Q^T) t = -r` (`Q` holds the locked eigenvectors and the current Ritz vector), solved approximately by GMRES; `precond`, if given, writes `K^{-1} r` for an approximation `K` of `A - theta I` and is made consistent with the projector. Harmonic Ritz extraction picks the Ritz vector with the smallest `|A - target|` residual direction, which is reliable for interior targets where ordinary Rayleigh-Ritz values do not converge to the nearest eigenvalues. Converged vectors are locked and deflated. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if a projected eigenproblem fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_jd_two_sided" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Two-sided Jacobi-Davidson for the `k` eigenvalues of a general real operator nearest to the (complex) `target`, with **left and right** eigenvectors. Two search spaces `V` (right) and `W` (left), each orthonormal, are expanded together. The projected pencil `(W^H (A - tau) V, W^H V)` gives Petrov-Galerkin pairs: the Ritz value `theta`, the right Ritz vector `u = V y` with residual `r = (A - tau - theta) u` orthogonal to `W`, and the left Ritz vector `w = W z` with residual `s = (A - tau - theta)^H w` orthogonal to `V`; the left vectors of the small pencil come from the inverse of its right-eigenvector matrix, so the *two-sided Rayleigh quotient* `theta = w^H A u / w^H u` is stationary (its error is quadratic in the residuals, unlike the one-sided quotient for a non-normal `A`). The two oblique correction equations `(I - u w^H / w^H u)(A - theta)(I - u w^H / w^H u) t = -r` and the adjoint one for `s` are solved by GMRES. Converged pairs are deflated *obliquely*: locked right vectors `Q` and left vectors `P` (scaled so that `p_j^H q_j = 1`, mutually biorthogonal for distinct eigenvalues) define the projector `I - Q (P^H Q)^{-1} P^H` that keeps both spaces away from them. `apply` is `x -> A x`, `apply_t` is `x -> A^T x` (the adjoint of the real operator). A pair is accepted when both residuals are below `opts.tol * max(|lambda|, 1)`. The standard (not harmonic) extraction is used, so `target` should lie near the wanted eigenvalues. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if a projected problem fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_krylov_schur" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Krylov-Schur eigensolver for a general real operator: the `k` eigenpairs selected by `which`. The Arnoldi factorisation `A V = V B + beta v e^T` of dimension `m` (`opts.ncv`, default `max(2k + 1, 20)`) is contracted by computing the Schur form of `B`, moving the wanted eigenvalues to the leading block and truncating; the factorisation is then extended again. Ritz residuals come from the last row of the Schur vectors and are exact for the Arnoldi relation. Conjugate pairs are kept together. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if the Schur iteration fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_shift_invert" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. General shift-invert eigensolver (see [`eigs_sym_shift_invert`]). # Errors As [`eigs`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_sym" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Thick-restart Lanczos for the `k` wanted eigenpairs of a symmetric operator on `R^n`. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if the projected eigenproblem fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_sym_block" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Block Lanczos with thick restart for the `k` wanted eigenpairs of a symmetric operator, using blocks of `block` vectors. Choose `block` at least as large as the multiplicity or the number of members of the tight clusters of the wanted part of the spectrum. The basis is kept orthonormal (two passes of Gram-Schmidt); the projected matrix is formed explicitly, so residuals are true residuals. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0`, `k > n` or `block == 0`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if the projected eigenproblem fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_eigs_sym_shift_invert" => "Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are preserved. Symmetric shift-invert eigensolver: the eigenvalues of `A` closest to `sigma`, given the action `solve(x, y)` of `(A - sigma I)^{-1}`. # Errors As [`eigs_sym`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_exact_lu" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. Exact partial-pivot LU with row-major factors satisfying `P A = L U`. Singular matrices retain valid factors with zero pivots. # Errors Returns [`ExactError::Shape`] for inconsistent dimensions or size overflow.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_exact_qr" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. An exact QR decomposition by modified Gram-Schmidt. The orthonormal columns of Q precede any unused zero columns. R retains the input's shape, including when the columns are dependent. # Errors Invalid matrix dimensions, size overflow, or an irrational column norm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_exact_sqrt" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. The exact square root of a non-negative rational, when it has one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_expm_krylov" => "Native matrix-free Krylov exponential action with explicit dimensionless A*t and preserved vector units. None reports native failure to meet its approximation controls; no algorithm substitution occurs. Action `exp(t A) v` for a matrix-free operator by Krylov projection. The action `exp(t A) v` for a matrix-free operator by Krylov projection: `exp(t A) v ~ |v| V_m exp(t H_m) e_1` with an Arnoldi basis of dimension `m`, advancing in sub-steps whose error estimate `|v| h_{m+1,m} |e_m^T exp(tau H_m) e_1|` stays below `tol` per unit time. Returns `None` if the sub-step shrinks below `1e-12 |t|`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_expm_multiply" => "Native sparse exponential action exp(tA)v. Matrix and exposure dimensions must multiply to one; vector units are preserved. Exposure/operator size are bounded before native stepping. Action `exp(t A) v` for a sparse matrix by a stepped Taylor series. The action `exp(t A) v` for a sparse matrix, by splitting `[0, t]` into `s` steps with `|t A|_1 / s <= 1` and summing the Taylor series of each step until the terms are below `1e-16` of the partial sum.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_fgmres" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. Flexible GMRES(`restart`) with a variable right preconditioner. Flexible GMRES(`restart`): right-preconditioned GMRES whose preconditioner `precond` may vary between applications (for instance an inner Krylov solve or a nonlinear smoother). The preconditioned directions are stored, costing one extra vector per iteration.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_field_faer_decompose" => "Actual f64 Field backend decomposition hook. Every returned factor carries its own normalized values and physical reference; orthogonal factors are dimensionless, Cholesky factors use sqrt(matrix reference), singular/eigenvalues use matrix reference.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_field_faer_inverse" => "Actual native f64 Field faer inverse hook; absent inverse is None, with physical inverse entry reference.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_field_faer_mul" => "Actual native f64 Field faer multiplication hook; product retains the independent matrix references.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_field_faer_solve" => "Actual native f64 Field faer multi-RHS solve hook; solution entries retain RHS/matrix reference dimensions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_field_inverse" => "Native f64 Field inverse; output dimension is the inverse of the measured scalar dimension. Inverse of a non-zero element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_field_is_invertible" => "Native f64 Field invertibility of a finite measured scalar.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_field_magnitude" => "Native f64 Field magnitude preserves the scalar's physical dimension.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_gaussian_new" => "Actual native low-rank/operator method. Matrices and state arrays retain explicit physical references; orthonormal factors are dimensionless. Shapes, iterations, callbacks and allocations are bounded. A generator; equal seeds give equal streams.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_gaussian_sample" => "Actual native low-rank/operator method. Matrices and state arrays retain explicit physical references; orthonormal factors are dimensionless. Shapes, iterations, callbacks and allocations are bounded. The next standard normal number.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_apply" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Applies the discrete operator on `level` (0 is the finest grid).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_cycle_once" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. One multigrid cycle for `A u = f` starting from the current `u`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_fmg" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Full multigrid: nested iteration from the coarsest grid upwards with `cycles` cycles per level, then (optionally) further cycles on the fine grid until the tolerance is met. One FMG sweep with one V-cycle per level already reaches discretisation accuracy. Uses the same residual and stopping criterion as [`Self::solve`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_level_size" => "Returns the number of interior samples at a zero-based geometric level. Level zero is the finest grid. Number of unknowns at a level, or `None` if the level is absent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_levels" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Number of levels (the coarsest has a single unknown).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_new" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Creates the hierarchy for `n = 2^k - 1` interior points per direction. Returns `None` when `dim` is not 1, 2 or 3, when `n` is not of the form `2^k - 1` (`k >= 1`), when the grid size overflows, or when `shift` is negative or non-finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_prolong" => "Interpolates a rank-one array from the next coarser level to the specified fine level, with homogeneous Dirichlet boundaries. The result keeps the input array's physical dimension and reference scale. Interpolate from the adjacent coarser level by multilinear interpolation. The `level` argument identifies the fine output. Missing boundary coarse points have value zero, matching homogeneous Dirichlet data. # Errors Returns an error for a missing adjacent level or incorrect buffer lengths. The output is untouched on such errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_residual_norm" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Absolute residual 2-norm `||f - A u||₂` on the fine grid. Returns infinity if the norm overflows and propagates a NaN stencil residual. The norm is in the same units as the right-hand side.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_restrict" => "Restricts a rank-one array from the specified fine level to its next coarser level by full weighting. The result keeps the input array's physical dimension and reference scale. Restrict a fine grid to the adjacent coarser level by full weighting. The stencil is `[1, 2, 1]/4` along each axis. Axis passes run in x, y, z order on flat buffers with x varying fastest. Each pass rounds to `f64` before the next. The same workspace may be used for later transfers in either direction. # Errors Returns an error for a missing adjacent level or incorrect buffer lengths. The output is untouched on such errors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_size" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Number of unknowns on the fine grid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_geometric_solve" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Solves `A u = f` by repeated cycles from `u = 0` (or `x0`). The history records `||f - A u||₂ / ||f||₂` for nonzero `f`. For an exactly zero right-hand side, the denominator is [`f64::MIN_POSITIVE`]. A nonzero relative residual that underflows is reported as the smallest positive subnormal. Non-finite right-hand sides or applied iterates give an undefined residual (NaN). Only a finite residual at or below the tolerance converges.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_gfp_inv" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The inverse of `a` modulo `p`, or `None` for zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_gfp_mul_mod" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `a * b mod p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_gfp_pow_mod" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `a^e mod p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_gfp_reduce" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The residue of an integer.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_gmres_with" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. Restarted GMRES with an explicit [`Preconditioner`]; see [`gmres`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_hooi" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain. Refine a truncated HOSVD using higher-order orthogonal iteration. Each coordinate step computes the leading singular subspace after projecting all other modes. Ranks are upper bounds and may be clamped to the available subspace. Initialization is deterministic HOSVD; HOOI can converge to a local optimum or stationary point. Inspect `relative_error` independently from `converged`. Exhausting the sweep budget returns the best observed approximation with `converged = false`. Residuals near floating-point roundoff also terminate the iteration. Input normalization protects intermediate norm calculations at uniformly small/large scales. No Gram matrices or condition-number squaring are used. Zero tensors use a rank-one zero core. # Errors Non-finite data, empty modes, invalid ranks/tolerance/budget, SVD failure, or arithmetic overflow return an error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_hosvd" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain. The higher-order SVD (Tucker decomposition) of a dense tensor. For each mode the tensor is unfolded to `n_k x prod(rest)`, the leading left singular vectors of the unfolding become the factor, and the core is the tensor contracted with every factor. `ranks` may be `None` for the numerical multilinear rank of the unfoldings (at most `min(n_k, prod(rest))`, and less when an unfolding is rank deficient in floating point); a requested rank above that is clamped to it. The decomposition is exact before truncation and gives `||T - C x U||_F <= sqrt(sum_k sum_{j > r_k} sigma_{k,j}^2)`. # Errors Fails when `ranks` does not have one entry per mode, when a requested rank is zero, when a mode is empty, or when a mode unfolding has no nonzero singular value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_hosvd_core" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_hosvd_factors" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_hosvd_ranks" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_hosvd_reconstruct" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain. Reconstructs the tensor: `C x_1 U_1 ... x_N U_N`. # Errors Fails only on an inconsistent shape, which a value produced by [`hosvd`] cannot have.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_ic0_apply" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Applies the preconditioner `M^-1 = L^-T D^-1 L^-1` by one forward substitution, a diagonal scaling and one backward substitution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_identity_precond" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Identity preconditioner helper.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_idr_s" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. [`idr_s_with`] with the options passed as separate arguments.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_idr_s_with" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. IDR(`s`) with right preconditioning. The shadow space is `s` fixed pseudo-random orthonormal vectors (`seed`); the method needs only about `n + n/s` matrix-vector products and `s + 4` vectors of storage, and approaches the speed of full GMRES for moderate `s` on nonsymmetric systems. `iterations` counts matrix-vector products.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_ilu0_apply" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Solves `L U z = r` (applies the preconditioner `M^-1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_ilut_apply" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Apply `L^-1` and `U^-1` without changing `out` on failure. # Errors Rejects mismatched/non-finite inputs and non-finite triangular solves.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_ilut_stats" => "Actual retained ILUT storage/drop diagnostics, with dimensionless counts. Factorisation work and retained storage counts.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_inner" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Computes the inner product of two tensors of the same shape. # Errors Returns an error if the shapes mismatch or if tensors are not contiguous.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_integrate_simplex" => "Native adaptive simplex integration of a retained compiled scalar field, with explicit independent coordinate and ordinate references. Each vertex is normalized by the coordinate reference; value/error dimensions are ordinate times coordinate^dimension. Native evaluation and dimension limits are preserved with a checked finite callback/work budget. Integrate `f` over the simplex defined by `n + 1` vertices in `R^n`. Dimensions 1 through 20 are accepted, although the underlying rule is practical only in much lower dimensions. The result uses unsigned volume, regardless of vertex orientation. A zero-volume simplex returns zero without evaluating `f`. At least one tolerance must be positive. The returned [`Integral::converged`] is false if the evaluation budget is exhausted. If the budget cannot cover the initial grid, its value and error are NaN and its evaluation count is zero, as in [`cubature_genz_malik`]. Error estimates are heuristic and cannot certify accuracy. # Errors Returns [`SimplexError::InvalidInput`] for malformed vertices or tolerances, and reports non-finite integrands and arithmetic overflow separately.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_integrate_simplex_map" => "Integrates a retained compiled scalar field over a prepared simplex using adaptive cubature. Geometry is reused. Give a positive value reference and normalized tolerances. Value and error have field dimension times coordinate dimension raised to the spatial rank. Integrates a scalar function with the prepared geometry. Uses the same tolerances and work budget as [`integrate_simplex`]. # Errors Returns an error for invalid tolerances, a non-finite integrand or a numerical overflow.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_interpolate_nd" => "Native normalized-only convenience API: coordinates and ordinates are strictly dimensionless. Corresponding physical adapters accept explicit independent coordinate and ordinate references. Point/order/allocation bounds are enforced. Interpolates scalar grid data in any number of dimensions (1D, 2D, 3D, ...) with the chosen [`TensorOrder`]; see [`multilinear_interpolation`] and [`tensor_cubic_spline_interpolation`]. # Errors As the underlying routine.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_interpolate_nd_physical" => "Native ND interpolation with separate positive physical references for every axis and the ordinate. Axis references and the query are records named axis0..axisN; this permits heterogeneous axis dimensions without mixing units in a numeric tensor. Sample axes/values are normalized by those explicit references. Interpolates scalar grid data in any number of dimensions (1D, 2D, 3D, ...) with the chosen [`TensorOrder`]; see [`multilinear_interpolation`] and [`tensor_cubic_spline_interpolation`]. # Errors As the underlying routine.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_interpolative_decomposition" => "Approximates a matrix using a subset of its original columns and dimensionless interpolation coefficients. Returns zero-based column indices and a coefficient matrix in the original column order. Computes a column interpolative decomposition by truncated pivoted QR. The actual rank is at most `max_rank`. The leading triangular block determines the interpolation coefficients; selected columns have identity coefficients. A zero-rank result has a zero-row coefficient matrix. # Errors Returns an error when the input has inconsistent storage or non-finite entries, the requested rank exceeds either extent, or the coefficients cannot be represented as finite floats.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_lagrange_interpolation" => "Native normalized-only convenience API: coordinates and ordinates are strictly dimensionless. Corresponding physical adapters accept explicit independent coordinate and ordinate references. Point/order/allocation bounds are enforced. Constructs a Lagrange interpolating polynomial that passes through a given set of points. Lagrange interpolation is a method of finding a polynomial that takes on certain values at specified points. The resulting polynomial is unique and passes exactly through all given points. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Polynomial` struct representing the interpolating polynomial. # Errors Returns an error for repeated coordinates, nonfinite samples, or overflowing divided differences or power-basis coefficients. Empty input returns zero. For nonempty input the polynomial retains one coefficient per sample. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::interpolate::lagrange_interpolation; let points = vec![(0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 1.0), (2.0, 4.0)]; let poly = lagrange_interpolation(&points).unwrap(); assert!((poly.eval(1.5) - 2.25).abs() < 1e-9); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_lagrange_physical" => "Native Lagrange interpolation with explicit independent X/Y references; normalized data define a polynomial in x/X and y/Y. Retained query closure checks physical X inputs and restores physical Y outputs. Constructs a Lagrange interpolating polynomial that passes through a given set of points. Lagrange interpolation is a method of finding a polynomial that takes on certain values at specified points. The resulting polynomial is unique and passes exactly through all given points. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Polynomial` struct representing the interpolating polynomial. # Errors Returns an error for repeated coordinates, nonfinite samples, or overflowing divided differences or power-basis coefficients. Empty input returns zero. For nonempty input the polynomial retains one coefficient per sample. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::interpolate::lagrange_interpolation; let points = vec![(0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 1.0), (2.0, 4.0)]; let poly = lagrange_interpolation(&points).unwrap(); assert!((poly.eval(1.5) - 2.25).abs() < 1e-9); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_linear_interpolation" => "Native interpolation over normalized samples with explicit coordinate and ordinate references. Physical queries and outputs are checked against retained domains; point/order/allocation bounds are enforced. Piecewise-linear interpolation through `(x, y)`. Second order in the node spacing for a smooth function: the error on a segment of length `h` is `f''(xi) h^2 / 8`. Values outside the node range are extrapolated with the first or the last segment. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples with strictly increasing `x`. # Returns A `Result` containing a closure `Arc<dyn Fn(f64) -> f64>` evaluating the interpolant. # Errors Returns an error if fewer than two points are given or the abscissae are not strictly increasing. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::interpolate::linear_interpolation; let f = linear_interpolation(&[(0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 1.0), (2.0, 4.0)]).unwrap(); assert!((f(0.5) - 0.5).abs() < 1e-12); assert!((f(1.5) - 2.5).abs() < 1e-12); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_lobpcg" => "Actual native low-rank/operator method. Matrices and state arrays retain explicit physical references; orthonormal factors are dimensionless. Shapes, iterations, callbacks and allocations are bounded. The `k` smallest eigenpairs of a symmetric operator of size `n` by LOBPCG. `apply(x, y)` writes `A x`. `precondition`, when given, writes an approximation of `A^-1 r` (it must be symmetric positive definite; the identity is used otherwise). Each iteration performs a Rayleigh-Ritz projection on the span of the current vectors, the preconditioned residuals and the previous search directions, with the basis re-orthonormalised every time, which is what keeps the method stable when the directions become nearly dependent close to convergence. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] when `k` is zero or more than `n / 3` would allow (`3 k <= n` is needed for the trial subspace), or a product is not finite, or when the tolerance is not positive and finite. Returns [`LowRankError::NoConvergence`] when `max_iterations` pass with a residual above `tol * max(1, |lambda|)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_lyapunov_plan_new" => "Prepares the continuous-time Lyapunov equation A X + X A^T = Q from one square native array. Reuse one real Schur decomposition for different right-hand sides. Prepares a reusable solver for `A X + X A^T = Q`. # Errors Returns an error for invalid matrix storage, non-finite values, cancellation or a failed Schur reduction.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_mat_at" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Entry `(i, j)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_mat_from_rows" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Builds a matrix from row slices. # Errors Returns [`DenseError::Shape`] when rows have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_mat_identity" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Identity matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_mat_is_finite" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Whether every entry is finite (no NaN, no infinity).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_mat_matvec" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Matrix-vector product.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_mat_norm_fro" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Frobenius norm with a serial SIMD reduction and scaling at extreme magnitudes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_mat_set" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Sets entry `(i, j)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_mat_zeros" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. Zero matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_minpoly_wiedemann" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The minimal polynomial of a square matrix over `GF(p)` by Wiedemann's algorithm, monic, lowest degree first. The algorithm flattens `matrix` once into a Montgomery-domain CSR workspace, multiplies by the matrix `2n` times per trial with delayed `u128` row accumulation, and leases Krylov vectors from [`crate::kernels::reuse::global_pool`]: no entry of the matrix is ever changed, so there is no fill-in. One random projection yields a divisor of the minimal polynomial, and the right one with probability at least `1 - 2n/p`; the result here is the least common multiple over `trials` projections (seeded, so the function is deterministic). `None` when the matrix is not square.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_multigrid_mean_factor" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced. Geometric mean convergence factor over the recorded cycles.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_multigrid_result" => "Native multigrid hierarchy in an explicit positive operator reference. RHS array references determine solution units RHS/operator. Initial iterates are normalized in that solution reference. Native cycle choices and convergence histories are preserved; setup/cycle/storage bounds are enforced.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_optimized_det" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. The determinant of a square matrix. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_optimized_inv" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. The inverse of a square matrix. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_optimized_lstsq" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. Minimum-norm least-squares solve using the shared thin SVD and an absolute singular-value threshold of `1e-12`, retained for host API compatibility. # Errors Inconsistent shapes, non-finite input, or failed SVD convergence.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_optimized_lu" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. `P A = L U` with partial pivoting. `faer` reports the row permutation as a pair of index arrays; the `P` this crate promises is the permutation *matrix*, so it is materialised from the forward array as `P[i][forward[i]] = 1`, which is `(P A)[i] = A[forward[i]]`. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_optimized_matmul" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. `A B` for `A` of shape `rows × inner` and `B` of `inner × cols`. Large products use the active Rayon pool. Inputs and output use row-major storage throughout. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_optimized_qr" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. QR factors of A. Q is orthonormal and has shape `rows × rows`. R stores its first `min(rows, cols)` rows with `cols` columns. The stored part reconstructs A with the same number of leading columns of Q. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_optimized_solve" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. `A x = b` for a square `A`. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_outer" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Computes the outer product of two tensors. The outer product of two tensors `A` (rank `r`) and `B` (rank `s`) results in a new tensor `C` of rank `r + s`. Each component of `C` is the product of a component from `A` and a component from `B`. # Arguments * `a` - The first tensor (`ndarray::ArrayD<f64>`). * `b` - The second tensor (`ndarray::ArrayD<f64>`). # Returns The resulting outer product tensor as an `ndarray::ArrayD<f64>`. # Errors Returns an error if input tensors are not contiguous.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_pchip_interpolation" => "Native interpolation over normalized samples with explicit coordinate and ordinate references. Physical queries and outputs are checked against retained domains; point/order/allocation bounds are enforced. Monotone piecewise cubic Hermite interpolation (PCHIP, Fritsch-Carlson). The interpolant is `C^1` and, unlike a plain cubic spline, preserves the shape of the data: on every interval its values stay inside the range of the two end ordinates, so monotone data stay monotone and a step is not overshot. Third order in the node spacing where the slope has one sign, second order at a data extremum, where the slope estimate is clipped to zero — the price of shape preservation. Values outside the node range are extrapolated with the first or the last Hermite cubic. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples with strictly increasing `x`. # Returns A `Result` containing a closure `Arc<dyn Fn(f64) -> f64>` evaluating the interpolant. # Errors Returns an error if fewer than two points are given or the abscissae are not strictly increasing. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::interpolate::pchip_interpolation; // A step: the monotone interpolant stays within [0, 1]. let step = [(0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 0.0), (2.0, 1.0), (3.0, 1.0)]; let f = pchip_interpolation(&step).unwrap(); for k in 0..=30 { let v = f(f64::from(k) / 10.0); assert!((-1e-12..=1.0 + 1e-12).contains(&v)); } ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_phi_matrices" => "Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates. Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs use the same declared reference basis. The `phi_k(A)` functions, `k = 0..=p`, from one augmented matrix exponential. The functions `phi_0(A), ..., phi_p(A)` where `phi_0(z) = e^z` and `phi_k(z) = (phi_{k-1}(z) - 1/(k-1)!) / z` (so `phi_1(z) = (e^z - 1)/z`), from one matrix exponential of the augmented matrix `[[A, I, 0, ..], [0, 0, I, ..], ..]` (Sidje). # Errors As [`expm`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_pivoted_qr" => "Actual native low-rank/operator method. Matrices and state arrays retain explicit physical references; orthonormal factors are dimensionless. Shapes, iterations, callbacks and allocations are bounded. Column-pivoted Householder QR (Businger and Golub). At each step the remaining column of largest norm is brought forward, so the diagonal of `R` decreases in magnitude and its decay reveals the numerical rank. Column norms are downdated and recomputed when cancellation makes the downdate unreliable. Each reflector applies to contiguous columns, with large independent column batches running in parallel. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] for inconsistent storage or non-finite entries, and [`LowRankError::NoConvergence`] if the resulting factor exceeds the representable floating-point range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_pivoted_qr_rank" => "Actual native low-rank/operator method. Matrices and state arrays retain explicit physical references; orthonormal factors are dimensionless. Shapes, iterations, callbacks and allocations are bounded. The numerical rank: the number of diagonal entries of `R` above `tol * |R[0][0]|`. `None` picks `max(m, n) * eps`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_pivoted_qr_solve" => "Actual native low-rank/operator method. Matrices and state arrays retain explicit physical references; orthonormal factors are dimensionless. Shapes, iterations, callbacks and allocations are bounded. The basic solution of the least-squares problem `min |A x - b|`: the solution with zeros in the `n - rank` columns the pivoting put last. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] when `b` has the wrong length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_preconditioner_apply" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Writes `M^-1 r` into `z`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_prime_sparse_new" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_randomized_svd" => "Approximates the leading singular triplets of a finite matrix with a seeded Gaussian sketch. Oversampling widens the sketch and power iterations improve separation of nearby singular values. The columns of u and v are dimensionless. Multiply each entry of s by singular_value_reference to obtain a singular value in the matrix's physical units. A rank-`k` approximate singular value decomposition of an `m x n` operator given by its products: `matvec(x, y)` writes `A x` (`x` of length `n`, `y` of length `m`) and `rmatvec(y, x)` writes `A^T y`. The range of `A` is sampled with `k + oversample` random vectors, sharpened by power iterations with re-orthogonalisation between them, and the small projected matrix is decomposed densely. The cost is at most `2 (power_iterations + 1) (k + oversample)` products. Extreme sampled columns are rescaled before orthogonalisation. Singular values retain the scale of the original operator. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] when `k` is zero or exceeds `min(m, n)`, or a product returns non-finite numbers; [`LowRankError::NoConvergence`] when the dense decomposition of the projected matrix fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_domain_add" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `a + b` in the field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_domain_div" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `a / b` in the field, `None` when `b` is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_domain_is_zero" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. Whether a coefficient is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_domain_mul" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `a * b` in the field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_domain_neg" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `-a` in the field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_domain_one" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The one of the field, in canonical form.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_domain_sub" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `a - b` in the field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_domain_zero" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The zero of the field, in canonical form.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_reconstruct" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. Rational reconstruction: the fraction `n / d` with `n = a d (mod m)` and `|n|, d <= sqrt(m / 2)`, if there is one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_add" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `self + other`, or `None` when the shapes differ.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_det" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The determinant of a square matrix, or `None` when it is not square.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_det_modular" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The determinant of a square rational matrix, exactly, by modular determinants combined with the Chinese remainder theorem up to Hadamard's bound. `None` when the matrix is not square. Evaluates word-sized prime moduli in parallel via [`crate::kernels::par::map_indexed`] when [`crate::execution::should_split_work`] indicates enough work, and folds the modular determinants in deterministic prime order via incremental CRT.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_echelon" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. Gaussian elimination with sparsity-preserving pivoting. Pivots are only taken in columns below `pivot_cols`, which lets a caller append right-hand sides as extra columns. `record` keeps the list of row operations (needed to solve for further right-hand sides later). Pivot ties are broken by the original row and column indices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_from_dense" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. A sparse copy of a dense matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_from_triplets" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. Builds a matrix from `(row, column, value)` triplets. Repeated positions are added and zeros dropped. # Errors When a triplet lies outside the shape.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_matmul" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `self * other`, or `None` when the inner dimensions differ. Each result row is accumulated from the rows of `other` it touches (Gustavson's algorithm) using a sparse dense-indexed accumulator with pooled index scratch, so the cost follows the number of products that are actually non-zero without `BTreeMap` node allocations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_matvec" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `self * x`, or `None` when the length of `x` is not the column count.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_nnz" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. Number of stored entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_nullspace" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. A basis of the null space `{x : A x = 0}`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_rank" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The rank of a matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_scale" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. `c * self`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_solve" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. Solves `A x = b`. Returns `None` when the matrix and right-hand side row counts disagree or the augmented column count cannot be represented by the workspace.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_solve_dixon" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. Solves a square non-singular rational system `A x = b` exactly by Dixon's `p`-adic lifting. The matrix is factored once modulo a word-sized prime; every further `p`-adic digit of the solution costs one sparse solve modulo `p` and one sparse integer product, so no rational number ever appears during the elimination. The lifted residue is turned back into fractions by rational reconstruction and the result is checked by substitution, which makes the answer exact rather than \"correct up to a bound\". Returns `None` when the matrix is not square, `b` has the wrong length, or the matrix is singular modulo several primes in a row (which, for these primes, means singular); use [`solve`] to analyse a singular system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_to_dense" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The dense form.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_transpose" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The transpose.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_triplets" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The entries as `(row, column, value)`, in row-major order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_rational_sparse_zeros" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The zero matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_reference_det" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. Square determinant using the independent sequential LU reference kernel. # Errors Invalid shapes or non-finite entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_reference_inv" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. Square inverse using the sequential LU reference kernel. # Errors Invalid shapes, non-finite entries, or a singular matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_reference_lu" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. Row-major permutation, lower and upper factors, including zero pivots. # Errors Invalid shapes or non-finite entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_reference_matmul" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. Matrix product with the first product as accumulator, preserving IEEE bits. Inputs follow the historical unchecked reference API: lengths must match `rows*inner` and `inner*cols`; a zero inner dimension produces positive zeros.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_reference_qr" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. Householder QR with the historical thin Q zero-padded to square storage. # Errors Invalid shapes or non-finite entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_reference_solve" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. Square solve using the sequential LU reference kernel. # Errors Invalid shapes, non-finite data, or a singular matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_reference_tril" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. The lower triangle of a matrix, the rest zeroed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_reference_triu" => "Native row-major linear calculation in explicitly normalized reference coordinates. Shapes, coefficient/extent bounds and factor work limits are checked before native allocation. The upper triangle of a matrix, the rest zeroed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_reorder_schur" => "Native reordered complex Schur form; a retained compiled real key accepts coherent real/imaginary eigenvalue parts and returns a dimensionless ordering key. Both returned matrices retain native layouts and the t entry reference. Reorders a complex Schur form so that its diagonal is sorted by `key` (smallest first) for the first `count` positions; `Q` is updated so that `A = Q T Q^H` still holds.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_simplex_map_new" => "Prepares the affine geometry of an n-dimensional simplex from n+1 rows of normalized coordinates. The positive coordinate reference records the physical unit and scale for volume and repeated integrations. Builds a reusable map from a simplex's vertices in Euclidean space. # Errors Returns an error for invalid vertex data or unrepresentable geometry.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_simplex_map_volume" => "Returns the unsigned volume of the prepared simplex in coordinate units raised to its spatial dimension. Unsigned simplex volume, rounded to a floating-point value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_solve_linear_system" => "Native RREF linear solve for normalized matrix/RHS arrays with independent physical references. Particular solutions carry RHS/matrix units; nullspace coefficients are dimensionless and scale the free physical parameters. Reports Unique, Parametric or NoSolution. Solves a system of linear equations `Ax = b`. This function constructs an augmented matrix `[A | b]`, computes its Reduced Row Echelon Form (RREF), and then analyzes the RREF to determine the nature of the solution: - **Unique Solution**: Returns a unique solution vector. - **Parametric Solution**: Returns a particular solution and a basis for the null space. - **No Solution**: Indicates an inconsistent system. # Arguments * `a` - The coefficient matrix `A`. * `b` - The constant vector `b`. # Returns A `Result` containing a `LinearSolution` enum, or an error string. # Errors Returns an error if the matrix and vector dimensions are incompatible, or if RREF/null space computation fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_solve_wiedemann" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. Solves a square non-singular system `A x = b` over `GF(p)` by Wiedemann's algorithm. Flattens `matrix` once into a Montgomery-domain CSR workspace and leases Krylov vectors from [`crate::kernels::reuse::global_pool`]. Returns `None` when the projection does not determine a solution (singular matrix, or an unlucky projection in each of the `trials`). A returned vector always satisfies `A x = b`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_lu_dim" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Dimension of the factored matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_lu_nnz" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Number of stored entries in `L` and `U` (a measure of fill-in).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_lu_permutation" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. The symmetric fill-reducing permutation used (`perm[new] = old`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_lu_solve" => "Native sparse factor/preconditioner with its matrix entry reference. Application returns a normalized result with physical reference RHS/matrix; shapes, unit domains and factor work are checked. Solves `A x = b`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_cg" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. Unpreconditioned conjugate gradients for a symmetric positive-definite matrix, starting from zero. # Errors Returns shape and input-domain errors, or [`NumericError::NotPositiveDefinite`] when a search direction has nonpositive curvature or the iteration breaks down.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_cg_precond" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. Preconditioned conjugate gradients from a zero start. `block` is the block shape of a block-CSR operand (`None` otherwise); it selects rssn's block Jacobi / block ILU(0) for those kinds. A factorisation that breaks down falls back to Jacobi, and the kind that was really applied is reported in the result. # Errors [`NumericError::NotSquare`], [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`], or [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite inputs or invalid tolerance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_cholesky_solve" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. Solves `A x = b` by sparse Cholesky (`A = L Lᵀ`, lower triangle read). # Errors [`NumericError::NotPositiveDefinite`] when `A` is not symmetric positive definite (or the factorisation breaks down), plus the shape errors of [`lu_solve`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_cholesky_solve_many" => "Uses one sparse Cholesky factorization for the columns of a rank-two right-hand side. The matrix must be symmetric positive definite. The solution retains the right-hand-side units divided by the matrix units. Solves `A X = B` with one sparse Cholesky factorisation for all columns. The RHS and result have row-major shape `(A.rows, rhs_cols)`. The stored matrix is checked for symmetry after adding duplicate entries. For a nonempty matrix, zero columns return an empty result after CSR and RHS validation. # Errors As [`cholesky_solve`], including an overflow or mismatch in the RHS shape.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_krylov_precond" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. Preconditioned GMRES or `BiCGSTAB` from a zero start, for a **general** (possibly nonsymmetric) square matrix. The preconditioners are those of [`cg_precond`] except that `Ic0` and the symmetric `Auto` choice need a symmetric matrix: here `Ic0` is replaced by `Ilu0` and `Auto` tries `Ilu0` then `Jacobi`. A factorisation that breaks down falls back to Jacobi, and the kind actually applied is reported. The Krylov recurrence is sequential. The sparse products can run in parallel with the same summation order per row, so the result is independent of the thread count. # Errors [`NumericError::NotSquare`], [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`], or [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite inputs or invalid tolerance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_lu_solve" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. Solves `A x = b` by sparse LU. # Errors [`NumericError::NotSquare`], [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] for a bad right-hand side, and [`NumericError::Singular`] when the factorisation fails or produces a non-finite solution. [`NumericError::Domain`] covers non-finite matrix entries and failed allocation of the dense solution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_lu_solve_many" => "Uses one sparse LU factorization for the columns of a rank-two right-hand side. The solution reference and physical dimension are those of the right-hand side divided by the matrix. Solves `A X = B` with one sparse LU factorisation for all columns of `B`. The input and output are row-major arrays of shape `(A.rows, rhs_cols)`. A zero-row matrix or zero-column RHS returns an empty result after input validation. # Errors As [`lu_solve`], including an overflow or mismatch in the RHS shape.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_matvec" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. `y = A x`. # Errors [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] when `x` does not have `cols` entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_solve_auto" => "Native sparse solver with explicit matrix/RHS reference domains. Solutions retain RHS/matrix units; initial vectors use that normalized solution reference. Native method/preconditioner choices, residuals and convergence reports are preserved; shape and work budgets are checked. Solves `A x = b`: sparse Cholesky when `A` is symmetric positive definite, otherwise (or when the factorisation fails) sparse LU. The second component names the factorisation used (`\"Cholesky\"` or `\"LU\"`). # Errors As [`lu_solve`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sparse_native_solve_auto_many" => "Solves a rank-two right-hand side with one sparse Cholesky or LU factorization. The result contains the normalized solution array with its quotient reference and the chosen algorithm name. Solves a batch of right-hand sides with sparse Cholesky or sparse LU. The RHS and result use row-major shape `(A.rows, rhs_cols)`. The returned method name is `\"Cholesky\"` or `\"LU\"` as in [`solve_auto`]. An empty batch is tagged `\"LU\"` after input validation. # Errors As [`lu_solve_many`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sylvester_plan_new" => "Prepares the Sylvester equation A X + X B = C from two square native arrays with one coefficient dimension. The larger of their positive reference scales is shared by both coefficients. A nonzero coefficient that underflows in that reference returns an error. Reuse the resulting plan for different right-hand sides. Prepares a Sylvester solver for square matrices `A` and `B`. # Errors Returns an error for invalid matrix storage, non-finite values, cancellation or a failed Schur reduction.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_sylvester_plan_solve" => "Solves a prepared Sylvester or Lyapunov equation for a native right-hand side with the expected row and column counts. The solution uses the dimension and positive reference of the right-hand side divided by those of the coefficients. Solves the original equation for a new right-hand side with the plan's shape. # Errors Returns an error for inconsistent shape, non-finite input or arithmetic, cancellation, or a singular Sylvester operator.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_tensor_vec_mul" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Performs tensor-vector multiplication. # Errors Returns an error if the tensor has zero dimensions or if the last dimension mismatches the vector size.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_tensordot" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Contracts two tensors along the paired axes in `axes_a` and `axes_b`. The free axes of `a` precede the free axes of `b`, each in its original order. See [`tensordot_views`] for the shape and borrowing rules. # Errors Returns the axis and output shape errors of [`tensordot_views`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_add" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Add two compressed tensors by direct sums of their internal bonds. Use [`Self::round`] afterwards to reduce the resulting bond ranks. # Errors Returns an error for different tensor shapes, SVD failure, or arithmetic overflow.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_core_data" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Row-major core elements.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_core_new" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Construct a finite, nonempty row-major core, validating all extents. # Errors Returns an error for zero/inconsistent extents, non-finite data, or size overflow.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_core_shape" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. The two bond dimensions and physical dimension.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_cores" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Compressed cores.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_entry" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Evaluate one element without constructing the dense tensor. # Errors Returns an error for invalid indices or an unrepresentable contraction.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_from_cores" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Import compressed cores without materializing their dense tensor. Boundary bonds must be one and neighboring bond dimensions must agree. A left orthogonalization restores the invariant used by norm and rounding. The truncation error is zero relative to these supplied cores; it is not a certificate about their original source data. Logical tensor size may exceed usize, in which case dense conversion fails while entry evaluation and compressed contractions remain usable. # Errors Returns an error for empty/mismatched bonds, SVD failure, or arithmetic overflow.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_hadamard" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Entrywise product using Kronecker products of the bond dimensions. # Errors Returns an error for different tensor shapes, SVD failure, or arithmetic overflow.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_inner" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Inner product by contraction of bond matrices, without decompression. Each physical slice advances the bond matrix `E` by a term `A_k^T E B_k`. The contraction order is selected from the bond ranks, and the same two bond buffers are reused across the cores. # Errors Returns an error for different shapes or an unrepresentable contraction.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_inner_workspace_len" => "Reports the number of Float work slots for contracting two equal-shape tensor trains. Counts depend on bond ranks. A single core or two rank-one trains require no work slots. Number of `f64` entries required by [`Self::inner_with_workspace`]. The workspace is sized by the bond ranks. Two rank-one tensor trains need no workspace. # Errors Returns an error if the shapes differ or the workspace extent is unrepresentable.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_norm" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Frobenius norm, evaluated with scaled sum-of-squares arithmetic. TT-SVD makes every preceding core left-orthonormal, so the final core alone has the same norm as the reconstructed tensor.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_round" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Reduce bond dimensions without constructing the dense tensor. The tolerance controls additional Frobenius error relative to this represented tensor. The returned error also includes the input's previous truncation estimate, using the triangle inequality. # Errors Returns an error for invalid tolerance/rank, SVD failure, or arithmetic overflow.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_shape" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Original tensor shape.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_storage_len" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Number of stored scalar elements.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_to_dense" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Reconstruct the dense, row-major tensor. Allocates prod(shape) elements. # Errors Returns an error for size/allocation overflow or unrepresentable arithmetic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_to_dense_with_limit" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Dense conversion with an explicit limit on the output element count. Use this when loading cores supplied by another process or when the logical tensor may be exponentially larger than its compressed storage. # Errors Returns an error when size exceeds the limit, allocation fails, or arithmetic overflows.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_tolerance_met" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Whether the requested relative tolerance was met despite a rank cap.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_train_truncation_error" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Frobenius norm estimate of discarded singular values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_tt_svd" => "Native numerical method. Normalized coordinates and positive physical reference scales are checked; native object kind/session/lifetime and allocation/work budgets are enforced. Compress a finite, nonempty dense tensor with TT-SVD. Each split is assigned relative tolerance / sqrt(order - 1). A rank cap may prevent satisfying that tolerance; inspect `tolerance_met` and the returned truncation estimate. Zero tensors use bond rank one. A one-mode tensor is stored exactly. Input is normalized before decomposition to avoid squaring the input's scale in intermediate norm computations. # Errors Returns an error for invalid shape/options, non-finite input, SVD failure, allocation/size overflow or unrepresentable rescaling.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_tucker_core" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_tucker_diagnostics" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_tucker_factors" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_tucker_reconstruct" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain. The dense tensor `core x_1 U_1 .. x_N U_N` of a Tucker model, with `factors[k]` of size `n_k x r_k` and `core` of shape `r_1 x .. x r_N`. # Errors Returns an error if the number of factors differs from the rank of the core, a factor's column count differs from its core extent, or the result would be empty.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_tucker_reconstruct_method" => "Native tensor decomposition with explicit reference scale and bounded factor/order/sweep storage. Factors are normalized; reconstructed tensors retain the input physical domain. Reconstruct the dense approximation. # Errors Returns an error if the public core/factor dimensions are inconsistent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linear_word_primes" => "Native exact sparse/domain operation specialized to BigRational or a validated prime field. Coefficients are normalized dimensionless exact values; finite shape, coefficient-bit and work budgets are enforced. The primes below `2^62`, largest first: the moduli of the modular methods. Large enough that an unlucky prime (one dividing a determinant) is practically never met, small enough for `u128` products.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_linf_norm" => "Infinity vector norm. Computes the Infinity norm (\$L_\\\\infty\$ norm) of a vector. Formula: \$||\\\\mathbf{v}||_\\\\infty = \\\\max_{i} |`v_i`|\$\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_ln_binomial" => "`num_ln_binomial(n, k)` returns the natural logarithm of the binomial coefficient on 0 <= k <= n. The result stays finite when the coefficient would overflow binary64. The logarithm of the binomial coefficient `ln C(n, k)`. For a small reduced `k`, sum the logarithms of the multiplicative factors. Otherwise combine the leading Stirling terms algebraically and add the corrections. Both paths avoid subtracting nearly equal log-factorials for large, unbalanced inputs. Returns negative infinity when `k > n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_ln_factorial" => "`ln(n!)` for real `n > -1`, through `ln Γ(n + 1)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_log_gamma" => "Numerical log gamma function. Computes the natural logarithm of the gamma function, ln(Γ(x)).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_log_integral" => "The logarithmic integral `li(x) = Ei(ln x)` for `x > 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_logit" => "Computes the logit function logit(p) = ln(p / (1-p)); `-inf` at 0, `+inf` at 1, NaN outside `[0, 1]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_lowrank_qr_pivoted" => "Column-pivoted Householder QR (Businger and Golub). At each step the remaining column of largest norm is brought forward, so the diagonal of `R` decreases in magnitude and its decay reveals the numerical rank. Column norms are downdated and recomputed when cancellation makes the downdate unreliable. Each reflector applies to contiguous columns, with large independent column batches running in parallel. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] for inconsistent storage or non-finite entries, and [`LowRankError::NoConvergence`] if the resulting factor exceeds the representable floating-point range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_lp_norm" => "Computes the \$`L_p`\$ norm of a vector. Formula: \$||\\\\mathbf{v}||_p = (\\\\sum_{i=1}^n |`v_i|^p)^{1/p`}\$\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_lstsq" => "Full-column-rank least-squares solution using QR. Least-squares solution of `min |A x - b|_2` (`m >= n`, full column rank) by QR. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] if `m < n` or `b` has the wrong length, [`DenseError::Singular`] if `A` is rank deficient.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matfun_expm" => "Matrix exponential `exp(A)` of a square matrix. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] for non-square input, [`DenseError::Singular`] if a Pade denominator is singular (not expected for finite input).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matfun_logm" => "Principal logarithm of a matrix without eigenvalues on the closed negative real axis, by inverse scaling and squaring.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matfun_norm1" => "Matrix 1-norm (maximum absolute column sum).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matfun_powm_psd" => "Real power `A^exponent` of a symmetric positive semidefinite matrix. Positive powers keep zero eigenvalues, and a zero exponent gives the identity, including for singular matrices. Negative powers require a positive definite matrix. Tiny negative eigenvalues from rounding the symmetric eigendecomposition are treated as zero for nonnegative powers. # Errors Returns [`DenseError::Shape`] for malformed, nonsquare or nonsymmetric matrices, [`DenseError::NonFinite`] for non-finite entries or exponent, [`DenseError::Singular`] outside the spectral domain, and [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] if the algorithm cannot compute a finite result.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
