    "num_matrix_real_lu_decomposition" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the LU decomposition of a square matrix. # Returns A tuple containing the LU matrix and the number of row swaps. # Errors Returns an error if: - The matrix is not square. - The matrix is singular.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_mul_strassen" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. # Strassen's Matrix Multiplication This function multiplies two matrices using Strassen's algorithm, which is more efficient for large matrices than the naive O(n^3) algorithm. ## Arguments * `other` - The matrix to multiply with. ## Returns A new `Matrix` representing the product of the two matrices. ## Errors Returns an error if: - Matrix multiplication is not possible due to incompatible dimensions. - The matrices are not square (for the internal recursive calls).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_new" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Creates a new `Matrix` from dimensions and a flat `Vec` of data. # Arguments * `rows` - The number of rows. * `cols` - The number of columns. * `data` - A `Vec` containing the matrix elements in row-major order. # Panics Panics if the product of the dimensions overflows or differs from `data.len()`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_null_space" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes a basis for the null space (kernel) of the matrix. The null space of a matrix `A` is the set of all vectors `x` such that `Ax = 0`. This method finds the null space by first computing the RREF of the matrix, identifying pivot and free variables, and then constructing basis vectors. # Returns A `Matrix` whose columns form a basis for the null space. # Errors Returns an error if the RREF calculation fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_rank" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the rank of the matrix. # Errors Returns an error if the RREF computation fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_rows" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Returns the number of rows in the matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_rref" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the reduced row echelon form (RREF) of the matrix in-place. This method applies Gaussian elimination to transform the matrix into its RREF. It is used for solving linear systems, finding matrix inverses, and determining rank. # Returns The rank of the matrix (number of non-zero rows in RREF). # Errors Returns an error if a division by zero occurs during the elimination process, which can happen if the matrix elements are not from a proper field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_set_backend" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Updates the backend for the matrix in-place.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_trace" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the trace of the matrix (sum of diagonal elements). # Errors Returns an error if the matrix is not square.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_transpose" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the transpose of the matrix. The transpose of a matrix `A` (denoted `A^T`) is obtained by flipping the matrix over its diagonal; that is, it switches the row and column indices of the matrix. # Returns A new `Matrix` representing the transpose.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_with_backend" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Sets the backend for the matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_matrix_real_zeros" => "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Creates a new `Matrix` filled with the zero element of type `T`. # Arguments * `rows` - The number of rows. * `cols` - The number of columns. # Returns A new `Matrix` of the specified dimensions, with all elements initialized to `T::zero()`. # Panics Panics if the product of the dimensions overflows.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_max" => "Computes the maximum value of a slice of data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mean" => "Arithmetic mean; zero for an empty sample. Computes the mean of a slice of data. # Arguments * `data` - A unmutable slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The mean of the data as an `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_median" => "Computes the median of a slice of data. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The median of the data as an `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_meshgen_boundary_faces" => "`num_meshgen_boundary_faces(tetrahedra)` returns the oriented triangular faces incident to exactly one tetrahedron, in first occurrence order. Tetrahedron rows have four distinct zero-based vertex indices. Positively oriented tetrahedra give outward face winding. The faces of `tets` that belong to exactly one tetrahedron. Each face is returned once, with the winding it has in the tetrahedron that owns it, so the faces of a closed mesh point outwards.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_meshgen_delaunay_2d_incremental" => "`num_meshgen_delaunay_2d_incremental(points)` inserts between 3 and 4096 distinct planar points in input order and returns counter-clockwise triangles of zero-based vertex indices. Points may carry coordinate units. The triangulation contains indices only. Incremental Delaunay triangulation of `points`. This is the incremental entry point of the same Bowyer–Watson insertion used by [`delaunay_2d`]: points are inserted one at a time into the growing triangulation, and each insertion only re-triangulates the cavity of triangles whose circumcircle contains the new point. # Errors Returns `Err` under the same conditions as [`delaunay_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_meshgen_min_angle_deg" => "`num_meshgen_min_angle_deg(points, triangles)` returns the smallest planar triangle angle as a scalar in degrees. Points have two columns and triangles have three zero-based vertex indices. An empty mesh or a degenerate triangle gives zero. Out-of-range indices are errors. The smallest angle in degrees over all `triangles`. Returns `0.0` for an empty mesh or a degenerate triangle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_meshgen_tetra_volumes" => "`num_meshgen_tetra_volumes(points, tetrahedra)` returns nonnegative volumes in input order, with cubed coordinate units. Points have three columns and tetrahedra have four zero-based vertex indices. Degenerate tetrahedra have zero volume. Out-of-range indices are errors. The volume of every tetrahedron in `tets`, always non-negative. Tetrahedra whose indices do not address `points` are reported as `0.0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_meshgen_triangle_areas" => "`num_meshgen_triangle_areas(points, triangles)` returns an area for each triangle in input order, with squared coordinate units. Points have two columns and triangles have three zero-based vertex indices. Degenerate triangles have zero area. Out-of-range indices are errors. The area of every triangle in `triangles`. Triangles whose indices do not address `points` are reported as `0.0`. Large batches use the current execution pool and preserve input order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_min" => "Computes the minimum value of a slice of data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_ball_abs" => "Returns a dyadic enclosure of the absolute values in the closed interval at the requested precision. The absolute value, as the hull of the endpoints' absolute values. # Errors Fails when an exponent leaves the range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_ball_contains_zero" => "Tests whether the closed interval contains zero, including either endpoint. Whether the interval contains zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_ball_div" => "Encloses all quotients of two closed dimensionless intervals. Both arguments and the result have canonical dyadic midpoint and radius fields. The radius is nonnegative and can have a different bit precision from the midpoint. The divisor interval must exclude zero, including at its endpoints. An out-of-range exponent is a domain error. Encloses the quotients of the two intervals when the divisor excludes zero. # Errors Fails when the divisor contains zero or an exponent leaves the range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_ball_endpoints" => "Returns the lower endpoint rounded down and upper endpoint rounded up at the requested precision. The two endpoints, `mid - rad` and `mid + rad`. # Errors Fails when an exponent leaves the range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_ball_neg" => "Reflects a closed interval about zero and retains its radius. The value negated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_ball_new" => "Creates a closed interval from canonical dyadic midpoint and radius. The radius must be nonnegative. The ball of radius `rad` around `mid`. # Errors Fails for a negative radius or when an exponent leaves the range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_ball_point" => "Creates a closed interval containing exactly the given dyadic. The degenerate ball holding exactly `mid`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_ball_width" => "Returns twice the radius as a dyadic. An out-of-range exponent is a domain error. The width `2 * rad`. # Errors Fails when an exponent leaves the range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_abs" => "Returns the absolute value of an exact dyadic without rounding. The absolute value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_add" => "Adds two canonical dyadic records and rounds the exact sum once to the requested bit precision and mode. The sum, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_compare" => "Compares canonical dyadic records exactly. Returns -1, 0, or 1 and allows different operand precisions. Total order: `-1`, `0` or `1` as `self` is below, equal to or above `other`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_div" => "Divides two canonical dyadic records and rounds the exact quotient once to the requested bit precision and mode. A zero divisor or an out-of-range normalized exponent is a domain error. The quotient, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails on division by zero or when the exponent leaves the range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_floor_log2" => "Returns floor(log2(abs(value))) or None if value is zero. `floor(log2(|x|))`, or `None` for zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_is_integer" => "Tests whether a dyadic has no fractional binary digits. Whether the value is an exact integer.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_is_zero" => "Tests whether a canonical dyadic is zero. Whether the value is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_mul" => "Multiplies two canonical dyadic records and rounds the exact product once to the requested bit precision and mode. The product, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_neg" => "Negates an exact dyadic at its existing precision. The value negated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_round" => "Rounds mantissa times 2^exponent once to the requested bit precision. Returns the integer fields mantissa, exponent and precision. Nonzero mantissas have exactly precision bits and zero has exponent zero. Precision is 1 to 32768 bits, input mantissas may have up to 65536 bits and exponents must lie within +/-262144. Accepts nearest (ties to even), zero, away, up, or down. `mant * 2^exp` rounded to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_scale_pow2" => "Multiplies a dyadic by an integer power of two exactly. Zero keeps its canonical exponent. A requested exponent outside the representable range is a domain error. `self * 2^k`, exact. # Errors Fails when the exponent leaves the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_sqrt" => "Rounds the square root of a nonnegative dyadic at the requested bit precision. A negative input is a domain error. The square root, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails for a negative operand or an exponent outside the range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_sub" => "Subtracts two canonical dyadic records and rounds the exact difference once to the requested bit precision and mode. The difference, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_trunc_to_bigint" => "Truncates a dyadic toward zero and returns an exact integer. The dyadic representation bounds the result to 294912 bits. The value truncated toward zero to an integer. # Errors Fails when the exponent leaves the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_mp_real_with_precision" => "Rounds an existing canonical dyadic to a new bit precision. The rounding modes are nearest, zero, away, up and down. The same value rounded to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_multinomial" => "`num_multinomial(n, parts)` returns the floating-point multinomial coefficient. Parts are nonnegative integers and must sum exactly to n. The empty partition of zero gives one. A coefficient that exceeds binary64 produces a domain error. Computes a floating-point multinomial coefficient as a product of binomials. Returns `None` if the parts do not sum to `n`, and `Some(infinity)` when the coefficient overflows. Zero parts and the empty partition of zero have no effect on the product.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_multivariate_normal_log_pdf_scaled" => "Multivariate log density relative to coordinate_scale^n, where n is the vector length. An explicit positive scale makes the logarithm dimensionless and defines the reference measure. Dimensionless multivariate normal log density with a row-major covariance matrix. Rejects non-finite data, mismatched dimensions and non-positive-definite covariance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_aaa_build" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. AAA algorithm (Nakatsukasa, Sete, Trefethen) on real samples `(z_i, f_i)`: greedy support-point selection and a least-squares weight vector from the SVD of the Loewner matrix. # Errors [`ApproxError::Invalid`] for fewer than 3 samples.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_aaa_degree" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Degree (`numerator = denominator = m - 1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_aaa_eval" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Evaluates the rational function at `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_affine2_apply" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Applies the transformation to a point.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_affine2_new" => "Construct an affine map in explicitly nondimensionalized coordinate space; coefficients represent a,b,c,d,e,f in the native map. Creates a new affine transformation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_affine_ifs_generate" => "Generates a dimensionless two-column point tensor after the requested burn-in, using an exact unsigned 64-bit seed. The starting point is excluded. A zero point count returns shape [0, 2]. Nonfinite trajectories and excessive work return errors. Generates `num_points` transformed points after `skip` burn-in steps. The seed selects the same draw stream on every call. The starting point itself is excluded. A request for zero points skips the burn-in. # Errors A nonfinite starting point or step, work count overflow, or allocation failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_affine_ifs_new" => "Prepares a session-owned affine iterated function system from up to 4096 dimensionless rows (a,b,c,d,e,f) and matching finite nonnegative relative weights. A positive weight is required. Reuse the plan across independent starting points and seeds. Prepares an affine IFS from finite coefficients and relative weights. Zero-weight transformations are excluded. Bounds are inclusive at the upper end of each draw interval. # Errors Invalid shape, weights, coefficients or a failed allocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_augmented_lagrangian_scaled" => "Native scaled global/constrained optimization with shared actual host evaluation limit. Nelder: x,xscale,yscale,step,tol,iterations,budget,f. Differential evolution: lo,hi,xscale,yscale,population,generations,budget,seed,f. Annealing: x,xscale,yscale,step,temperature,cooling,iterations,budget,seed,f. Constrained: x,xscale,yscale,equality_scale,inequality_scale,tol,iterations,budget,f,eq,ineq. Constraints independently carry C/H units; SQP returns physical multipliers E/C and E/H, native status and second-order verdict. Second-order: x,lambda,mu,xscale,yscale,eqscale,ineqscale,budget,f,eq,ineq. No global-optimality claim; native iteration/status semantics retained. Augmented Lagrangian for `min f(x)` subject to `h(x) = 0` and `g(x) <= 0`; the inner problems are solved by BFGS.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_backend_compile_multi" => "Compile multiple elementary expressions with physical coordinate, parameter and output references. Uses the actual selected native backend and bounded source work.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_eval_jacobian" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The Jacobian of a real system over a box.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_eval_jacobian_terms" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The complex Jacobian `∂f_i/∂z_j` over the box. Each entry is an enclosure. A missing or zero exponent gives no contribution to that column.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_eval_polys" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `f_i(X)` of a real system over a box.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_eval_terms" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `f_i(X)` for every equation of the system (an enclosure of the range over the box).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_eval_value_and_jacobian_terms" => "Evaluates a polynomial system and its Jacobian over one complex box, sharing term products. Returns {values, jacobian} as enclosure handles. Value units equal the residual unit. Jacobian units are residual divided by the coordinate unit. Encloses both the values and the complex Jacobian of a system over a box. The evaluator can reuse powers across rows and shares each term's products across the value and partial derivatives. Missing exponents are zero, and exponents beyond the number of coordinates are ignored.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_jacobian_at" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The Jacobian of a real system at a point.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_jacobian_at_terms" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The complex Jacobian at a point.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_real_terms" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Lifts a real system to complex terms.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_values_and_jacobian_at_terms" => "Evaluates a polynomial system and its Jacobian at one complex point. Returns {values, jacobian} with residual units and residual-per-coordinate units respectively. The Jacobian is a rank-two tensor with one row per equation. Evaluates the values and complex Jacobian at a point using one power cache. The arithmetic is ordinary complex `f64` arithmetic. The exponent convention matches [`eval_value_and_jacobian_terms`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_values_at" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The values of a real system at a point.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_ball_values_at_terms" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The values `f_i(x)` at a point, in plain `f64` arithmetic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_correct" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Corrects up to `t` bit errors in a (possibly shortened) codeword. Returns the corrected codeword and the number of corrected bits. # Errors Fails if the word is empty, longer than `n`, not binary, or if the error pattern is uncorrectable (more than `t` errors that are detected; patterns of more than `t` errors may also be mis-decoded to a different codeword, as for every bounded-distance decoder).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_decode" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Decodes a (possibly shortened) codeword to its message bits, correcting up to `t` errors. # Errors See [`Bch::correct`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_encode" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Encodes `data` (`1..=k` bits) into a systematic codeword `data ++ parity` of `data.len() + (n - k)` bits. # Errors Fails for an empty message, a message longer than `k`, or entries other than `0` and `1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_for_data_len" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Builds the smallest-length code of degree `m <= 16` correcting `t` errors that carries at least `data_bits` information bits. # Errors Fails if no supported code is large enough.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_generator" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Generator polynomial coefficients over GF(2), lowest power first.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_is_codeword" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether `word` is a codeword (all syndromes vanish).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_k" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Dimension `k = n - deg g` (message bits per block).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_m" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Field degree `m` (the block length is `2^m - 1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_n" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Block length `n = 2^m - 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_new" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Builds the BCH code of length `2^m - 1` correcting `t` errors. # Errors Fails if `m` is outside `2..=16`, `t == 0`, or `t` is so large that the code has no information bits (`k < 1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_parity_len" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Number of parity bits `n - k`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_bch_t" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Designed error-correction capability `t`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_binomial_dist_cdf" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the cumulative distribution function (CDF) value at `k`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_binomial_dist_new" => "Checked native legacy distribution. Continuous parameters and queries retain coherent physical units; inverse CDF restores sample units and PDF has inverse sample units. Creates a new `BinomialDist` instance. # Errors Returns an error if `p < 0.0` or `p > 1.0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_binomial_dist_pmf" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the probability mass function (PMF) value at `k`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_categorical_categories" => "Return the original weight count, including zero-weight categories. Returns the length of the original weight slice.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_categorical_fill" => "Return an integer tensor of zero-based draws in stream order. It advances the supplied session random stream exactly as repeated scalar sampling would. Rust callers can fill existing storage. Fills an existing slice with the same draws as repeated calls to [`Self::sample`], including the advancement of the random stream.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_categorical_new" => "Prepare repeated sampling of up to 32768 categories. Provide finite nonnegative dimensionless relative weights with at least one positive entry. The same table can be used with different random streams in the session. Prepares a sampler from finite, nonnegative relative weights. Returns indices in the original weight order and never selects a zero-weight entry. At least one weight must be positive. The total need not be finite before scaling. Conditional alias probabilities are rounded down to multiples of `2^-64`. # Errors Returns an error for an empty slice, an invalid weight, an all-zero slice or an allocation failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_categorical_sample" => "Draw one zero-based index and advance the supplied session random stream as needed. Zero-weight categories are excluded. A single supported category does not consume random values. Draws one zero-based category index from the supplied random stream. A single possible category consumes no random values. A uniform distribution chooses an unbiased column. A weighted distribution also draws an independent raw value for that column's cutoff.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_centre" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The centre.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_contains" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether `z` lies in the rectangle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_div" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `self / other`. Returns `None` when the denominator enclosure cannot be separated from zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_half_width" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The larger of the two half-widths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_inside_strictly" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether this rectangle lies in the interior of `outer` (strictly in both coordinates, which is the hypothesis of the Krawczyk theorem).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_intersect" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The intersection, when the rectangles meet.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_is_finite" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether both parts are finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_modulus_lower" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A lower bound of `|z|` for `z` in the rectangle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_modulus_upper" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. An upper bound of `|z|` over the rectangle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_of" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A [`Cx`] as a point ball.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_one" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_point" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The point `re + i im`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_powi" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A rectangular enclosure of `z^n` by binary exponentiation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_rectangle" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A ball with separate end points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_square" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The square `|z - (re + i im)|_inf <= radius` (one radius for both parts), widened outward.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cball_zero" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cdd_new" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Builds a value from two `f64` components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cdd_norm" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Fast `f64` modulus of the leading components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cdd_norm_dd" => "Computes the modulus of a complex double-double value and keeps both components of the real double-double result. The result preserves the input's physical dimension. Modulus computed from both parts of each component.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cdd_recip" => "Computes the reciprocal of a complex double-double value. The retained result has the inverse physical dimension. Zero inputs and nonfinite results are rejected. Multiplicative inverse, using the same scale handling as division. The inverse of zero has NaN components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cheb_adaptive" => "Construct a portable native Chebyshev series from a checked serial callback with independent physical coordinate and ordinate units. Bounds, degree, finite callback values, original callback failures and evaluation budgets are checked. Adaptive max_degree is a power of two from 8 through 64. Returns the same coefficient model accepted by native evaluation, derivative, integral and root methods. Adaptive construction: doubles the degree (up to `max_degree`) until the trailing coefficients fall below `tol * max|c|`, then chops the negligible tail. Values at shared nodes are reused when refining the grid. The same coordinate should always give the same value when supplied to `f`.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_cheb_coefficients" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cheb_definite_integral" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Definite integral over `[a, b]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cheb_degree" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Degree of the series.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cheb_derivative" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Derivative series.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cheb_eval" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Value at `x in [a, b]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cheb_from_fn" => "Construct a portable native Chebyshev series from a checked serial callback with independent physical coordinate and ordinate units. Bounds, degree, finite callback values, original callback failures and evaluation budgets are checked. Adaptive max_degree is a power of two from 8 through 64. Returns the same coefficient model accepted by native evaluation, derivative, integral and root methods. Interpolates `f` at the Chebyshev extrema in [`chebyshev_points`] order. Degree zero and equal endpoints use one function evaluation.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_cheb_from_values" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Interpolates already sampled values in [`chebyshev_points`] order. `values[0]` is the sample at `b` for two or more values. One value represents a constant on the whole interval. An empty slice represents zero. With equal endpoints the first value is used.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cheb_integral" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Antiderivative series vanishing at `a`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_cheb_roots" => "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Real roots in `[a, b]` from the eigenvalues of the colleague matrix. # Errors [`ApproxError::Singular`] if the eigenvalue iteration fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_gravitational_potential_energy" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Calculates the total gravitational potential energy of a system of particles.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_particle_data" => "Returns the measured mass, position and velocity of a retained classical particle. The position and velocity vectors each have three components in x, y, z order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_particle_kinetic_energy" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Kinetic energy of the particle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_particle_momentum" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Momentum magnitude.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_particle_new" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Creates a new particle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_projectile_motion_with_drag" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Simulates projectile motion with air resistance (drag). Uses Euler integration to solve equations of motion: `F_drag` = -0.5 * ρ * A * Cd * v² * `v_hat` # Arguments * `params` - Simulation parameters # Returns Vector of (time, x, y, vx, vy) tuples\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_simulate_ising_model" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Simulates a 2D Ising model using the Metropolis-Hastings algorithm. The Ising model is a mathematical model of ferromagnetism in statistical mechanics. This function simulates the evolution of a 2D lattice of spins using the Metropolis-Hastings algorithm to reach thermal equilibrium at a given temperature. # Arguments * `size` - The dimension of the square lattice (e.g., `size x size`). * `temperature` - The temperature of the system. * `steps` - The number of Monte Carlo steps to perform. # Returns A `Vec<Vec<i8>>` representing the final spin configuration of the lattice.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_simulate_n_body" => "Simulates classical gravitational motion from 1 to 512 retained particle handles. Supply a positive measured Time step, a nonnegative step count and a gravitational constant in Length cubed per Mass per Time squared. The integrator applies a gravitational velocity update followed by a position update. The outer result contains the initial snapshot followed by one snapshot per step. Each snapshot preserves input particle order and contains records with measured mass and three-component position and velocity. The input handles are unchanged. The adapter checks the pair-step work and encoded history budgets before integration. If evolution produces a nonfinite state, the call returns an error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_simulate_particle_motion" => "Actual native RK4 particle motion. Force receives seconds plus a record with separately measured position and velocity; force results are Newtons. Positive mass/ordered finite times and step/callback budgets are checked, and first callback failure aborts immediately. Simulates the 3D motion of a particle under a force field. This function integrates Newton's second law (`F=ma`) for a single particle in a given force field using the fourth-order Runge-Kutta method. # Arguments * `force` - The force `(Fx, Fy, Fz)` as a function of time and the state `[x, y, z, vx, vy, vz]`. * `mass` - The mass of the particle. * `initial_pos` - The initial position `(x, y, z)`. * `initial_vel` - The initial velocity `(vx, vy, vz)`. * `t_range` - The time interval `(t_start, t_end)` for the simulation. * `num_steps` - The number of time steps. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<Vec<f64>>` representing the trajectory over time, or an error string if evaluation fails. # Errors Returns an error if the integration produces a non-finite state.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_1d_schrodinger" => "Native normalized Schrodinger operator -onehalfLaplacian+V, mapped to physical SI using positive mass and coordinate reference with energy=hbar^2/(mass*length^2). Potential callbacks receive physical coordinates and return energy. Eigenvectors are dimensionless discrete coefficients; no continuum wavefunction normalization is fabricated. Dense eigensystem limited256nodes. Solves the 1D time-independent Schrödinger equation `Hψ = Eψ` using the finite difference method. This function discretizes the 1D Schrödinger equation on a grid, transforming it into an eigenvalue problem for the Hamiltonian matrix. The eigenvalues correspond to energy levels, and the eigenvectors correspond to the wave functions. All supplied grid points are unknowns, including the range endpoints. The stencil assumes zero values one grid spacing beyond each endpoint. Assumes `hbar = 1` and `m = 1`. # Arguments * `potential` - The potential `V(x)` as a function of the coordinates. * `range` - The spatial range `(x_min, x_max)`. * `num_points` - The number of grid points. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(eigenvalues, eigenvectors_matrix)`, or an error string if the number of points is too small or evaluation fails. # Errors Returns an error if `num_points` is less than 3, if symbolic evaluation fails, or if the decomposition fails to converge.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_2d_schrodinger" => "Native normalized Schrodinger operator -onehalfLaplacian+V, mapped to physical SI using positive mass and coordinate reference with energy=hbar^2/(mass*length^2). Potential callbacks receive physical coordinates and return energy. Eigenvectors are dimensionless discrete coefficients; no continuum wavefunction normalization is fabricated. Dense eigensystem limited256nodes. Solves the 2D time-independent Schrödinger equation `Hψ = Eψ` on a rectangular grid. This function discretizes the 2D Schrödinger equation using a 5-point Laplacian finite-difference stencil, converting it into a large eigenvalue problem. Homogeneous Dirichlet conditions are imposed at the supplied grid boundary. Interior modes have zero boundary coefficients. The full grid shape is retained with separate boundary modes at the diagonal values `1e12 + V`. Assumes `hbar = 1` and `m = 1`. Warning: For large grids, this dense matrix approach can be very slow and memory-intensive. # Arguments * `potential_fn` - The potential `V(x,y)` as a function of the coordinates. * `ranges` - The spatial ranges `(x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max)`. * `grid` - The number of grid points `(nx, ny)` in each direction (including boundaries). Eigenvectors are columns. Grid rows vary `y` fastest. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(eigenvalues, eigenvectors_matrix)`, or an error string if grid dimensions are too small or evaluation fails. # Errors Returns an error if grid dimensions are less than 3, if symbolic evaluation fails, or if the decomposition fails to converge.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_3d_schrodinger" => "Native normalized Schrodinger operator -onehalfLaplacian+V, mapped to physical SI using positive mass and coordinate reference with energy=hbar^2/(mass*length^2). Potential callbacks receive physical coordinates and return energy. Eigenvectors are dimensionless discrete coefficients; no continuum wavefunction normalization is fabricated. Dense eigensystem limited256nodes. Solves the 3D time-independent Schrödinger equation `Hψ = Eψ` on a rectangular grid. This function discretizes the 3D Schrödinger equation using a 7-point Laplacian finite-difference stencil, converting it into a large eigenvalue problem. Homogeneous Dirichlet conditions are imposed at the supplied grid boundary. Interior modes have zero boundary coefficients. The full grid shape is retained with separate boundary modes at the diagonal values `1e12 + V`. Assumes `hbar = 1` and `m = 1`. Warning: For large grids, this dense matrix approach can be extremely slow and memory-intensive. Consider smaller grids or sparse/iterative methods for higher performance. # Arguments * `potential_fn` - The potential `V(x,y,z)` as a function of the coordinates. * `ranges` - The spatial ranges `(x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max, z_min, z_max)`. * `grid` - The number of grid points `(nx, ny, nz)` in each direction (including boundaries). Eigenvectors are columns. Grid rows vary `z` fastest. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(eigenvalues, eigenvectors_matrix)`, or an error string if grid dimensions are too small, too large, or evaluation fails. # Errors Returns an error if grid dimensions are less than 3, if the total number of points exceeds 25,000, if symbolic evaluation fails, or if the decomposition fails to converge.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_heat_equation_1d_crank_nicolson" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D Heat Equation `u_t = alpha * u_xx` using the Crank-Nicolson method. The Crank-Nicolson method is a finite difference method for numerically solving the heat equation and similar partial differential equations. It is implicit, unconditionally stable, and second-order accurate in both space and time. Assumes Dirichlet boundary conditions `u(a,t)=u(b,t)=0`. # Arguments * `init_func` - A closure representing the initial condition `u(x,0)`. * `alpha` - The thermal diffusivity constant. * `range` - The spatial range `(a, b)`. * `nx` - The number of spatial grid points. * `t_range` - The time range `(t_start, t_end)`. * `nt` - The number of time steps. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<Vec<f64>>` where each inner `Vec` is the solution `u` at a given time step, or an error string if input dimensions are invalid. # Errors Returns an error if `nx` < 3 or `nt` < 1.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_heat_equation_2d_crank_nicolson" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. 2D Crank-Nicolson heat equation; 2D counterpart of [`solve_heat_equation_1d_crank_nicolson`]. `init_func(x, y)`; arrays are flat with `x` fastest. # Errors As [`solve_heat_equation_nd_crank_nicolson`].\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_heat_equation_3d_crank_nicolson" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. 3D Crank-Nicolson heat equation; see [`solve_heat_equation_2d_crank_nicolson`]. # Errors As [`solve_heat_equation_nd_crank_nicolson`].\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_heat_equation_nd_crank_nicolson" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Heat equation `u_t = alpha Laplacian u` on a box by Crank-Nicolson. Factorised ADI, any of 1D, 2D, 3D, with zero Dirichlet boundaries. `ranges[a]` is the extent of axis `a`, `nodes[a]` its node count (flat arrays, `x` fastest) and `init_func` receives the coordinates. Returns `nt + 1` snapshots like [`solve_heat_equation_1d_crank_nicolson`]. # Errors For a bad shape (1 to 3 axes, >= 3 nodes each), `nt < 1` or a bad range.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_wave_equation_1d" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D Wave Equation `u_tt = c^2 * u_xx` using an explicit finite-difference method. This function implements a basic explicit finite-difference scheme for the 1D wave equation. It requires initial displacement and velocity profiles and applies Dirichlet boundary conditions. Stability is governed by the CFL condition (`c*dt/dx <= 1`). # Arguments * `initial_u` - Initial displacement vector (length `num_points`). * `initial_ut` - Initial velocity vector (length `num_points`). * `c` - Wave speed. * `dx` - Spatial step size (uniform). * `dt` - Time step size (must satisfy CFL condition). * `num_steps` - Number of time steps to march. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<Vec<f64>>` where each inner `Vec` is the solution `u` at a given time step, or an error string if input dimensions mismatch or CFL is violated. # Errors Returns an error if initial vectors have different lengths, if `num_points` < 3, or if the CFL stability condition is violated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_wave_equation_2d" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. 2D wave equation `u_tt = c^2 (u_xx + u_yy)` by explicit leapfrog. Zero Dirichlet boundaries, flat arrays with `x` fastest. 2D counterpart of [`solve_wave_equation_1d`]; stable for `|c| dt sqrt(1/dx^2 + 1/dy^2) <= 1`. # Errors For inconsistent lengths or a violated CFL condition.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_native_classical_solve_wave_equation_3d" => "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. 3D wave equation by explicit leapfrog; see [`solve_wave_equation_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_wave_equation_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
