    "num_pde_fvm_lax_friedrichs_adaptive" => "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive scalar finite-volume conservation law on fixed uniform cells. The selected flux/reconstruction is retained on every full and half step. Classical Lax-Friedrichs has timestep-dependent numerical viscosity; strict temporal tolerances may therefore fail at a fixed spatial grid, explicitly. # Errors Invalid domain/state, non-finite flux/speed, CFL underflow or error-budget exhaustion.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_pde_fvm_lax_friedrichs_adaptive_event" => "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive scalar finite-volume conservation law on fixed uniform cells. The selected flux/reconstruction is retained on every full and half step. Classical Lax-Friedrichs has timestep-dependent numerical viscosity; strict temporal tolerances may therefore fail at a fixed spatial grid, explicitly. # Errors Invalid domain/state, non-finite flux/speed, CFL underflow or error-budget exhaustion.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
