    "num_pde_heat_explicit_adaptive" => "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive heat evolution in one, two, or three spatial dimensions. BE and CN solve their full implicit operators; ADI uses Douglas splitting. Explicit trials are additionally bounded by the diffusion CFL condition. # Errors Malformed grids/BCs, invalid controls, inconsistent state, or failed convergence.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_heat_explicit_adaptive_event" => "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive heat evolution in one, two, or three spatial dimensions. BE and CN solve their full implicit operators; ADI uses Douglas splitting. Explicit trials are additionally bounded by the diffusion CFL condition. # Errors Malformed grids/BCs, invalid controls, inconsistent state, or failed convergence.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_pde_native_chebyshev_helmholtz" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a bounded Helmholtz problem by Chebyshev collocation. Solves the 1D/2D/3D Helmholtz / Poisson equation `-Δu + σ u = f(x)` on a bounded box `[lo, hi]` using Chebyshev–Gauss–Lobatto spectral collocation (`crate::sim::spectral::ChebGrid::solve_helmholtz`) with Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions. Returns `(chebyshev_node_coords, nodal_values)`. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid degrees (`< 2`), bounds, or BCs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_dg_advection_1d" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve periodic linear advection with discontinuous Galerkin elements. Solves 1D linear advection `u_t + a u_x = 0` using Nodal Discontinuous Galerkin (`crate::sim::dg::solve_dg_1d`) of polynomial degree `poly_degree >= 1` on `n_elem` elements over `[x0, x1]` with periodic boundary conditions. Returns `(lgl_nodes_x, nodal_values)` at `t = t_end`. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if parameters are invalid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_fem_1d" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a one-dimensional reaction-diffusion boundary problem by FEM. Solves the 1D reaction-diffusion BVP `-(k u_x)_x + c u = f(x)` on `[x0, x1]` with `nx` simplex intervals using `P1` or `P2` Lagrange finite elements (`crate::sim::fem_simplex`) and boundary conditions `bc` (Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin). # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if the mesh, coefficients, or BCs are invalid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_fem_2d" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a two-dimensional reaction-diffusion boundary problem by FEM. Solves the 2D elliptic PDE `-∇·(k ∇u) + c u = f(x, y)` on `[x0, x1] × [y0, y1]` triangulated into `2 * nx * ny` triangles using `P1` or `P2` Lagrange finite elements (`crate::sim::fem_simplex`) and per-face boundary conditions `bc` (Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin). # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if the mesh, coefficients, or BCs are invalid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_fem_3d" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a three-dimensional reaction-diffusion boundary problem by FEM. Solves the 3D elliptic PDE `-∇·(k ∇u) + c u = f(x, y, z)` on a tetrahedral box mesh `[lo, hi]` with `cells = [nx, ny, nz]` using `P1` or `P2` Lagrange finite elements (`crate::sim::fem_simplex`) and per-face boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if the mesh, coefficients, or BCs are invalid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_fem_adaptive_2d" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solves a 2D elliptic PDE `-∇·(k ∇u) + c u = f(x, y)` on a triangle mesh with adaptive Dörfler residual-based mesh refinement (`crate::sim::fem_triangles`). # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if the mesh or parameters are invalid.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_fem_data" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_fem_max_error" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Maximum nodal error `max_i |u_h(x_i) - exact(x_i)|`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_fvm_shallow_water_1d" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solves the 1D Shallow Water Equations (`h_t + (hu)_x = 0`, `(hu)_t + (hu^2 + g h^2 / 2)_x = 0`) using `crate::sim::physics_fvm::solve_shallow_water_1d`. Returns `(height_h, momentum_hu)` at `t = steps * dt`. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial_h.len() != initial_hu.len()`, [`FdmError::Domain`] for non-positive parameters.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_solve_fvm_burgers_1d" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solves the 1D inviscid Burgers' equation `u_t + (u^2 / 2)_x = 0` using `crate::sim::physics_fvm::solve_burgers_1d` or high-resolution MUSCL-Hancock FVM. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or step parameters.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_solve_fvm_conservation_1d" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a scalar conservation law with finite volumes. Solves a 1D scalar conservation law `u_t + f(u)_x = 0` on `num_cells` finite volumes covering `[0, domain_size]` using `scheme` (`Upwind`, `LaxFriedrichs`, `MusclMinmod`, or `MusclVanLeer`, leveraging `crate::sim::physics_fvm` limiters and fluxes) with periodic or transmissive boundary conditions. Returns `(cell_centers, cell_averages)` at `t = steps * dt`. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] for an invalid grid (including a cell width that rounds to zero), invalid time step or non-finite initial state. Returns [`FdmError::NotConverged`] if flux evaluation produces a non-finite state.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_spectral_etdrk4" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Integrate a periodic reaction-diffusion equation by ETDRK4. Integrates a periodic reaction-diffusion or heat equation `u_t = \\\\alpha Δu - \\\\sigma u + N(t, u)` in 1D, 2D, or 3D on a periodic Fourier pseudospectral grid using 4th-order Exponential Time Differencing Runge–Kutta (`crate::sim::spectral::etdrk4`) with 2/3 dealiasing on the nonlinear term. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid extents or time span, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len()` does not match the grid size.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_native_spectral_helmholtz" => "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a periodic Helmholtz problem by Fourier inversion. Solves the periodic Helmholtz / screened Poisson equation `-Δu + σ u = f` in 1D, 2D, or 3D on a periodic box of lengths `lengths` and power-of-two grid sizes `extents` using Fourier pseudospectral inversion (`crate::sim::spectral::SpectralGrid`). For `σ == 0.0` (pure periodic Poisson `-Δu = f`), the source `f` must have zero mean and the returned solution has zero mean. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if extents are not powers of two (`>= 4`) or if `σ == 0` with non-zero mean source, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `source.len()` does not match.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_spectral_etdrk4_adaptive" => "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive Fourier ETDRK4 reaction-diffusion evolution in one to three dimensions. Both linear diffusion/reaction and nonlinear callback physical times are retained on each restarted ETDRK4 step. The temporal order remains four. # Errors Invalid periodic grid, shape, coefficients, callback shape/domain or temporal controls.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_pde_spectral_etdrk4_adaptive_event" => "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive Fourier ETDRK4 reaction-diffusion evolution in one to three dimensions. Both linear diffusion/reaction and nonlinear callback physical times are retained on each restarted ETDRK4 step. The temporal order remains four. # Errors Invalid periodic grid, shape, coefficients, callback shape/domain or temporal controls.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_pde_wave_adaptive" => "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive wave evolution by variable-step velocity Verlet. Each state concatenates displacement and velocity. `velocity_atol` has displacement/time dimensions, independently of `options.atol`. # Errors Malformed grid/BCs, invalid state/tolerances, or exhausted error/CFL budgets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pde_wave_adaptive_event" => "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive wave evolution by variable-step velocity Verlet. Each state concatenates displacement and velocity. `velocity_atol` has displacement/time dimensions, independently of `options.atol`. # Errors Malformed grid/BCs, invalid state/tolerances, or exhausted error/CFL budgets.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_permgroup_compose" => "`p ∘ q`: first `q`, then `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_conjugate" => "`g p g⁻¹`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_cycle_type" => "The cycle type: the sorted cycle lengths, fixed points included. # Panics Panics if an image is outside the permutation's degree.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_cycles" => "Cycles of a checked bijective permutation (fixed points included). CSR offsets/indices preserve independently sized cycles; degree is bounded by 4096. The cycles of `p` (fixed points included), each starting at its least point. # Panics Panics if an image is outside the permutation's degree.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_element_order" => "The order of `p` (the lcm of its cycle lengths). # Panics Panics if an image is outside the permutation's degree.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_identity" => "The identity of degree `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_inverse" => "The inverse permutation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_is_identity" => "Whether `p` fixes every point.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_is_permutation" => "Whether `p` is a permutation of `0..p.len()`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_is_primitive" => "Whether a transitive group is primitive (no block system other than the trivial ones). Intransitive groups are not primitive.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_minimal_block_systems" => "All native minimal block systems under a finite degree/work budget. Top-level offsets group CSR block rows into systems; no rectangular padding changes block membership. The minimal non-trivial block systems of a transitive group (each a list of blocks), without repetitions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_minimal_blocks" => "Minimal block system identifying two checked points. Generators must be bijections of the declared degree, and seed points must be in range. Output uses CSR offsets/indices. The finest block system of a transitive group in which `a` and `b` lie in one block (Atkinson's algorithm): the blocks, each sorted, in order of their least point.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_orbits" => "Orbits of generators encoded as rows of a common-degree permutation matrix. Output uses CSR offsets/indices with finite degree and group-work budgets. The orbits of the group generated by `gens` on `0..n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_power" => "`p^k` for `k ≥ 0`, using direct composition for powers that need few compositions and cycle rotations for other powers.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_permgroup_power_exact" => "Raise a dimensionless permutation of degree up to 4096 to an exact signed integer power. A negative exponent rotates cycles in reverse, and the magnitude can have at most 65536 bits. Invalid images return an error. Raises a permutation to an arbitrary signed integer power. A negative exponent rotates each cycle in reverse. Returns `None` if the input images do not form a bijection of their degree.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_pinv" => "Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse. Moore-Penrose pseudo-inverse through the SVD. # Errors As [`svd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_poincare_disk_distance" => "Returns the dimensionless hyperbolic geodesic distance between two complex points inside the open Poincare unit disk. Hyperbolic distance in the Poincaré unit disk `|z| < 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polyfit_normalized" => "Fit a polynomial in x/coordinate_scale. Positive scale carries coordinate units; all returned coefficients retain ordinate units, so powers never require a heterogeneous coefficient tensor. The coefficients of the least-squares polynomial of `degree`, ascending. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] when the samples differ in length, and [`InterpError::Domain`] when there are fewer points than coefficients.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_add" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_compose" => "Compose outer(inner(x)). Inner ordinates must have the dimension of the outer coordinate. The result preserves the inner coordinate scale and the outer ordinate dimension. The maximum result degree is 128. Computes the polynomial composition \$P(\\\\text{inner}(x))\$ where `self` is \$P\$. The arithmetic runs in double-double and the result is rounded to `f64` once, at the end. Composition is ill-conditioned whenever the intermediate terms \$`c_k` Q(x)^k\$ are much larger than the result: a `f64` Horner and a `f64` Brent-Kung composition then disagree in the last digits, not because either is wrong but because they round at different points. Carrying the same two algorithms over [`Dd`] drops the accumulated error to about `1e-32` relative, so both round to the same `f64` and the answer is the correctly-rounded one rather than whichever strategy happened to run.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_degree" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Returns the degree of the polynomial.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_derivative" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Returns the derivative of the polynomial. The derivative is computed by applying the power rule to each term. For a term `c*x^n`, its derivative is `(c*n)*x^(n-1)`. # Returns A new `Polynomial` representing the derivative.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_div" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_div_scalar" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Divides the polynomial by a scalar. # Arguments * `rhs` - The scalar to divide by. # Returns A `Result` containing the new `Polynomial`, or an error if the scalar is zero. # Errors Returns an error if the divisor scalar is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_eval" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Evaluates the polynomial at a given point `x` using Horner's method. Horner's method is an efficient algorithm for evaluating polynomials. For a polynomial `P(x) = c_n*x^n + c_{n-1}*x^{n-1} + ... + c_1*x + c_0`, it computes `P(x) = (...((c_n*x + c_{n-1})*x + c_{n-2})*x + ... + c_1)*x + c_0`. # Arguments * `x` - The point at which to evaluate the polynomial. # Returns The value of the polynomial at `x` as an `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_eval_batch" => "Evaluate a descending-coefficient polynomial at a rank-one tensor of up to 16384 physical coordinates. The output retains the input shape, sample order and polynomial ordinate dimension. An empty tensor produces an empty result. Evaluates the polynomial at a slice of points `xs` using 4-way unrolled Horner evaluation and deterministic multi-core parallelism when the workload exceeds the execution threshold. The points are split into disjoint contiguous chunks; every chunk runs the same unrolled kernel, so each output element is produced by exactly one call and the result is bit-identical to [`Self::eval`] for every thread count.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_find_roots" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Finds the real roots of the polynomial. This method combines Sturm's theorem for root isolation with Newton's method for refining the roots. Sturm's theorem provides disjoint intervals, each containing exactly one real root, which are then used as starting points for Newton's method to converge to the root. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<f64>` of the real roots found, or an error string if root isolation or refinement fails. # Errors Returns an error if root isolation or refinement fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_from_roots" => "Build a portable polynomial from up to 128 real roots and a positive coordinate scale in the same units. The result is the monic product in x/coordinate_scale with dimensionless coefficients. Repeated roots retain their multiplicity. An empty root tensor gives the constant one. Builds the monic polynomial \$\\\\prod_{i} (x - `r_i`)\$ from its roots using a balanced divide-and-conquer subproduct tree.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_integral" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Returns the indefinite integral of the polynomial (constant of integration = 0).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_interpolate_lagrange" => "Build a portable polynomial from 1 to 129 distinct coordinates, their ordinates and a positive coordinate scale. Coordinates and scale share units. The model stores descending coefficients in ordinate units for x/coordinate_scale. Computes the unique interpolating polynomial of degree `< xs.len()` passing through `(xs[i], ys[i])` using Newton divided differences converted to the descending power basis. # Errors Returns an error if `xs` and `ys` have different lengths, are empty, contain non-finite values or duplicate `x` nodes, or the divided differences or power-basis coefficients overflow.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_is_zero" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Checks if the polynomial is zero (within epsilon).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_long_division" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Performs polynomial long division. The divisor must have a nonzero leading coefficient. Use [`Self::try_long_division`] to validate the inputs and results. # Returns A tuple `(quotient, remainder)` as `Polynomial`s. # Panics Panics if the divisor has no coefficients.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_mul" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_mul_fft" => "Multiply two descending-coefficient polynomials with the same coordinate reference using the native FFT kernel. Coefficient units multiply. Multiplies two polynomials using a packed 2-for-1 real-input complex FFT backed by [`crate::kernels::reuse::fft_plan`] and [`crate::kernels::reuse::fft_scratch_for`]. Falls back to [`Self::mul_karatsuba`] if any input coefficient is non-finite. # Accuracy The FFT accumulates a relative error of a few `f64` epsilons times `log n`, where the schoolbook loop stays at one or two epsilons and Karatsuba at a handful. That is a deliberate trade for the 1.4–3x speedup above the threshold; a caller that needs the tighter association should call [`Self::mul_karatsuba`] directly, and [`Self::compose`] sidesteps the question by accumulating in double-double. `pow` inherits whatever tier its operands select, which is why `p.pow(3)` and `p * p * p` agree exactly: both go through this same dispatch.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_mul_karatsuba" => "Multiply two descending-coefficient polynomials with the same coordinate reference using the native Karatsuba kernel. Coefficient units multiply. Multiplies two polynomials using recursive Karatsuba multiplication backed by a reusable scratch buffer from [`crate::kernels::reuse::f64_scratch`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_mul_schoolbook" => "Multiply two descending-coefficient polynomials with the same coordinate reference using schoolbook convolution. Coefficient units multiply. Multiplies two polynomials using the 4-way unrolled \$O(n m)\$ schoolbook algorithm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_new" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Creates a new `Polynomial` from coefficients (highest degree first).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_scale" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_sub" => "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_model_try_long_division" => "Divide measured descending-coefficient polynomials with the same coordinate reference. Leading zeros in the divisor are skipped. Returns quotient coefficients in dividend units divided by divisor units and remainder coefficients in dividend units. Zero divisors and nonfinite results are errors. Divides finite coefficients by a nonzero descending-coefficient polynomial. Leading zeros in the divisor are skipped. A fused update can retain a finite coefficient when a product overflows before it is subtracted. # Errors Returns an error for zero divisors, nonfinite inputs or nonfinite results.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polynomial_roots_scaled" => "Aberth-Ehrlich complex roots of a polynomial expressed in powers of x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients may share arbitrary ordinate units; roots carry coordinate units. All complex roots of a polynomial (coefficients ascending) by the Aberth-Ehrlich method with Cauchy-bound initial guesses. Leading zero coefficients are dropped; roots at zero are handled by the iteration itself. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if 500 iterations do not suffice.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_polyval_normalized" => "Evaluate coefficients with ordinate units at normalized x/coordinate_scale; returns ordinate units. Evaluates a polynomial given its coefficients in ascending order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_project" => "Project a vector onto another; zero direction produces zero. Projects vector `v1` onto vector `v2`. Formula: \$\\\\text{proj}_{\\\\mathbf{v2}}(\\\\mathbf{v1}) = \\\\frac{\\\\mathbf{v1} \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{v2}}{||\\\\mathbf{v2}||^2} \\\\mathbf{v2}\$ # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_qr" => "Householder QR with q square and r matching the input. Householder QR: returns `(Q, R)` with `Q` of size `m x m`, `R` of size `m x n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_quadrature_clenshaw_curtis_rule" => "Clenshaw-Curtis nodes and weights on `[-1, 1]` with `n + 1` points (`n` is rounded up to an even number, at least 2).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_quadrature_gauss_legendre_rule" => "Gauss-Legendre nodes and weights on `[-1, 1]` with `n` points (Newton iteration on the Legendre recurrence).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_quadrature_halton_point" => "The `index`-th point (`index >= 1`) of the Halton sequence in `dim <= 16` dimensions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_range" => "Difference between sample maximum and minimum. Computes the range (max - min) of a slice of data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_rank_tol" => "SVD rank using a nonnegative absolute singular-value tolerance. Numerical rank: singular values above `tol` (or the default `max(m, n) * eps * sigma_max` when `tol` is `None`). # Errors As [`svd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_rbf_scaled" => "RBF interpolation with independent coordinate and value units. Positive coordinate scale nondimensionalizes distances. Kernel names: gaussian, multiquadric, inverse_multiquadric, thin_plate, cubic, linear. Radial basis function (RBF) scattered-data interpolation in `R^d`. # Errors [`InterpError::Empty`] for no samples, [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] for inconsistent dimensions, [`InterpError::Domain`] for non-finite data, an invalid kernel scale, or an unrepresentable result, and [`InterpError::Singular`] for a singular interpolation matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_reflect" => "Reflect about a unit normal supplied by the caller. Reflects vector `v` about a normal vector `n`. Formula: \$\\\\mathbf{r} = \\\\mathbf{v} - 2(\\\\mathbf{v} \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{n})\\\\mathbf{n}\$ Note: The normal vector `n` must be normalized. The result can remain finite even when the intermediate correction overflows. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_riemann_zeta" => "Computes the Riemann zeta function ζ(s) for real `s`. * `s > 1`: Euler-Maclaurin summation (via [`hurwitz_zeta`] with q = 1): the first terms directly, the tail by its integral plus Bernoulli-number corrections; accurate to about 1e-15. * `s = 1`: `+∞` (the pole). * `-1/2 < s < 1`: the alternating eta series `η(s) = (1 - 2^(1-s)) ζ(s)` accelerated with Borwein's algorithm (relative error about `1e-15`); `s = 0` is exactly `-1/2`. * `s <= -1/2`: the analytic continuation by the functional equation `ζ(s) = 2^s π^(s-1) sin(πs/2) Γ(1-s) ζ(1-s)`. The trivial zeros at the negative even integers are returned as exactly `0`, and `ζ(-1) = -1/12`, `ζ(-3) = 1/120`, ... follow from the same formula. Returns `NaN` for `NaN` or `-∞`, and `±∞` where the value overflows (roughly `s < -170`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_bandwidth_scott" => "Scott's rule bandwidth `1.06 sd n^(-1/5)`. Requires at least two finite observations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_bandwidth_silverman" => "Silverman's rule-of-thumb bandwidth `0.9 min(sd, IQR/1.34) n^(-1/5)`. Uses `sd` when `IQR` is zero. Requires at least two finite observations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_biweight_location" => "Tukey biweight location estimator (tuning constant `c`, 4.685 typical).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_ecdf" => "Empirical CDF of `data` at `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_ecdf_batch" => "Empirical CDF at several query points, in the supplied order. NaN observations are ignored and NaN queries yield NaN. An empty effective sample yields NaN at every query. The ordered sample is prepared once and each query uses a binary search.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_huber_location" => "Huber M-estimator of location with tuning constant `k` (1.345 is the classical choice), scale fixed to the normalised MAD.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_huber_location_with_scale" => "Huber location using a caller-supplied scale in the units of the sample. The clipping distance is `k * scale`. NaNs are ignored. The tuning constant and scale must both be positive and finite. Returns NaN for invalid parameters or an empty effective sample. For a flat interval containing the median, returns the median.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_iqr" => "Interquartile range (type 7 quantiles).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_kde" => "Kernel density estimate of `data` evaluated at `x` with bandwidth `h`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_mad" => "Median absolute deviation; multiplied by 1.4826 when `normal` is true so it estimates the standard deviation for Gaussian data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_median" => "Median of `data` (NaNs ignored).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_quantile" => "Sample quantile of `data` at probability `p` using the Hyndman-Fan definition `kind` (1..=9; 7 is the NumPy/R default). NaNs are ignored. Returns NaN for empty data or an invalid `p`/`kind`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_quantiles" => "Hyndman-Fan sample quantiles in query order, using one shared sort. NaN observations are ignored. An empty sample or invalid definition yields NaN at every query. An invalid probability yields NaN at that query.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_trimmed_mean" => "Mean after discarding the fraction `trim` from each tail.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_weighted_ecdf" => "Fraction of total weight on observations at or below `x`. Weights must be finite and nonnegative. Returns NaN for a NaN query, unequal lengths, invalid weights, NaN observations with positive weight or zero total weight.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_weighted_quantile" => "Smallest positive-weight observation whose weighted empirical CDF is at least `p`. At `p = 0` and `p = 1` this gives the minimum and maximum positive-weight observations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_weighted_quantiles" => "Weighted empirical quantiles, sharing one sort and cumulative weight scan. Zero-weight observations are ignored. Invalid weights yield NaN at every query and an invalid probability yields NaN at that query.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_robust_winsorized_mean" => "Mean after replacing the lowest and highest fraction `trim` by the nearest retained values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_romberg" => "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. Romberg integration with Richardson extrapolation of the trapezoid rule. The error is the last difference of diagonal entries.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_scalar_mul" => "Multiplies a vector by a scalar. Formula: \$\\\\mathbf{r}_i = s \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{v}_i\$ # Arguments * `v` - The vector to scale. * `s` - The scalar factor.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_schur_eigvals_generalized" => "Generalized eigenvalues `alpha_i / beta_i` of `A v = lambda B v`. # Errors As [`qz`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_schur_lyapunov" => "Solves the continuous-time Lyapunov equation `A X + X A^T = Q`. # Errors As [`sylvester`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_schur_norm_inf" => "Infinity norm `max_i sum_j |A_{i,j}|`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_schur_qz" => "Real QZ (generalized Schur) decomposition of `(A, B)`. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`], [`DenseError::NonFinite`], or [`DenseError::NoConvergence`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_schur_schur" => "Real Schur decomposition `A = Q T Q^T`, where `Q` is orthogonal and `T` is quasi-upper-triangular (1x1 and 2x2 diagonal blocks).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_schur_sylvester" => "Solves the continuous-time Sylvester equation `A X + X B = C` by real Schur reduction and block back-substitution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sde_adaptive_diag" => "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` from `y0`. `drift` returns `n` values; `diffusion` returns `n` values for [`Structure::Diagonal`] (the diagonal entries) or `n × m` values in row-major order for [`Structure::General`]. Fixed/uniform-grid schedules use prescribed increments. Adaptive schedules retain the requested method and use a bounded noise tree with coupled step doubling, a separate drift-error check, and explicit resolution/work errors. See the module documentation for tolerance scaling and finite allocations. # Errors `E0802` for shapes/parameters, `E0806` for a non-finite state, `E0807` for a step collapse. No method/structure/step-policy combination is refused with `E1001` any more.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_sde_adaptive_general" => "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` from `y0`. `drift` returns `n` values; `diffusion` returns `n` values for [`Structure::Diagonal`] (the diagonal entries) or `n × m` values in row-major order for [`Structure::General`]. Fixed/uniform-grid schedules use prescribed increments. Adaptive schedules retain the requested method and use a bounded noise tree with coupled step doubling, a separate drift-error check, and explicit resolution/work errors. See the module documentation for tolerance scaling and finite allocations. # Errors `E0802` for shapes/parameters, `E0806` for a non-finite state, `E0807` for a step collapse. No method/structure/step-policy combination is refused with `E1001` any more.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_sde_jump_adaptive_diag" => "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates a jump-diffusion SDE `dX_t = a(t, X_{t-}) dt + G(t, X_{t-}) dW_t + \\\\sum_{q=1}^{dN_t} c(t, X_{t-}, Z_q)` over a uniform step grid specified in `options`. * `drift(t, y)` returns the drift vector `a(t, y) in R^n`. * `diffusion(t, y)` returns the diffusion coefficients (`n` for `Diagonal`, `n * m` for `General`). * `jump_coeff(t, y, z)` returns the state jump increment `c(t, y, z) in R^n` produced by a single jump mark `z` (for example, in Merton's geometric jump-diffusion `dS = \\\\mu S dt + \\\\sigma S dW + S (e^Z - 1) dN`, `c(t, S, z) = [S_0 * (z_0.exp() - 1.0)]`). Supports `EulerMaruyama`, `Milstein`, `HeunStratonovich`, `DriftImplicitEuler`, `ThetaMaruyama`, and `DriftImplicitMilstein` for the continuous diffusion part. A fixed or uniform schedule allows up to 1,048,576 integration steps and simulated jumps each. Stored path and Wiener arrays each allow up to 8,388,608 scalar values. The same value limit also bounds the state and mark components processed across all jumps. The expected jump count must fit the event budget before sampling. If the realized count exceeds the budget, the solver stops before applying that step's jumps. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] for invalid parameters or a non-finite per-step Poisson mean, [`SdeError::StepLimit`] for an exceeded work budget, [`SdeError::Shape`] for invalid shapes, or [`SdeError::NonFinite`] for a non-finite state.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_sde_jump_adaptive_general" => "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates a jump-diffusion SDE `dX_t = a(t, X_{t-}) dt + G(t, X_{t-}) dW_t + \\\\sum_{q=1}^{dN_t} c(t, X_{t-}, Z_q)` over a uniform step grid specified in `options`. * `drift(t, y)` returns the drift vector `a(t, y) in R^n`. * `diffusion(t, y)` returns the diffusion coefficients (`n` for `Diagonal`, `n * m` for `General`). * `jump_coeff(t, y, z)` returns the state jump increment `c(t, y, z) in R^n` produced by a single jump mark `z` (for example, in Merton's geometric jump-diffusion `dS = \\\\mu S dt + \\\\sigma S dW + S (e^Z - 1) dN`, `c(t, S, z) = [S_0 * (z_0.exp() - 1.0)]`). Supports `EulerMaruyama`, `Milstein`, `HeunStratonovich`, `DriftImplicitEuler`, `ThetaMaruyama`, and `DriftImplicitMilstein` for the continuous diffusion part. A fixed or uniform schedule allows up to 1,048,576 integration steps and simulated jumps each. Stored path and Wiener arrays each allow up to 8,388,608 scalar values. The same value limit also bounds the state and mark components processed across all jumps. The expected jump count must fit the event budget before sampling. If the realized count exceeds the budget, the solver stops before applying that step's jumps. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] for invalid parameters or a non-finite per-step Poisson mean, [`SdeError::StepLimit`] for an exceeded work budget, [`SdeError::Shape`] for invalid shapes, or [`SdeError::NonFinite`] for a non-finite state.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_sde_levy_adaptive_diag" => "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates an `\\\\alpha`-stable Lévy-driven SDE `dX_t = a(t, X_{t-}) dt + G(t, X_{t-}) dL_t^{(\\\\alpha, \\\\beta)}` where `dL_t` has independent `S_\\\\alpha(dt^{1/\\\\alpha}, \\\\beta, 0)` increments. # Errors Returns [`SdeError`] for invalid `\\\\alpha \\\\notin (0, 2]`, `\\\\beta \\\\notin [-1, 1]`, mismatched callback shapes, non-finite states, or a Brownian-only method requested for non-Gaussian stable noise.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_sde_levy_adaptive_general" => "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates an `\\\\alpha`-stable Lévy-driven SDE `dX_t = a(t, X_{t-}) dt + G(t, X_{t-}) dL_t^{(\\\\alpha, \\\\beta)}` where `dL_t` has independent `S_\\\\alpha(dt^{1/\\\\alpha}, \\\\beta, 0)` increments. # Errors Returns [`SdeError`] for invalid `\\\\alpha \\\\notin (0, 2]`, `\\\\beta \\\\notin [-1, 1]`, mismatched callback shapes, non-finite states, or a Brownian-only method requested for non-Gaussian stable noise.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_series_evaluate_power_series" => "Evaluates the power series `sum_n coefficients[n] * (x - center)^n` by Horner's scheme. An empty coefficient list gives 0.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sigmoid" => "Logistic sigmoid. Computes the logistic (sigmoid) function σ(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_analytic_envelope" => "Returns the amplitude envelope `hypot(x, hilbert(x))` of real samples. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite samples or an unrepresentable envelope.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_analytic_signal" => "Forms the complex analytic signal of real periodic samples. The real part is the original signal and the imaginary part is its discrete Hilbert transform. Positive-frequency bins are doubled and negative bins removed. DC and the Nyquist bin of an even-length signal are preserved. An empty input gives an empty result. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite samples or an unrepresentable transform.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_autocorrelation" => "Computes sample autocorrelations at lags from zero through `max_lag`. Divides each biased sample autocovariance by the lag-zero autocovariance. The lag-zero result is one. Input scaling cancels before the division, so the correlation remains computable when the variance itself would overflow or underflow. # Errors Returns an error for empty or non-finite samples, a lag at least as large as the sample count, a constant series, or an unrepresentable work extent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_autocovariance" => "Computes biased sample autocovariances at lags from zero through `max_lag`. For lag `k`, divides the sum of `(samples[t] - mean) * (samples[t + k] - mean)` by the full sample count. Lag zero is the population variance of the input. A constant series gives zero at all lags. Computes short lag ranges directly and uses a zero-padded FFT for sufficiently long ranges. # Errors Returns an error for empty or non-finite samples, a lag at least as large as the sample count, or an unrepresentable autocovariance or work extent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_blackman_window" => "Generates a Blackman window of length `n`. `w[i] = 0.42 - 0.5 cos(2 pi i / (n - 1)) + 0.08 cos(4 pi i / (n - 1))`, symmetric with both endpoints at zero (in exact arithmetic). # Arguments * `n` - The number of points in the output window.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_butterworth_lowpass" => "Digital low-pass Butterworth filter `(b, a)` of order `order` with normalized cutoff `cutoff` in `(0, 1)` (where `1.0` is Nyquist).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_convolve" => "Computes the one-dimensional discrete linear convolution of two sequences. Convolution is a mathematical operation that blends two functions to produce a third. In signal processing, it is used to describe the effect of a linear time-invariant system on an input signal. # Arguments * `a` - The first input sequence. * `v` - The second input sequence. # Returns The discrete linear convolution of `a` and `v`. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::signal::convolve; let a = vec![1.0, 2.0, 3.0]; let v = vec![0.0, 1.0, 0.5]; let res = convolve(&a, &v); assert_eq!(res, vec![0.0, 1.0, 2.5, 4.0, 1.5]); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_cross_correlation" => "Computes the discrete cross-correlation of two sequences. Cross-correlation is a measure of similarity of two series as a function of the displacement of one relative to the other. # Arguments * `a` - The first input sequence. * `v` - The second input sequence. # Returns The discrete cross-correlation of `a` and `v`. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::signal::cross_correlation; let a = vec![1.0, 2.0, 3.0]; let v = vec![0.0, 1.0, 0.5]; let res = cross_correlation(&a, &v); // correlation(a, v)[k] = sum_i a[i] * v[i-k] ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_dct" => "Orthonormal Type-II Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT-II) in `O(n log n)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_dst" => "Orthonormal Type-II Discrete Sine Transform (DST-II) in `O(n log n)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_filtfilt" => "`num_signal_filtfilt(b, a, samples)` filters forward and backward with odd reflection. Coefficients are dimensionless. The result preserves the length and units of the samples. Checked zero-phase forward-backward digital filter. Uses odd reflection up to `3 * (max(b.len(), a.len()) - 1)` samples on each side, capped at the signal length minus one. Coefficients are normalized by `a[0]`. The returned vector has the same length as `x`. # Errors Rejects empty coefficient vectors, a zero leading denominator, an undefined steady state, non-finite inputs or intermediate values, and an unallocatable output extent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_firwin_bandpass" => "Designs a symmetric band-pass FIR with unit gain at the passband center. The cutoffs are ordered and normalized to Nyquist, with `0 < low < high < 1`. The window is Kaiser, parameterized by `beta`. # Errors Errors for invalid parameters or unrepresentable coefficients.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_firwin_lowpass" => "Designs a symmetric, unit-DC-gain low-pass FIR using a Kaiser window. The cutoff is normalized to Nyquist and must lie strictly between zero and one. A larger absolute `beta` improves sidelobe attenuation at the expense of transition width. # Errors Errors for invalid parameters or unrepresentable coefficients.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_frequency_response" => "Evaluates a digital transfer function at frequencies in cycles per sample. Uses the coefficient convention of [`lfilter`]. Frequencies are periodic modulo one, with Nyquist at `0.5`. Returns a complex response per frequency. # Errors Errors for empty coefficients, non-finite inputs or a singular or unrepresentable response at a requested frequency.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_hamming_window" => "Generates a Hamming window of length `n`. # Arguments * `n` - The number of points in the output window.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_hann_window" => "Generates a Hann window of length `n`. # Arguments * `n` - The number of points in the output window.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_hilbert" => "Returns the discrete Hilbert transform of real periodic samples. A positive-frequency cosine becomes a sine, and DC and Nyquist components contribute zero. See [`analytic_signal`] for the boundary convention. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite samples or an unrepresentable transform.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_idct" => "Inverse of [`dct`] (orthonormal Type-III DCT).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_idst" => "Inverse of [`dst`] (orthonormal Type-III DST).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_inverse_stft" => "Reconstructs a real signal from full [`stft`] spectra. Applies the same window on synthesis and divides each sample by its sum of squared window weights. Returns the interval covered by the frames, of length `(frames.len() - 1) * hop + window.len()`. The imaginary part after the inverse transform is discarded. Use a window and hop that have nonzero overlap weights across that interval, or pad the analysis when endpoint samples must be preserved. # Errors Returns an error for invalid parameters, inconsistent or non-finite frames, uncovered samples, output size overflow or non-finite reconstruction.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_kaiser_window" => "Generates a symmetric Kaiser window with shape parameter `beta`. Either sign of a finite `beta` produces the same coefficients. For `n > 1`, a non-finite shape parameter returns NaNs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_lfilter" => "Linear FIR/IIR digital filter (`a[0] * y[n] = sum_k b[k]*x[n-k] - sum_{k>=1} a[k]*y[n-k]`) evaluated via Direct Form II Transposed with pooled scratch state.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_partial_autocorrelation" => "Computes Yule–Walker partial autocorrelations at lags zero through `max_lag`. Uses the Durbin–Levinson recursion on the biased sample autocorrelations. For lag `k > 0`, the result is the final coefficient of the order-`k` autoregression. The lag-zero result is one. After calculating the autocorrelations, the recursion takes `O(max_lag²)` time and `O(max_lag)` space. # Errors Returns the same input errors as [autocorrelation] and also rejects an autoregression with numerically singular prediction-error variance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_resample_poly" => "Resamples a real signal by a rational factor using a centered polyphase FIR. Reduces the factors by their greatest common divisor, then designs a Kaiser low-pass filter with 10 taps on each side per rate phase (`beta = 5`). The input is zero extended at the boundaries. Output time zero aligns with input time zero, and the output contains `ceil(samples.len() * up / down)` samples. # Errors Errors for a zero factor, non-finite input or output, or unallocatable filter or output dimensions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_savgol_coeffs" => "Savitzky-Golay smoothing filter coefficients for odd `window_len` and polynomial degree `poly_order < window_len`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_savgol_filter" => "Applies a Savitzky-Golay smoothing filter of odd `window_len` and degree `poly_order` to `x` with clamped boundary padding.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_sos_filter" => "Applies a zero-state cascade of second-order digital sections. Each row of `sections` is `[b0, b1, b2, a0, a1, a2]`. The coefficients are normalized by `a0` and evaluated with transposed direct form II. An empty cascade is the identity. # Errors Errors for a zero leading denominator, non-finite input, or unrepresentable state or output.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_spectrogram" => "Power spectrogram `|STFT|^2` of `x` with `window` and `hop`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_upfirdn" => "Upsamples, applies a finite impulse response filter and downsamples. This implements zero insertion by `up`, linear convolution with `taps`, then retention of every `down`th sample starting at index zero. It works directly on filter phases without allocating the upsampled signal or multiplying by its inserted zeros. The boundaries are zero extended. The supplied taps determine gain, passband, alias rejection and delay. If either input slice is empty the result is empty. Otherwise the output length is `floor(((samples.len() - 1) * up + taps.len() - 1) / down) + 1`. # Errors Returns an error for a zero factor, non-finite data, non-finite output or an output extent that overflows or cannot be allocated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_welch_cross_spectrum" => "Estimates one-sided cross-spectral density and magnitude-squared coherence. Applies the same complete-frame Welch convention as [`welch_psd`] to both inputs. For the Fourier bins `X` and `Y`, it averages `conj(X) * Y` across frames to obtain the cross-spectral density `Pxy`, and computes `abs(Pxy)^2 / (Pxx * Pyy)`. Auto-spectra and cross-spectra share the same window energy and one-sided scaling. # Errors Returns an error on unequal lengths or on the invalid input domains described by [`welch_psd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_welch_psd" => "Estimates the one-sided power spectral density of real samples by Welch's method. Averages the periodograms of complete windowed frames, whose starts are `hop` samples apart. Pass the window coefficients explicitly, for example with [`hann_window`]. The transform uses the window length and performs no detrending. Frequencies have the same unit as `sample_rate`. Only bins with a negative-frequency partner are doubled. Multiplying each bin by the frequency spacing and summing gives the mean window-weighted frame power. Trailing samples that do not complete a frame are ignored. Large batches average fixed blocks in order so the result is independent of the worker count. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid window, hop or sample rate, insufficient samples, non-finite data in a complete frame or non-finite computed power.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_signal_zoom_fft" => "Computes arbitrary equally spaced Fourier samples using a chirp convolution. Bin `k` is `sum_n x[n] exp(-2 pi i (start + k * step) n)` with `start` and `step` measured in cycles per input sample. Frequencies are periodic modulo one. This is the unit-circle chirp z-transform. It can resolve a sub-band without evaluating the rest of the spectrum. A zero step repeats one frequency. Zero bins give an empty result and an empty input gives `bins` zeros. Time and storage scale with the input and output extents, using an FFT of length `next_power_of_two(x.len() + bins - 1)`. Repeated moderate transforms with the same input length, bin count, and step can share the transformed chirp, even when the start varies. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite frequencies or input, unrepresentable intermediate arithmetic, or an output or work extent that is too large.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sinc" => "Unnormalised sinc, sin(x)/x. Computes the sinc function sinc(x) = sin(πx) / (πx).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sine_integral" => "The sine integral `Si(x) = ∫_0^x sin(t)/t dt` (odd).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sinh_integral" => "The hyperbolic sine integral `Shi(x) = ∫_0^x sinh(t)/t dt` (odd).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_skewness" => "Computes the adjusted Fisher-Pearson sample skewness coefficient `G1` (the estimator used by Excel's `SKEW` and by `scipy.stats.skew(bias=False)`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_softplus" => "Softplus function. Computes the softplus function softplus(x) = ln(1 + e^x).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_solve_dg" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Integrates `q_t + ∇·F(q) = 0` with nodal DG to `t_end`. `bcs` lists `[XMin, XMax, YMin, YMax]`; 1D meshes use the first two. # Errors If the mesh is invalid, periodic faces are inconsistent or the initial data have the wrong number of components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_solve_dg3d" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Integrates `q_t + ∇·F(q) = 0` on a hexahedral mesh to `t_end` with SSP-RK3. `bcs` lists `[XMin, XMax, YMin, YMax, ZMin, ZMax]`. # Errors For an invalid mesh, inconsistent periodic faces, a wrong component count in the initial state or a fixed face used by the spatial operator, or a non-finite wave speed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_solve_dg_limited" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. As [`solve_dg`] with an optional slope / positivity limiter applied after every Runge–Kutta stage. # Errors See [`solve_dg`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_solve_dg_with" => "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Integrates `q_t + ∇·F(q) = 0` with nodal DG and the full option set (characteristic / hierarchical / WENO limiters, entropy-stable fluxes). # Errors If the mesh is invalid, periodic faces are inconsistent or the initial data have the wrong number of components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_auto_model_apply" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Writes `M^-1 r` into `z`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_auto_model_borrow_apply" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Borrows the selected factors as a [`Preconditioner`] for [`cg_with`], [`gmres_with`] and [`bicgstab_with`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_auto_model_build" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Builds the strongest incomplete factorisation that succeeds; the chain always terminates with Jacobi, so this never fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_auto_model_kind" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. The preconditioner that [`AutoPreconditioner::new`] selected.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_ic_model_apply" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Applies the preconditioner `M^-1 = L^-T D^-1 L^-1` by one forward substitution, a diagonal scaling and one backward substitution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_ic_model_build" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. IC(0) factorisation `A ≈ L D L^T` (incomplete Cholesky without fill). The lower triangular factor uses the strictly lower pattern of `A`; the upper triangle of `A` is ignored, so the matrix must be symmetric and positive definite for `M = L D L^T` to be a valid CG preconditioner. On a symmetric matrix this is the same operator as [`Csr::ilu0`] written as `L D L^T` instead of `L U`. # Returns `None` for malformed CSR, a missing diagonal, a nonpositive pivot, or unrepresentable lower-triangular factor entries. The factor stores only the strict lower triangle, its transpose and the pivots.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_ilu_model_apply" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Solves `L U z = r` (applies the preconditioner `M^-1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_ilu_model_build" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. ILU(0) factorisation (incomplete LU keeping the sparsity pattern). The factors are stored in the pattern of `A`: the strictly lower entries of `L` (unit diagonal) and the entries of `U` in the same arrays. No fill is created and no pivoting is performed. # Returns `None` when the matrix has no entry on the diagonal of some row or when a zero pivot is met (structurally or after elimination). The factorisation never returns `NaN`: on `None` the caller keeps the unpreconditioned path or falls back to [`Csr::jacobi_inverse`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_lu_model_build" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Sparse LU factorisation with a fill-reducing symmetric ordering and threshold partial pivoting (left-looking Gilbert-Peierls with a depth-first symbolic reach); see [`SparseLu`]. # Errors [`SparseLuError::NotSquare`] or [`SparseLuError::Singular`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_lu_model_dim" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Dimension of the factored matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_lu_model_nnz" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Number of stored entries in `L` and `U` (a measure of fill-in).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_lu_model_permutation" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. The symmetric fill-reducing permutation used (`perm[new] = old`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_sparse_lu_model_solve" => "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Solves `A x = b`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bernoulli_number" => "Computes the n-th Bernoulli number `B_n`. Orders up to `BERNOULLI_TABLE_MAX` are served from a small bucketed table cache (the three buckets are 32, 64 and 128), so repeated polynomial and zeta evaluations do not rebuild the recurrence.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bernoulli_poly" => "Computes the n-th Bernoulli polynomial `B_n(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bessel_i" => "Modified Bessel function of the first kind `I_ν(x)`, by `(1/π)∫_0^π e^{x cos θ} cos νθ dθ - (sin νπ/π)∫_0^∞ e^{-x cosh t - νt} dt` (`x < 0` for integer orders by parity).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bessel_i0_batch" => "[`bessel_i0`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bessel_i0e" => "Exponentially scaled modified Bessel I₀, `exp(-|x|) I₀(x)`. The scaled form stays finite when the unscaled function overflows. Both infinities map to zero and NaN is propagated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bessel_j" => "Bessel function of the first kind `J_ν(x)` for real order `ν` and real `x` (`x < 0` only for integer orders, through `J_n(-x) = (-1)^n J_n(x)`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bessel_j0_batch" => "[`bessel_j0`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bessel_j1_batch" => "[`bessel_j1`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bessel_k" => "Modified Bessel function of the second kind `K_ν(x)` for `x > 0`, by [its positive integral representation](https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.32.E9).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_bessel_y" => "Bessel function of the second kind `Y_ν(x)` for real order and `x > 0`, by `(1/π)∫_0^π sin(x sin θ - νθ) dθ - (1/π)∫_0^∞ (e^{νt} + e^{-νt} cos νπ) e^{-x sinh t} dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_beta_numerical" => "Computes the beta function, B(a, b) = Γ(a)Γ(b)/Γ(a+b). Defined here for `a, b > 0`; any other argument (zero, negative, NaN) gives NaN rather than reaching `statrs`, which panics on it.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_chebyshev_t" => "Computes the Chebyshev polynomial of the first kind `T_n(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_chebyshev_u" => "Computes the Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind `U_n(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_digamma_batch" => "[`digamma_numerical`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_double_factorial" => "Computes the double factorial n!! n!! = n * (n-2) * (n-4) * ... * (1 or 2)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_elliptic_pi_plan_complete" => "Returns the dimensionless complete third-kind elliptic integral of the prepared parameters. Returns the complete integral for the prepared parameters.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_elliptic_pi_plan_eval" => "Evaluates the incomplete elliptic integral of the third kind at an amplitude in radians, tracking complete periods of the prepared parameters. Evaluates the incomplete integral at amplitude `phi` in radians.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_elliptic_pi_plan_eval_many" => "Evaluates the incomplete elliptic integral of the third kind in amplitude order. All amplitudes are in radians and share the same prepared characteristic, parameter and complete integral. Evaluates the incomplete integral at amplitude `phi` in radians.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_elliptic_pi_plan_new" => "Prepares the third-kind elliptic integral at fixed characteristic n and parameter m. Use finite dimensionless values below one. Prepares a complete integral and its incomplete continuation. # Errors The characteristic or parameter is non-finite or greater than or equal to one, or the computed complete integral is unrepresentable.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_erf_batch" => "[`erf_numerical`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_erfc_batch" => "[`erfc_numerical`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_exact_chebyshev_t_exact" => "Chebyshev polynomial of the first kind `T_n(x)` over the rationals. Uses logarithmically many integer operations before reduction.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_exact_chebyshev_tu_mod" => "Returns `(T_n(x), U_n(x))` reduced modulo a positive modulus. The residues are in `0..modulus`, including for negative `x` and composite moduli. Runs in `O(log n)` modular operations for any `u64` degree. A zero modulus gives `None`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_exact_chebyshev_u_exact" => "Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind `U_n(x)` over the rationals. Uses logarithmically many integer operations before reduction.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_exact_hermite_h_exact" => "`H_n(x)` over the rationals.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_exact_laguerre_l_exact" => "`L_n(x)` over the rationals.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_exact_legendre_p_exact" => "`P_n(x)` over the rationals.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_anger_j" => "The Anger function `J_ν(x) = (1/π) ∫_0^π cos(νθ - x sin θ) dθ` for real `ν` and `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_appell_f1" => "Appell's first double hypergeometric function `F_1(a; b_1, b_2; c; x, y)` `= Σ_{m,n≥0} (a)_{m+n} (b_1)_m (b_2)_n / ((c)_{m+n} m! n!) x^m y^n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_bessel_in" => "Modified Bessel function of the first kind `I_n(x)` for integer order. Use [`bessel_in_scaled`] or [`bessel_in_log_abs`] when the unscaled magnitude is outside the floating-point range. Nonfinite inputs return NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_bessel_in_log_abs" => "Natural logarithm of the real magnitude `|I_n(x)|` for integer order. The logarithm remains available after either value representation underflows or overflows. The result is even in both arguments. It is zero for `n = 0, x = 0`, negative infinity for nonzero order at zero, and positive infinity for `x = ±∞`. A NaN argument returns NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_bessel_in_scaled" => "Exponentially scaled modified Bessel function `exp(-|x|) I_n(x)` for integer order. Its limit at either infinity is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_bessel_jn" => "Bessel function of the first kind `J_n(x)` for integer order. Small arguments (`x^2/4 <= n + 1`) use the power series; large arguments use the Hankel asymptotic expansion (with the phase reduced exactly, so the accuracy does not degrade with `x`, cost `O(1)`); everything else uses Miller's backward recurrence started at an asymptotically chosen order (`~ max(n, x) + 20 max(n, x)^(1/3)`) and normalised by `J_0 + 2 sum J_{2k} = 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_carlson_rd" => "Carlson's `R_D(x, y, z)` for finite non-negative `x, y`, not both zero, and finite positive `z`; invalid arguments or nonconvergence return NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_carlson_rf" => "Carlson's symmetric elliptic integral `R_F(x, y, z)`. Arguments must be finite and non-negative, with at most one zero; invalid arguments or failure of bounded duplication return NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_carlson_rg" => "Carlson's symmetric elliptic integral of the second kind `R_G(x, y, z)`. The arguments must be nonnegative and finite. Invalid arguments return NaN. Two zero arguments are allowed, with `R_G(0, 0, z) = sqrt(z) / 2`. The implementation uses [DLMF 19.21.10](https://dlmf.nist.gov/19.21.E10) with the median argument in its distinguished position to avoid cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_coulomb_c" => "The Coulomb normalising constant `C_l(η)` (DLMF 33.2.5), `C_l(η) = 2^l e^{-πη/2} |Γ(l + 1 + iη)| / Γ(2l + 2)`, evaluated in logarithmic form so that large `|η|` neither overflows nor underflows prematurely.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_coulomb_f" => "The regular Coulomb function `F_l(η, ρ)` (DLMF 33.2.4). Domain and accuracy: see [`coulomb_fg`]; `None` where no representation is available.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_coulomb_f_prime" => "The derivative `dF_l/dρ` of the regular Coulomb function. Domain and accuracy: see [`coulomb_fg`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_coulomb_fg" => "The regular and irregular Coulomb functions and their derivatives. `F_l(η, ρ)` is the solution regular at the origin and `G_l(η, ρ)` the irregular solution normalised by the Wronskian `F' G - F G' = 1` (so that `G ~ cos θ` at large `ρ`). The dimensionless variables are the Sommerfeld parameter `η` and `ρ = k r`; `l ≥ 0` is the integer angular momentum. The result is `None` outside the domain of the implemented representations (`ρ ≤ 0`, non-finite arguments, `|η| > 10⁶`, `l > 64`, or `24 < ρ ≤ 34` outside the classically allowed region), and also when Steed's fraction fails to certify the physical solution. References: DLMF §§33.2, 33.8 and 33.11; the series is evaluated in double-double arithmetic beyond `ρ = 3` to defeat the `e^{2ρ}` cancellation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_coulomb_g" => "The irregular Coulomb function `G_l(η, ρ)`, normalised by `F' G - F G' = 1` (DLMF 33.2.6).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_coulomb_g_from_u" => "The irregular Coulomb function `G_l(η, ρ)` and its derivative from the Tricomi function. (DLMF 33.2.7): `H⁺ = G + iF = e^{iθ} (-2iρ)^{l+1+iη} U(l + 1 + iη, 2l + 2, -2iρ)` with `θ = ρ - η ln 2ρ - lπ/2 + σ_l`, and `dU/dρ = 2i a U(a + 1, b + 1, -2iρ)` for the derivative. Unlike Steed's continued fraction for `H⁺'/H⁺`, which converges slowly and loses digits at small `ρ`, this needs no certification; its accuracy is that of [`crate::kernels::hypergeometric::hyp_u_cplx`] on the imaginary axis. `None` for `ρ <= 0`, `l > 64` or non-finite input.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_coulomb_g_prime" => "The derivative `dG_l/dρ` of the irregular Coulomb function. Domain and accuracy: see [`coulomb_fg`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_coulomb_sigma" => "The Coulomb phase shift `σ_l(η) = arg Γ(l + 1 + iη)` (DLMF 33.2.10).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_elliptic_e" => "Complete elliptic integral of the second kind `E(m)`, `m <= 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_elliptic_e_inc" => "Incomplete elliptic integral of the second kind `E(phi, m)`. The amplitude `phi` must be finite and the real parameter `m <= 1`. Invalid amplitudes or parameters return NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_elliptic_f" => "Incomplete elliptic integral of the first kind `F(phi, m)`. The amplitude `phi` must be finite and the real parameter `m <= 1`. At `m = 1` the integral has signed infinity at or beyond the first pole `|phi| = pi / 2`. Invalid amplitudes or parameters return NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_elliptic_k" => "Complete elliptic integral of the first kind `K(m)`, `m < 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_hyp1f1" => "Kummer confluent hypergeometric function `1F1(a; b; x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_hyp2f1" => "Gauss hypergeometric function `2F1(a, b; c; z)` for real arguments. * `z = 0` and terminating series (`a` or `b` a non-positive integer) are evaluated exactly for any `z`; * `0 <= z <= 0.6`: power series; * `0.6 < z < 1`: the `1 - z` connection formula, including the logarithmic cases when `c - a - b` is an integer (digamma expansions of Abramowitz-Stegun 15.3.10-15.3.11) and Euler's transformation for negative integer `c - a - b`; * `z < 0`: Pfaff's transformation `z -> z / (z - 1)` followed by the above, which also realises the `1 / (1 - z)` and `1 / z` continuations for large `|z|`; * `z = 1`: Gauss's summation theorem (infinite when divergent); * `z > 1`: the value is complex in general, so NaN, except for polynomials (terminating series, or `c - a`, `c - b` a non-positive integer with integer `c - a - b`). Accuracy is about `1e-13` generically; when `c - a - b` is within `1e-4` of, but not equal to, an integer the evaluation switches to the analytic continuation of the differential equation, which has no cancellation (exact integers use the logarithmic limit formulas). A non-positive integer `c` (other than a terminating series) gives NaN. For `z > 1` use [`hyp2f1_complex`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_hyp2f1_complex" => "Gauss hypergeometric function `2F1(a, b; c; z)` for a complex argument and real parameters, on the principal branch (cut along `[1, inf)`; real `z > 1` gives the limit from the upper half plane).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_jacobi_amplitude" => "Jacobi amplitude `am(u | m)`, the continuous real inverse of [`elliptic_f`] for `m` in `[0, 1]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_jacobi_elliptic" => "Jacobi elliptic functions `(sn, cn, dn)` of `u` with parameter `m = k^2` in `[0, 1]`. Nonfinite amplitudes or invalid parameters give three NaNs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_jacobi_elliptic_batch" => "Evaluates the Jacobi elliptic functions at several points with one parameter. Each output row holds `[sn, cn, dn]` for the corresponding input. Invalid arguments produce the same NaN rows as [`jacobi_elliptic`]. The Landen plan is reused across all rows, and large batches can run in parallel without changing result order or arithmetic.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_kelvin_bei" => "The Kelvin function `bei_ν(x) = Im J_ν(x e^(3πi/4))`; domain and accuracy as [`kelvin_ber`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_kelvin_ber" => "The Kelvin function `ber_ν(x) = Re J_ν(x e^(3πi/4))` for an integer order `ν <= 100` and `x >= 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_kelvin_kei" => "The Kelvin function `kei_ν(x)`, the imaginary part of `e^(-iπν/2) K_ν(x e^(iπ/4))`; domain and accuracy as [`kelvin_ker`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_kelvin_ker" => "The Kelvin function `ker_ν(x) + i kei_ν(x) = e^(-iπν/2) K_ν(x e^(iπ/4))` for an integer order `ν <= 100` and `x > 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_lerch" => "The Lerch transcendent `Φ(z, s, a) = Σ_{k=0}^∞ z^k / (k+a)^s` for real `s`, real `a > 0` and `z < 1` (the standard range).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_polylog" => "The polylogarithm `Li_s(z)` for real `s` and real `z`. Real on `z <= 1` (including the negative axis); for `z > 1` the principal value is complex in general and `NaN` is returned, except for non-positive integer `s`, where `Li_{-n}(z)` is the real rational function `z A_n(z)/(1-z)^(n+1)`. At `z = 1` the analytic continuation `ζ(s)` is returned (and `+∞` at the pole `s = 1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_polylog_complex" => "The polylogarithm `Li_s(z)` for complex `s` and `z`, on the principal branch (cut along `[1, ∞)`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ext_weber_e" => "The Weber function `E_ν(x) = (1/π) ∫_0^π sin(νθ - x sin θ) dθ` for real `ν` and `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_gamma_batch" => "[`gamma_numerical`] over a slice. Elements are independent and the outputs are collected in input order, so the result is bit-identical to calling the scalar function on each element, for every thread count.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_generalized_laguerre" => "The generalised Laguerre polynomial `L_n^{(α)}(x)` by the three-term recurrence.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_hermite_h" => "Computes the (physicists') Hermite polynomial `H_n(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_hurwitz_zeta" => "Computes the Hurwitz zeta function ζ(s, q) for real s and q. Defined as Σ_{n=0}^∞ 1/(n+q)^s.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_incomplete_beta" => "Computes the incomplete beta function, B(x; a, b) = ∫₀ˣ t^(a-1) (1-t)^(b-1) dt.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_laguerre_l" => "Computes the Laguerre polynomial `L_n(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_lambert_w" => "The Lambert W function: `W(x) e^{W(x)} = x`. `upper = true` is the principal branch `W₀` (defined for `x ≥ -1/e`); `upper = false` the branch `W₋₁` (for `-1/e ≤ x < 0`). NaN outside the domain. Halley's iteration from a branch-appropriate start.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_legendre_p" => "Computes the Legendre polynomial `P_n(x)` using recurrence relation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ln_bessel_k" => "Natural logarithm of the modified Bessel function `K_ν(x)` for real order. Closed forms or a positive integral centered and scaled at its peak keep the result available even if `K_ν(x)` itself overflows or underflows. Even in `ν`. At zero the result is positive infinity, and at positive infinity it is negative infinity for finite order. Negative arguments and an infinite argument of infinite order give NaN.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ln_beta_numerical" => "Computes the natural logarithm of the beta function, ln(B(a, b)). Defined for `a, b > 0`; other arguments give NaN (see [`beta_numerical`]).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_ln_gamma_batch" => "[`ln_gamma_numerical`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_lower_incomplete_gamma" => "Computes the lower incomplete gamma function, γ(s, x) = ∫₀ˣ t^(s-1) e^(-t) dt. Uses series expansion for computation. Computed as `P(s, x) Gamma(s)` from the regularised function (series or continued fraction), so it is accurate for large `x` too.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_polygamma_numerical" => "Computes the polygamma function ψ^(n)(z) for real z > 0 and integer n >= 0. The 0-th polygamma is the digamma function.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_regularized_beta" => "Computes the regularized incomplete beta function, `I_x(a`, b) = B(x; a, b) / B(a, b). Uses continued fraction expansion for better convergence.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_regularized_lower_gamma" => "The regularised lower incomplete gamma function `P(s, x)`, accurate in both tails; `P(0, x) = 1` for `x > 0` and NaN for `s < 0` or `x < 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_regularized_upper_gamma" => "The regularised upper incomplete gamma function `Q(s, x) = 1 - P(s, x)`, computed directly so that `Q` of order `1e-300` keeps its digits.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_special_upper_incomplete_gamma" => "The upper incomplete gamma function `Gamma(s, x)`, computed from the complementary tail (no cancellation against `Gamma(s)`); defined for every real `s` when `x > 0` (`s <= 0` through the recurrence and `E1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_spectral_radius" => "Largest absolute eigenvalue. Spectral radius estimate through [`eigenvalues`]. # Errors As [`eigenvalues`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_spline_clamped" => "Clamped cubic spline with explicit endpoint derivatives, each in ordinate per abscissa units. Cubic spline interpolation with selectable boundary conditions (`Natural`, `Clamped`, or `NotAKnot`). # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`], [`InterpError::Empty`], or [`InterpError::Domain`] when knots are not strictly increasing.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_spline_not_a_knot" => "Not-a-knot cubic spline with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Cubic spline interpolation with selectable boundary conditions (`Natural`, `Clamped`, or `NotAKnot`). # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`], [`InterpError::Empty`], or [`InterpError::Domain`] when knots are not strictly increasing.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_standard_error" => "Standard error of the mean. Computes the sample standard error of the mean (SEM = `std_dev` / sqrt(n)).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_chi_squared_test" => "Performs a chi-squared goodness-of-fit test. Tests if observed frequencies match expected frequencies. Returns (chi-squared statistic, p-value).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_adjust_p_bh" => "Benjamini–Hochberg adjusted p-values for false discovery rate control. The guarantee holds under independence or positive regression dependence of the null tests. Results retain the original order. An empty input yields an empty vector. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if any p-value is outside `[0, 1]` or non-finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_adjust_p_by" => "Benjamini–Yekutieli adjusted p-values for false discovery rate control under arbitrary dependence. Results retain the original order. An empty input yields an empty vector.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_adjust_p_holm" => "Holm step-down adjusted p-values for family-wise error rate control. Results retain the original order. An empty input yields an empty vector.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_anova_one_way" => "One-way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) across `k >= 2` groups. Returns `TestResult` with `statistic = F`, `df = Some(df_between)`, and upper-tail `p_value`. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if fewer than 2 groups, any group is empty, total observations `<= groups.len()`, or within-group variance is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_chi_squared_goodness_of_fit" => "Pearson's chi-squared goodness-of-fit test. # Errors [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`] if lengths differ, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if fewer than 2 categories or any expected count is `<= 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_chi_squared_independence" => "Pearson's chi-squared test of independence on a row-major `rows × cols` contingency table. # Errors [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`] if `observed.len() != rows * cols`, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if `rows < 2`, `cols < 2`, any entry is negative, or any row/column marginal total is zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_kde" => "Gaussian Kernel Density Estimation evaluated at `points`. When `bandwidth` is `None`, uses Silverman's rule of thumb. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`] if `samples` is empty, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if `bandwidth <= 0` or cannot be determined from a zero-spread sample.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_kendall" => "Kendall's tau-b rank correlation, adjusted for ties in either sample. Lexicographic sorting and merge-based inversion counting take `O(n log n)` time and `O(n)` scratch space. # Errors Returns an error for mismatched lengths, fewer than two observations, non-finite observations, or a constant sample.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_ks_test_two_sample" => "Two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov test comparing two empirical distributions. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`] when either sample is empty.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_lasso" => "Lasso (L1-regularized) linear regression via coordinate descent, minimizing `(1 / (2n)) ||y - Xβ||₂² + alpha ||β||₁`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_logistic_regression" => "Binary logistic regression via Newton-Raphson (IRLS). Expects `y[i]` in `[0, 1]` (typically `0.0` or `1.0`). # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`], [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`], [`StatsExtError::Domain`] for invalid `y` or tolerances, or [`StatsExtError::NotConverged`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_mann_whitney_u" => "Two-sided Mann–Whitney U test (Wilcoxon rank-sum test) with tie-corrected normal approximation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_ols" => "Ordinary least squares with a minimum-norm solution for deficient designs. # Errors Rejects invalid dimensions, non-finite data or results, or failed decomposition.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_pca" => "Principal Component Analysis via SVD of the mean-centered data matrix. `data` is row-major `rows × cols`. If `n_components` is `None`, retains `min(rows, cols)` components. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`], [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`], [`StatsExtError::Domain`] when `n_components` is 0 or exceeds `min(rows, cols)`, or [`StatsExtError::NotConverged`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_ridge" => "Ridge (L2-regularized) linear regression minimizing `||y - Xβ||₂² + alpha ||β||₂²`. Uses the thin SVD of the design without allocating a square feature matrix. # Errors Rejects invalid dimensions, non-finite data or results, and failed decomposition. A negative or non-finite `alpha` is invalid. For `alpha == 0`, a design without full column rank returns [`StatsExtError::Singular`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_spearman" => "Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (with average ranks for ties). # Errors [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`] when lengths differ, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] when either sample has zero variance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_t_test_one_sample" => "One-sample Student's t-test against population mean `mu0`. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Domain`] for fewer than two observations, non-finite observations or null mean, or a sample with zero variance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_t_test_paired" => "Paired-samples t-test on `left - right`. # Errors [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`] if lengths differ, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if fewer than 2 pairs or differences have zero variance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_ext_t_test_two_sample" => "Two-sample t-test with pooled variance or Welch's unequal-variance formula. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Domain`] for fewer than two observations per sample, non-finite data, or zero combined standard error.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_kruskal_wallis" => "Kruskal–Wallis rank test for independently sized groups encoded as CSR offsets/values. Samples share one measurement unit. The tie-adjusted statistic, chi-square right-tail probability and degrees of freedom are dimensionless. All pooled samples tied gives a domain error. Kruskal–Wallis rank test across independent groups. Pools all observations, assigns average ranks to ties, and returns the tie-corrected H statistic and its upper-tail chi-squared approximation with `df = groups.len() - 1`. # Errors Returns [`StatsExtError::Empty`] for no groups or an empty group. Returns [`StatsExtError::Domain`] for one group, non-finite observations, all observations tied, or pooled data that cannot be allocated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_mode" => "Finds the mode (most frequent value) of a slice of data. For continuous data, rounds to a specified number of decimal places. Returns None if no mode exists (all values unique or empty).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_one_way_anova" => "One-way ANOVA on independently sized groups encoded as CSR offsets/values. Input groups have one shared measurement unit; F statistic and p-value are dimensionless. Performs a One-Way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) test. Tests the null hypothesis that the means of two or more groups are equal. # Arguments * `groups` - A slice of slices, where each inner slice is a group of data. # Returns A tuple containing the F-statistic and the p-value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_percentile" => "Measured percentile with a private mutable copy of the input. The native sorting/selection routine cannot mutate the caller's tensor; percentile p is dimensionless in 0 through 100. Computes the p-th percentile of a slice of data. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `f64` data points. * `p` - The desired percentile (e.g., 50.0 for median). # Returns The p-th percentile of the data (fractional `p` allowed), or NaN for empty data, data containing NaN, or `p` outside `0..=100`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_simple_linear_regression" => "Least-squares regression on separately measured equal-length predictors and responses. The slope carries response divided by predictor units, and the intercept carries response units. Finite moments are recovered at extreme coordinate scales when possible. Fits the least-squares line `y = intercept + slope * x` to paired samples. Returns `(slope, intercept)`. Empty and nonfinite samples return two NaNs. A constant predictor returns a NaN slope and the response mean. The calculation borrows the observations and rescales centered moments when the unscaled moments exceed the Float range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_two_sample_t_test" => "Performs an independent two-sample t-test to determine if two samples have different means. # Returns A tuple containing the t-statistic and the p-value.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_variance_with_type" => "Computes the variance of a slice of data with a specified variance type (Population or Sample). This implementation uses Welford's algorithm to compute the variance in a single pass, which is highly numerically stable even for very large numbers or shifted datasets. # Arguments * `data` - A slice of `f64` data points. * `var_type` - The type of variance to compute (Population or Sample). # Returns `Some(variance)` if `data.len() >= 2`, otherwise `None`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_stats_welch_t_test" => "Performs Welch's t-test for two samples with potentially unequal variances. Returns (t-statistic, p-value).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_std_dev" => "Sample standard deviation. Computes the standard deviation of a slice of data. # Arguments * `data` - A slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The standard deviation of the data as an `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_svd" => "Thin SVD A = U diag(s) V^T; returns u, s and v. Thin singular-value decomposition using the bidiagonal SVD backend. Rank-deficient matrices retain orthonormal null-space columns. No Gram matrix is formed, so small singular values do not lose accuracy through squaring the condition number. # Errors Returns [`DenseError::Shape`] for inconsistent storage or non-finite input, and [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] if the backend does not converge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_tanh_sinh" => "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. Tanh-sinh quadrature with an explicit refinement budget. A zero budget returns an unconverged estimate. Refinement is capped at 20 levels to keep its exponentially growing node count bounded.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_timeseries_integrate" => "Undo repeated first differences using the original leading samples. Input vectors share a dimension, which the result preserves. The returned length is the sum of the input lengths. Inverse of [`difference`]. `initial` holds the first `initial.len()` values of the undifferenced series, which fixes the integration order. The returned vector has `d.len() + initial.len()` entries. An empty `initial` copies `d` unchanged.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_timeseries_kalman_filter" => "Filter a homogeneous-dimensional linear Gaussian state-space model with explicit initial mean and covariance. Return filtered means, predicted means, filtered covariance matrices, and log likelihood relative to a positive observation scale. Runs the Kalman filter for a linear Gaussian state-space model `x_t = F x_{t-1} + w_t` with `w_t ~ N(0, Q)` and `y_t = H x_t + v_t` with `v_t ~ N(0, R)`, started from `x_0 ~ N(x0, p0)`. # Errors Returns a diagnostic for invalid shapes, non-finite data, nonsymmetric or indefinite model covariances and an innovation covariance that is not positive definite. Model covariances may be positive semidefinite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_timeseries_kalman_smooth" => "Rauch–Tung–Striebel smoothed state means with explicit initial mean and covariance. State components share a dimension and observations may have a different common dimension. Returns one state per observation. Rauch–Tung–Striebel smoothed state means. Runs the filter and then the backward recursion `x_t^s = x_t + C_t (x_{t+1}^s - x_{t+1})` with `C_t = P_t F' P_{t+1}^{-1}`, returning one smoothed state per observation. # Errors Returns the errors of [`kalman_filter`], plus a message when a predicted covariance is singular and the smoother cannot be formed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_timeseries_rolling_moments" => "Compute means and variances for complete windows of a finite real time series. Use ddof 0 for population variance or ddof 1 for sample variance. The result contains empty vectors when the positive window width exceeds the input length. Means retain sample units and variances square them. Computes a mean and variance for every complete window in `samples`. Result `i` describes `samples[i..i + window]`. The result is empty when `window` exceeds the input length. Pass `ddof = 0` for population variance or `ddof = 1` for sample variance. Samples are always required to be finite. Windows are evaluated in linear time. Windows of at most 16 samples use two passes around an anchor when the whole series has the same sign, its magnitudes are within a factor of two, and their exponents leave enough headroom to square the deviations. For other series and window widths, fixed blocks of window starts combine suffix and prefix moments. Large inputs use the configured worker pool, with identical arithmetic order for any worker count. # Errors Returns [`RollingError`] for a zero window width, `ddof >= window`, a non-finite sample, an overflowing variance, or host cancellation. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::timeseries::rolling::RollingMoment; use rssn::kernels::timeseries::rolling::rolling_moments; assert_eq!( rolling_moments(&[1.0, 3.0, 5.0], 2, 0)?, vec![ RollingMoment { mean: 2.0, variance: 1.0 }, RollingMoment { mean: 4.0, variance: 1.0 }, ], ); ```\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_betti_number" => "The homology Betti number `b_k` of a closed complex: `dim ker d_k - rank d_{k+1}`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_betti_numbers_at_radius" => "The Betti numbers `b_0 ..= b_max_dim` of the Vietoris-Rips complex at radius `epsilon`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_boundary_matrix" => "The boundary matrix of `cols` (k-simplices) onto `rows` ((k-1)-simplices); `rows.len()` rows and `cols.len()` columns.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_close_complex" => "The simplices of `simplices` closed under faces, without duplicates, sorted by dimension and then lexicographically. Each simplex is sorted and deduplicated first; empty simplices are dropped.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_cohomology_betti_number" => "The cohomology Betti number `b^k`, from the ranks of the coboundary matrices (the transposes of the boundary matrices).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_complex_dimension" => "The dimension of a closed complex (`None` if empty).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_compute_persistence" => "The persistence diagrams of a point cloud, `max_dim` and below. This is a compatibility wrapper around [`persistent_homology_rips`]: the diagrams are now exact, not sampled on a grid of radii, so `steps` is ignored. Intervals of classes still alive at `max_epsilon` are closed at `max_epsilon` (the convention of the former grid-based routine; use [`persistent_homology_rips`] to get `f64::INFINITY` instead). If the filtration is too large to build, empty diagrams are returned.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_euclidean_distance" => "The Euclidean distance between two points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_euler_characteristic" => "The Euler characteristic `sum (-1)^k #k-simplices`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_find_connected_components" => "The connected components of a graph given by adjacency lists (BFS). Neighbours outside `0..adj.len()` are ignored.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_grid_complex" => "The triangulated `width x height` grid: two triangles per cell.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_int_matmul" => "The product of two integer matrices (`a` is `r x m`, `b` is `m x c`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_integer_rank" => "The rank of an integer matrix, computed exactly over the rationals by Gaussian elimination.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_persistent_homology" => "Exact persistent homology over `Z/2` of a filtered simplicial complex. `filtration` lists `(value, simplex)` pairs in any order; every proper face of a simplex must be present with a value no larger than the simplex's. A repeated simplex enters at its earliest value, and both signs of zero share a filtration level. Simplices are ordered by value, then dimension, then lexicographically. The boundary matrix is reduced by the standard left-to-right column algorithm with *clearing* (the twist optimisation): dimensions are processed from the top down, and a column that is the pivot of a reduced column of the next dimension is known to reduce to zero and is skipped. Returns one [`PersistenceDiagram`] per dimension `0..=max_dim`; each interval is an exact `(birth, death)` pair of filtration values and a class that never dies has `death = f64::INFINITY`. Zero-length intervals are omitted. Intervals are sorted by birth then death. # Errors [`PersistenceError::InvalidFiltration`] if the input is not a filtration.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_persistent_homology_rips" => "Exact Vietoris-Rips persistent homology of a point cloud. The filtration contains every simplex up to dimension `max_dim + 1` whose edges are all at most `max_epsilon` long, entering at its diameter (the longest edge); it is processed by [`persistent_homology`], so every `(birth, death)` pair is exact (a distance between two input points). Classes alive at `max_epsilon` get `death = f64::INFINITY`. # Errors [`PersistenceError::TooManySimplices`] if more than `simplex_limit` simplices are needed (use [`DEFAULT_SIMPLEX_LIMIT`] when in doubt).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_simplex_boundary" => "The boundary of an oriented simplex: `(sign, face)` pairs, with sign `(-1)^i` for the face omitting vertex `i`. Empty for a vertex.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_simplices_of_dim" => "The `k`-simplices of a complex, in order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_torus_complex" => "The triangulated torus obtained from an `m x n` grid by identifying opposite edges (a valid simplicial complex for `m, n >= 3`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_transpose" => "The transpose of an integer matrix with `cols` columns.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_verify_boundary_property" => "Whether `d_k d_{k+1} = 0` for every `k`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_verify_coboundary_property" => "Whether the coboundaries satisfy `d^{k+1} d^k = 0` for every `k`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_vietoris_rips_complex" => "The Vietoris-Rips complex of a point cloud. It holds every set of points that are pairwise within `epsilon`, up to dimension `max_dim`. Simplices are returned by increasing dimension, lexicographically within a dimension.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_topology_vietoris_rips_filtration" => "The Vietoris-Rips filtration restricted to its 1-skeleton: for each of `steps + 1` radii `max_epsilon * s / steps` the vertices and the edges no longer than the radius.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_transforms_irfft" => "Inverts [`rfft`]: reconstructs the real signal of length `n` from its `n/2 + 1` non-redundant bins, with the `1/n` normalisation of [`ifft`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_transforms_rfft" => "Computes the real-input FFT: the `n/2 + 1` non-redundant bins of the DFT of a real sequence.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_transpose" => "Transpose a dense matrix. Transpose (cache-blocked in 32x32 tiles and parallelised for large matrices).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_boundary_vertices" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Vertices on the boundary (on an edge with a single triangle).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_compacted" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The mesh without unused vertices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_data" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_error_indicators" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Residual error indicators `η_T` of a P1 solution (one per triangle).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_l2_error" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The `L²` error against `exact`, by quadrature on every element.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_l_shape" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The L-shaped domain `[-1, 1]² \\\\ [0, 1] × [-1, 0]` with mesh size `1/n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_max_nodal_error" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The maximum nodal error against `exact`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_prepare" => "Prepares the geometry, boundary flags and reference quadrature of a triangular mesh for repeated P1 or P2 solves. Use degree 1 or 2. The plan retains the mesh's physical frame and admits at most 1024 degrees of freedom. Prepares the P1 or P2 finite-element layout on `mesh`. # Errors Rejects non-finite vertex coordinates, invalid indices and degenerate triangles.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_prepared_dofs" => "Returns the number of nodal degrees of freedom, including prescribed boundary nodes, in a prepared triangular P1 or P2 mesh. The number of finite-element degrees of freedom.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_problem" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_rectangle" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The rectangle `[x0, x1] × [y0, y1]` cut into `nx × ny` squares, each split into two triangles.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_refine" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Refines the triangles flagged in `marked` (and as many neighbours as conformity needs) by bisection.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_solve" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Solves the problem on `mesh` with elements of `degree`. # Errors For invalid mesh geometry, callback values or a non-finite solve.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_solve_adaptive" => "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Adaptive P1 solution. Solve, estimate, mark the smallest set of triangles carrying the fraction `theta` of `Σ η²` (Dörfler), refine; `steps` times. Returns the final mesh, solution and the estimator history `(degrees of freedom, (Σ η²)^(1/2))`. # Errors A degenerate triangle.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_triangle_solve_prepared" => "Solves a triangular diffusion–reaction problem using a prepared mesh. The problem must use the same physical references. Each solve evaluates its diffusion, reaction, source and Dirichlet data and returns the ordinary triangular solution handle. Solves another problem on the prepared layout. # Errors Rejects invalid callback values or non-finite assembled systems or solutions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_trust_region_scaled" => "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. Trust-region method with the dogleg step. The Hessian is supplied by `hess`; pass `None` for a finite-difference Hessian from gradients.\n\nReturns a dynamic value: this operator is higher-order, its function parameters are host callbacks, and a callable passed for one is registered with the signature the operator declares.",
    "num_tt_svd" => "Tensor-train SVD of row-major data; rank cap must be positive. Returns dimensionless normalized cores, shape, scale, Frobenius truncation error and tolerance status; reconstruction multiplies the core contraction by scale. Compress a finite, nonempty dense tensor with TT-SVD. Each split is assigned relative tolerance / sqrt(order - 1). A rank cap may prevent satisfying that tolerance; inspect `tolerance_met` and the returned truncation estimate. Zero tensors use bond rank one. A one-mode tensor is stored exactly. Input is normalized before decomposition to avoid squaring the input's scale in intermediate norm computations. # Errors Returns an error for invalid shape/options, non-finite input, SVD failure, allocation/size overflow or unrepresentable rescaling.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_uhp_distance" => "Returns the dimensionless hyperbolic geodesic distance between two complex points with positive imaginary parts. Hyperbolic distance in the upper half-plane `Im(z) > 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_uhp_to_disk" => "Maps a dimensionless complex point in the open upper half-plane to the open Poincare unit disk. A nonpositive input imaginary part or a result that rounds outside the open disk produces a domain error. Cayley transform mapping the upper half-plane `{ Im(z) > 0 }` to the unit disk `{ |w| < 1 }`: `w = (z - i) / (z + i)`. Returns `None` at `z = -i`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_variance" => "Population variance; zero for an empty sample. Computes the variance of a slice of f64 values (defaults to population variance). # Arguments * `data` - An immutable slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The variance of the data as an `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_add" => "Element-wise sum of equal-length vectors. Adds two vectors element-wise. Formula: \$\\\\mathbf{r}_i = \\\\mathbf{v1}_i + \\\\mathbf{v2}_i\$ # Arguments * `v1` - The first vector. * `v2` - The second vector. # Returns * `Ok(Vec<f64>)` - The sum of the two vectors. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_axpby" => "`num_vector_axpby(alpha, x, beta, y)` computes alpha*x + beta*y with the native SIMD kernel. Both scaled terms must have the same dimension. Returns a new vector in those units. Computes `y[i] = alpha * x[i] + beta * y[i]` in place (BLAS-1 AXPBY). # Errors Returns an error if `x` and `y` have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_axpy" => "`num_vector_axpy(alpha, x, y)` computes alpha*x + y with the native SIMD kernel. The scaled increment and the retained input y must have the same dimension. Returns a new vector. Computes `y[i] += alpha * x[i]` in place (BLAS-1 AXPY). # Errors Returns an error if `x` and `y` have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_hadamard_div" => "Computes the element-wise (Hadamard) quotient `v1[i] / v2[i]`. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_hadamard_mul" => "Computes the element-wise (Hadamard) product `v1[i] * v2[i]`. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_is_orthogonal" => "Checks if two vectors are orthogonal. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_is_parallel" => "Checks if two vectors are parallel. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_lerp" => "Linearly interpolates between two vectors. Formula: \$\\\\mathbf{r} = (1 - t)\\\\mathbf{v1} + t\\\\mathbf{v2}\$ # Arguments * `t` - Interpolation factor, typically in \$[0, 1]\$. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_outer_product" => "`num_vector_outer_product(left, right)` returns a row-major matrix with shape [left length, right length] and product units. The result may contain at most 8388608 entries. Computes the outer product \$u v^T\$ as a dense matrix of shape `(u.len(), v.len())`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_vector_sub" => "Element-wise difference of equal-length vectors. Subtracts one vector from another element-wise. Formula: \$\\\\mathbf{r}_i = \\\\mathbf{v1}_i - \\\\mathbf{v2}_i\$ # Arguments * `v1` - The first vector (minuend). * `v2` - The second vector (subtrahend). # Returns * `Ok(Vec<f64>)` - The difference of the two vectors. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_voronoi_2d" => "Planar Voronoi diagram dual to Delaunay, with measured vertices and dimensionless topology/unit ray directions. Input requires 3..=1024 finite nondegenerate sites. Cells are CSR-style cell_offsets/cell_indices; unbounded cells are described by ray_vertices/ray_directions rather than fabricated finite polygons. Computes the 2D Voronoi diagram of `points` as the dual of [`delaunay_2d`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_wavelets_frft" => "Discrete fractional Fourier transform of order `a` of the complex signal `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_wavelets_frft_matrix" => "The row-major matrix of the discrete fractional Fourier transform of order `a`. `a = 1` is the unitary DFT. The unitary transforms compose by addition of their orders. For repeated orders, construct one [`FrftPlan`]. # Errors Returns an error for `n < 2`, a non-finite order, an unrepresentable matrix size or an eigensolver failure.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_wavelets_inverse_wavelet_packet" => "Reconstructs a signal from the complete leaf level of [`wavelet_packet`]. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the number of bands is not a power of two, their lengths differ, or they are empty.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_wavelets_iswt" => "Reconstructs a periodic signal from [`swt`] bands. Uses the dual synthesis filters and averages all decimation phases. Input bands must be equal length and in the order returned by [`swt`]. # Errors Errors for malformed or non-finite bands, unsupported level counts or non-finite reconstruction.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_wavelets_swt" => "Periodic stationary wavelet transform without decimation. Returns `[approximation_L, detail_L, ..., detail_1]`. Each band has the same length as `samples`. The filter-tap spacing doubles each level, giving a shift-equivariant decomposition. Odd lengths are supported. At least one level is required, and the longest tap spacing must be strictly less than the signal length. # Errors Errors for an unsupported length or level count or non-finite inputs or computed coefficients.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_wavelets_wavelet_hard_threshold" => "Denoises a signal by zeroing wavelet details below a magnitude threshold. Uses the same decomposition and threshold units as [`wavelet_soft_threshold`]. A detail coefficient exactly at the threshold is retained. # Errors As [`wavelet_soft_threshold`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_wavelets_wavelet_packet" => "Decomposes both the low and high branches of a periodic wavelet transform. Returns `2^levels` equally sized leaf bands in tree order, with each low child before its high sibling. For example, level two returns `LL`, `LH`, `HL`, `HH`. Level zero returns one band containing the input. # Errors [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when the signal is empty or its length is incompatible with the requested depth.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_wavelets_wavelet_soft_threshold" => "Denoises a signal by soft-thresholding its wavelet detail coefficients. Keeps the coarsest approximation and uses [`wavedec_with`] with the chosen wavelet and boundary rule. Detail magnitudes are shrunk toward zero by the threshold, in the same units as `samples`. # Errors Errors for non-finite data, a negative threshold, an unrepresentable signal or unsupported depth or shape.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_weierstrass_invariants_complex" => "Computes the Weierstrass invariants g2 and g3 as a named complex record. The dimensionless arguments omega1 and omega2 are nonzero, non-collinear half-periods. An invalid lattice or a nonfinite result produces a domain error. Weierstrass invariants `(g2, g3)` for half-periods `(omega1, omega2)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_weierstrass_p_prime_complex" => "Numerically evaluates the Weierstrass derivative at z. The arguments omega1 and omega2 are nonzero, non-collinear half-periods, so the basic periods are 2*omega1 and 2*omega2. All arguments and the result are dimensionless. A detected lattice pole, an invalid lattice or a nonfinite result produces a domain error. Weierstrass elliptic derivative `wp'(z; omega1, omega2)` via central complex step or theta.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "num_z_scores" => "Sample z scores. Computes the z-scores (standard scores) for each data point. z = (x - mean) / `std_dev`\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it. Pass `checked=true` to call it with native arguments and receive a Value.",
    "ode_asymptotic" => "`ode_asymptotic(eq, y(x), x0, n, x1, k)` evaluates formal solution `k` of `eq` at `x1` using up to `n` terms around singular point `x0`. Returns the real part, imaginary part and scaled magnitude of the first omitted term. If all terms are used, the final supplied term gives the estimate. `k` starts at zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_borel" => "`ode_borel(eq, y(x), x0, n, x1, k)` uses Borel–Padé resummation to evaluate formal solution `k` of `eq` at `x1` with `n` terms around singular point `x0`. Returns the real part, imaginary part, error estimate and Stokes indicator. `k` starts at zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_classify" => "`ode_classify(ode, y)`: classifies an ordinary differential equation `ode` for dependent function `y`, returning a list of applicable solver classes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_darboux" => "`ode_darboux(dx, dy, x, y, [max_deg])`: finds Darboux polynomials and cofactors `[P, k]` for planar autonomous system `x' = dx, y' = dy`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_etdrk4" => "`ode_etdrk4(L, f, vars, t, u0, t0, t1, steps)`: `u(t1)` for `u' = L u + f(t, u)` by fourth-order exponential time differencing.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_fourier" => "`ode_fourier(ode, y)`: solves linear constant-coefficient `ode` for `y` using Fourier transform.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_frobenius" => "`ode_frobenius(eq, y(x), x0, n)` expands a homogeneous linear second-order equation about rational `x0` or `oo`, with order `n` up to 60. Returns the solution, indicial roots or formal exponent pairs, and a recurrence equation when available. The recurrence entry is an empty list otherwise.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_imex" => "`ode_imex(f_ex, f_im, vars, t, y0, t0, t1, steps, scheme)`: `y(t1)` for `y' = f_ex + f_im` with explicit `f_ex` and stiff implicit `f_im` using `euler`, `ars222`, or `ars343`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_jacobi_last_multiplier" => "`ode_jacobi_last_multiplier(dx, dy, x, y, [max_deg])`: computes a Jacobi last multiplier (integrating factor) for planar system `x' = dx, y' = dy`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_series" => "`ode_series(ode, y, x0, order)`: computes a power-series solution `y(x) = ...` of `ode` about `x0` up to `order`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_solve" => "`ode_solve(rhs, vars, t, y0, t0, t1, tol, method)`: `y(t1)` for `y' = rhs(t, y)` with `y(t0) = y0` using `dopri5`, `rosenbrock23`, `radau5`, `bdf`, `sdirk3`, or `exprb32`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_symmetries" => "`ode_symmetries(ode, y)`: finds Lie point symmetry generators `[xi, eta]` admitted by `ode` for dependent function `y`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ode_symplectic" => "`ode_symplectic(accel, vars, q0, p0, t0, t1, steps, method)`: `list(q, p)` at `t1` for `q' = p`, `p' = accel(q)` using `verlet`, `yoshida4`, `yoshida6`, or `yoshida8`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "odeint" => "`odeint(f, y, x, x0, y0, x1)`: numerically integrates `dy/dx = f(x, y)` from `(x0, y0)` to `x1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "omega" => "`omega(n)`: number of distinct prime factors of `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "one_parameter_subgroup" => "`one_parameter_subgroup(A, t)`: one-parameter subgroup `exp_map(t * A)` generated by matrix `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "oo" => "`oo`: positive real infinity (`+infinity`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_add" => "`op_add(A, B, ...)`: sum `A + B + ...` of linear operators.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_apply" => "`op_apply(A, f)`: apply linear operator `A` to function or vector `f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_commutator" => "`op_commutator(A, B)`: commutator `[A, B] = A B - B A` of operators `A` and `B`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_compose" => "`op_compose(A, B, ...)`: composition `A o B o ...` of linear operators (rightmost acts first).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_d" => "`op_d(x)`: differential operator `f -> d/dx(f)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_identity" => "`op_identity()`: identity linear operator `f -> f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_int" => "`op_int(a, x)`: Volterra integration operator `f -> int_a^x f(t) dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_laplacian" => "`op_laplacian(vars)`: Laplacian differential operator `sum_i d^2 / dx_i^2` over `vars`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_mul" => "`op_mul(g)`: linear multiplication operator `f -> g * f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_power" => "`op_power(A, n)`: `n`-fold composition `A^n` of linear operator `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "op_scale" => "`op_scale(c, A)`: linear operator `A` scaled by `c`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "operator_adjoint" => "`operator_adjoint(A)`: formal `L^2` adjoint `A*` of differential/multiplication operator `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "or" => "`or(p1, p2, ...)`: variadic logical disjunction, true when at least one operand is true.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "orbit_density" => "Computes an `nx` by `ny` 2D occupancy histogram of `points` over `[xmin, xmax] × [ymin, ymax]`. Computes the density distribution of orbit points in a 2D histogram. # Arguments * `points` - The orbit trajectory. * `x_bins` - Number of bins in x direction. * `y_bins` - Number of bins in y direction. * `x_range` - Range for x axis. * `y_range` - Range for y axis. # Returns A 2D vector of counts for each bin.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "orbit_entropy" => "Computes the Shannon entropy of a 2D occupancy histogram `density`. Computes the entropy of an orbit from its density distribution. Uses Shannon entropy: H = -`sum(p_i` * `log(p_i)`) # Arguments * `density` - A 2D density histogram from `orbit_density`. # Returns The Shannon entropy of the distribution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "orbit_iterate" => "`orbit_iterate(f, z, z0, n)`: symbolic `n`-fold iterate `f^{o n}(z0)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "orthogonal_coefficients" => "`orthogonal_coefficients(f, basis, x, a, b)`: Fourier coefficients `<e_i, f> / <e_i, e_i>` of `f` in orthogonal `basis`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "orthogonalize" => "`orthogonalize(vecs)`: Gram-Schmidt orthogonal basis from a list of vectors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "orthographic" => "Constructs a 4x4 OpenGL-style orthographic projection matrix from `(left, right, bottom, top, near, far)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "orthonormalize" => "`orthonormalize(vecs)`: Gram-Schmidt orthonormal basis from a list of vectors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "oscillatory_integral" => "`oscillatory_integral(f, g, x, a, b, w, n)`: `list(re, im)` of `int_a^b f(x) exp(i w g(x)) dx` by Levin collocation at `n` Chebyshev points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "outer" => "`outer(u, v)`: outer product matrix `u v^T` of vectors `u` and `v`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pacf" => "`pacf(x, max_lag)`: the partial autocorrelation of `x` for lags `1..=max_lag`, by the Durbin–Levinson recursion. Partial autocorrelation function, from the Durbin–Levinson recursion. Returns `max_lag + 1` values with `result[0] = 1`; entry `k` is the last coefficient of the order-`k` autoregression fitted to the sample autocorrelations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "parabolic_cylinder_d_complex" => "`parabolic_cylinder_d_complex(nur, nui, zr, zi)`: `list(re, im)` of `D_nu(z)`. Parabolic cylinder function `D_nu(z)` via asymptotic expansion for large `|z|` in `|arg(z)| < 3*pi/4` and `1F1` connection formula with `rgamma` elsewhere.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pareto" => "`pareto(scale, shape)`: Pareto distribution with minimum scale `scale` and shape parameter `shape`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "partition_function" => "`partition_function(energies, T)`: canonical partition function `sum_i exp(-E_i / (k_B T))` over energy levels `energies`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "partition_list" => "`partition_list(n)`: list of all integer partitions of `n` in non-increasing part order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "partitions" => "`partitions(n)`: integer partition function `p(n)`, counting ways to write `n` as a sum of positive integers.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "partitions_distinct" => "`partitions_distinct(n)`: number of integer partitions of `n` into distinct positive parts.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "partitions_k" => "`partitions_k(n, k)`: number of integer partitions of `n` into exactly `k` positive parts.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "partitions_max" => "`partitions_max(n, k)`: number of integer partitions of `n` whose parts are at most `k`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "path" => "`path(g, t, t0, t1)`: parameterized contour `g(t)` as `t` runs from `t0` to `t1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pauli_matrices" => "`pauli_matrices`: list of the three 2x2 Pauli matrices `list(pauli_x, pauli_y, pauli_z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pauli_x" => "`pauli_x`: 2x2 Pauli matrix `sigma_x = [[0, 1], [1, 0]]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pauli_y" => "`pauli_y`: 2x2 Pauli matrix `sigma_y = [[0, -i], [i, 0]]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pauli_z" => "`pauli_z`: 2x2 Pauli matrix `sigma_z = [[1, 0], [0, -1]]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_classify" => "`pde_classify(pde, u)`: classifies `pde` for unknown `u` by type (elliptic, parabolic, hyperbolic), order, dimension, and linearity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_complete_integral" => "`pde_complete_integral(pde, u)`: complete integral `u(x, y) = ...` of a first-order PDE via Charpit's method.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_crum_kdv" => "`pde_crum_kdv(u, psis)`: iterated Crum-Darboux transformation of KdV seed `u` using eigenfunctions `psis`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_darboux_kdv" => "`pde_darboux_kdv(u, psi)`: single Darboux-Bäcklund transformation `u - 2 d^2/dx^2 ln(psi)` for KdV.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_greens_ball" => "`pde_greens_ball(radius, dimension)`: Dirichlet Green's function for the negative Laplacian on a disk (`dimension = 2`) or ball (`dimension = 3`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_greens_ball_neumann" => "`pde_greens_ball_neumann(radius, dimension)`: zero-mean Neumann Green's function for the negative Laplacian on a disk (`dimension = 2`) or ball (`dimension = 3`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_greens_ball_robin" => "`pde_greens_ball_robin(radius, h)`: Green's function for the negative Laplacian inside a three-dimensional ball with Robin coefficient `h`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_greens_exterior_robin" => "`pde_greens_exterior_robin(radius, h, dimension)`: Green's function for the negative Laplacian outside a disk (`dimension = 2`) or ball (`dimension = 3`) with Robin coefficient `h`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_greens_function" => "`pde_greens_function(equation, u(x, …)[, conditions])`: Green's function of a linear constant-coefficient PDE. Homogeneous conditions on a box use eigenfunction series. Supported free-space and orthant cases use fundamental solutions or heat and wave kernels, with reflected sources for homogeneous Dirichlet or Neumann walls. The source coordinate for `x` is `s_x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_greens_robin" => "`pde_greens_robin(dimension, h)`: Green's function for the negative Laplacian on a half-space with Robin coefficient `h` (`dimension = 2` or `3`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_hodograph" => "`pde_hodograph(sys, vars)`: derives the linear hodograph PDE `(lambda - mu) t_rs + lambda_r t_s - mu_s t_r = 0` for a 2x2 hyperbolic system.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_hodograph_riemann" => "`pde_hodograph_riemann(gamma)`: Riemann function for the Euler-Poisson-Darboux hodograph equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_hodograph_solution" => "`pde_hodograph_solution(F, G, ...)`: hodograph-transformed parametric solution `list(x(r, s), t(r, s))` in Riemann invariants.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_kdv_reflectionless" => "`pde_kdv_reflectionless(params, x_t)`: KdV time evolution of the reflectionless potential `-N(N+1) kappa^2 sech^2(kappa x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_kdv_scattering" => "`pde_kdv_scattering(data, x, t)`: numeric KdV inverse-scattering (Gelfand-Levitan-Marchenko) evaluation at `(x, t)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_kdv_soliton" => "`pde_kdv_soliton(kappas, x_t)`: N-soliton Hirota tau-function solution `-2 d^2/dx^2 ln det(I + A)` of the KdV equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_nls_soliton" => "`pde_nls_soliton(params)`: bright soliton solution of the focusing nonlinear Schrodinger equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_order" => "`pde_order(pde, u)`: highest partial-derivative order of `u` appearing in `pde`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_poisson_integral" => "`pde_poisson_integral(g, radius, dimension)`: harmonic extension of `g(phi)` on a disk (`dimension = 2`) or `g(theta, phi)` on a ball (`dimension = 3`). The positive `radius` is measured in the same units as `r`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_riemann_shallow_water" => "`pde_riemann_shallow_water(hL, uL, hR, uR, g)`: exact Riemann problem solution `list(h*, u*, left_wave, right_wave)` for 1D shallow-water equations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_riemann_shallow_water_at" => "`pde_riemann_shallow_water_at(hL, uL, hR, uR, g, xi)`: self-similar shallow-water depth `h(x/t = xi)` from the Riemann solver.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_riemann_shallow_water_velocity" => "`pde_riemann_shallow_water_velocity(hL, uL, hR, uR, g, xi)`: self-similar shallow-water velocity `u(x/t = xi)` from the Riemann solver.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_separate" => "`pde_separate(pde, u)`: separated-variables ansatz and decoupled ODEs for `pde` and unknown `u`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_similarity" => "`pde_similarity(pde, u, generator)`: similarity reduction `list(u = g F(z), ODE)` along a scaling/symmetry `generator`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_sine_gordon_soliton" => "`pde_sine_gordon_soliton(params, x_t)`: kink/antikink or breather soliton solution of the sine-Gordon equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_symmetries" => "`pde_symmetries(pde, u)`: Lie point symmetry infinitesimal generators of `pde` for unknown `u`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pde_traveling_wave" => "`pde_traveling_wave(pde, u, c)`: traveling-wave ansatz `u = F(x - c t)` and reduced ODE for speed `c`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pdf" => "`pdf(d, x)`: probability density function (or probability mass function) of distribution `d` at point `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pdsolve" => "`pdsolve(pde, u, ...)`: general symbolic/numeric PDE solver for unknown `u` with optional boundary/initial conditions. For homogeneous heat or undamped wave equations on disks and balls, the numerical modal fallback accepts general initial data with homogeneous Neumann boundaries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pell" => "`pell(D)`: fundamental solution `list(x, y)` to Pell's equation `x^2 - D*y^2 = 1` for non-square `D > 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "percentile" => "`percentile(xs, p)`: `p`-th percentile (`quantile(xs, p / 100)`) of list `xs`. Computes the p-th percentile of a slice of data. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `f64` data points. * `p` - The desired percentile (e.g., 50.0 for median). # Returns The p-th percentile of the data (fractional `p` allowed), or NaN for empty data, data containing NaN, or `p` outside `0..=100`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perfect_power" => "`perfect_power(n)`: `list(b, e)` with maximal `e >= 1` such that `b^e = n` (for `n >= 2`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "period" => "`period(sequence)`: minimal positive period of a repeating list `sequence`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_compose" => "Composes two permutations `p` and `q` (1-based image lists) as `(p * q)(i) = p(q(i))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_cycles" => "Decomposes permutation `p` into its non-trivial disjoint cycles.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_are_conjugate" => "Returns `true` if permutations `x` and `y` are conjugate in `<gens>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_base" => "Returns a Schreier-Sims base (points whose pointwise stabilizer is trivial) for the group generated by `gens`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_basic_orbits" => "Returns the lengths of the basic orbits along the stabilizer chain (whose product equals the group order).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_blocks" => "Computes the finest block system of `<gens>` placing points `a` and `b` in the same block.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_center" => "Returns generators of the center `Z(G)` of the permutation group generated by `gens`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_centralizer" => "Returns generators of the centralizer of permutation `x` in the permutation group generated by `gens`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_class_count" => "Returns the number of conjugacy classes of `<gens>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_composition_factors" => "Returns the prime orders `p` of the cyclic composition factors `C_p` of a solvable permutation group `<gens>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_conjugacy_classes" => "Returns the conjugacy classes of `<gens>` as `list(list(representative, size), ...)` ordered by element order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_conjugating_element" => "Finds a permutation `g` in `<gens>` such that `g * x * g^(-1) = y`, or returns `false` if none exists.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_contains" => "Tests whether permutation `p` belongs to the permutation group generated by `gens` by sifting through the stabilizer chain.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_derived_series" => "Computes the orders of the derived series `G = G_0 > G_1 > ...` of the group generated by `gens`, stopping at the perfect core.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_derived_subgroup" => "Returns generators of the commutator (derived) subgroup `G'` of the group generated by `gens`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_elements" => "Enumerates all elements of the permutation group generated by `gens` (for group orders up to 5040).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_exponent" => "Computes the group exponent (LCM of all element orders) of `<gens>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_intersection" => "Returns generators of the intersection `<gens> ∩ <hgens>` of two permutation groups.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_is_abelian" => "Returns `true` if the permutation group generated by `gens` is abelian.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_is_nilpotent" => "Returns `true` if the permutation group generated by `gens` is nilpotent.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_is_normal" => "Returns `true` if `<hgens>` is a normal subgroup of `<gens>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_is_primitive" => "Returns `true` if `<gens>` acts primitively (transitively with no non-trivial block system).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_is_solvable" => "Returns `true` if the permutation group generated by `gens` is solvable.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_is_transitive" => "Returns `true` if the permutation group generated by `gens` acts transitively on `1..n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_lower_central_series" => "Computes the orders of the lower central series `G = gamma_1 > gamma_2 > ...` of the group generated by `gens`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_minimal_block_systems" => "Enumerates all minimal non-trivial block systems of `<gens>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_normal_closure" => "Returns generators of the normal closure of `<xs>` in `<gens>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_normalizer" => "Returns generators of the normalizer of `<hgens>` in `<gens>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_orbits" => "Returns the orbits of the permutation group generated by `gens` on `1..n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_order" => "Computes the exact order of the permutation group generated by `gens` using the Schreier-Sims algorithm.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_random_element" => "Draws a deterministic uniformly random element from `<gens>` using integer `seed`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_set_stabilizer" => "Returns generators of the setwise stabilizer of `points` in `<gens>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_stabilizer_order" => "Computes the order of the point stabilizer of `i` in the permutation group generated by `gens`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_strong_generators" => "Returns a strong generating set relative to the Schreier-Sims base for the group generated by `gens`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_group_sylow" => "Returns generators of a Sylow `p`-subgroup of `<gens>` for prime `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_inverse" => "Computes the inverse permutation `p^(-1)` of a 1-based permutation list `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_order" => "Computes the order (LCM of cycle lengths) of permutation `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perm_sign" => "Computes the sign (`1` for even, `-1` for odd) of permutation `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "permutation_order" => "`permutation_order(perm)`: group order (LCM of disjoint cycle lengths) of permutation `perm`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "permutation_rank" => "`permutation_rank(perm)`: 0-based lexicographic rank (Lehmer code) of permutation `perm` of `1..=n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "permutation_sign" => "`permutation_sign(perm)`: parity sign (`1` for even, `-1` for odd) of permutation `perm`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "permutation_unrank" => "`permutation_unrank(n, rank)`: permutation of `1..=n` at 0-based lexicographic `rank`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "permutations" => "`permutations(n, k)`: number of `k`-permutations of `n` items, `n! / (n - k)!`. Computes the number of permutations `P(n, k) = n! / (n-k)!`. # Arguments * `n` - The total number of items. * `k` - The number of items to choose. # Returns The number of permutations as an `f64`. Returns `0.0` if `k > n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "persistence" => "Computes persistence intervals `list(birth, death)` per dimension up to `max_dim` by tracking Betti numbers over `steps + 1` radii up to `max_eps`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "persistent_homology" => "Computes persistent homology barcodes over `Z/2` for a filtered complex up to `max_dim`, returning `list(birth, death)` pairs per dimension. Exact persistent homology over `Z/2` of a filtered simplicial complex. `filtration` lists `(value, simplex)` pairs in any order; every proper face of a simplex must be present with a value no larger than the simplex's. A repeated simplex enters at its earliest value, and both signs of zero share a filtration level. Simplices are ordered by value, then dimension, then lexicographically. The boundary matrix is reduced by the standard left-to-right column algorithm with *clearing* (the twist optimisation): dimensions are processed from the top down, and a column that is the pivot of a reduced column of the next dimension is known to reduce to zero and is skipped. Returns one [`PersistenceDiagram`] per dimension `0..=max_dim`; each interval is an exact `(birth, death)` pair of filtration values and a class that never dies has `death = f64::INFINITY`. Zero-length intervals are omitted. Intervals are sorted by birth then death. # Errors [`PersistenceError::InvalidFiltration`] if the input is not a filtration.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "perspective" => "Constructs a 4x4 OpenGL-style perspective projection matrix from `(fovy, aspect, near, far)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pgcd" => "`pgcd(p, q, var)`: computes the polynomial greatest common divisor of `p` and `q` in `var`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pi" => "`pi`: Archimedes' constant, the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter. `pi` at `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when an exponent leaves the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pid_tune" => "`pid_tune(num, den, rule, lambda)`: `list(kp, ki, kd)` from a first-order-plus-dead-time model of the plant; `rule` is `ziegler_nichols`, `cohen_coon`, `imc`, `amigo` or `chien_hrones_reswick`. Tunes a PID controller for `plant` with the selected rule. `lambda` is the closed-loop time constant of the IMC rule; it is ignored by the other rules and defaults to `max(0.1 tau, theta)`. # Errors Returns an error when the plant step response does not identify a finite first-order-plus-dead-time model or when `lambda` is not positive.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pinv" => "`pinv(A)`: Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse `A^+` of matrix `A`. Moore-Penrose pseudo-inverse through the SVD. # Errors As [`svd`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "planck_law" => "`planck_law(nu, T)`: Planck blackbody spectral radiance at frequency `nu` and temperature `T`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "plasma_frequency" => "`plasma_frequency(n, e, eps, m)`: plasma angular frequency `sqrt(n e^2 / (eps m))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pochhammer_complex" => "`pochhammer_complex(zr, zi, wr, wi)`: `list(re, im)` of `(z)_w`. Complex Pochhammer symbol `(z)_w = Gamma(z + w) / Gamma(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_charge_potential" => "`point_charge_potential(q, r)`: electrostatic potential `q / (4 pi epsilon_0 r)` of a point charge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group" => "Constructs the point group named by Schoenflies symbol `name` (e.g. `C2v`, `D4h`, `Td`, `Oh`, `Ih`) as a `group` term of 3x3 orthogonal matrices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_character_table" => "Returns the exact character table of point group `name` as rows of class values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_class_sizes" => "Returns the sizes of the conjugacy classes of point group `name`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_classes" => "Returns the conjugacy class labels (e.g. `E`, `8C3`, `3C2`) of point group `name`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_crystal_system" => "Returns the crystal system of crystallographic point group `name`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_decompose" => "Decomposes class function `chi` of point group `name` into a symbolic linear combination of Mulliken irreps (e.g. `2*A1 + B2`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_from_hm" => "Converts a Hermann-Mauguin crystallographic point group symbol `hm` to its Schoenflies symbol.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_function_irreps" => "Returns for each of `x, y, z, x^2, y^2, z^2, x*y, x*z, y*z` the irreps of point group `name` in which it transforms.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_hm" => "Converts a crystallographic Schoenflies point group symbol `name` to its Hermann-Mauguin symbol.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_ir_active" => "Returns the Mulliken irreps of point group `name` that are infrared-active (transforming as `x`, `y`, or `z`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_irrep_dimensions" => "Returns the dimensions of the irreducible representations of point group `name` (`2` for combined complex-conjugate `E` pairs).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_irreps" => "Returns the Mulliken labels of the irreducible representations of point group `name`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_is_crystallographic" => "Returns `true` if point group `name` is crystallographic (all element orders in `{1, 2, 3, 4, 6}`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_multiplicities" => "Returns the integer multiplicities of class function `chi` aligned with `point_group_irreps(name)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_order" => "Returns the order (number of symmetry operations) of point group `name`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_raman_active" => "Returns the Mulliken irreps of point group `name` that are Raman-active (transforming as quadratic forms `x^2`, `xy`, etc.).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_rotation_character" => "Returns the character of the axial rotation `(Rx, Ry, Rz)` representation in point group `name`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "point_group_vector_character" => "Returns the character of the polar vector `(x, y, z)` representation in point group `name`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "poisson" => "`poisson(rate)`: Poisson distribution with rate (mean) parameter `rate`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "poisson_bracket" => "`poisson_bracket(f, g, q, p)`: canonical Poisson bracket `{f, g} = (df/dq)(dg/dp) - (df/dp)(dg/dq)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "poisson_fd" => "`poisson_fd(rhs, bc_val)`: finite-difference Poisson solver `-Delta u = rhs` on the unit domain with uniform Dirichlet boundary `bc_val`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "poisson_fd_bc" => "`poisson_fd_bc(rhs, bc, bc_val)`: finite-difference Poisson solver `-Delta u = rhs` with per-face Dirichlet/Neumann boundary condition `bc`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "poles" => "`poles(f, z)`: list of `list(p, order)` pairs for the poles of meromorphic `f(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "polygamma" => "`polygamma(n, x)`: `n`-th derivative of the digamma function `psi^(n)(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "polygamma_complex" => "`polygamma_complex(n, re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `psi^(n)(z)`. Complex polygamma function `psi^(n)(z) = d^n/dz^n digamma(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "polygon" => "`polygon(vertices)`: closed polygonal contour through `list(z1, ..., zn)` in order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "polylog" => "`polylog(s, z)`: polylogarithm `Li_s(z) = sum_{k=1}^infty z^k / k^s`. The polylogarithm `Li_s(z)` for real `s` and real `z`. Real on `z <= 1` (including the negative axis); for `z > 1` the principal value is complex in general and `NaN` is returned, except for non-positive integer `s`, where `Li_{-n}(z)` is the real rational function `z A_n(z)/(1-z)^(n+1)`. At `z = 1` the analytic continuation `ζ(s)` is returned (and `+∞` at the pole `s = 1`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "polylog_complex" => "`polylog_complex(s, re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `Li_s(z)`. The polylogarithm `Li_s(z)` for complex `s` and `z`, on the principal branch (cut along `[1, ∞)`). * `|z| <= 1/2`: the defining power series; * non-positive integer `s = -n`: the rational function `z A_n(z)/(1-z)^(n+1)` with Eulerian numbers `A_n`; * positive integer `s`: the logarithmic (Bernoulli/Hurwitz) continuation for `|z| <= 3/2` and Kummer's inversion relation beyond, so every `z` is covered; * real `s >= -15.5` and real `z < 1` outside the series' fast region: the same continuation as [`lerch`] with `a = 1`; * other `s`: the logarithmic expansion about `z = 1` for `|ln z| < 4.5` (the expansion is valid for `|ln z| < 2π`, but `ζ(s-k)` overflows `f64` before it converges near that bound); * `z = 1`: `ζ(s)` (the analytic continuation, as in `mpmath`). Outside the stated domain the result is `NaN + NaN i`. Accuracy is about `1e-13` for moderate parameters.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "polynomial_regression" => "`polynomial_regression(xs, ys, degree)`: polynomial least-squares fit returning coefficients `list(c0, c1, ..., cd)` up to degree `degree`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "polynomial_system_roots" => "`polynomial_system_roots(system)`: finite roots of a square polynomial system by total-degree homotopy continuation as `list(list(re, im), ...)`. Solves a square polynomial system by total-degree homotopy continuation. Solves the square polynomial system `system[i] = 0` (terms of equation `i`, exponent vectors with one entry per variable) by total-degree homotopy continuation in projective coordinates with a Cauchy endgame and path-jumping protection; see the module documentation. One [`PathEnd`] is returned per start solution (the product of the degrees). Regular finite endpoints are Newton-polished; singular finite endpoints and endpoints at infinity are classified with their winding number. [`HomotopyOptions::precision`] selects double, double-double or adaptive extended-precision tracking. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for an empty system, exponent vectors of the wrong length, nonfinite coefficients, equations without a positive declared degree, or a product of equation degrees above 100 000.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pooled_t_test" => "`pooled_t_test(xs, ys)`: two-sample pooled t-test returning `list(statistic, p_value)` assuming equal population variances.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "position_operator" => "`position_operator(x)`: 1D position operator multiplying by `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "potential_energy_gravity_uniform" => "`potential_energy_gravity_uniform(m, h, g)`: gravitational potential energy `m g h` in a uniform field.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "potential_energy_gravity_universal" => "`potential_energy_gravity_universal(m1, m2, r, G)`: Newtonian gravitational potential energy `-G m1 m2 / r`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "potential_energy_spring" => "`potential_energy_spring(k, x)`: elastic potential energy `(1/2) k x^2` of a Hookean spring.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pow" => "`pow(base, exponent)` raises `base` to `exponent` and is printed with `^`. For positive real bases, the scalar overload also accepts fractional exponents. The symbolic overload represents powers as terms and can preserve an exact expression such as `2^(1/2)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pow_cut" => "`pow_cut(z, w, theta0)`: complex power `exp(w * log_cut(z, theta0))` with branch cut along ray `theta0`. Complex power `exp(w * log_cut(z, theta0))` with branch cut along ray `theta0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "power" => "`power(F, v)`: instantaneous mechanical power `F . v`. `p^k` for `k ≥ 0`, using direct composition for powers that need few compositions and cycle rotations for other powers.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "power_branch" => "`power_branch(z, w, k)`: branch `k` of the complex power `exp(w * log_branch(z, k))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "powmod" => "`powmod(b, e, m)`: modular exponentiation `b^e mod m` for `e >= 0` and `m > 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "poynting_vector" => "`poynting_vector(E, B)`: electromagnetic energy flux `(E x B) / mu_0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "presentation_abelianization" => "Computes the abelianization of a finitely presented group `list(n, relators)` via Smith normal form.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pressure_from_helmholtz" => "`pressure_from_helmholtz(A, V)`: thermodynamic pressure `-dA/dV` from Helmholtz free energy `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "prevprime" => "`prevprime(n)`: largest prime strictly less than `n` (for `n > 2`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "prime_implicants" => "`prime_implicants(formula)`: computes the list of prime implicants of propositional `formula`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "prime_pi" => "`prime_pi(n)`: prime-counting function `pi(n)`, the number of primes `<= n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "primes" => "`primes(n)`: list of all prime numbers `<= n`. Prime numbers strictly below `limit`; negative limits yield an empty list.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "primitive_root" => "`primitive_root(n)`: smallest primitive root modulo `n`, if `(Z/nZ)^*` is cyclic. Returns the smallest positive primitive root modulo `n` if the multiplicative group \$(\\\\mathbb{Z}/n\\\\mathbb{Z})^\\\\times\$ is cyclic (\$n \\\\in \\\\{2, 4, p^k, 2p^k\\\\}\$ for an odd prime \$p\$), or `None` otherwise.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "probability_density" => "`probability_density(psi)`: probability density `|psi|^2 = conj(psi) * psi`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "product" => "`product(expr, k, lo, hi)` multiplies the values of `expr` over a bound index `k` from `lo` through `hi`, inclusive. Small literal integer ranges substitute each index value. A factor independent of `k` can be raised to the number of terms. Symbolic bounds can reduce through factorials or a known finite-product formula. An unsupported request remains unevaluated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "project" => "`project(u, v)`: orthogonal projection of vector `u` onto vector `v`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "project_onto" => "`project_onto(f, g, x, a, b)`: orthogonal projection of `f` onto `g` in `L^2([a, b])`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "propagator" => "`propagator(p, m)`: Feynman scalar propagator `i / (p^2 - m^2 + i epsilon)` in momentum space.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "proportion_z_test" => "`proportion_z_test(k, n, p0)`: two-sided one-sample z-test `list(statistic, p_value)` for proportion given `k` successes in `n` trials against null `p0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "pullback" => "`pullback(omega, vars, phi, params)`: pullback `phi* omega` of form `omega(vars)` along map `phi(params)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "q_e" => "`q_e`: elementary charge in C (exact SI).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "qcd_lagrangian" => "`qcd_lagrangian(psibar, psi, G, m, gs)`: symbolic QCD Lagrangian density for quark field `psi` and gluon field `G`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "qed_lagrangian" => "`qed_lagrangian(psibar, psi, A, m, e)`: symbolic QED Lagrangian density for Dirac spinor `psi` and gauge field `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "qm_apply" => "`qm_apply(A, psi)`: applies quantum operator `A` to wavefunction `psi`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "qp_eq" => "`qp_eq(Q, c, A, b)`: solves the equality-constrained quadratic program `min 1/2 x^T Q x + c^T x` subject to `A x = b`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "qp_solve" => "`qp_solve(q, c, a, rel, b, bounds)`: `list(x, objective, iterations, active)` minimising `0.5 x'Qx + c'x` subject to `a x (rel) b` and `bounds` (`list(list(lo, hi), ..)`, `inf` for no upper bound). Solves a convex quadratic program. # Errors Returns an error string when the dimensions disagree, when an entry is not finite, when `q` has a negative eigenvalue (a non-convex problem), when the problem is infeasible, when a positive semidefinite problem is unbounded below on its feasible set, or when the active-set iteration exceeds `max_iter`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "qr" => "`qr(A)`: QR factorization `list(Q, R)` with orthogonal `Q` and upper triangular `R`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "qr_pivoted_diagonal" => "`qr_pivoted_diagonal(a)`: non-increasing absolute diagonal `|R_ii|` from column-pivoted QR of `a`. Column-pivoted Householder QR (Businger and Golub). At each step the remaining column of largest norm is brought forward, so the diagonal of `R` decreases in magnitude and its decay reveals the numerical rank. Column norms are downdated and recomputed when cancellation makes the downdate unreliable. Each reflector applies to contiguous columns, with large independent column batches running in parallel. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] for inconsistent storage or non-finite entries, and [`LowRankError::NoConvergence`] if the resulting factor exceeds the representable floating-point range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "qr_pivoted_permutation" => "`qr_pivoted_permutation(a)`: 0-based column permutation vector `perm` from column-pivoted QR of `a`. Column-pivoted Householder QR (Businger and Golub). At each step the remaining column of largest norm is brought forward, so the diagonal of `R` decreases in magnitude and its decay reveals the numerical rank. Column norms are downdated and recomputed when cancellation makes the downdate unreliable. Each reflector applies to contiguous columns, with large independent column batches running in parallel. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] for inconsistent storage or non-finite entries, and [`LowRankError::NoConvergence`] if the resulting factor exceeds the representable floating-point range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "qr_pivoted_rank" => "`qr_pivoted_rank(a, tol)`: numerical rank of matrix `a` from column-pivoted QR diagonal entries above `tol * max(|R_ii|)`. Column-pivoted Householder QR (Businger and Golub). At each step the remaining column of largest norm is brought forward, so the diagonal of `R` decreases in magnitude and its decay reveals the numerical rank. Column norms are downdated and recomputed when cancellation makes the downdate unreliable. Each reflector applies to contiguous columns, with large independent column batches running in parallel. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] for inconsistent storage or non-finite entries, and [`LowRankError::NoConvergence`] if the resulting factor exceeds the representable floating-point range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quadratic_residues" => "`quadratic_residues(n)`: sorted list of nonzero quadratic residues modulo `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quantile" => "`quantile(xs, p)`: `p`-th quantile (interpolated order statistic, `p in [0, 1]`) of list `xs`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quantile_hf" => "`quantile_hf(data, p, kind)`: sample quantile at probability `p` in `[0, 1]` using Hyndman-Fan definition `kind` (1 to 9). Sample quantile of `data` at probability `p` using the Hyndman-Fan definition `kind` (1..=9; 7 is the NumPy/R default). NaNs are ignored. Returns NaN for empty data or an invalid `p`/`kind`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quantile_of" => "`quantile_of(d, p)`: quantile function (inverse CDF) of distribution `d` at probability `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quantity" => "`quantity(value, unit)`: constructs a dimensional quantity pairing numeric or symbolic `value` with `unit`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quat_conj" => "Computes the quaternion conjugate `list(w, -x, -y, -z)` of `q`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quat_from_axis_angle" => "Constructs a unit rotation quaternion `list(cos(theta/2), ax*sin(theta/2), ...)` from `axis` and angle `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quat_inverse" => "Computes the multiplicative inverse `q* / |q|^2` of quaternion `q`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quat_mul" => "Computes the Hamilton product `q * r` of two quaternions `list(w, x, y, z)`. Quaternion product `a * b` (scalar first).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quat_norm" => "Computes the Euclidean norm `sqrt(w^2 + x^2 + y^2 + z^2)` of quaternion `q`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quat_normalize" => "Normalizes quaternion `q` to unit length.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quat_rotate" => "Rotates 3D vector `v` by unit quaternion `q` via `q * (0, v) * q*`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quat_slerp" => "Computes numeric spherical linear interpolation (SLERP) between quaternions `q1` and `q2` at parameter `t`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quat_to_matrix" => "Converts quaternion `q` into a 3x3 rotation matrix. Rotation matrix (body to space) of a unit quaternion.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "quo" => "`quo(p, q, var)`: returns the polynomial quotient of `p` divided by `q` in `var`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "r_squared" => "`r_squared(actual, predicted)`: coefficient of determination `R^2 = 1 - SS_res / SS_tot` between `actual` and `predicted` lists.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "radical" => "`radical(n)`: radical `rad(n)`, the product of the distinct prime factors of `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "radius_of_convergence" => "`radius_of_convergence(f, z, a)`: distance from `a` to the nearest singularity of `f(z)` (`oo` if entire).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "raise_index" => "`raise_index(W, g)`: contravariant vector `g^ij W_j` obtained by raising index of `W` with inverse of metric `g`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "randomized_singular_values" => "`randomized_singular_values(a, k)`: top `k` singular values of dense matrix `a` via randomized SVD.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "range_of" => "`range_of(xs)`: range `max(xs) - min(xs)` of list `xs`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rank" => "`rank(A)`: rank of matrix `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rational_approx" => "`rational_approx(f, x, a, b)`: AAA barycentric rational approximant of `f(x)` on `[a, b]`, including evaluation at the selected interpolation sites.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ray" => "`ray(p, theta)`: half-line `{p + r * exp(I * theta) : r >= 0}` representing a branch cut.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ray_plane" => "Intersects ray `(origin, dir)` with plane `(point, normal)`, returning `list(t, hit_point, normal)` or `false`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ray_sphere" => "Intersects ray `(origin, dir)` with sphere `(center, radius)`, returning `list(t, hit_point, normal)` or `false`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ray_triangle" => "Intersects ray `(origin, dir)` with triangle `(a, b, c)`, returning `list(t, hit_point, normal)` or `false`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rayleigh" => "`rayleigh(sigma)`: Rayleigh distribution with scale parameter `sigma`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "re" => "`re(z)`: real part of complex number `z`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "real_integral" => "`real_integral(f, x)`: real-valued antiderivative of `f` with respect to `x` using `ln(abs(...))` where appropriate.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "real_qe" => "`real_qe(formula, vars)`: eliminates quantifiers over `vars` from a first-order real polynomial `formula` via cylindrical algebraic decomposition.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "real_root_intervals" => "`real_root_intervals(poly, var)`: returns isolating rational intervals `[lo, hi]` for all real roots of `poly` in `var`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "reciprocal_lattice_vectors" => "`reciprocal_lattice_vectors(a1, a2, a3)`: reciprocal lattice basis `list(b1, b2, b3)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "reconstruct_holomorphic" => "`reconstruct_holomorphic(u, x, y)`: constructs holomorphic `u + I*v` from harmonic real part `u(x, y)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "refine_quality" => "`refine_quality(points, triangles, min_angle, max_area)`: `list(points, triangles)` after Ruppert-style circumcentre insertion until no triangle is below `min_angle` degrees or above `max_area`. Refines `triangles` until every triangle satisfies an angle and area bound. `min_angle_deg` is the smallest angle a triangle may have, and `max_area` the largest area it may have. New vertices are inserted at circumcentres (or at convex-hull edge midpoints when the circumcentre leaves the mesh), and the triangulation is rebuilt as a Delaunay mesh after every pass. # Errors Returns `Err` when fewer than three points are given, when a triangle index is out of range, when `min_angle_deg` is not in `(0, 60)`, or when `max_area` is not positive and finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "reflect" => "Computes the reflection vector `d - 2 * dot(d, n) * n` of incident vector `d` across unit normal `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "reflection" => "Constructs a homogeneous reflection matrix across a line (2D angle or normal) or hyperplane (normal vector).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "reflection_2d" => "Constructs a 3x3 homogeneous 2D reflection matrix across the line through the origin at angle `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "reflection_3d" => "Constructs a 4x4 homogeneous 3D reflection matrix across the plane through the origin with normal `list(a, b, c)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "refract" => "Computes the numeric Snell-refracted direction for incident vector `d`, normal `n`, and index ratio `eta`, or `false` on total internal reflection. Computes the refraction of a vector through a surface. Uses Snell's law with `eta` = n1/n2 (ratio of refractive indices). Returns `None` for total internal reflection.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "reg_beta_complex" => "`reg_beta_complex(ar, ai, br, bi, zr, zi)`: `list(re, im)` of `I_z(a, b)`. Regularized incomplete beta `I_z(a, b) = B_z(a, b) / B(a, b)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "reg_lower_gamma_complex" => "`reg_lower_gamma_complex(ar, ai, zr, zi)`: `list(re, im)` of `P(a, z)`. Regularized lower incomplete gamma `P(a, z) = gamma(a, z) / Gamma(a)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "reg_upper_gamma_complex" => "`reg_upper_gamma_complex(ar, ai, zr, zi)`: `list(re, im)` of `Q(a, z)`. Regularized upper incomplete gamma `Q(a, z) = Gamma(a, z) / Gamma(a)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "relativistic_energy" => "`relativistic_energy(m, p)`: relativistic energy `sqrt((p c_0)^2 + (m c_0^2)^2)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "relativistic_momentum" => "`relativistic_momentum(m, v)`: relativistic momentum `gamma m v`. Relativistic momentum: p = γmv # Arguments * `mass` - Rest mass (kg) * `velocity` - Velocity (m/s)\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rem" => "`rem(p, q, var)`: returns the polynomial remainder of `p` divided by `q` in `var`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rencontres" => "`rencontres(n, k)`: rencontres number `D_{n, k}`, counting permutations of `n` elements with exactly `k` fixed points.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "representation_decompose" => "Decomposes matrix representation `mats` of finite group `G` into multiplicities of irreducible representations.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "representation_is_valid" => "Verifies whether matrix list `mats` (aligned with `G`'s elements) satisfies `M(a*b) = M(a)*M(b)` for all pairs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "residuals" => "`residuals(xs, ys)`: residuals `y_i - f(x_i)` from simple linear regression on pairs `(xs, ys)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "residue" => "`residue(f, z, a)` returns the coefficient of `(z - a)^(-1)` in `f(z)` near `a`. It returns zero at a regular point and for a removable singularity. It uses a pole calculation or a Laurent expansion and leaves a request unevaluated if the coefficient cannot be determined.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "residue_at_infinity" => "`residue_at_infinity(f, z)`: residue of `f(z)` at `z = infinity`, defined as `residue(-f(1/w)/w^2, w, 0)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "residue_real_integral" => "`residue_real_integral(f, x)`: evaluates `int_{-oo}^{oo} f(x) dx` by summing upper half-plane residues.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "residue_trig_integral" => "`residue_trig_integral(f, theta)`: evaluates `int_0^{2*pi} f(theta) dtheta` by unit-circle residue substitution.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "restrict" => "`restrict(formula, var, val)`: cofactors `formula` by assigning Boolean constant `val` (`true`/`false`) to `var`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "resultant" => "`resultant(p, q, var)` computes the Sylvester resultant of the nonzero polynomials `p` and `q` in `var`. Coefficients may be exact rationals or expressions independent of `var`. A positive-degree common factor gives zero. The resultant of two nonzero constants is one.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rewrite_with" => "`rewrite_with(term, rules, vars)`: normalizes `term` to a normal form by exhaustively applying the rewrite `rules` over `vars`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rgamma_complex" => "`rgamma_complex(re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `1 / Gamma(z)`. Reciprocal gamma function `1 / Gamma(z)`, which is entire on `C` with zeros at the non-positive integers.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rhat" => "`rhat(chains)`: the split Gelman–Rubin potential scale reduction of a list of chains of the same parameter; values near 1 indicate the chains have mixed. Split-Rhat potential scale reduction factor of several chains. Every chain is cut in half, the two halves are treated as independent chains, and the ratio of the pooled variance estimate to the mean within-chain variance is returned. Values near one indicate that the chains have mixed; values well above one indicate that they disagree. # Errors Returns an error for fewer than two chains, chains of different lengths, chains shorter than four draws, or a zero within-chain variance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ricci" => "`ricci(g, vars)`: Ricci curvature tensor `R_sn = R^r_srn` for metric `g` in `vars`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ricci_scalar" => "`ricci_scalar(g, vars)`: scalar curvature `R = g^sn R_sn` for metric `g` in `vars`. Ricci scalar `R = g^{μν} R_{μν}` at `point`. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid or singular metric at a connection stencil point, or an unrepresentable stencil or result.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "riemann" => "`riemann(g, vars)`: Riemann curvature tensor `R^r_smn` for metric `g` in coordinates `vars`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "riemann_genus" => "`riemann_genus(n, d)`: topological genus of the superelliptic Riemann surface `w^n = prod_{k=1}^d (z - r_k)` with distinct roots by the Riemann-Hurwitz formula.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "riemann_zeta_complex" => "`riemann_zeta_complex(re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `zeta(s)`. Complex Riemann zeta function `zeta(s)` over the entire complex plane. Uses `eta(s) / (1 - 2^(1 - s))` for `Re(s) >= 0.5`, and the functional equation `zeta(s) = 2^s * pi^(s - 1) * sin(pi * s / 2) * Gamma(1 - s) * zeta(1 - s)` for `Re(s) < 0.5`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rips_filtration" => "Constructs the Vietoris-Rips filtration of `points` up to diameter `max_eps` and simplex dimension `max_dim + 1`. The Vietoris-Rips filtration of a finite metric space. The space is given by its distance matrix: every clique of the graph of edges of length at most `max_eps`, up to dimension `max_simplex_dim`, with value its diameter. `None` when more than `limit` simplices would be needed.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rips_persistence" => "Computes Vietoris-Rips persistence barcodes `H_0 .. H_max_dim` for `points` up to `max_eps`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rips_persistence_diagram" => "Computes the Vietoris-Rips persistence diagram for `points` up to `max_eps` and `max_dim` as a flat list of `list(dim, birth, death)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rising" => "`rising(x, k)`: rising factorial (Pochhammer symbol) `x * (x + 1) * ... * (x + k - 1)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "root_branch" => "`root_branch(z, n, k)` gives `|z|^(1/n) * exp(I * (arg(z) + 2*pi*k) / n)`. For a positive integer degree `n`, integer indices that differ by a multiple of `n` select the same root.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "root_of" => "`root_of(k, c0, c1, ..., cn)` represents the root with zero-based index `k` of `c0 + c1*x + ... + cn*x^n`. For exact rational coefficients, distinct real roots appear in ascending order, followed by the non-real roots ordered numerically by real and imaginary parts.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rossler_orbit" => "Integrates the Rossler system `(p0, dt, n, a, b, c)` for `n` steps of size `dt` from `p0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rotation" => "Constructs a 3x3 homogeneous 2D rotation matrix by angle `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rotation_2d" => "Constructs a 3x3 homogeneous 2D rotation matrix by angle `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rotation_3d_x" => "Constructs a 4x4 homogeneous 3D rotation matrix about the x-axis by angle `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rotation_3d_y" => "Constructs a 4x4 homogeneous 3D rotation matrix about the y-axis by angle `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rotation_3d_z" => "Constructs a 4x4 homogeneous 3D rotation matrix about the z-axis by angle `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rotation_axis_angle" => "Constructs a 4x4 homogeneous 3D Rodrigues rotation matrix about normalized axis `list(ax, ay, az)` by angle `theta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rotational_kinetic_energy" => "`rotational_kinetic_energy(inertia, w)`: rotational kinetic energy `(1/2) I w^2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rouche_compare" => "`rouche_compare(f, g, z, center, radius)`: returns `1` if `|f(z)| > |g(z)|` on `|z - center| = radius`, `0` if `|f(z)| < |g(z)|`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "round" => "`round(x)`: nearest integer to `x`, rounding half away from zero.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rowspace" => "`rowspace(A)`: basis of the row space of matrix `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rref" => "`rref(A)`: reduced row echelon form of matrix `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rs_check" => "Verifies whether a Reed-Solomon codeword `c` with `n` parity symbols has zero syndromes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rs_decode" => "Decodes a Reed-Solomon codeword `c` with `n` parity symbols, correcting up to `n/2` symbol errors and returning the data part.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rs_encode" => "Encodes `data` using systematic Reed-Solomon over GF(2^8) with `n` parity symbols.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rs_error_count" => "Estimates the number of errors in a Reed-Solomon codeword `c` with `n` parity symbols as the degree of the Berlekamp-Massey error locator polynomial.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rsa_decrypt" => "Computes RSA decryption `c^d mod n` for ciphertext `c`, private exponent `d`, and modulus `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rsa_encrypt" => "Computes RSA encryption `m^e mod n` for message `m`, public exponent `e`, and modulus `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rsa_keygen" => "Generates an RSA key tuple `list(n, e, d)` from distinct primes `p`, `q` and public exponent `e` with `d*e = 1 (mod (p-1)*(q-1))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rsolve" => "`rsolve(equation, target, initial)`: solve a linear recurrence `equation` for `target` given `initial` conditions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "rsolve_z" => "`rsolve_z(rec, seq, [ics...])`: solves a linear constant-coefficient recurrence relation `rec` for sequence `seq` via Z-transform.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "saddle_point_asymptotic" => "`saddle_point_asymptotic(g, phi, z, z0, lambda)`: leading saddle-point asymptotic approximation of `int g(z) exp(lambda * phi(z)) dz` near `z0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sample_std" => "`sample_std(xs)`: sample standard deviation of list `xs`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sample_variance" => "`sample_variance(xs)`: sample variance `(1/(n-1)) sum (x_i - mean)^2` of list `xs`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sat_model" => "`sat_model(formula)`: returns one satisfying assignment for `formula` (or `false` if unsatisfiable).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "satisfiable" => "`satisfiable(formula)`: decides whether propositional `formula` has at least one satisfying assignment (`true` or `false`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc" => "Inert simplicial complex wrapper `sc(list(simplex, ...))` where each simplex is a sorted list of integer vertices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_betti" => "Computes the `k`-th Betti number of simplicial complex `K` over the rationals.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_boundary" => "Computes the oriented boundary chain `list(list((-1)^i, face_i), ...)` of simplex `s`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_boundary_matrix" => "Returns the integer boundary matrix `d_k` of simplicial complex `K` with `(k-1)`-simplices as rows and `k`-simplices as columns.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_chain_boundary" => "Computes the boundary of a `k`-chain in simplicial complex `K`, simplifying coefficients and dropping zero terms.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_coboundary_matrix" => "Returns the integer coboundary matrix `d^k` (transpose of `d_{k+1}`) of simplicial complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_cohomology" => "Computes the exact integral cohomology group `H^k(K; Z)` via the universal coefficient theorem.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_cohomology_betti" => "Computes the `k`-th cohomology Betti number of simplicial complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_complex" => "Constructs a normalized simplicial complex `sc(...)` closed under faces from a list of simplices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_components" => "Returns the connected components of the 1-skeleton of simplicial complex `K` as sorted vertex lists.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_cone" => "Constructs the cone `CK` over simplicial complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_dimension" => "Returns the dimension of simplicial complex `K`, or `-1` for the empty complex.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_dunce_cap" => "Constructs a triangulation of the dunce cap with `k >= 3` edges per boundary arc (contractible but not collapsible).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_euler_characteristic" => "Computes the Euler characteristic `sum (-1)^k * |K_k|` of simplicial complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_euler_from_betti" => "Computes the Euler characteristic `sum (-1)^k * b_k` from the Betti numbers of simplicial complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_fundamental_group" => "Computes a simplified edge-path presentation `list(n, relators)` of the fundamental group `pi_1(K)` for the component of the smallest vertex.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_fundamental_group_raw" => "Computes the unsimplified edge-path presentation `list(n, relators, edges)` of `pi_1(K)` before Tietze simplification.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_grid" => "Constructs a triangulated `w` by `h` rectangular grid simplicial complex.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_homology" => "Computes the exact integral homology group `H_k(K; Z)` via Smith normal form as `list(rank, list(torsion...))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_homology_all" => "Computes exact integral homology groups `H_k(K; Z)` for all dimensions `k = 0..dim(K)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_homology_mod" => "Computes `dim H_k(K; Z/p)` of simplicial complex `K` over prime field `Z/p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_induced_homology" => "Computes the rational matrix of the induced homomorphism `f_* : H_k(K; Q) -> H_k(L; Q)` for simplicial map `f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_induced_homology_rank" => "Computes the rank of the induced homomorphism `f_* : H_k(K; Q) -> H_k(L; Q)` for simplicial map `f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_intersection" => "Returns the intersection `K ∩ L` of two simplicial complexes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_is_simplicial_map" => "Returns `true` if vertex map `f` sends every simplex of `K` to a simplex of `L`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_is_subcomplex" => "Returns `true` if simplicial complex `L` is a subcomplex of `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_join" => "Constructs the simplicial join `K * L` of complexes `K` and `L`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_klein_bottle" => "Constructs a triangulated `m` by `n` Klein bottle simplicial complex (`m, n >= 3`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_mayer_vietoris_check" => "Verifies rank-level exactness and Euler characteristic additivity of the Mayer-Vietoris sequence for `A` and `B`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_mayer_vietoris_ranks" => "Computes the ranks of the connecting maps in the Mayer-Vietoris exact sequence for `A` and `B`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_pi1_abelianization" => "Computes the abelianization of `pi_1(K)` via Smith normal form as `list(rank, list(torsion...))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_product" => "Constructs the triangulated Cartesian product `K × L` of two simplicial complexes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_projective_plane" => "Constructs a triangulation of the real projective plane `RP^2` (`k = 0` gives the minimal 6-vertex triangulation; `k >= 3` refines the boundary to `2k` edges).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_reduced_homology" => "Computes the reduced integral homology group `H~_k(K; Z)` as `list(rank, list(torsion...))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_relative_homology" => "Computes the exact relative integral homology group `H_k(K, L; Z)` for subcomplex `L` of `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_simplex" => "Constructs the standard `n`-simplex simplicial complex on `n + 1` vertices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_simplex_dimension" => "Returns the dimension `len(s) - 1` of simplex `s`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_simplices" => "Returns the list of `k`-simplices in simplicial complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_skeleton" => "Returns the `d`-skeleton of simplicial complex `K` (all simplices of dimension `<= d`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_sphere" => "Constructs the `n`-sphere `S^n` as the boundary of the `(n+1)`-simplex.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_suspension" => "Constructs the suspension `SK` of simplicial complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_torus" => "Constructs a triangulated `m` by `n` torus simplicial complex (`m, n >= 3`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_union" => "Returns the union `K ∪ L` of two simplicial complexes.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_verify_boundary" => "Verifies that the boundary operator satisfies `d_k * d_{k+1} = 0` for all dimensions in complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_verify_coboundary" => "Verifies that the coboundary operator satisfies `d^{k+1} * d^k = 0` for all dimensions in complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_verify_euler" => "Verifies that the alternating sum of Betti numbers equals the alternating simplex count of complex `K`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sc_wedge" => "Constructs the wedge sum `sc_wedge(K, L[, u, v])` by identifying vertex `u` of `K` with `v` of `L` (defaulting to smallest vertices).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "scalar_field_lagrangian" => "`scalar_field_lagrangian(phi, m, x)`: free real scalar field Lagrangian density `(1/2)(partial_mu phi)^2 - (1/2) m^2 phi^2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "scale_dist" => "`scale_dist(c, d)`: distribution of scaled random variable `c * X` where `X ~ d`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "scale_factors" => "`scale_factors(sys, vars)`: Lamé scale factors `h_i = sqrt(g_ii)` of orthogonal coordinate system `sys` in `vars`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "scaling" => "Constructs an `(n+1)x(n+1)` homogeneous scaling matrix from `n` scale factors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "scaling_2d" => "Constructs a 3x3 homogeneous 2D scaling matrix for `(sx, sy)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "scaling_3d" => "Constructs a 4x4 homogeneous 3D scaling matrix for `(sx, sy, sz)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "scattering_amplitude" => "`scattering_amplitude(psi_i, psi_f, V, x)`: Born-approximation transition matrix element `<psi_f|V|psi_i>`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "scattering_cross_section" => "`scattering_cross_section(M, flux, dphi)`: differential cross section `|M|^2 / flux * dphi`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "schroeder" => "`schroeder(n)`: `n`-th large Schroeder number, counting lattice paths from `(0, 0)` to `(n, n)` with steps `(1, 0)`, `(0, 1)`, `(1, 1)` not crossing `y = x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "schwarzian_derivative" => "`schwarzian_derivative(f, z)`: Schwarzian derivative `(f'''/f') - (3/2)*(f''/f')^2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "schwarzschild_metric" => "`schwarzschild_metric(M, coords)`: Schwarzschild metric tensor in `(-, +, +, +)` signature for mass `M` and `coords = list(t, r, theta, phi)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "schwarzschild_radius" => "`schwarzschild_radius(M)`: Schwarzschild event-horizon radius `2 G_N M / c_0^2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sde_levy_areas" => "`sde_levy_areas(seed, h, dw, terms)`: the `m*m` row-major iterated Ito integrals over one step, conditioned on the `m` increments `dw`, with a seeded Fourier bridge truncated at `terms` modes. Requires finite inputs and results, at most 4,194,304 output entries and 67,108,864 pair-mode terms.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sde_scheme_info" => "`sde_scheme_info(name)`: the metadata of one stochastic scheme as `list(strong_order, ito, commutative_only, adaptive, levy_areas)` — the strong order for commutative noise and four flags (1 or 0). Unknown names stay a request.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sde_wiener" => "`sde_wiener(seed, m, steps, h)`: the `steps x m` matrix of seeded Brownian increments `dW[k][j] ~ N(0, h)`, reproducible bit for bit from `seed`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sdp_solve" => "`sdp_solve(c, f0, fs, a, rel, b, max_iter)`: `list(x, objective, iterations, primal_residual, dual_residual, min_eigenvalue)` minimising `c . x` subject to `F_0 + sum x_i F_i >= 0` and the equality rows `a x = b`. Solves a semidefinite program by SDPAD. # Errors Returns an error string when the dimensions disagree, when an entry is not finite, when `max_iter` is zero, or when the `x`-update system is singular.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sec" => "`sec(x)`: secant `1 / cos(x)` of angle `x` in radians.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sech" => "`sech(x)`: hyperbolic secant `1 / cosh(x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "second_order_energy_correction" => "`second_order_energy_correction(V, states, energies, n, x)`: second-order Rayleigh-Schrodinger energy shift `sum_{m != n} |<m|V|n>|^2 / (E_n - E_m)` over basis `states` in variable `x` (1-based index `n`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "shear" => "Constructs a homogeneous shear matrix from 2 (`2D`) or 6 (`3D`) shear parameters, or a general off-diagonal list.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "shear_2d" => "Constructs a 3x3 homogeneous 2D shear matrix from `(shx, shy)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "shear_3d" => "Constructs a 4x4 homogeneous 3D shear matrix from 6 off-diagonal shear factors.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "shi" => "`shi(x)`: hyperbolic sine integral `Shi(x) = int_0^x sinh(t)/t dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "shift_dist" => "`shift_dist(c, d)`: distribution of shifted random variable `X + c` where `X ~ d`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "si" => "`si(x)`: sine integral `Si(x) = int_0^x sin(t)/t dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sigma" => "`sigma(n, k)`: sum of `k`-th powers of positive divisors `sigma_k(n)` of `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sigma_SB" => "`sigma_SB`: Stefan-Boltzmann constant in W/(m^2*K^4).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sign" => "`sign(x)`: signum function (`-1` for `x < 0`, `0` at `0`, `1` for `x > 0`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sign_complex" => "`sign_complex(z)`: complex signum `z / abs(z)` on the unit circle. Complex signum `z / |z|`, or `0` if `z == 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "similarity_dimension" => "Computes the similarity dimension `-ln(N)/ln(r)` for equal contraction ratios, or builds the Moran equation `sum r_i^D = 1`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "simplify_logic" => "`simplify_logic(formula)`: simplifies propositional `formula` using Quine-McCluskey minimization.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "simplify_radicals" => "`simplify_radicals(expr)`: simplifies radical expressions in `expr` by denesting nested roots and combining or rationalizing surds.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "simplify_units" => "`simplify_units(expr)`: reduces a dimensional expression `expr` to coherent SI base units.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "simplify_with_relations" => "`simplify_with_relations(expr, relations, vars)`: simplifies `expr` modulo polynomial side `relations` over `vars`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sin" => "`sin(x)`: sine of angle `x` in radians.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sin_integral_complex" => "`sin_integral_complex(re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `Si(z)`. Complex sine and cosine integrals `(Si(z), Ci(z))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sinc" => "`sinc(x)`: normalized cardinal sine `sin(pi * x) / (pi * x)` with `sinc(0) = 1`. Computes the sinc function sinc(x) = sin(πx) / (πx).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "singularity" => "`singularity(f, z, a)`: classification of `f(z)` at `a` (`regular`, `removable`, `list(pole, m)`, or `essential`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sinh" => "`sinh(x)`: hyperbolic sine `(e^x - e^-x) / 2`. `sinh(x)` as a ball. # Errors Fails when an exponent leaves the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sinh_integral_complex" => "`sinh_integral_complex(re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `Shi(z)`. Complex hyperbolic sine and cosine integrals `(Shi(z), Chi(z))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "skewness" => "`skewness(xs)`: Pearson skewness `m3 / m2^(3/2)` of list `xs`. Computes the adjusted Fisher-Pearson sample skewness coefficient `G1` (the estimator used by Excel's `SKEW` and by `scipy.stats.skew(bias=False)`). With `g1 = m3 / m2^(3/2)` (central moments divided by `n`), `G1 = sqrt(n (n - 1)) / (n - 2) * g1`. Returns `NaN` for fewer than three points and `0.0` for constant data.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "skewness_of" => "`skewness_of(d)`: skewness (standardized 3rd central moment) of distribution `d`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl2_basis" => "`sl2_basis()`: Chevalley basis `(h, e, f)` of `sl(2)` with `[h, e] = 2e`, `[h, f] = -2f`, `[e, f] = h`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_basis" => "`sl_basis(n)`: standard traceless matrix basis of the special linear Lie algebra `sl(n)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_density" => "`sl_density(lambda, epsilon, coeffs...)`: spectral density `Im(m(lambda + i * epsilon)) / pi` of a Sturm-Liouville operator.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_eigen" => "`sl_eigen(n, coeffs...)`: `n`-th eigenvalue of a regular Sturm-Liouville problem `-(p y')' + q y = mu w y` via Chebyshev collocation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_mfun_im" => "`sl_mfun_im(lambda, epsilon, coeffs...)`: imaginary part of the Weyl-Titchmarsh `m`-function at `lambda + i * epsilon`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_mfun_re" => "`sl_mfun_re(lambda, epsilon, coeffs...)`: real part of the Weyl-Titchmarsh `m`-function at `lambda + i * epsilon`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_mode" => "`sl_mode(n, x, coeffs...)`: `n`-th normalized Sturm-Liouville eigenfunction evaluated at `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_neg_root" => "`sl_neg_root(alpha1, beta1, alpha2, beta2, L)`: unique negative Sturm-Liouville eigenvalue root (if any) for Robin boundary conditions on `[0, L]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_neg_root_n" => "`sl_neg_root_n(alpha1, beta1, alpha2, beta2, L, k)`: `k`-th negative Sturm-Liouville eigenvalue root on `[0, L]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_root" => "`sl_root(alpha1, beta1, alpha2, beta2, L, k)`: `k`-th positive Sturm-Liouville eigenvalue root on `[0, L]` with general linear boundary conditions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sl_weight" => "`sl_weight(x, coeffs...)`: Sturm-Liouville weight function `w(x)` evaluated at `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "smith" => "`smith(A)`: Smith normal form `list(S, U, V)` of integer matrix `A` with `U A V = S`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "smul" => "`smul(c, A)`: scalar multiplication `c * A` of scalar `c` and matrix or vector `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "so3_basis" => "`so3_basis()`: standard basis `(Lx, Ly, Lz)` of `so(3)` with `[Li, Lj] = eps_ijk Lk`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "so_basis" => "`so_basis(n)`: standard skew-symmetric matrix basis `E_ij - E_ji` of `so(n)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sobolev_h1" => "`sobolev_h1(f, x, a, b)`: Sobolev `H^1` norm `sqrt(||f||_2^2 + ||f'||_2^2)` on `[a, b]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "socp_solve" => "`socp_solve(c, a, rel, b, cones, bounds, max_iter)`: `list(x, objective, iterations, primal_residual, dual_residual, gap)` of a second-order cone program; each cone is `list(a, b, c, d)` standing for `||A x + b|| <= c . x + d`. Solves a second-order cone program. # Errors Returns an error string when the dimensions disagree, when an entry is not finite, when `max_iter` is zero, or when the KKT system is singular.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve" => "`solve(eq_or_system, var_or_vars)`: symbolically solves an equation, inequality, or system for `var_or_vars`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_box" => "`solve_box(system, vars, box_bounds)`: isolates all roots of `system` in `vars` within `box_bounds`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_burgers_equation" => "`solve_burgers_equation(pde, u, ...)`: solves Burgers' inviscid or viscous nonlinear PDE.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_complex" => "`solve_complex(poly, var)`: finds all complex roots of polynomial `poly` in `var`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_euler_lagrange" => "`solve_euler_lagrange(L, y, x)`: solves the Euler-Lagrange differential equation `euler_lagrange(L, y, x) = 0` for `y`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_general" => "`solve_general(eq_or_system, var_or_vars)`: solves an equation or system symbolically, including parameterized/underdetermined solution sets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_heat_equation" => "`solve_heat_equation(pde, u, ...)`: solves the heat/diffusion equation `u_t = alpha laplacian(u) + f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_heat_equation_1d" => "`solve_heat_equation_1d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 1D heat equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_heat_equation_2d" => "`solve_heat_equation_2d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 2D heat equation. Solves a 2D heat equation `u_t = alpha * ∇²u` using the finite difference method.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_heat_equation_3d" => "`solve_heat_equation_3d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 3D heat equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_helmholtz_equation" => "`solve_helmholtz_equation(pde, u, ...)`: solves the Helmholtz equation `laplacian(u) + k^2 u = f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_klein_gordon_equation" => "`solve_klein_gordon_equation(pde, u, ...)`: solves the Klein-Gordon wave equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_laplace_equation" => "`solve_laplace_equation(pde, u, ...)`: solves Laplace's equation `laplacian(u) = 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_laplace_equation_1d" => "`solve_laplace_equation_1d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 1D Laplace equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_laplace_equation_2d" => "`solve_laplace_equation_2d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 2D Laplace equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_laplace_equation_3d" => "`solve_laplace_equation_3d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 3D Laplace equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_pde" => "`solve_pde(pde, u, ...)`: alias for `pdsolve`; solves `pde` for unknown `u`. Unified time-dependent PDE evolution driver supporting both fixed and adaptive step sizes via [`OdeStepMode`](crate::kernels::ode::OdeStepMode). `step_fn(t, state, dt)` advances `state` by a single step `dt` from time `t`. In [`OdeStepMode::Fixed`](crate::kernels::ode::OdeStepMode::Fixed), it takes uniform steps of size `dt` (with the final step landing on `t_span.1`). In [`OdeStepMode::Adaptive`](crate::kernels::ode::OdeStepMode::Adaptive), it delegates to [`adaptive_evolve`] with Richardson step-doubling of `order`. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid time spans, step sizes, tolerances, non-finite states, or step-budget exhaustion.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_pde_by_characteristics" => "`solve_pde_by_characteristics(pde, u, ...)`: solves first-order or quasilinear PDEs via the method of characteristics.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_pde_by_greens_function" => "`solve_pde_by_greens_function(pde, u, ...)`: solves linear inhomogeneous PDEs via Green's function integration.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_pde_by_separation_of_variables" => "`solve_pde_by_separation_of_variables(pde, u, ...)`: solves linear boundary-value PDEs by separation of variables.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_poisson_equation" => "`solve_poisson_equation(pde, u, ...)`: solves Poisson's equation `laplacian(u) = f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_poisson_equation_1d" => "`solve_poisson_equation_1d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 1D Poisson equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_poisson_equation_2d" => "`solve_poisson_equation_2d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 2D Poisson equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_poisson_equation_3d" => "`solve_poisson_equation_3d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 3D Poisson equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_schrodinger_equation" => "`solve_schrodinger_equation(pde, u, ...)`: solves the time-dependent or stationary Schrodinger PDE.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_second_order_pde" => "`solve_second_order_pde(pde, u, ...)`: general solver for linear second-order PDEs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_time_independent_schrodinger" => "`solve_time_independent_schrodinger(H, psi, x)`: returns `list(E, psi)` for eigenstate `psi` of `H`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_wave_equation" => "`solve_wave_equation(pde, u, ...)`: solves the wave equation `u_tt = c^2 laplacian(u) + f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_wave_equation_1d" => "`solve_wave_equation_1d(pde, u, conditions)` solves a symbolic wave equation and returns an equation for the unknown function. Supply the unknown as an application such as `u(x, t)`. The optional list of conditions gives initial values, initial velocity, or boundary data. This spelling uses the same solver as `solve_wave_equation`. The arguments of the unknown function determine the spatial dimension.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_wave_equation_1d_dalembert" => "`solve_wave_equation_1d_dalembert(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 1D wave equation via d'Alembert's traveling-wave formula.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_wave_equation_2d" => "`solve_wave_equation_2d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 2D wave equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_wave_equation_3d" => "`solve_wave_equation_3d(pde, u, ...)`: solves the 3D wave equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "solve_with_fourier_transform" => "`solve_with_fourier_transform(pde, u, ...)`: solves linear constant-coefficient PDEs using the Fourier transform.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_add" => "`sp_add(A, B)`: returns the sum `A + B` of two `sparse` matrices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_basis" => "`sp_basis(n)`: standard symplectic matrix basis of `sp(2n)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_det" => "`sp_det(A)`: computes the exact determinant of a square `sparse` matrix `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_det_mod" => "`sp_det_mod(A, p)`: computes the determinant of integer `sparse` matrix `A` modulo prime `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_diag" => "`sp_diag(entries)`: constructs a sparse diagonal matrix from a list of diagonal entries.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_dims" => "`sp_dims(sp)`: returns `[rows, cols]` for a `sparse` matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_from_dense" => "`sp_from_dense(mat)`: converts a dense matrix (list of row lists) into a canonical `sparse` matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_identity" => "`sp_identity(n)`: constructs the `n x n` sparse identity matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_matmul" => "`sp_matmul(A, B)`: returns the matrix product `A * B` of two `sparse` matrices.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_matvec" => "`sp_matvec(A, v)`: multiplies a `sparse` matrix `A` by a dense vector `v`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_minpoly_mod" => "`sp_minpoly_mod(A, p)`: computes the minimal polynomial coefficients of integer `sparse` matrix `A` modulo prime `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_nnz" => "`sp_nnz(sp)`: returns the number of nonzero entries in a `sparse` matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_nullspace" => "`sp_nullspace(A)`: computes a basis for the right nullspace `{x : A * x = 0}` of a `sparse` matrix `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_rank" => "`sp_rank(A)`: computes the exact rank of a `sparse` matrix `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_rank_mod" => "`sp_rank_mod(A, p)`: computes the rank of integer `sparse` matrix `A` modulo prime `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_scale" => "`sp_scale(scalar, A)`: scales a `sparse` matrix `A` by `scalar`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_solve" => "`sp_solve(A, b)`: solves the square linear system `A * x = b` for a `sparse` matrix `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_solve_general" => "`sp_solve_general(A, b)`: solves a general (possibly rectangular or rank-deficient) sparse linear system `A * x = b`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_solve_mod" => "`sp_solve_mod(A, b, p)`: solves `A * x = b (mod p)` for integer `sparse` matrix `A` and prime `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_to_dense" => "`sp_to_dense(sp)`: converts a `sparse` matrix into a dense list of row lists.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sp_transpose" => "`sp_transpose(sp)`: returns the transpose of a `sparse` matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sparse" => "`sparse(rows, cols, entries)`: canonical sparse matrix constructor with dimensions `rows x cols` and sorted nonzero `[row, col, value]` triples.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sparse_solve" => "`sparse_solve(entries, b, tol, method)`: solve sparse `A x = b` from `(i, j, v)` triplets using `minres`, `fgmres`, `bicgstab_l`, or `idr_s`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spectral" => "`spectral(A)`: spectral decomposition `list(eigenvalues, projectors)` of diagonalizable matrix `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spectral_analysis" => "Returns `list(eigenvals(M), eigenvects(M))` for square matrix `M`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spence_complex" => "`spence_complex(re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `Li_2(1 - z)`. Spence's dilogarithm `spence(z) = Li_2(1 - z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spherical_harmonic" => "`spherical_harmonic(l, m, theta, phi)`: orthonormal spherical harmonic `Y_l^m(theta, phi)` with the Condon-Shortley phase as an explicit trigonometric expression.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spherical_in_complex" => "`spherical_in_complex(n, re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `i_n(z)`. Modified spherical Bessel function `i_n(z) = sqrt(pi / (2z)) * I_{n + 1/2}(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spherical_jn_complex" => "`spherical_jn_complex(n, re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `j_n(z)`. Spherical Bessel function `j_n(z) = sqrt(pi / (2z)) * J_{n + 1/2}(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spherical_kn_complex" => "`spherical_kn_complex(n, re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `k_n(z)`. Modified spherical Bessel function `k_n(z) = sqrt(2 / (pi*z)) * K_{n + 1/2}(z)` (DLMF 10.47.9: `sqrt(pi/(2z)) * K_{n+1/2}(z)`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spherical_yn_complex" => "`spherical_yn_complex(n, re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `y_n(z)`. Spherical Bessel function `y_n(z) = sqrt(pi / (2z)) * Y_{n + 1/2}(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spheroidal_eigen" => "`spheroidal_eigen(m, n, gamma)`: numeric angular spheroidal eigenvalue `lambda_{mn}(gamma)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spheroidal_mode" => "`spheroidal_mode(m, n, gamma, x)`: normalized angular spheroidal wave function `S_{mn}(gamma, x)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spheroidal_radial" => "`spheroidal_radial(kind, m, n, gamma, xi)`: prolate (`kind > 0`) or oblate radial spheroidal wave function at `xi`. The kind, coordinate and `gamma` must be finite, and the degree and order must be integers with `0 <= m <= n <= 10000`. The radial coordinate is at least one for prolate and at least zero for oblate.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "spin_operator" => "`spin_operator(sigma)`: spin-1/2 operator `(hbar / 2) * sigma`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sqrt" => "`sqrt(x)`: principal square root of `x`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sqrt_branch" => "`sqrt_branch(z, k)` selects the principal square root for an even integer `k` and its negative for an odd integer `k`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sqrt_cut" => "`sqrt_cut(z, theta0)`: square root with branch cut along ray `theta0`. Square root `sqrt(|z|) * cis(arg_branch(z, theta0) / 2)` with branch cut along ray `theta0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sqrtmod" => "`sqrtmod(a, p)`: modular square root `r` satisfying `r^2 = a (mod p)` for prime `p`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "square_free_factors" => "`square_free_factors(poly, var)` decomposes a nonzero polynomial in the symbol `var` over the rationals. Returns `list(unit, list(list(factor, multiplicity), ...))`. The unit is the leading coefficient. Each factor is monic and square-free, and the factors are pairwise coprime and ordered by increasing multiplicity. A nonzero constant has an empty factor list. The zero polynomial and coefficients outside the rationals leave the request unevaluated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ss_transfer" => "`ss_transfer(a, b, c, d)`: `list(num, den)`, the transfer function from the first input to the first output. Converts a state-space model to one transfer function per input/output pair, ordered output-major (output `0` against every input, then output `1`, and so on). The denominator is obtained by the Faddeev-LeVerrier algorithm and the numerator from the Markov parameters `C A^k B` plus the feedthrough term. # Errors Returns an error when the model has inconsistent dimensions or the polynomial construction fails.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "standard_error" => "`standard_error(xs)`: standard error of the mean `sample_std / sqrt(n)` of list `xs`. Computes the sample standard error of the mean (SEM = `std_dev` / sqrt(n)).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "std" => "`std(xs)`: population standard deviation of list `xs`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "std_of" => "`std_of(d)`: standard deviation `sqrt(Var(X))` of distribution `d`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "stefan_boltzmann_law" => "`stefan_boltzmann_law(T)`: blackbody total emissive power `sigma_SB T^4`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "stirling1" => "`stirling1(n, k)`: unsigned Stirling number of the first kind `|s(n, k)|`, counting permutations of `n` elements with `k` cycles.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "stirling2" => "`stirling2(n, k)`: Stirling number of the second kind `S(n, k)`, counting partitions of an `n`-element set into `k` non-empty subsets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "stokes_theorem" => "`stokes_theorem(F, surface, u, v, u0, u1, v0, v1)`: circulation of `F` around the boundary of `surface(u, v)` via Stokes' theorem.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "structure_constants" => "`structure_constants(basis[, coords])`: structure constants tensor `c[i][j][k]` satisfying `[e_i, e_j] = sum_k c_ijk e_k`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "struve_h" => "`struve_h(nu, z)`: Struve function `H_nu(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "struve_h_complex" => "`struve_h_complex(nu, re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `H_nu(z)`. Complex Struve function `H_nu(z)` via ascending power series (using `rgamma` to handle negative half-integer `nu`) and large-`|z|` asymptotic expansion.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "struve_l" => "`struve_l(nu, z)`: modified Struve function `L_nu(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "struve_l_complex" => "`struve_l_complex(nu, re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `L_nu(z)`. Complex modified Struve function `L_nu(z) = -i * exp(-i*nu*pi/2) * H_nu(i*z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "student_t" => "`student_t(nu)`: Student's t-distribution with `nu` degrees of freedom.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sturm_sequence" => "`sturm_sequence(poly, var)` returns the Sturm chain of a univariate polynomial with exact rational coefficients. The output is a `list(...)` of polynomials in `var`. For a nonconstant polynomial the chain starts with the polynomial and its derivative, followed by successive negated nonzero remainders. A constant produces a singleton list, including `list(0)` for zero. For example, `sturm_sequence(x^2 - 2, x)` returns `list(x^2 - 2, 2*x, 2)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "su2_basis" => "`su2_basis()`: standard basis `-I * sigma_k / 2` of `su(2)` with `[e_i, e_j] = eps_ijk e_k`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "su_basis" => "`su_basis(n)`: standard skew-Hermitian traceless matrix basis of `su(n)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sub" => "`sub(a, b)`: the difference `a - b`. The engine's normal form has no subtraction node: this request is rewritten to `add(a, mul(-1, b))` and prints as `a - b`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sum" => "`sum(expr, k, lo, hi)` or `sum(tensor, [axis])`: symbolic/numeric summation over bound index `k` from `lo` to `hi`, or tensor reduction along `axis`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sum_dist" => "`sum_dist(d1, d2)`: distribution of the sum `X + Y` of independent random variables `X ~ d1` and `Y ~ d2`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "surface_integral" => "`surface_integral(f, r, u, v, u0, u1, v0, v1)`: scalar surface integral `int_{v0}^{v1} int_{u0}^{u1} f(r(u, v)) ||r_u x r_v|| du dv`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "svd" => "`svd(A)`: singular value decomposition `list(U, S, V)` of matrix `A`. Thin singular-value decomposition using the bidiagonal SVD backend. Rank-deficient matrices retain orthonormal null-space columns. No Gram matrix is formed, so small singular values do not lose accuracy through squaring the condition number. # Errors Returns [`DenseError::Shape`] for inconsistent storage or non-finite input, and [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] if the backend does not converge.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "sym" => "A symbol leaf.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "symmetric_group" => "Constructs the symmetric group `S_n` (`n <= 6`) of permutations in lexicographic order.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "syt_count" => "`syt_count(shape)`: number of standard Young tableaux of `shape` via the hook-length formula.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "t_test" => "`t_test(sample, mu)`: one-sample Student's t-test returning `list(statistic, p_value)` for `sample` against population mean `mu`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tan" => "`tan(x)`: tangent `sin(x) / cos(x)` of angle `x` in radians. `tan(x)` as a ball. # Errors Fails where the cosine interval contains zero, or for an argument beyond `2^20` in magnitude.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tanh" => "`tanh(x)`: hyperbolic tangent `sinh(x) / cosh(x)`. `tanh(x)` as a ball. # Errors Fails when an exponent leaves the representable range.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tautology" => "`tautology(formula)`: decides whether propositional `formula` holds under all variable assignments (`true` or `false`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "taylor" => "`taylor(f, x, a, n)`: Taylor polynomial of `f` about `x = a` up to degree `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tensor_add" => "`tensor_add(A, B)`: componentwise sum `A + B` of tensors of the same shape.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tensor_contract" => "`tensor_contract(t, i, j)`: self-contraction (generalized trace) of tensor `t` over axes `i` and `j`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tensor_decomposition" => "`tensor_decomposition(T, hw1, hw2)`: irreducible decomposition `list(list(hw, m), ...)` of `V(hw1) (x) V(hw2)` for type `T`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tensor_inner" => "`tensor_inner(a, b)`: Frobenius inner product of two tensors `a` and `b` of identical shape.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tensor_norm" => "`tensor_norm(t)`: Frobenius norm of tensor `t`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tensor_outer" => "`tensor_outer(A, B)`: outer (tensor) product `A (x) B` of tensors `A` and `B`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tensor_rank" => "`tensor_rank(T)`: rank (number of indices) of nested-list tensor `T`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tensor_smul" => "`tensor_smul(c, T)`: tensor `T` scaled componentwise by scalar `c`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tensordot" => "`tensordot(a, b, axes_a, axes_b)`: tensor contraction of `a` and `b` along axis lists `axes_a` and `axes_b`. Contracts two tensors along the paired axes in `axes_a` and `axes_b`. The free axes of `a` precede the free axes of `b`, each in its original order. See [`tensordot_views`] for the shape and borrowing rules. # Errors Returns the axis and output shape errors of [`tensordot_views`].\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tf_bode" => "`tf_bode(num, den, omega)`: `list(magnitude_db, phase_deg)` of the frequency response at `omega`. Bode data: magnitude in decibels and unwrapped phase in degrees. # Errors Returns an error when a frequency is negative or not finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tf_nyquist" => "`tf_nyquist(num, den, omega)`: `list(re, im)` of `G(i omega)` at `omega`. Nyquist plot points `(Re G(j w), Im G(j w))`. # Errors Returns an error when a frequency is negative or not finite.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tf_poles" => "`tf_poles(num, den)`: the poles of the transfer function as `list(re, im)` pairs. Poles: roots of the denominator.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tf_state_space" => "`tf_state_space(num, den)`: `list(a, b, c, d)`, the controllable canonical realisation of the transfer function `num(s)/den(s)` (descending powers). Converts to a continuous-time state-space model in controllable canonical form. # Errors Returns an error when the numerator is improper or the denominator has no nonzero leading coefficient.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "time_dependent_schrodinger_equation" => "`time_dependent_schrodinger_equation(H, psi, t)`: residual `i hbar d(psi)/dt - H psi`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "time_independent_schrodinger_equation" => "`time_independent_schrodinger_equation(H, psi, E)`: residual `H psi - E psi`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tinkerbell_orbit" => "Computes an `n`-step orbit of the 2D Tinkerbell map `(p0, n, a, b, c, d)` starting from `p0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "to_cartesian" => "`to_cartesian(sys, vars)`: Cartesian coordinates expressed in coordinate system `sys` (`cartesian`, `polar`, `cylindrical`, `spherical`) with variables `vars`. Inverse map to Cartesian: converts a point of `from` to Cartesian. # Errors Returns an error if `point` has the wrong number of components.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "to_polar" => "`to_polar(z)`: polar coordinates `list(abs(z), arg(z))`. Polar coordinates `(r, theta)` with `r = |z|` and `theta = arg(z) in (-pi, pi]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "to_riemann_sphere" => "`to_riemann_sphere(z)`: stereographic projection `(X, Y, Z)` onto the unit Riemann sphere `S^2`. Stereographic projection from `C` to the unit Riemann sphere `S^2`. Maps `0` to `(0, 0, -1)` and `infinity` to `(0, 0, 1)`. Uses `1/z` scaling when `|z| > 1` to avoid overflow for huge `|z|`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "todd_coxeter" => "Runs Todd-Coxeter coset enumeration on `<gens | relators>` over `subgroup_gens`, returning `list(index, list(perm_per_gen))`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "todd_coxeter_index" => "Computes the index of `subgroup_gens` in the finitely presented group `<gens | relators>` by Todd–Coxeter enumeration. The result counts live cosets with complete inverse transitions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "together" => "`together(expr)`: combines sums of rational expressions over a common denominator.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "torque" => "`torque(r, F)`: torque vector `r x F` about the origin.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "totient" => "`totient(n)`: Euler's totient function `phi(n)`, counting integers in `[1, n]` coprime to `n`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "trace" => "`trace(A)`: trace `sum_i A_{ii}` of square matrix `A`. Computes the trace of a square sparse matrix. # Errors Returns an error if the matrix is not square.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "transform_covector" => "`transform_covector(W, from, from_vars, to, to_vars)`: covariant vector `W` transformed from `from(from_vars)` to `to(to_vars)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "transform_expression" => "`transform_expression(f, from, from_vars, to, to_vars)`: scalar expression `f` rewritten from `from(from_vars)` into `to(to_vars)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "transform_point" => "`transform_point(from, to, point)`: coordinates of `point` converted from system `from` to system `to`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "transform_tensor2" => "`transform_tensor2(T, from, from_vars, to, to_vars)`: contravariant rank-2 tensor `T` transformed from `from(from_vars)` to `to(to_vars)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "transform_vector" => "`transform_vector(V, from, from_vars, to, to_vars)` converts the contravariant components of `V` between coordinate systems. Supply the source coordinates as `from_vars` and the destination coordinates as `to_vars`. The result is a `list(...)` of components in the destination variables. The operator rewrites the source components in the destination coordinates and multiplies by the Jacobian of the destination coordinates with respect to the source coordinates.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "translation" => "Constructs an `(n+1)x(n+1)` homogeneous translation matrix from `n` translation offsets.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "translation_2d" => "Constructs a 3x3 homogeneous 2D translation matrix for `(tx, ty)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "translation_3d" => "Constructs a 4x4 homogeneous 3D translation matrix for `(tx, ty, tz)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "transpose" => "`transpose(A)`: transpose `A^T` of matrix `A`. Transpose of a rectangular slice-of-rows matrix.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "trimmed_mean" => "`trimmed_mean(x, alpha)`: symmetric trimmed mean of `x`, discarding fraction `alpha` from each tail. Mean after discarding the fraction `trim` from each tail.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "true" => "`true`: Boolean truth constant.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "truth_table" => "`truth_table(formula)`: returns the truth table of `formula` as a list of variable-assignment rows and outputs.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tt_dense" => "`tt_dense(cores)`: reconstruct dense tensor from a list of tensor-train cores. Dense conversion with an explicit limit on the output element count. Use this when loading cores supplied by another process or when the logical tensor may be exponentially larger than its compressed storage. # Errors Returns an error when size exceeds the limit, allocation fails, or arithmetic overflows.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tt_svd" => "`tt_svd(t, tol, max_rank)`: tensor-train SVD of `t` returning `list(cores, error)`. Compress a finite, nonempty dense tensor with TT-SVD. Each split is assigned relative tolerance / sqrt(order - 1). A rank cap may prevent satisfying that tolerance; inspect `tolerance_met` and the returned truncation estimate. Zero tensors use bond rank one. A one-mode tensor is stored exactly. Input is normalized before decomposition to avoid squaring the input's scale in intermediate norm computations. # Errors Returns an error for invalid shape/options, non-finite input, SVD failure, allocation/size overflow or unrepresentable rescaling.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "tucker_dense" => "`tucker_dense(core, factors)`: reconstruct dense tensor from Tucker `core` and mode factor matrices. The dense tensor `core x_1 U_1 .. x_N U_N` of a Tucker model, with `factors[k]` of size `n_k x r_k` and `core` of shape `r_1 x .. x r_N`. # Errors Returns an error if the number of factors differs from the rank of the core, a factor's column count differs from its core extent, or the result would be empty.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "two_squares" => "`two_squares(n)`: representation `list(x, y)` with `x^2 + y^2 = n` for `n >= 0`, if one exists.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "uncertainty" => "`uncertainty(A, psi, x)`: standard deviation `sqrt(<A^2> - <A>^2)` in state `psi`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "uniform" => "`uniform(a, b)`: continuous uniform distribution on the interval `[a, b]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "unify_expression" => "`unify_expression(expr)`: evaluates and simplifies a dimensional expression `expr` into a unified `quantity(value, unit)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "upper_gamma_complex" => "`upper_gamma_complex(ar, ai, zr, zi)`: `list(re, im)` of upper incomplete gamma `Gamma(a, z)`. Upper incomplete gamma function `Gamma(a, z) = int_z^oo t^(a-1) e^(-t) dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "vandermonde" => "`vandermonde(nodes)`: square Vandermonde matrix `V_{ij} = nodes[i]^j`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "variance" => "`variance(xs)`: population variance `(1/n) sum (x_i - mean)^2` of list `xs`. Computes the variance of a slice of f64 values (defaults to population variance). # Arguments * `data` - An immutable slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The variance of the data as an `f64`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "variance_of" => "`variance_of(d)`: variance `Var(X) = E[(X - E[X])^2]` of distribution `d`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "variational_ground_state" => "`variational_ground_state(H, trial, x, a)`: Rayleigh-Ritz ground-state estimate `list(a*, E(a*))` minimizing `E(a) = <trial|H|trial> / <trial|trial>` over `a > 0`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "velocity_addition" => "`velocity_addition(v, u)`: relativistic collinear velocity addition `(v + u) / (1 + v u / c_0^2)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "verify_definite_integral" => "`verify_definite_integral(integrand, var, a, b, value)`: numerically and symbolically checks whether the definite integral of `integrand` over `[a, b]` equals `value`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "verify_derivative" => "`verify_derivative(f, x, g)` checks whether `diff(f, x) == g`. An exact residual must be zero. Approximate residuals use the numerical tolerance.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "verify_integral" => "`verify_integral(f, x, F)` checks whether `diff(F, x) == f`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "verify_inverse" => "`verify_inverse(A, B)` checks whether the matrix product `A*B` equals the identity.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "verify_limit" => "`verify_limit(f, x, a, L, [dir])` checks the limit of `f` as `x` approaches `a` against `L`, which may be finite or signed infinity. The optional `plus` or `minus` selects one side. Uses the same calculus reducer as `limit`. A claim with insufficient evidence returns `false`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "verify_maxwell_relation_helmholtz" => "`verify_maxwell_relation_helmholtz(A, T, V)`: mixed-partial symmetry check `d^2A/dTdV - d^2A/dVdT` for Helmholtz free energy `A`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "verify_ode_solution" => "`verify_ode_solution(equation, y(x), solution)` checks whether the proposed `solution` for `y(x)` satisfies the differential equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "verify_solution" => "`verify_solution(eq_or_expr, subst)`: checks whether substituting `subst` into `eq_or_expr` satisfies the equation or expression (returns `true` or `false`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "vietoris_rips" => "Constructs the Vietoris-Rips simplicial complex `vietoris_rips(points, eps[, max_dim])` for a point cloud at radius `eps` (`max_dim` defaults to `2`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "vietoris_rips_filtration" => "Constructs the Vietoris-Rips 1-skeleton filtration `list(list(eps, K), ...)` over `steps + 1` radii up to `max_eps`. The Vietoris-Rips filtration restricted to its 1-skeleton: for each of `steps + 1` radii `max_epsilon * s / steps` the vertices and the edges no longer than the radius.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "volterra_solve" => "`volterra_solve(f, lambda, K, x, t, a)`: exact solution of the Volterra integral equation `y(x) = f(x) + lambda int_a^x K(x, t) y(t) dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "volterra_successive" => "`volterra_successive(f, lambda, K, x, t, a, n)`: `n`-th Picard iterate for `y(x) = f(x) + lambda int_a^x K(x, t) y(t) dt`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "volterra_to_ode" => "`volterra_to_ode(f, lambda, K, x, t, a, y)`: equivalent differential equation for the Volterra integral equation.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "volume_element" => "`volume_element(g, vars)`: Riemannian volume density `sqrt(|det(g)|)` of metric `g` in `vars`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "volume_integral" => "`volume_integral(f, vars, bounds)`: iterated multiple integral of `f` over `vars` with `bounds = list(list(a, b), ...)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wave_fd" => "`wave_fd(c, u0, v0, bc_val, t_end, steps)`: finite-difference wave equation solver on the unit domain with uniform Dirichlet boundary `bc_val`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wave_fd_bc" => "`wave_fd_bc(c, u0, v0, bc, bc_val, t_end, steps)`: finite-difference wave equation solver with per-face Dirichlet/Neumann boundary condition `bc`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wavedec" => "`wavedec(x, wavelet, levels)`: multilevel periodized discrete wavelet decomposition `list(a_L, d_L, ..., d_1)`. Multilevel decomposition: `[approximation_L, detail_L, ..., detail_1]`. Requires `2^levels` to divide the length, as before; use [`wavedec_with`] for the general case. # Errors [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when `2^levels` does not divide the length, or the length is shorter than the filter at some level.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wavelet_packet" => "`wavelet_packet(x, wavelet, levels)`: equal-width leaf bands of a complete periodic wavelet packet tree, with each low child before its high sibling. Decomposes both the low and high branches of a periodic wavelet transform. Returns `2^levels` equally sized leaf bands in tree order, with each low child before its high sibling. For example, level two returns `LL`, `LH`, `HL`, `HH`. Level zero returns one band containing the input. # Errors [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when the signal is empty or its length is incompatible with the requested depth.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "waverec" => "`waverec(coeffs, wavelet)`: reconstruct signal from a `wavedec` coefficient list. Inverse of [`wavedec`]. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] for an inconsistent list.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "weak_compositions" => "`weak_compositions(n, k)`: number of weak compositions of `n` into `k` non-negative integer parts, `C(n + k - 1, k - 1)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "webere" => "`webere(nu, x)`: Weber function `E_nu(x) = (1/pi) int_0^pi sin(nu*theta - x*sin(theta)) dtheta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wedge" => "`wedge(omega, eta)`: exterior (wedge) product `omega ^ eta` of differential forms. The wedge sum, gluing vertex `vk` of `K` to vertex `vl` of `L`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "weibull" => "`weibull(shape, scale)`: Weibull distribution with shape parameter `shape` and scale parameter `scale`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "weierstrass_e" => "`weierstrass_e(L, y, x, p)`: computes the Weierstrass excess function `E = L(x, y, p) - L(x, y, y') - (p - y') * dL/dy'`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "weierstrass_p_complex" => "`weierstrass_p_complex(zr, zi, w1r, w1i, w2r, w2i)`: `list(re, im)` of `wp(z; w1, w2)`. Weierstrass elliptic function `wp(z; omega1, omega2)` via Jacobi theta functions.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "weight_multiplicity" => "`weight_multiplicity(T, hw, w)`: multiplicity of weight `w` in the irrep of type `T` with highest weight `hw`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "welch_test" => "`welch_test(xs, ys, delta)`: Welch's two-sample t-test returning `list(statistic, p_value)` for samples `xs` and `ys` with hypothesised difference `delta`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "whittaker_m_complex" => "`whittaker_m_complex(kr, ki, mr, mi, zr, zi)`: `list(re, im)` of `M_{kappa, mu}(z)`. Whittaker function `M_{kappa, mu}(z) = exp(-z/2) * z^(mu + 1/2) * 1F1(mu - kappa + 1/2; 2*mu + 1; z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "whittaker_w_complex" => "`whittaker_w_complex(kr, ki, mr, mi, zr, zi)`: `list(re, im)` of `W_{kappa, mu}(z)`. Whittaker function `W_{kappa, mu}(z) = exp(-z/2) * z^(mu + 1/2) * U(mu - kappa + 1/2, 2*mu + 1, z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wien_peak_wavelength" => "`wien_peak_wavelength(T)`: Wien's displacement peak wavelength `b / T` in meters.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wigner_3j" => "`wigner_3j(j1, j2, j3, m1, m2, m3)`: exact Wigner 3j symbol computed from the corresponding Clebsch-Gordan coefficient (half-integers allowed).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wilson_interval" => "`wilson_interval(k, n, c)`: Wilson score confidence interval `list(lo, hi)` for a binomial proportion at critical value `c`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "winding_number" => "`winding_number(p, C)` returns the signed number of turns of the circle or closed polygon `C` around `p`. Counterclockwise turns are positive. Points outside a simple contour have winding number zero. A point on the boundary has no winding number and leaves the request unevaluated.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "winsorized_mean" => "`winsorized_mean(x, alpha)`: symmetric Winsorized mean of `x`, clamping fraction `alpha` in each tail. Mean after replacing the lowest and highest fraction `trim` by the nearest retained values.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wirtinger_dz" => "`wirtinger_dz(f, z)`: Wirtinger derivative `1/2 * (d/dx - I * d/dy) f(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wirtinger_dzbar" => "`wirtinger_dzbar(f, z)`: Wirtinger derivative `1/2 * (d/dx + I * d/dy) f(z)` (vanishes for holomorphic `f`).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wkb_energy" => "`wkb_energy(c, nu, m, n)`: Bohr-Sommerfeld WKB energy level `n` for potential `V(x) = c |x|^nu` and mass `m`, in closed form via the beta function.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "work_constant_force" => "`work_constant_force(F, d)`: mechanical work `F . d` by a constant force `F` over displacement `d`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "work_isothermal_expansion" => "`work_isothermal_expansion(n, R, T, V1, V2)`: reversible isothermal ideal-gas work `n R T ln(V2 / V1)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "work_line_integral" => "`work_line_integral(F, curve, t, a, b)`: line integral of force field `F` along `curve(t)` for `t in [a, b]`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "wright_omega_complex" => "`wright_omega_complex(re, im)`: `list(re, im)` of `omega(z)`. Complex Wright omega function `omega(z)` solving `omega + ln(omega) = z` on the principal branch (Corless-Jeffrey Halley iteration).\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "xor" => "`xor(p1, p2, ...)`: variadic exclusive-or (parity), true when an odd number of operands are true.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "z_interval" => "`z_interval(sample, sigma, level)`: confidence interval `list(lo, hi)` for the population mean with known standard deviation `sigma` at level `level`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "z_test" => "`z_test(sample, mu, sigma)`: one-sample z-test returning `list(statistic, p_value)` for `sample` against mean `mu` with known standard deviation `sigma`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "zeilberger" => "`zeilberger(F, k, n)`: applies Zeilberger's creative telescoping to hypergeometric term `F(n, k)` over bound `k`, returning recurrence and certificate.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "zeros" => "`zeros(m, n)`: `m x n` zero matrix. Zero matrix with `rows` rows and `cols` columns.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "zeros_of" => "`zeros_of(f, z)`: list of `list(q, order)` pairs for the zeros of `f(z)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "zeta" => "`zeta(s)`: Riemann zeta function `sum_{n=1}^infty n^{-s}`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "zigzag" => "`zigzag(n)`: `n`-th Euler zigzag (up/down) number, counting alternating permutations of `n` elements.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "zscores" => "`zscores(xs)`: z-score standardization `(x_i - mean) / std` of list `xs`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
    "ztransform" => "`ztransform(f, n, z)`: unilateral Z-transform `sum_{n=0}^oo f(n) * z^(-n)`.\n\nReturns an unevaluated term; call `compute` to reduce it.",
)

